\section{Configuration}

The todo system stores its data in a configurable directory, defaulting
to [[~/.nytid/todos]]. We also allow configuring the maximum deadline
boost.  The work hours and work days that [[nytid schedule show]] uses
when slotting todos into free time used to be defined here as well;
they now live in the shared [[cli.utils.workweek]] module
(\cref{cli.utils.workweek}), because [[track]] needs them too and
cannot import this module without creating an import cycle.  Their
configuration keys keep the [[todo.]] prefix for that historical
reason.

Why a separate directory from track data? Because todos have a different
lifecycle---they are created, prioritized, and completed, whereas track
entries are append-only historical records. Keeping them separate avoids
coupling the two storage formats.

<<configuration>>=
TODO_DIR_CONFIG = "todo.data_dir"
TODO_MAX_BOOST_CONFIG = "todo.max_boost"
@

Resolving that directory must be free of side effects.  Almost every
subcommand asks for it---including the ones that only read, such as
[[nytid todo status]] and the shell completions that run on every
prompt.  An earlier version wrote the default back into the
configuration file the first time the key was missing, so merely
listing todos rewrote the user's configuration; it also re-read the
configuration file from disk on every single path lookup.

Routing the lookup through [[default]]
(\cref{cli.utils.defaults.cache}) removes both problems at once.
That helper answers from a process-lifetime cache built by a
[[Config]] object constructed \emph{without} a [[conf_file]]
argument, so it cannot write back even in principle, and the file is
read at most once per process.  The default location is consequently
no longer persisted: we recompute it from [[pathlib.Path.home()]] on
each call, which also keeps the answer correct for tests and scripts
that move [[HOME]] after import.

[[default]] applies its [[cast]] argument to the \emph{stored} value
only, never to the fallback, so passing [[cast=pathlib.Path]] would
leave the fallback a [[Path]] and the configured value a [[Path]] by
a different route.  We instead hand it a string and convert once,
which makes both branches take the same path through
[[pathlib.Path]].

<<configuration>>=
def get_todo_dir() -> pathlib.Path:
    """Get the todo data directory path."""
    fallback = (
        pathlib.Path.home() / ".nytid" / "todos"
    )
    return pathlib.Path(
        default(TODO_DIR_CONFIG, str(fallback))
    )


def get_todo_file() -> pathlib.Path:
    """Get the path to the todos JSON file."""
    return get_todo_dir() / "todos.json"


DEFAULT_MAX_BOOST = 10.0


def get_max_boost() -> float:
    """Get the maximum deadline boost value."""
    return default(TODO_MAX_BOOST_CONFIG,
                   DEFAULT_MAX_BOOST, float)
@

Worker-specific defaults need a different treatment from the global
settings above.%
\label{todo:worker-defaults}
A teacher may want every task for [[dan-claude]] to run
[[opencode]] in one working directory with one captured environment,
without baking those choices into each todo item.

We therefore read worker defaults lazily from paths of the form
[[todo.worker.<worker>.<attribute>]].  Missing keys simply mean
no default.  These values are consulted only when a loaded todo leaves
the corresponding attribute unset, so explicit task metadata still wins.
For [[env]] the config value should be a JSON object so it maps cleanly
to a Python dictionary.

Lazy here means \emph{often}, not \emph{rarely}.  The lookup hangs off
attribute access, through [[TodoItem.__getattribute__]] below, so
listing a hundred unassigned todos and reading each one's [[command]],
[[wd]] and [[env]] asks the configuration three hundred
questions.  Answering each
from disk, as [[typerconf.get]] does, is what makes that shape
expensive; answering them from the cached, write-free [[Config]] behind
[[default]] (\cref{cli.utils.defaults.cache}) makes the count
irrelevant.  Passing [[None]] as the fallback keeps the contract the
callers already rely on---a missing key still reads as
\enquote{no default}---so only the cost changes, not the meaning.

<<configuration>>=
WORKER_DEFAULT_ATTRIBUTES = (
    "command",
    "wd",
    "env",
)


def get_worker_default(worker, attribute):
    """Return the configured default for one worker attribute."""
    if not worker or not attribute:
        return None
    value = default(
        f"todo.worker.{worker}.{attribute}", None
    )

    if attribute == "env":
        if not isinstance(value, dict):
            return None
        return {
            str(key): str(val)
            for key, val in value.items()
        }
    return value
@

Let's verify that the many lookups really do collapse into one read.
Two attributes of the same worker are the smallest case that would
have cost two disk reads before, and the count must stay at one no
matter how many attributes we ask about.

<<test functions>>=
def test_get_worker_default_reads_config_once(
    monkeypatch,
):
    """Repeated worker-default lookups share the cached config."""
    fake = MagicMock()
    fake.get.return_value = "opencode"
    monkeypatch.setattr(
        defaults.typerconf, "Config", lambda: fake
    )
    defaults._reset_cache()
    try:
        assert get_worker_default(
            "dan-claude", "command"
        ) == "opencode"
        get_worker_default("dan-claude", "wd")
        assert fake.read_config.call_count == 1
    finally:
        defaults._reset_cache()
@

One launch decision is a preference rather than a fact about the
task: whether starting a task that has a working directory but no
command should open a shell there (\cref{todo:start-settle}).  A
teacher who works in tracked tmux windows and reads the task from
the prompt never wants that shell; an agent worker always does.  So
the preference is resolved like auto-suspend is in [[track]]
(\cref{track:auto-suspend}): the command-line flag wins, then the
worker's own key, then the global default, and finally the
historical behaviour of opening the shell.  The keys are read
through the cached [[default]] helper for the same reason the
worker defaults above are, and a missing key is simply the next
fallback.  We accept the same spellings of \emph{true} that
[[track]] does, so a user who has set [[track.auto-suspend]] can
set this key the same way.

<<constants>>=
SHELL_CONFIG = "todo.shell"
<<configuration>>=
def should_spawn_shell(worker, flag):
    """Resolve whether a wd-only start opens a shell.

    Checks, in order: the `--shell/--no-shell` flag, the
    `todo.shell.<worker>` key, the `todo.shell.default` key, and
    finally `True`.
    """
    if flag is not None:
        return flag
    for key in (
        f"{SHELL_CONFIG}.{worker}",
        f"{SHELL_CONFIG}.default",
    ):
        value = default(key, None)
        if value is not None:
            return str(value).lower() in ("true", "1", "yes")
    return True
@

Let's verify the resolution order: the flag beats the worker key,
the worker key beats the default, and both spellings of the value
are understood.

<<test functions>>=
def test_should_spawn_shell_resolution_order():
    """Flag, then worker key, then default, then True."""
    with patched_worker_config({}):
        assert should_spawn_shell("me", None) is True
        assert should_spawn_shell("me", False) is False
    with patched_worker_config({"todo.shell.default": False}):
        assert should_spawn_shell("me", None) is False
        assert should_spawn_shell("me", True) is True
    with patched_worker_config({
        "todo.shell.default": "false",
        "todo.shell.dan-claude": "yes",
    }):
        assert should_spawn_shell("me", None) is False
        assert should_spawn_shell("dan-claude", None) is True
@

