\section{Data Model}

\subsection{The TodoItem Dataclass}

Each todo item carries an integer ID, a title, labels that connect to
the track system, an optional numeric priority, a
status, timestamps for when it was created and when it was closed
(\cref{sec:todo-closed}), an optional deadline, an estimated duration in
hours, a longer description, the parents of a sub-item
(\cref{sec:todo-parents-field}), and an optional worker assignment.

To support work-session management (inspired by the UNIX [[at]]
command), each item can also capture the working directory and
environment at creation time, extended notes for the editor, and a
default command to run when starting the task. The [[wd]] and [[env]]
fields together let [[todo start]] restore the exact context in which
the task was conceived---solving the common problem of \enquote{where
was I working on this?} The [[worktree]] field refines [[wd]] for
tasks in a git repository: it names a git worktree (see
\cref{track:worktrees}) that [[todo start]] creates or reuses under
the repository containing [[wd]], so a task always resumes on its own
branch checkout.  The [[command]] field defaults to [[$SHELL]]
but can be set to tools like [[claude]] or [[opencode]].

The optional [[github]] field links a todo to a GitHub issue or pull
request, enabling bidirectional synchronization. We store
the repository, item type, and number as a dictionary rather than
a URL string because the [[gh]] CLI needs these components
individually---parsing them back from a URL would be fragile and
redundant.

The [[claimed_by]] field is runtime state rather than task data: it
names the worker whose session is working on the item while it is
on the active stack, and is empty otherwise.  It is separate from
[[who]] because the two answer different questions.  [[who]] is the
assignee, whose queue the item is in; [[claimed_by]] is the
claimant, who started it.  An agent picking up a task from the
user's queue claims it without becoming its assignee, and changing
the assignee of a running task does not change who is running it
(\cref{sec:todo-claim}).  [[ls]] lists it after its computed columns,
so that the columns of the headerless listing format keep their
positions.  [[context_claim]]
follows it and says how the claim was made: [[True]] when the item
was activated only as context for a descendant the claimant started,
which is what lets the claim go again when that descendant stops
(\cref{sec:todo-release-context}).

The [[discovered_from]] field records provenance rather than
placement: the ID of the todo whose work turned this one up.  It is
separate from [[parents]] because the two answer different
questions.  A parent says what an item is \emph{part of}, and
completing every part completes the whole; the item an agent stumbled
on while fixing something else is usually not part of that fix at all,
and filing it there would hold the fix open until the stray item was
done.  [[discovered_from]] keeps the context without that consequence
(\cref{sec:todo-discovered-from}).  It follows [[context_claim]], in
the dataclass and in [[ls]]'s records alike.

The [[blocked_by]] field is the third link between items, beside the
parents and the provenance: the IDs of the todos that have to be done
before this one can be started (\cref{sec:todo-blocked-by-field}).
Unlike a parent, a blocker says nothing about what the item is part
of, and unlike the provenance it changes what happens: an item with an
open blocker is not ready (\cref{sec:todo-blockers}).  It is the newest
stored field and so comes last, after [[discovered_from]].

We use a dataclass rather than a plain class because the fields are
pure data with no complex invariants---unlike [[ActiveSession]] in
track, which manages mutable state with batch semantics.

Worker defaults are runtime policy, not persisted task data.  That
distinction matters when an item is loaded, viewed, and then saved again:
we want the in-memory object to behave as if missing [[command]],
[[wd]], or [[env]] were filled from the worker's config, but we do not
want an unrelated edit to write those defaults into [[todos.json]].

We solve that tension inside [[TodoItem]] itself.  Attribute reads for a
small set of worker-scoped fields consult [[get_worker_default()]] when
the stored value is [[None]], while [[to_dict()]] serializes the raw
stored values from [[__dict__]] rather than the runtime-resolved ones.
This keeps worker defaults memory-only even when a loaded todo passes
through a save cycle.  In other words, loading applies policy, while
saving preserves only what the item explicitly stored before the load.

The local holding the resolved value is called [[configured]] rather
than the [[default]] the name would suggest, because
[[get_worker_default]] reaches the configuration through a
module-level helper of exactly that name; binding it locally here
would shadow that helper for anyone who later needs it in this method,
and Python would resolve the call to a string instead of raising.

<<data model>>=
@dataclass
class TodoItem:
    """A single todo item with priority and metadata.

    Items are stored in a JSON file and sorted by
    effective priority for display and scheduling.

    A `priority` of `None` means unranked; omitting it
    ranks the item at `0.0` instead, so an unranked
    item has to ask for `None` explicitly.
    """

    id: int
    title: str
    labels: list[str] = field(default_factory=list)
    priority: float | None = 0.0
    status: str = "pending"
    created: str = ""
    deadline: str | None = None
    estimated: float | None = None
    description: str = ""
    parents: list[int] = field(
        default_factory=lambda: [ROOT_ID]
    )
    who: str | None = None
    notes: str = ""
    wd: str | None = None
    worktree: str | None = None
    env: dict[str, str] | None = None
    command: str | None = None
    github: dict[str, str] | None = None
    closed: str | None = None
    claimed_by: str | None = None
    context_claim: bool = False
    discovered_from: int | None = None
    blocked_by: list[int] = field(default_factory=list)

    def to_dict(self) -> dict:
        """Convert explicit values to a JSON-serializable dict.

        Besides the stored fields, the dict carries
        `parent_id`, derived from `parents`, for readers
        that predate `parents`.
        """
        data = {}
        for f in self.__dataclass_fields__.values():
            if f.name == "parents":
                <<derive [[parent_id]] for older readers>>
            data[f.name] = self.__dict__.get(f.name)
        return data

    def __getattribute__(self, name):
        """Return explicit value or worker default.

        Only worker-scoped runtime fields participate in
        this fallback.
        """
        if name in WORKER_DEFAULT_ATTRIBUTES:
            raw = object.__getattribute__(
                self, "__dict__"
            ).get(name)
            if raw is None:
                worker = object.__getattribute__(
                    self, "__dict__"
                ).get("who") or default_username
                configured = get_worker_default(
                    worker, name
                )
                if configured is not None:
                    return configured
        return object.__getattribute__(self, name)

    <<normalize labels on assignment>>

    @classmethod
    def from_dict(cls, data: dict) -> "TodoItem":
        """Create from dictionary (JSON deserialization).

        Unknown keys are silently ignored for forward
        compatibility. Missing priority keeps the legacy
