\subsection{Ranked and Unranked Priorities}%
\label{sec:ranked-and-unranked}

[[priority]] carries two states rather than one number.  An item is
\emph{ranked} when it holds a float---which is [[0.0]] when nobody
said otherwise---and \emph{unranked} when it holds [[None]].  These
are not two ways of saying \enquote{no priority yet}: [[0.0]] is a
position in the ordering like any other, while [[None]] means the item
has no position at all and must not be compared numerically with
anything.

The constructor default is a number because the field predates the
unranked state.  A [[todos.json]] written before [[None]] became
possible has no [[priority]] key at all, and [[from_dict]] fills a
missing key from this very default; making it [[None]] would silently
unrank every item in every such file, which would move all of them to
the tail of the display order at once.  The asymmetry that buys us is
that the unranked state is reachable only by \emph{asking} for it:
passing [[priority=None]] to the constructor, or writing an explicit
[[null]] in the JSON.  Omitting the argument---in a caller, and just
as easily in a test---yields an item ranked at zero, not an unranked
one.

The distinction is load-bearing rather than cosmetic.
[[effective_priority]] returns [[None]] for an unranked item and
[[todo_display_sort_key]] sorts those into a separate tail
(\cref{todo:effective-priority}); [[require_stored_priority]] and the
placement helpers raise instead of guessing a number
(\cref{todo:placement}); [[compute_child_priority_range]] refuses an
unranked parent and skips unranked siblings
(\cref{sec:child-priority-helpers}); [[reprioritize]] with no ID
selects exactly the unranked items; and [[--skip-priority]] exists to
create them.  An item that fell into ranked-at-zero by accident is
therefore not merely mislabelled---it is silently admitted to every
comparison those helpers were written to keep it out of.

Let's pin all three doors into the two states, since it is precisely
the one that does \emph{not} lead where its shape suggests that costs
callers their afternoon:

<<test functions>>=
def test_priority_default_is_ranked_at_zero():
  """Only an explicit null yields an unranked item."""
  assert TodoItem(id=1, title="Test").priority == 0.0
  assert TodoItem.from_dict(
    {"id": 1, "title": "Test"}
  ).priority == 0.0
  assert TodoItem.from_dict(
    {"id": 1, "title": "Test", "priority": None}
  ).priority is None
@

\subsection{Labels Are a Set, Not a List}

Conceptually a todo's labels form a \emph{set}: applying the label
[[grading]] twice means the same thing as applying it once, and the order
in which labels were attached carries no meaning.  Storing them as a plain
[[list[str]]] lets duplicates accumulate --- for example when a child
inherits an ancestor's labels and then is given one of those same labels
again --- and a doubled label renders twice wherever labels are displayed.

We normalize on \emph{assignment} rather than in [[__post_init__]] because
labels are mutated in place after construction (the CLI-flags path and the
interactive editor both rebind [[item.labels]] directly).  A
[[__post_init__]] hook runs only once, at construction, so it would miss
those later writes.  Routing every write through [[__setattr__]] guarantees
construction, deserialization, inheritance, and edits all funnel through
one normalization point.  This mirrors what [[TrackingEntry]] already does
with [[sorted(list(set(labels)))]].  The visible consequence is that todo
labels now display in alphabetical order instead of insertion order, which
matches the [[track]] command.

<<normalize labels on assignment>>=
def __setattr__(self, name, value):
    """Keep `labels` a sorted, duplicate-free list,
    `parents` an ordered one (see `normalize_parents`) and
    `blocked_by` a sorted one (see `normalize_blockers`)."""
    if name == "labels" and value is not None:
        value = sorted(set(value))
    <<normalize [[parents]] on assignment>>
    <<normalize [[blocked_by]] on assignment>>
    object.__setattr__(self, name, value)
