\subsection{An Item's Parents}%
\label{sec:todo-parents-field}

An item lists its parents in [[parents]], a list of ids, and the top
level is a parent like the others, under the id [[ROOT_ID]]
(\cref{sec:todo-parents}).  [[[0] ]] means top-level only, [[[12] ]]
only under~\#12, and [[[0, 12] ]] both.  The list lives on the item,
rather than in a separate list of parent--child links, because the item
is what every command loads, edits and saves: a link stored elsewhere
would be one more thing to keep consistent with the items under the
lock, and one more place a hand edit of [[todos.json]] could get wrong.

Naming the top level by an id, instead of a separate
\enquote{top-level} flag next to the list, leaves no combination that
means nothing.  A flag that is false next to an empty list would be an
item that is nowhere; with the root among the ids, being top-level is
membership like any other, and an item is somewhere as long as the list
is not empty.  We make sure it never is.

Like the labels, the list is normalized on every assignment, for the
same reason: it is set in many places, by the constructor,
deserialization and the commands, and one normalization point covers
them all.  Unlike the labels, it is not sorted, because its order means
something: without other context, an item's line runs through its first
parent that is an item ([[primary_parent]]).  So a repeated id keeps its
first place.  An empty list becomes [[[ROOT_ID] ]], because an item
with no parent is a top-level item.  A [[None]] in the list is dropped,
so that a command holding an optional parent, where [[None]] means the
top level just as it did for [[parent_id]], can pass [[[parent] ]]
without a case of its own; and a single id where a list should be, as a
hand edit may leave it, is read as that one parent.

Anything else is refused.  An id is a non-negative integer, and a
value that merely looks like one---the string [[12]], the float
[[12.0]], a list [[[12] ]] nested in the list---is not, however
plausible the guess: [[1.5]] or [[true]] has no plausible guess at all,
and a rule that repairs some values and not others is one more thing a
person editing the file would have to know.  A boolean is excluded
explicitly, since Python counts [[True]] as the integer one.  The
refusal is its own exception, [[InvalidParentsError]], rather than a
[[ValueError]], because the file reader turns a [[ValueError]] into an
empty list and the next save would then wipe the file; the reader
instead stops with an error that names the file and the item
(\cref{sec:todo-refused-files}).

<<helpers>>=
class InvalidParentsError(Exception):
    """A parents value that is neither a todo id nor a list of
    them."""


def is_parent_id(value):
    """Tell whether `value` can be a parent id: a non-negative
    integer that is not a boolean."""
    return (
        isinstance(value, int)
        and not isinstance(value, bool)
        and value >= 0
    )


def normalize_parents(parents):
    """Return `parents` as a list of parent ids in order.

    Repeated ids keep their first place, `None` entries are
    dropped, a single id counts as a list of one, and an
    empty result means the top level, `[ROOT_ID]`.  Raises
    `InvalidParentsError` for anything that is not an id.
    """
    if parents is None or is_parent_id(parents):
        parents = [parents]
    if not isinstance(parents, (list, tuple)):
        raise InvalidParentsError(
            f"parents {parents!r}, which is not a list of ids"
        )
    normalized = []
    for parent_id in parents:
        if parent_id is None:
            continue
        if not is_parent_id(parent_id):
            raise InvalidParentsError(
                f"parent {parent_id!r}, which is not a todo id"
            )
        if parent_id not in normalized:
            normalized.append(int(parent_id))
    return normalized or [ROOT_ID]
<<normalize [[parents]] on assignment>>=
if name == "parents":
    value = normalize_parents(value)
@

