Files written before [[parents]] existed store a single [[parent_id]]
instead, [[null]] for a top-level item.  [[from_dict]] migrates it:
[[null]] becomes [[[0] ]] and [[N]] becomes [[[N] ]], both through the
normalization above.

A file that has both keys normally has them in agreement, since
[[to_dict]] derives one from the other (below).  When they disagree,
someone changed [[parent_id]] after nytid wrote the file: by hand, with
[[jq]], or with a script written before [[parents]] existed---users
have always been able to move an item that way.  We honour the edit as
a move, as [[edit --parent]] would make it: the item's parents become
[[[parent_id] ]], or the top level for [[null]].  Refusing the file
would punish a change that says clearly what was meant, and ignoring
it would silently undo that change on the next save.  A move drops the
item's other places, though, which the person who edited one key may
not have thought of, so we say what happened on standard error.  The
warning repeats on every load until a command saves the file, after
which the keys agree again.

