The other direction matters as much, because an older version of
nytid may read the same file: the deployment on another machine,
say.  It ignores keys it does not know, so without a [[parent_id]] it
would show every sub-item as a top-level item.  [[to_dict]] therefore
also writes [[parent_id]], derived from [[parents]]: the first parent
that is an item, or [[null]] for an item that is only top-level.  That
is the answer an older version can understand, and for an item with a
single parent it is the whole truth.  An older version that
\emph{saves} the file writes back only [[parent_id]], so further
parents are lost then; that is why the deployment must be upgraded
before an item is given a second parent.

The derived key comes right before [[parents]], which keeps it at the
position [[parent_id]] has always had in the dictionary.  Readers that
take the keys in order, the headerless listing formats among them,
find it where they always did; [[ls]] moves [[parents]] itself behind
its computed columns (\cref{sec:todo-ls-record}).  The derived key is
read back only to notice a hand edit (above), and
[[replay_item_edits]] does not copy it, since it is not a field
(\cref{sec:todo-read-modify-write}).

