\subsection{What an Item Waits For}%
\label{sec:todo-blocked-by-field}

An item lists its blockers in [[blocked_by]], a list of ids, empty by
default.  The link is stored on the blocked item and not on the
blocker for the reason [[parents]] is stored on the child: the question
every command asks is \enquote{can this item start?}, and the answer
should be in the item it is asked about.  The other direction,
\enquote{what does this item hold up?}, is a scan of the list, which
[[view]] makes (\cref{sec:todo-view-blockers}).

Like the labels, and unlike the parents, the list is a set: being
blocked by \#4 twice means what being blocked by it once means, and the
order the blockers were added in says nothing, whereas the first parent
decides an item's line (\cref{sec:todo-parents-field}).  So the list is sorted
and repeats are dropped, on every assignment, by the same
[[__setattr__]].  An item never waits for itself, and that entry is
dropped as well: it could never be satisfied, and would make the item
stuck for good (\cref{sec:todo-stuck}).  The item's own id is known
when [[blocked_by]] is assigned, because the dataclass assigns [[id]]
first.

A blocker that does not exist is kept, and counts as closed.  The
blocker may have been removed with [[rm]], and removing a todo has never
had to visit the items that point at it, as with [[discovered_from]];
work that waited for something which no longer exists has nothing left
to wait for.  What is refused is a value that is not an id at all, for
the reasons and in the way [[parents]] refuses one
(\cref{sec:todo-refused-files}): the exception is a kind of
[[InvalidParentsError]], so the file reader stops on it, naming the item,
instead of reading an empty list.  Zero is refused too, since it is the
top level's id and not a todo's.

<<helpers>>=
class InvalidBlockersError(InvalidParentsError):
    """A blocked_by value that is not a list of todo ids."""


def normalize_blockers(blockers, own_id=None):
    """Return `blockers` as a sorted list of todo ids.

    Repeats and `own_id` are dropped, and `None` or a single
    id is read as a list.  Raises `InvalidBlockersError` for
    anything that is not the id of a todo.
    """
    if blockers is None:
        return []
    if is_parent_id(blockers):
        blockers = [blockers]
    if not isinstance(blockers, (list, tuple)):
        raise InvalidBlockersError(
            f"blocked_by {blockers!r}, which is not a list of ids"
        )
    for blocker in blockers:
        if not is_parent_id(blocker) or blocker == ROOT_ID:
            raise InvalidBlockersError(
                f"blocker {blocker!r}, which is not a todo id"
            )
    return sorted({b for b in blockers if b != own_id})
<<normalize [[blocked_by]] on assignment>>=
if name == "blocked_by":
    value = normalize_blockers(value, self.__dict__.get("id"))
