\subsection{When an Item Was Closed}%
\label{sec:todo-closed}

[[created]] says when a piece of work entered the list; [[closed]] says
when it left.  It holds the moment the item last became done, in the
same local ISO~8601 form that [[created]] uses, so the two parse and
compare alike.  It is [[None]] for every item that is not done---and
also for an item that was completed before the field existed.  Nobody
recorded when that happened, and an invented time would look exactly
like a real one.

The field is declared last, not next to [[created]] where a reader
would look for it.  The order of the stored fields is the order of the
columns that [[ls]] writes to a pipe, and those rows have no header:
scripts pick a column by its position, as in [[cut -f12]]
(\cref{sec:todo-ls-dialect}).  A field inserted in the middle would
shift every column after it and silently hand those scripts the wrong
values.  Declaring it last is not quite enough on its own, because
[[ls]] appends computed columns after the stored ones; [[ls_record]]
therefore moves [[closed]] behind those as well
(\cref{sec:todo-ls-record}).  Any later stored field goes at the end
in both places for the same reason.

Keeping [[closed]] in step with [[status]] looks like a job for
[[__setattr__]], which already normalizes [[labels]].  It is the wrong
place, because [[from_dict]] builds a loaded item by handing every
stored field to the constructor, and the constructor assigns them
through [[__setattr__]] too.  A hook that stamped the time whenever
[[status]] became [[done]] would fire on every load of every done item.
Each one would get a closing time of whenever it was last read, and the
next save would write that time to disk.  Normalizing labels on every
assignment is safe because sorting a set twice changes nothing.
Stamping a time is not: it records an event, and the event is a status
\emph{change}, which a load is not.

So the change goes through a function instead, and every command that
completes or reopens an item calls [[set_status]] rather than assigning
[[status]].  That is [[done]] and its forced completion of descendants,
the automatic completion of a parent, the outcome of a run, and a
[[sync]] or [[import]] that finds an issue closed on GitHub.  The
status cache that the active stack rewrites on every save
(\cref{sec:todo-active-stack}) uses it too, which keeps [[closed]]
empty on every item that is not done.

Becoming done stamps the current time; any other status clears it, so a
reopened item does not carry the time of a completion that no longer
holds.  A caller that knows better when the work was finished passes
that time instead, as [[sync]] and [[import]] do with the time GitHub
closed the issue (\cref{sec:todo-github-closed}).

An item that is already done keeps its stamp.  Running
[[todo done]] twice, or a [[sync]] that meets a closed issue long after
the item was completed locally, says nothing new about when the work
finished.

<<data model>>=
def set_status(item, status, closed_at=None):
    """Set `item.status` and keep `item.closed` in step.

    Becoming `done` stamps `closed` with `closed_at`, a
    local ISO time, or the current local time if that is
    `None`, unless the item was done already; any other
    status clears it.
    """
    if status != "done":
        item.closed = None
    elif item.status != "done":
        item.closed = (
            closed_at or datetime.datetime.now().isoformat()
        )
    item.status = status
@

