The claim (\cref{sec:todo-claim}) is tied to the stack in the same
way: an item that is not active has no claimant.  Rather than
clearing [[claimed_by]] at every place that takes an item off the
stack---[[stop]], [[done]], [[rm]], the run finalizers, and the
normalization that drops done items---we clear it where the state is
read and written, so no path can forget.  Loading releases the claims
of inactive items, which also repairs a file written by an interrupted
command.  Saving writes them released but leaves the objects in memory
alone, unlike the status cache.  That difference is deliberate: the
command that has just taken an item off the stack still holds those
objects, and it needs to know whose tracking to stop next.

<<active stack helpers>>=
def release_inactive_claims(
    todos: list[TodoItem], stack: list[int]
):
    """Clear the claim on items not on the stack."""
    active_ids = set(stack)
    for item in todos:
        if item.id not in active_ids:
            item.claimed_by = None
            item.context_claim = False


def stored_todo_record(item, active_ids):
    """Return `item` as stored, unclaimed unless active."""
    record = item.to_dict()
    if item.id not in active_ids:
        record["claimed_by"] = None
        record["context_claim"] = False
    return record
