\paragraph{Files we refuse to read}%
\label{sec:todo-refused-files}

One stored id cannot be read at all: zero.  It is [[ROOT_ID]], the id
the top level has among parent ids (\cref{sec:todo-parents}), and the
commands never hand it out, since ids start at one.  A file edited by
hand can still contain it, and then every item whose parents list~0
might be top-level or a sub-item of that todo; nothing in the file
says which.

We refuse to load such a file rather than repair it.  Skipping the
item would lose it, because the next save writes back only what was
loaded.  Renumbering it would not lose it, but would silently change
an id that notes, branch names, tracking labels such as [[todo:0]]
and GitHub links may refer to, and it still could not tell which
items listing~0 were meant to be its children.  Only the person who
wrote the file can, so we name the file and the problem and stop.
The check is not a [[ValueError]], which the reader turns into an
empty list: that would make the next save wipe the file.  Exiting
inside a [[mutate_todo_state]] callback abandons the write instead
(\cref{sec:todo-read-modify-write}), so the file stays as it is.

<<refuse a todo whose id is the root's>>=
if any(t.id == ROOT_ID for t in todos):
    refuse_todo_file(
        todo_file,
        f"holds a todo with id {ROOT_ID}, which stands for the "
        "top level; give it another id, and its children their "
        "new parent",
    )
@

A [[parents]] value that is not a list of ids
(\cref{sec:todo-parents-field}) is refused the same way, and for the
same reasons: only the person who wrote it knows what it should have
been, and the alternative to stopping, an empty list, would cost the
whole file at the next save.  The error names the item, since the file
may hold hundreds.

<<read one stored todo or refuse the file>>=
try:
    todos.append(TodoItem.from_dict(record))
except InvalidParentsError as error:
    refuse_todo_file(
        todo_file,
