Both refusals share one helper, so that they look alike and both end
in [[typer.Exit]], whose meaning inside a callback is what keeps the
file safe.

<<active stack helpers>>=
def refuse_todo_file(todo_file, problem):
    """Exit with an error naming `todo_file` and `problem`.

    Inside a `mutate_todo_state` callback the exit also
    abandons the write, so the file stays as it is.
    """
    typer.echo(
        f"Error: {todo_file} {problem}; fix it by hand",
        err=True,
    )
    raise typer.Exit(1)
@

A stored [[next_id]] is repaired rather than refused, because its
meaning is not in doubt: it is the id the next [[add]] takes, and the
only values that work are those above every stored id.  One that is
too low, after a hand edit or a merge of two files, would make the
next item share an id with an old one, and one below~1 would hand out
the root's id or a negative one.  We take the largest of the stored
value, one, and one above the highest id in the file, so a correct
value is kept as it is.

<<keep [[next_id]] above every stored id>>=
next_id = max(
    data.get("next_id", 1), 1,
    *(t.id + 1 for t in todos),
)
