\subsection{Every Read-Modify-Write Goes Through the Lock}%
\label{sec:todo-read-modify-write}

[[mutate_todo_state]] protects exactly what happens inside its
callback, and nothing else.  A command that loads the todos with
[[load_todos]], changes its copy and hands the copy to [[save_todos]]
still races, even though [[save_todos]] takes the lock: the lock covers
the write, but the data being written was read before it.  Two
commands that overlap show what that costs.  [[todo add]] and
[[todo note]] start at the same moment and both read the file; [[note]]
stores its note; [[add]] then writes its copy plus the new item, and
the note is gone.  Two overlapping [[add]] commands are worse: both
read the same [[next_id]], so the later one replaces the earlier
item with a new one under the same ID.  These overlaps are not
exotic here, since agents run [[note]] and [[done]] from their own
sessions while the user works in the same list.

The rule every command follows is therefore:
\begin{description}
\item[Snapshot] A command may read the state without the lock to decide
  what to do: resolve which item is meant, ask the user to confirm or to
  compare priorities, open an editor, or talk to GitHub.
\item[Commit] The change itself is made inside one [[mutate_todo_state]]
  callback, on the state that callback receives.  The callback looks
  the items up again by ID, checks that what the decision assumed still
  holds, and takes any new IDs from the [[next_id]] it was given.
\end{description}

Prompts and network calls stay out of the callback because they take
unbounded time.  Holding the lock across a [[typer.confirm]] would
block every other [[nytid todo]] command, the agents' included, for as
long as the user takes to answer.

A check in the commit step can fail.  The commonest case is an item
that was removed while the command was waiting, and the answer is
then to stop with an error rather than to write the snapshot's copy
back, which would bring a deleted item back from the dead.  Raising
inside the callback is what makes this safe: [[mutate_todo_state]]
writes only after the callback returns, so an exception leaves the
files untouched.  Values the command worked out from the snapshot are,
by contrast, applied as they are.  A priority chosen by binary search
answers questions about the siblings the user was shown; if a sibling
changed meanwhile, the number is still where the user put the item,
and asking again would cost more than a slightly stale rank.

Most callbacks begin with the same look-up, so we give the
\enquote{no longer exists} error one home.

<<active stack helpers>>=
def require_todo(todos, todo_id):
    """Return the item with `todo_id` from a fresh state.

    Exits with an error if it is gone, which inside a
    `mutate_todo_state` callback also abandons the write.
    """
    item = next(
        (t for t in todos if t.id == todo_id), None
    )
    if item is None:
        typer.echo(
            f"Error: todo #{todo_id} no longer exists",
            err=True,
        )
        raise typer.Exit(1)
    return item
@

Commands that \emph{set} fields, such as [[edit]], need one more tool.
They change their snapshot copy across many chunks, some of which
prompt, so moving all of that into a callback would mean moving the
prompts under the lock too.  Instead we remember what the item looked
like when the command read it, let the command edit its copy as
before, and at commit time copy to the fresh item exactly the fields
that differ from the remembered version.  Those differences are what
the user asked for; every other field keeps whatever the store holds
\emph{now}, so a note or a status change that another process wrote
meanwhile survives the edit.

Comparing stored values is what makes the difference precise.
[[to_dict]] reports what the item holds rather than the worker defaults
that attribute reads fall back to (\cref{todo:worker-defaults}), so a
configured default never looks like an edit.  The remembered version
must be a deep copy, because [[to_dict]] hands out the item's own label
list and environment dictionary, which a later in-place change would
otherwise alter on both sides of the comparison.

Sometimes the right \enquote{before} is not what was read.  An editor
buffer is not stored verbatim: saving it strips the title and notes and
normalizes the front matter (\cref{sec:edit-via-editor}).  The changes
a user made in a buffer are therefore the differences from what saving
the \emph{untouched} buffer would store, and comparing with the raw
item instead would mistake a lost trailing newline for an edit.  That
baseline still differs from what was read, though, and saving an
untouched buffer has always tidied such values in the store.  The
optional [[read]] argument keeps that: a field the user left alone is
still written if the store holds exactly what was read, which means no
other process changed it meanwhile.  Without [[read]], the baseline is
what was read and the rule is the simple one above.

The replay is right for setting a field and wrong for adding to one: if
two commands both append to [[notes]], each sees its own text as the
change and the later one erases the earlier.  Appends are therefore
never replayed; they are performed inside the callback, as [[note]]
does.  The same goes for any edit that is relative to the current
value, such as [[edit --add-label]]: replaying the resulting label list
would drop a label that another process added meanwhile.  The [[skip]]
argument keeps such fields out of the replay, and the caller applies
the increment to the fresh item instead.

Only fields are replayed.  The dictionary also carries [[parent_id]],
which is derived from [[parents]] for older readers
(\cref{sec:todo-parents-field}); replaying it would create a stray
attribute of that name rather than move the item, and the move it
reflects arrives through [[parents]] anyway.

<<active stack helpers>>=
def item_fields(item):
    """Return a detached copy of the item's stored fields."""
    return copy.deepcopy(item.to_dict())


def replay_item_edits(
    target, before, edited, read=None, *, skip=(),
):
    """Copy to `target` the fields `edited` changed.

    `before` is the baseline `edited` started from,
    normally `item_fields` of the item as it was read.
    A field equal to `before` is left as `target` has
    it, unless `read` (the fields as read, if they
    differ from `before`) shows it unchanged in the
    store, in which case `edited`'s value is written.
    Fields named in `skip` are never copied.
    """
    if read is None:
        read = before
    current = target.to_dict()
    for key, value in edited.to_dict().items():
        if key in skip or key not in TodoItem.__dataclass_fields__:
            continue
        if (
            before.get(key) != value
            or current.get(key) == read.get(key)
        ):
            setattr(target, key, copy.deepcopy(value))
@

