Testing the rule needs the dangerous interleaving on demand rather than
by luck.  We get it by holding each thread at its first state read, the
unlocked snapshot, until every thread has taken one.  After that
barrier both commands are working from the same old state, which is
precisely the situation in which an unlocked save loses data.  The
barrier must not stop a later read: the reads inside
[[mutate_todo_state]] happen with the lock held, and a thread waiting
there for a partner that is itself waiting for the lock would never
wake up.

Each thread runs a real command and so takes the real lock.  That
matters because [[flock]] locks belong to an open file description:
[[mutate_todo_state]] opens the lock file afresh on every call, so two
threads exclude each other just as two processes would, and a mocked
lock would prove nothing.

<<test functions>>=
def run_with_shared_snapshot(*commands):
    """Run each command in its own thread from one old state.

    Every thread is held after its first todo-state read
    until all threads have made one.  Returns the results
    and re-raises the first exception from any thread.
    """
    import threading
    from concurrent.futures import ThreadPoolExecutor
    from nytid.cli import todo as todo_module

    real_load = todo_module.load_todo_state
    barrier = threading.Barrier(len(commands), timeout=10)
    held = set()

    def load_then_wait(*args, **kwargs):
        state = real_load(*args, **kwargs)
        me = threading.get_ident()
        if me not in held:
            held.add(me)
            barrier.wait()
        return state

    with patch(
        "nytid.cli.todo.load_todo_state",
        side_effect=load_then_wait,
    ):
        with ThreadPoolExecutor(len(commands)) as pool:
            futures = [pool.submit(c) for c in commands]
            return [f.result() for f in futures]
