\section{Sub-item Helpers}%
\label{todo:sub-item-helpers}

Sub-items give the flat todo list a structure.  We need helpers to
navigate it: finding parents and children, finding siblings (for
priority comparison), walking up and down, and checking whether a
parent's children are all done.

Why store sub-items flat, with each item naming its parents, rather
than nesting them?  Because flat storage simplifies JSON
serialization, avoids recursive data structures, and lets commands
like [[todo ls --flat]] work without flattening logic.

Priority comparison uses siblings---items sharing the same parent, and
for an item with several parents, the parent it is ranked under
(\cref{sec:ranking-context}).  When multiple workers share a parent
(say a course-level epic), each worker's priorities are independent:
it makes no sense to ask \enquote{is your grading task more urgent
than my lecture-prep task?}  The optional [[who]] parameter on
[[get_active_siblings]] restricts the comparison set to a single
worker so that binary-search prioritisation stays meaningful.

The active stack is global (all workers share one
[[active_stack.json]]), but stack-based commands like [[stop]] and
[[done]] should operate on the current worker's topmost item, not
another worker's. [[filter_ids_by_worker]] narrows a list of todo IDs
to those belonging to a given worker, treating [[who=None]] as
[[default_username]] for backward compatibility with older items.

\subsection{Parents, the top level, and context}%
\label{sec:todo-parents}

An item may have several parents, all equal, and it may be a
top-level item and a sub-item at the same time---a prerequisite shared
by two projects, say.  The items then form a directed acyclic graph
rather than a forest, and questions that have one answer in a tree,
such as \enquote{which is the parent?} or \enquote{what is the path
from the top?}, get several.  [[add --parent]] repeated and
[[edit --add-parent]] give an item several parents
(\cref{sec:todo-edit-places}), and a hand-edited [[todos.json]] can
too (\cref{sec:todo-parents-field}); every part of the module must
then still behave.

We ensure that by asking every such question through the helpers of
this subsection.  They are the only code that navigates by
[[parents]]; the few other places that touch the list treat it as a
stored value, not as a question about the graph.  [[edit]] compares
it with the snapshot to see whether the item moved and replaces it on
a move, and the placement helpers ([[set_priority_with_subtree]],
[[commit_flag_edits]]) keep a copy of it as part of the item's old
place.  Each helper answers its question as it is asked of a graph;
with one parent per item, the answer is exactly the one the tree code
it replaced gave.

The top level is treated as a parent of its own: a virtual root with
the id [[ROOT_ID]], zero.  That turns \enquote{top-level} from a special
case, a [[None]] where a parent should be, into membership like any
other.  The top-level items are simply the children of the root, and
an item that is both top-level and a sub-item will list the root among
its parents.  Zero is free, because item ids start at one, and it is
already what [[edit --parent 0]] means (\cref{sec:todo-edit-parent}).
The root is not an item, though: it appears among parent ids but never
in a list of items.  [[real_parents_of]] is the view without it, for
the many callers that go on to look the parents up as items.

<<constants>>=
ROOT_ID = 0
<<sub-item helpers>>=
def parents_of(item: TodoItem) -> list[int]:
    """Return the ids of `item`'s parents.

    The top level counts as a parent with the id `ROOT_ID`,
    so a top-level item answers `[ROOT_ID]`.
    """
    return item.parents


def real_parents_of(item: TodoItem) -> list[int]:
    """Return the ids of `item`'s parents that are items,
    that is, without `ROOT_ID`."""
    return [p for p in parents_of(item) if p != ROOT_ID]
@

Children follow from parents: the children of an id are the items that
list it among theirs, and the children of [[ROOT_ID]] are the
top-level items.  Siblings follow from children, but in a graph an
item has one set of siblings per parent, and only the caller knows
which parent it is asking about---the one the item is being ranked
under, for instance.  [[siblings_of]] therefore takes that parent as
an argument instead of guessing.  A caller with no particular parent
in mind passes the item's [[primary_parent]], defined below.

<<sub-item helpers>>=
def children_of(
    todos: list[TodoItem], parent_id: int
) -> list[TodoItem]:
    """Return the items in `todos` that have `parent_id`
    among their parents.

    `ROOT_ID` gives the top-level items.
    """
    return [t for t in todos if parent_id in parents_of(t)]


def siblings_of(
    todos: list[TodoItem],
    item: TodoItem,
    context_parent: int,
) -> list[TodoItem]:
    """Return the other children of `context_parent`.

    These are `item`'s siblings at the place under
    `context_parent`; `ROOT_ID` gives the other top-level
    items.
    """
    return [
        t for t in children_of(todos, context_parent)
        if t.id != item.id
    ]
@

The transitive walks are where a graph differs from a tree in a way
that can hurt.  Two paths may lead down to the same item, a diamond,
which a walk that trusts the shape would visit twice; and a cycle,
which a hand-edited file can contain whatever the commands allow,
would make the walk run forever.  Both walks keep the set of ids they
have seen, which settles both cases: the bottom of a diamond is listed
once, and a cycle ends where it closes.  The starting id is seen from
the outset, so an item never turns up among its own descendants or
ancestors even when a cycle leads back to it.

Direct children are the right unit for display and for priority
comparison, because both work one level at a time.  Deletion is
different: it is the one operation whose correctness depends on the
\emph{whole} subtree, since an item whose parent no longer exists is
treated as a visible root by [[get_visible_roots]] and would reappear
at the top of [[ls]].  That is what the transitive walk down is for.
[[descendants_of]] lists in depth-first order, each item before its
own children, as the tree walk it replaces did.  [[ancestors_of]]
follows \emph{every} parent rather than one line, because the
questions it answers are about all of them---above all, whether an
item lies above another, which is what keeps a move from making an
item its own ancestor (\cref{sec:todo-edit-parent}).  It lists the
nearest ancestors first and never includes the root, which is not an
item.  An ancestor missing from [[todos]] ends that path, as it does
in [[context_path]] below.

<<sub-item helpers>>=
def descendants_of(
    todos: list[TodoItem], parent_id: int
) -> list[TodoItem]:
    """Return every item below `parent_id`, once each.

    Depth-first, each item before its children.  A cycle
    or a diamond in the parents cannot repeat an item or
    hang the walk.
    """
    found = []
    seen = {parent_id}

    def visit(current):
        for child in children_of(todos, current):
            if child.id not in seen:
                seen.add(child.id)
                found.append(child)
                visit(child.id)

    visit(parent_id)
    return found


def ancestors_of(
    todos: list[TodoItem],
    item: TodoItem,
    by_id: dict[int, TodoItem] | None = None,
) -> list[TodoItem]:
    """Return every item above `item` along any of its
    parents, nearest first, once each.

    The root is not an item and is never returned; a
    missing parent ends its path, and a cycle cannot
    hang the walk.  `by_id` may pass a prebuilt map from
    id to item.
    """
    if by_id is None:
        by_id = {t.id: t for t in todos}
    found = []
    seen = {item.id}
    frontier = real_parents_of(item)
    while frontier:
        parent_id = frontier.pop(0)
        if parent_id in seen or parent_id not in by_id:
            continue
        seen.add(parent_id)
        parent = by_id[parent_id]
        found.append(parent)
        frontier.extend(real_parents_of(parent))
    return found
@

Some questions want a single line through the graph nevertheless.
The shell prompt prints one path; starting a sub-item pushes one line
of ancestors onto the active stack; and the child range of a parent is
bounded by the line of ranges above it
(\cref{sec:child-priority-helpers}).  [[primary_parent]] picks the
parent such a line goes through.

The context decides first.  When one of the parents is on the active
stack, or in the current tmux window's context, that is the line the
user is working in, and the item's other places are elsewhere.  The
caller passes those ids as [[context_ids]], since only the caller
knows which context applies.  Without a parent in the context, the
first parent that exists in [[todos]] is taken, and only then a missing
one, so that an orphan---an item whose only parent was deleted---keeps
answering what it always has, and [[get_parent]] goes on placing it
against the top level.  The root comes last, for a top-level item with
no other parent.  An item with only one parent has nothing to choose
between, so it answers that parent without looking anything up, which
keeps the common case as cheap as reading the one parent.

[[context_path]] follows primary parents up to the root and returns
the line root first, the reading order of a path ([[353 > 356 > 359]])
and the activation order that a parent-before-child stack needs.  The
root itself is left out.  The walk stops at a missing parent and at a
repeated id, so a cycle written by a broken editor session cannot hang
the caller.

<<sub-item helpers>>=
def primary_parent(
    todos: list[TodoItem],
    item: TodoItem,
    context_ids=(),
    by_id: dict[int, TodoItem] | None = None,
) -> int:
    """Return the id of the parent `item`'s line runs
    through.

    That is the first parent in `context_ids` (the active
    stack or the tmux context), else the first parent
    present in `todos`, else the first real parent, else
    `ROOT_ID`.  `by_id` may pass a prebuilt map from id
    to item.
    """
    parents = real_parents_of(item)
    if len(parents) <= 1:
        return parents[0] if parents else ROOT_ID
    for parent_id in parents:
        if parent_id in context_ids:
            return parent_id
    if by_id is None:
        by_id = {t.id: t for t in todos}
    for parent_id in parents:
        if parent_id in by_id:
            return parent_id
    return parents[0]


def context_path(
    todos: list[TodoItem],
    item: TodoItem,
    context_ids=(),
    *,
    by_id: dict[int, TodoItem] | None = None,
) -> list[TodoItem]:
    """Return the line of ancestors above `item`, root first.

    The line follows `primary_parent` with `context_ids`
    and stops at the top level, a missing parent, or a
    cycle.  `by_id` may pass a prebuilt map from id to
    item, for callers that ask about many items.
    """
    if by_id is None:
        by_id = {t.id: t for t in todos}
    path = []
    seen = {item.id}
    current = item
    while True:
        parent_id = primary_parent(
            todos, current, context_ids, by_id
        )
        if (
            parent_id == ROOT_ID
            or parent_id not in by_id
            or parent_id in seen
        ):
            break
        seen.add(parent_id)
        current = by_id[parent_id]
        path.append(current)
    path.reverse()
    return path
@

Let's verify the helpers on what one parent per item can already
express.  The root is a parent like any other: it has the top-level
items as its children, and top-level items are each other's siblings
under it.  The walks agree with the tree: one line of ancestors, root
first for [[context_path]] and nearest first for [[ancestors_of]].
The context does not matter while there is only one parent to choose.

<<test functions>>=
def test_root_is_the_parent_of_top_level_items():
  """`ROOT_ID` stands for the top level among parent ids."""
  todos = [
    TodoItem(id=1, title="Top"),
    TodoItem(id=2, title="Child", parents=[1]),
    TodoItem(id=3, title="Other top"),
  ]
  assert parents_of(todos[0]) == [ROOT_ID]
  assert real_parents_of(todos[0]) == []
  assert real_parents_of(todos[1]) == [1]
  assert [t.id for t in children_of(todos, ROOT_ID)] == [1, 3]
  assert [t.id for t in children_of(todos, 1)] == [2]
  assert [
    t.id for t in siblings_of(todos, todos[0], ROOT_ID)
  ] == [3]
  assert primary_parent(todos, todos[0]) == ROOT_ID
  assert primary_parent(todos, todos[1], context_ids=[3]) == 1


def test_walks_follow_the_single_parent_line():
  """Ancestors nearest first, the path root first."""
  todos = [
    TodoItem(id=1, title="A"),
    TodoItem(id=2, title="B", parents=[1]),
    TodoItem(id=3, title="C", parents=[2]),
    TodoItem(id=4, title="D", parents=[1]),
  ]
  assert [t.id for t in ancestors_of(todos, todos[2])] == [2, 1]
  assert [t.id for t in context_path(todos, todos[2])] == [1, 2]
  assert [t.id for t in descendants_of(todos, 1)] == [2, 3, 4]
  assert [
    t.id for t in descendants_of(todos, ROOT_ID)
  ] == [1, 2, 3, 4]
@

A cycle cannot be made through the commands any more
(\cref{sec:todo-edit-parent}), but a file edited by hand, or written
before that check existed, can still hold one.  Every walk must then
end, and must not list the item it started from:

<<test functions>>=
def test_walks_survive_a_parent_cycle():
  """A cycle ends every walk, without listing the start."""
  todos = [
    TodoItem(id=1, title="A", parents=[3]),
    TodoItem(id=2, title="B", parents=[1]),
    TodoItem(id=3, title="C", parents=[2]),
  ]
  assert [t.id for t in descendants_of(todos, 1)] == [2, 3]
  assert [t.id for t in ancestors_of(todos, todos[0])] == [3, 2]
  assert [t.id for t in context_path(todos, todos[0])] == [2, 3]
  assert descendants_of(todos, ROOT_ID) == []
@

Several parents bring the shapes a tree cannot have.  In a diamond,
\#4 is below both \#2 and \#3, which are both below \#1: the walks
list \#4 and \#1 once each.  An item that is also top-level has the
root among its parents, yet the root never turns up as an ancestor or
on a path, since it is not an item.

<<test functions>>=
def test_walks_list_a_diamond_once():
  """Below and above, a shared item is listed once."""
  todos = [
    TodoItem(id=1, title="Top"),
    TodoItem(id=2, title="Left", parents=[1]),
    TodoItem(id=3, title="Right", parents=[1]),
    TodoItem(id=4, title="Shared", parents=[2, 3]),
    TodoItem(id=5, title="Also top-level", parents=[ROOT_ID, 4]),
  ]
  assert [t.id for t in children_of(todos, 1)] == [2, 3]
  assert [t.id for t in descendants_of(todos, 1)] == [2, 4, 5, 3]
  assert [t.id for t in ancestors_of(todos, todos[3])] == [2, 3, 1]
  assert [t.id for t in ancestors_of(todos, todos[4])] == [4, 2, 3, 1]
  assert [t.id for t in context_path(todos, todos[4])] == [1, 2, 4]
  assert [
    t.id for t in children_of(todos, ROOT_ID)
  ] == [1, 5]
@

Which parent a line runs through follows the rules of
[[primary_parent]]: the context first, then the first parent that
exists, and a missing one only when no other is left.

<<test functions>>=
def test_primary_parent_prefers_context_then_existing():
  """Context first, then an existing parent, then any."""
  todos = [
    TodoItem(id=1, title="A"),
    TodoItem(id=2, title="B"),
    TodoItem(id=3, title="Child", parents=[1, 2]),
    TodoItem(id=4, title="Orphan", parents=[98, 99]),
    TodoItem(id=5, title="Half orphan", parents=[99, 2]),
  ]
  assert primary_parent(todos, todos[2]) == 1
  assert primary_parent(todos, todos[2], context_ids=[2]) == 2
  assert [
    t.id for t in context_path(todos, todos[2], [2])
  ] == [2]
  assert primary_parent(todos, todos[3]) == 98
  assert primary_parent(todos, todos[4]) == 2
  assert get_parent(todos, todos[3]) is None
@

\subsection{Helpers for the commands}

The commands ask more specific questions than the ones above: which
siblings to compare an item with, which item is the parent, whether
all children are done, which task is next.  The rest of this section
answers them on top of the parent helpers.

The parent helpers also replace four older ones that assumed a tree:
[[get_children]], [[get_siblings]], [[get_descendants]] and, further
down, [[get_ancestors]].  The module no longer calls them, but the tests
written against the tree still do, and they pin exactly the behaviour
that one parent per item must keep.  We therefore keep the names,
defined through the new helpers, so that those tests check the new
helpers without being rewritten.  A [[None]] parent still means the
top level to [[get_children]], as it did when [[None]] was what a
top-level item stored.

<<sub-item helpers>>=
def get_children(
    todos: list[TodoItem], parent_id: int | None
) -> list[TodoItem]:
    """Get all direct children of a todo item.

    Tree-era name for `children_of`; `None` means the top
    level.
    """
    if parent_id is None:
        parent_id = ROOT_ID
    return children_of(todos, parent_id)


def get_siblings(
    todos: list[TodoItem], item: TodoItem
) -> list[TodoItem]:
    """Get siblings of an item (same parent level).

    Tree-era name for `siblings_of` under the item's
    `primary_parent`.
    """
    return siblings_of(
        todos, item, primary_parent(todos, item)
    )


def get_descendants(
    todos: list[TodoItem], parent_id: int
) -> list[TodoItem]:
    """Get every descendant of a todo item.

    Tree-era name for `descendants_of`.
    """
    return descendants_of(todos, parent_id)
@

Let's verify that the transitive walk reaches past the first level and
that the one-level helper still does not:

<<test functions>>=
def test_get_descendants_reaches_grandchildren():
  """Descendants include deeper levels, children do not."""
  todos = [
    TodoItem(id=1, title="Root"),
    TodoItem(id=2, title="Child", parents=[1]),
    TodoItem(id=3, title="Grandchild", parents=[2]),
    TodoItem(id=4, title="Unrelated"),
  ]
  assert {t.id for t in get_children(todos, 1)} == {2}
  assert {
    t.id for t in get_descendants(todos, 1)
  } == {2, 3}
@

Going the other way---from an item to the parent whose range bounds
it---is the lookup every re-ranking path needs before it can place
anything, because the parent supplies both the interval and the
question of whether a numeric position is available at all.  We give
it a name so that the three commands that ask it cannot drift apart on
what \enquote{no parent} means: a top-level item and an item whose
parent has been deleted both answer [[None]], and both are placed
against the top level.  The parent is the item's ranking context, the
parent whose range its number lies in (\cref{sec:ranking-context}),
rather than its [[primary_parent]]: the question is where the item is
ranked, not which line the user is working in.  With one parent the
two are the same.

<<sub-item helpers>>=
def get_parent(
    todos: list[TodoItem], item: TodoItem
) -> TodoItem | None:
    """Get the parent `item` is ranked under, or `None`
    at the top level."""
    parent_id = ranking_context(todos, item)
    if parent_id == ROOT_ID:
        return None
    return next(
        (t for t in todos if t.id == parent_id),
        None,
    )
@

The [[add]], [[reprioritize]], and [[import]] commands compare a
todo against its siblings to place it in the priority order.
When multiple workers share a parent, the comparison set must be
restricted to one worker's items; otherwise the binary search
would ask the user to rank unrelated tasks against each other.
The [[who]] filter is applied inside the list comprehension so
that the sort operates only on the relevant subset.  The number the
item finally gets is still chosen among every worker's siblings
(\cref{sec:placement-number}), since they share one flat list.  The
siblings are those ranked under the item's ranking context
(\cref{sec:ranking-context}); a caller that places an item in another
of its places hands in a stand-in whose only parent is that place.

<<sub-item helpers>>=
def get_active_siblings(
    todos: list[TodoItem],
    item: TodoItem,
    who: str | None = None,
    ranked_only: bool = False,
) -> list[TodoItem]:
    """Get pending/in-progress siblings in the shared
    priority order.

    When ``who`` is given, only siblings assigned to
    that worker are returned. When ``ranked_only`` is
    true, siblings with unset priorities are excluded.
    """
    siblings = ranking_siblings_of(todos, item)
    active = [
        s
        for s in siblings
        if s.status != "done"
        and (
            who is None
            or (s.who or default_username) == who
        )
    ]
    if ranked_only:
        active = [
            s for s in active
            if s.priority is not None
        ]
        active.sort(
            key=require_effective_priority,
            reverse=True,
        )
    else:
        active = sort_todos_for_display(active)
    return active
@

The active stack ([[active_stack.json]]) is global---all workers
push and pop on the same list.  Stack-based commands like [[stop]]
and [[done]] need to ignore entries that belong to other workers,
otherwise one worker's implicit resolution would land on another
worker's item.  [[filter_ids_by_worker]] performs that narrowing.

An entry on the stack belongs to the worker whose session is working
on it: its claimant (\cref{sec:todo-claim}), not its assignee.  An
agent that started a task from the user's queue must find it with
its own [[todo stop --who]], and the user's [[todo stop]] must not.
[[session_worker]] answers that question for every place that asks
whose session an item belongs to---resolving ID-less commands,
deciding whose tracking to stop, and checking a claim.  For an item
that is not active it falls back to the assignee, which is also what
it does for an item that an older version put on the stack before
claims were recorded.  Items created before the [[who]] field
existed have [[who=None]]; we treat those as belonging to
[[default_username]] so they remain reachable.

<<sub-item helpers>>=
def session_worker(item):
    """Return the worker whose session works on `item`.

    That is the claimant while the item is active; for an
    inactive item, or one activated before claims were
    recorded, the assignee or the default user.
    """
    return item.claimed_by or item.who or default_username


def filter_ids_by_worker(
    todos: list[TodoItem],
    ids: list[int],
    who: str,
) -> list[int]:
    """Keep only todo IDs whose session is ``who``'s.

    See `session_worker` for whose an item is.
    """
    by_id = {t.id: t for t in todos}
    return [
        i
        for i in ids
        if i in by_id
        and session_worker(by_id[i]) == who
    ]
@

Several callers need the whole line of ancestors above an item rather
than one parent: [[ensure_ancestors_active]] activates all of them,
the shell prompt (\cref{prompt}) prints the chain from the root down
to the current task, and the depth of an item is simply the length
of that line.  The line is the one [[context_path]] walks
(\cref{sec:todo-parents}), with whatever context the caller has.
[[get_ancestors]] is its tree-era name, with no context and an
optional map from id to item as before; [[todo_depth]] counts the
line without context, which for one parent per item is the depth in
the tree.

<<sub-item helpers>>=
def get_ancestors(
    todos: list[TodoItem],
    item: TodoItem,
    by_id: dict[int, TodoItem] | None = None,
) -> list[TodoItem]:
    """Return the ancestors of ``item``, root first.

    Tree-era name for `context_path` without context.
    Stops at a missing parent or a cycle.  ``by_id`` may
    pass a prebuilt map from id to item.
    """
    return context_path(todos, item, by_id=by_id)


def todo_depth(
    todos: list[TodoItem],
    item: TodoItem,
) -> int:
    """Return the depth of ``item`` in the todo tree."""
    return len(context_path(todos, item))
<<sub-item helpers>>=
def check_parent_completion(
    todos: list[TodoItem], parent_id: int
) -> bool:
    """Check if all children of a parent are done.

    Returns True if the parent has children and all are
    done. Returns False if there are no children or any
    child is not done.
    """
    children = children_of(todos, parent_id)
    if not children:
        return False
    return all(c.status == "done" for c in children)
@

The [[next]] command and the cron script both need to find the
highest-priority pending task, optionally scoped to a specific
parent.  Extracting this into a function (rather than a reusable
chunk) makes the dependency on [[todos]] and [[who]] explicit
through parameters.  The worker filter follows the same backward-
compatibility rule as the active stack: older items with [[who=None]]
still count as belonging to [[default_username]].

<<sub-item helpers>>=
def find_pending_by_priority(
    todos: list[TodoItem],
    who: str | None = None,
    parent_id: int | None = None,
) -> TodoItem | None:
    """Find highest-priority pending task.

    Optionally filtered by assignee regex and parent.
    Treats ``who=None`` as ``default_username``.
    Returns None if no matching task is found.
    """
    candidates = [
        t for t in todos
        if t.status == "pending"
        and (parent_id is None
             or parent_id in parents_of(t))
    ]
    if who:
        candidates = [
            t for t in candidates
            if re.search(
                who, t.who or default_username
            )
        ]
    now = datetime.datetime.now()
    candidates.sort(
        key=lambda item: todo_display_sort_key(item, now)
    )
    return candidates[0] if candidates else None
@

Status reporting and auto-parenting both read the authoritative active
stack, but they need different views of it.  [[status]] needs every
active item in stack order so it can render a forest of active
hierarchies.  [[next]], [[add]], and [[import]] instead need a single
leaf: the active todo for the current tmux and worker context.  Keeping
those helpers separate prevents the old single-chain model from leaking
back in.

For commands that operate on the current user's own work, the fallback
outside tmux is that worker's latest active entry, and only that.
Another worker's entry is another session's work.  An earlier version
fell back to the plain top of the stack when the worker had no active
entry of its own, so that a single foreign task would still resolve
the way it did when there was only one stack.  With claims recorded
(\cref{sec:todo-claim}) that fallback does harm: a cron job running
[[next --who dan-claude]] while the user had any item active would
descend into the user's item, find no pending child there for the
agent, and exit with failure every time, and an [[add]] would file
the new item under somebody else's task.  So a worker without an
active entry has no context, and [[next]] picks top-level work.  An
empty worker, the \enquote{any worker} filter of [[next --who ""]],
still means the plain top of the stack.

<<sub-item helpers>>=
def get_active_todos(
    todos: list[TodoItem],
) -> list[TodoItem]:
    """Return active todos in normalized stack order."""
    by_id = {item.id: item for item in todos}
    stack = load_active_stack()
    return [by_id[todo_id] for todo_id in stack if todo_id in by_id]


def get_current_context_todo(
    todos: list[TodoItem],
    who: str | None = default_username,
) -> TodoItem | None:
    """Return the active todo for the current context.

    When tmux labels identify one of several parallel active
    hierarchies, return the deepest active todo in that context.
    Inside tmux without a tracked local todo, return
    ``None`` rather than inheriting another window's
    branch. Outside tmux, return that worker's latest
    active entry, or ``None`` if it has none; an empty
    or ``None`` worker means the plain stack top.
    """
    by_id = {item.id: item for item in todos}
    stack = load_active_stack()
    if not stack:
        return None
    try:
        context_id = resolve_todo_from_tmux_context(
            who or default_username, stack,
        )
    except NotInTmuxError:
        context_id = None
        in_tmux = False
    else:
        in_tmux = True
    if in_tmux and context_id is None:
        return None
    if context_id is not None:
        resolved_id = context_id
    elif who:
        worker_stack = filter_ids_by_worker(
            todos, stack, who,
        )
        if not worker_stack:
            return None
        resolved_id = worker_stack[-1]
    else:
        resolved_id = stack[-1]
    return by_id.get(resolved_id)
@

