\subsection{Context-Aware Child Priorities}%
\label{sec:child-priority-helpers}

When children are created via the editor or reconciled during edits,
they need priorities that place them \emph{between} their parent and
the parent's next-lower-priority sibling. Without this, children
receive absolute sequential priorities (1.0, 2.0, \ldots) and can
sort below unrelated lower-priority items in flat view.

Consider: parent~A at priority~5.0, parent~B at priority~3.0. If
children of~A get priorities 1.0 and~2.0, a flat sort places them
\emph{below}~B---the opposite of what the user intended. Instead,
children of~A should receive priorities in the range $(3.0, 5.0)$,
so they always appear between~A and~B.

The first helper finds the available priority range for children of
a given parent. We look at the parent's siblings to find the closest
one at or below the parent.  The siblings are the other items ranked
under the same parent; for an item with several parents that is the
parent its number was placed under, its ranking context, and the
siblings exclude the items placed elsewhere
(\cref{sec:ranking-context}).  With one parent per item these are
simply the other children of that parent.
The range runs from the parent's priority down to that sibling's
priority. If no such sibling exists, the floor comes from further up
the tree, and only at the top level from a generous default; we return
to this case below.

Both bounds read \emph{stored} priorities, for the reason placement
gives (\cref{todo:placement}): a deadline boost is temporary by design
(\cref{todo:effective-priority}), and everything derived from this
range---the child priorities from
[[distribute_priorities_in_range]], the starting point from
[[default_priority_without_siblings]]---is written straight into
[[priority]] and outlives the deadline that justified it.  The upper
bound was always the parent's stored number; taking the lower bound
from [[require_effective_priority]] mixed the two scales in a single
comparison.  A sibling one day from its deadline then reads as if it
sat far above where it is stored, so it drops out of the lower set
entirely and the floor collapses to the default: children of a parent
at~5.0 whose neighbour sits at~4.0 would be spread from~$-5.0$
upwards, below the very sibling the range exists to keep them above.
Reading both bounds from the stored numbers makes the comparison
meaningful again, and the range describes the same gap tomorrow as
today.

Siblings at exactly the parent's priority count as lower bounds too.
Excluding them would send the floor to the default and let children
sort below a sibling that the parent is level with---the same failure
in a quieter form.  It does leave one degenerate outcome: the closest
sibling can then equal the parent, and a zero-width (or, if the stored
numbers ever invert, a backwards) interval gives
[[distribute_priorities_in_range]] nothing to distribute over, since
every division point would land on the bound itself.  At the top
level we open a unit-wide range below the parent in that case, which is
the same one-unit step [[assign_priority_at_position]] takes when it
runs off the end of a list; deeper down the range must also stay inside
the enclosing one, which we return to below.

<<child priority helpers>>=
def compute_child_priority_range(
    todos: list[TodoItem],
    parent: TodoItem,
) -> tuple:
    """Find the priority range for children.

    Returns ``(high, low)`` from stored priorities: high
    is the parent's own, and low is that of the closest
    sibling at or below it.  A parent with no such
    sibling inherits the low end of its own parent's
    range, up the tree; at the top level the floor is
    ten below.  A parent tied with its sibling gets
    half the room left above the enclosing floor, or a
    unit below it where nothing encloses it.
    """
    if parent.priority is None:
        raise ValueError(
            "cannot compute child range for "
            "unprioritized parent"
        )
    high = parent.priority
    <<find the nearest lower bound up the ancestor chain>>
    return (high, low)


def closest_lower_sibling_priority(
    todos: list[TodoItem],
    item: TodoItem,
) -> float | None:
    """Return the stored priority of the closest ranked,
    not-done sibling at or below `item`, or `None`."""
    return max(
        (
            require_stored_priority(t)
            for t in ranking_siblings_of(todos, item)
            if t.status != "done"
            and t.priority is not None
            and t.priority <= item.priority
        ),
        default=None,
    )
@

The floor for a parent without a lower sibling is where the ranges
stop nesting unless we are careful.  Take top-level siblings A at~5.0
and B at~3.0, and a single child X of~A, placed in the middle of A's
range at~4.0.  X has no sibling at all, so a floor of [[high - 10]]
would give X's own children the range $(-6.0, 4.0)$, and X's first
child would start at~$-1.0$: below~B, in a flat list that is meant to
show A's whole subtree between A and~B.  The range X's children may
use is not X's to choose.  It is bounded by the range X itself lives
in, which here ends at B's~3.0, so X's children get $(3.0, 4.0)$.

In general we walk up the tree: the parent's closest lower sibling
bounds the range if there is one; otherwise the parent's parent's
does, and so on.  Each step is the lower end of the range the
previous level was placed in, so whatever bound we reach is the
tightest one that keeps the subtree inside every enclosing range.
Only an item whose ancestors all lack a lower sibling reaches the top
level, and there nothing bounds it; it gets the generous default of
ten below the top-level ancestor, so that the whole subtree shares
one floor.  The walk goes up the line of ranking contexts,
[[ranking_path]] (\cref{sec:ranking-context}), which is the line of
ranges the parent's number is nested in; with one parent per item it
is the plain line of parents.  It stops at a cycle, and at an unranked
ancestor too, since a ranked item under an unranked one has no
enclosing range to inherit.  The default floor is then taken from the
last ranked level we saw.

A level whose closest sibling sits level with it has no room of its
own, and the unit-wide range we open below it must not leave the range
the level itself lives in.  Two children of A tied at~4.8, where A's
range is $(4.5, 5.0)$, would get $(3.8, 4.8)$ and spread their own
children below A's lower sibling.  So a tie takes the midpoint between
the level and the enclosing floor instead: $(4.65, 4.8)$.  That needs
the enclosing floor, so the walk is in two passes.  Going up, we
collect the levels until one has a sibling strictly below it, or the
top is reached; that level's floor is known without looking further.
Coming back down, each level's floor is its strict lower sibling, the
midpoint for a tie, or the inherited floor when it has no sibling.
Only where nothing encloses the level---at the top, or under an
unranked ancestor---does a tie fall back to the unit step, and an
inverted floor (a level stored below what it inherits, which only
legacy data has) to the same step, rather than a backwards interval.

<<find the nearest lower bound up the ancestor chain>>=
chain = []
for level in [parent, *reversed(ranking_path(todos, parent))]:
    if level.priority is None:
        break
    sibling = closest_lower_sibling_priority(todos, level)
    chain.append((level, sibling))
    if sibling is not None and sibling < level.priority:
        break
low = None
for level, sibling in reversed(chain):
    low = level_floor(level.priority, sibling, low)
@

The rule for one level is a function of three numbers, so that the
index of \cref{sec:ranking-index}, which works out every range at once,
applies exactly the same rule.

<<child priority helpers>>=
def level_floor(priority, sibling, low):
    """Return the floor of a level at `priority`, given its
    closest lower sibling `sibling` and the floor `low` it
    inherits; either may be `None`."""
    enclosed = low is not None and low < priority
    if sibling is not None and sibling < priority:
        return sibling
    if sibling is not None:
        return (priority + low) / 2.0 if enclosed else priority - 1.0
    if low is None:
        return priority - 10.0
    if not enclosed:
        return priority - 1.0
    return low
@

The second helper distributes [[count]] priorities evenly within a
range, highest first. We divide the range into [[count + 1]] equal
segments and place one priority at each interior division point.
This leaves room at both ends for future insertions---the same
\enquote{leave room for midpoints} philosophy used by
[[assign_priority_at_position]].

<<child priority helpers>>=
def distribute_priorities_in_range(
    count: int, high: float, low: float,
) -> list[float]:
    """Distribute priorities evenly in (low, high).

    Returns a list of ``count`` values, highest first.
    For example, 3 items in (3.0, 5.0) gives
    [4.5, 4.0, 3.5].
    """
    if count == 0:
        return []
    step = (high - low) / (count + 1)
    return [high - step * (i + 1) for i in range(count)]
@

The third helper answers the degenerate case the first two cannot: a
level with no ranked item in it yet.  There is no neighbour to take a
midpoint against, so the question becomes where to start.  Starting in
the \emph{middle} of the parent's range rather than at one end is what
lets the second and third arrival be placed above or below the first
without renumbering anything---the same reason
[[distribute_priorities_in_range]] leaves room at both ends.  At the
top level there is no range to halve, so we fall back to [[1.0]] and
let later items grow the scale in either direction.

An unranked parent has no range at all, and no number we could invent
would mean anything relative to it.  We raise rather than guess,
because the callers each have their own right answer: [[add]] and
[[edit]] leave the item unranked, while [[reprioritize]] tells the
user to rank the parent first.

<<child priority helpers>>=
def default_priority_without_siblings(
    parent: TodoItem | None = None,
    todos: list[TodoItem] | None = None,
) -> float:
    """Return the priority for the first ranked item at
    a level.

    With a ranked `parent` this is the middle of that
    parent's child range; at the top level it is `1.0`.
    Raises `ValueError` for an unranked parent.
    """
    if parent is None or todos is None:
        return 1.0
    if parent.priority is None:
        raise ValueError(
            "cannot assign numeric priority under "
            "an unranked parent"
        )
    high, low = compute_child_priority_range(
        todos, parent
    )
    return (high + low) / 2.0
@

