\subsection{Children Follow a Re-ranked Parent}%
\label{sec:subtree-follows-parent}

The range is right only at the moment the children are placed in it.
It is defined by the parent's number and its lower sibling's, so
re-ranking the parent moves the range while the children keep the
numbers they were stored with.  Take top-level siblings A at~5.0,
B at~3.0 and C at~1.0, with A's children at 4.5, 4.0 and~3.5 inside
A's range $(3.0, 5.0)$.  Demote A to~2.0, between C and~B, and the
children are still at 4.5 to~3.5: in the flat list they now sit above
B, two places from their parent, and [[next]] offers them before B
although the user has just said that A's work matters less than B's.
Every path that changes a ranked item's number must therefore move its
subtree along with it.

The obvious repair, shifting every descendant by the amount the parent
moved, works only when the new range is as wide as the old one.  Here
A moves by~$-3.0$ and its children would go to 1.5, 1.0 and~0.5: one
ties with C and one falls below it, because A's new range $(1.0, 2.0)$
is half as wide as the old.  We map the old range onto the new one
instead.  With $\ell$ and~$h$ the low and high ends of the old range,
and $\ell'$ and~$h'$ those of the new one, a descendant at~$p$ goes to
\[
  p' = \ell' + (p - \ell) \cdot \frac{h' - \ell'}{h - \ell},
\]
which in the example gives 1.75, 1.5 and~1.25: strictly between C and
A, in the same order, and spread in proportion to how they were spread
before.

This is correct in general, not only for the example.  The map is
strictly increasing, since both widths are positive
([[compute_child_priority_range]] widens a degenerate range), so it
keeps the order among the descendants.  It sends $\ell$ to~$\ell'$ and
$h$ to~$h'$, so what was inside the old range lands inside the new
one.  When the two ranges are equally wide the factor is one and the
map \emph{is} the shift; a promotion is the same formula with a range
higher up.  It also keeps the nesting at every depth.  A grandchild
lies in its own parent's range, the interval between two numbers in
A's old range, and an increasing map takes the interval between two
numbers to the interval between their images.  The grandchild
therefore stays between its mapped parent and that parent's mapped
lower sibling, as the child-range rule requires.  This is why we apply
the map once, to all descendants together, rather than level by
level: recomputing a child's range after moving it and mapping its
children again would move the grandchildren twice.

A demotion to the bottom of a level leaves the parent with no lower
sibling, and its new range ends at the default floor
(\cref{sec:child-priority-helpers}): ten below the parent at the top
level.  Demoting A to~0.0, below every sibling, gives the range
$(-10.0, 0.0)$ and the children $-2.5$, $-5.0$ and~$-7.5$.  The
subtree then looks exactly as it would had it been built at the bottom
from the start, which is the point of having one rule for both.

Which descendants move is decided by their stored numbers, not by
ancestry alone.
\begin{description}
\item[Ranked and in the old range] These move.  The range is taken as
  closed, so that a descendant level with the parent, or with the
  lower sibling, which only hand-set numbers produce, still moves with
  the subtree.  Its image is kept strictly inside the new range,
  though, one floating-point step from the end it would land on: a tie
  with the parent is exactly what the ranges exist to prevent, and
  rounding alone can produce one when the new range is narrow---a
  child just below the parent's old number can round onto its new
  one.
\item[Ranked but outside the old range] These stay.  They come from
  data written before children were placed by range (or before a lone
  child's range was bounded by its parent's), and outside the range
  the map extrapolates: a narrowing pulls a far-away number towards
  the new range and a widening pushes it further out, neither of which
  says anything the user chose.  Leaving such an item alone is the
  conservative answer; [[reprioritize]] can place it.
\item[Unranked] These stay in the unranked tail, since there is no
  number to map.
\item[Done] These stay too.  A done item no longer bounds any range
  and is never offered by [[next]], so its number orders nothing, and
  rewriting it would only change the record of where it was when it
  was closed.
\end{description}
The same test settles the shared descendants
(\cref{sec:ranking-context}).  One ranked under the parent, or under a
parent within its subtree, lies in the old range and moves with it.
One ranked elsewhere is a guest here: its number lies above the
parent's range, it stays, and its place elsewhere is not disturbed by a
parent it does not depend on.  A shared descendant that moves may land
below the range of one of its other parents, which
[[settle_placement]] repairs afterwards.
A parent that was unranked before the change had no range, and one that
is unranked after it has none to move into; in both cases nothing
moves.  Finally, an edit that leaves the range as it was (a new title,
say) returns early, so that it does not rewrite every descendant with
a value that differs only by rounding.

The rule moves the re-ranked item's own subtree and nothing else.  The
sibling it lands below loses part of its range at the same time, and
\cref{sec:sibling-above-squeezed} deals with that subtree.

<<child priority helpers>>=
def move_subtree_priorities(
    todos: list[TodoItem],
    parent: TodoItem,
    old_range: tuple | None,
    new_range: tuple | None,
    keep=frozenset(),
) -> list[TodoItem]:
    """Carry `parent`'s descendants from its old child
    range to its new one, and return those it moved.

    The ranges are `(high, low)` pairs as returned by
    `compute_child_priority_range`, or `None` for an
    unranked parent, in which case nothing moves.  Each
    ranked, not-done descendant whose stored priority
    lies in the closed old range is mapped linearly onto
    the new range, strictly inside it; the others keep
    their numbers, and so do those whose ids are in
    `keep`.
    """
    if old_range is None or new_range is None:
        return []
    if old_range == new_range:
        return []
    old_high, old_low = old_range
    new_high, new_low = new_range
    scale = (new_high - new_low) / (old_high - old_low)
    <<collect the descendants of [[parent]] once each>>
    inside = (
        math.nextafter(new_low, new_high),
        math.nextafter(new_high, new_low),
    )
    moved = []
    for item in descendants:
        if (
            item.status == "done" or item.priority is None
            or item.id in keep
        ):
            continue
        if old_low <= item.priority <= old_high:
            mapped = new_low + (item.priority - old_low) * scale
            item.priority = min(
                max(mapped, inside[0]), inside[1]
            )
            moved.append(item)
    return moved
@

Each descendant must be mapped once.  [[descendants_of]] guarantees
that (\cref{sec:todo-parents}): it lists the bottom of a diamond once,
and it never lists the parent itself, even when a hand-edited file
makes the subtree a cycle, so the parent's new number is not mapped a
second time.

<<collect the descendants of [[parent]] once each>>=
descendants = descendants_of(todos, parent.id)
@

The callers each hold an item whose number is about to change.  Both
ranges have to come from the same list, the one about to be saved: the
old one read before the number changes and the new one after it.
Computing them from an earlier snapshot would describe a sibling list
that another process may have changed in the meantime, so the callers
do this inside their locked callback
(\cref{sec:todo-read-modify-write}).  An unranked item has no range,
and [[compute_child_priority_range]] refuses one, so we ask through a
helper that answers [[None]] instead; that is the [[None]] that
[[move_subtree_priorities]] treats as nothing to move.  The same
helper lets the commands refuse a number they were handed, by
[[--prio]] or [[--keep-prio]], that lies outside the parent's range,
where no subtree move could make it a position among the parent's
children; the top level and an unranked parent bound nothing.  It answers
[[None]] for a missing parent too, which is how placement asks for the
range a level is bounded by: the top level has no parent and no range
(\cref{todo:binary-search}).

<<child priority helpers>>=
def child_priority_range_or_none(
    todos: list[TodoItem],
    parent: TodoItem | None,
) -> tuple | None:
    """Return `parent`'s child range, or `None` when
    `parent` is unranked or `None` (the top level)."""
    if parent is None or parent.priority is None:
        return None
    return compute_child_priority_range(todos, parent)


def refuse_number_outside_range(
    todos: list[TodoItem],
    parent: TodoItem | None,
    number: float,
    subject: str,
    alternatives: str,
) -> None:
    """Exit with an error unless `number` lies strictly
    inside `parent`'s child range.

    The top level and an unranked parent bound nothing,
    so any number passes there.  `subject` names the
    number in the message and `alternatives` the flags
    that would place the item instead.
    """
    bounds = child_priority_range_or_none(todos, parent)
    if bounds is None or bounds[1] < number < bounds[0]:
        return
    typer.echo(
        f"Error: {subject} is outside #{parent.id}'s child "
        f"range ({bounds[1]:g}, {bounds[0]:g}); "
        f"use {alternatives} to place it",
        err=True,
    )
    raise typer.Exit(1)


def set_priority_with_subtree(
    todos: list[TodoItem],
    item: TodoItem,
    priority: float | None,
) -> None:
    """Store `priority` on `item` and move the subtrees
    its new place affects (see `settle_placement`)."""
    old_range = child_priority_range_or_none(todos, item)
    old_place = (item.priority, list(item.parents))
    item.priority = priority
    settle_placement(todos, item, old_range, old_place)
@

