\subsection{The Sibling Above Gives Up Part of Its Range}%
\label{sec:sibling-above-squeezed}

Moving the re-ranked item's own subtree is half of keeping the ranges
nested.  The other half is the sibling the item lands \emph{below}.
Its range ran down to the next sibling under it, and the item is now
that sibling: dropping A to~2.0 between B at~3.0 and C at~1.0 shrinks
B's range from $(1.0, 3.0)$ to $(2.0, 3.0)$.  A child of B stored
at~1.5 is still in B's old range but now sorts after A, among A's own
children.  The same happens when a new item is placed below B by
[[add]] or [[import]], so this is a consequence of placing an item, of
any kind and by any command, not of re-ranking alone.

The answer is the map from \cref{sec:subtree-follows-parent} once more,
applied to B: its descendants are compressed from the old range onto
the new one, $(1.0, 3.0)$ onto $(2.0, 3.0)$, which keeps their order
and nesting for the same reasons as before.  The child at~1.5 goes
to~2.25 and one at~2.5 to~2.75, both between B and A.  Only the sibling
directly above the new place is affected: siblings further up are
bounded by siblings that did not move, and the sibling below the item
keeps the range under its own number.  The sibling that was directly
above the item's \emph{old} place gains room instead of losing it;
its children are still inside its range, so we leave them where they
are rather than spread them out.

The old range of that sibling is the range it had before the item
arrived, which we compute from a copy of the list where the item is
back at its old place---or absent, for a new item.  Comparing the low
ends tells a range that shrank from one that did not; when nothing
changed, or the range grew, nothing moves.  A done or unranked item
bounds no range, so placing one squeezes nothing.

<<child priority helpers>>=
def squeeze_sibling_above(
    todos: list[TodoItem],
    item: TodoItem,
    old_place: tuple | None = None,
    before: list[TodoItem] | None = None,
    keep: set | None = None,
) -> list[TodoItem]:
    """Compress the subtree of the sibling just above
    `item`, whose child range `item` has just cut short,
    and return the items it moved.

    `old_place` is `item`'s `(priority, parents)`
    before it moved, or `None` when `item` is new.
    `before` may pass the list as it was before anything
    moved, from `list_before_move`, and `keep` the ids of
    `item` and the subtree ranked under it.
    """
    if item.priority is None or item.status == "done":
        return []
    above = [
        s for s in ranking_siblings_of(todos, item)
        if s.status != "done"
        and s.priority is not None
        and s.priority >= item.priority
    ]
    if not above:
        return []
    nearest = min(s.priority for s in above)
    <<rebuild the list as it was before [[item]] moved>>
    moved = []
    for sibling in above:
        if sibling.priority != nearest:
            continue
        old_range = compute_child_priority_range(
            before, sibling
        )
        new_range = compute_child_priority_range(
            todos, sibling
        )
        if new_range[1] > old_range[1]:
            <<keep the descendants ranked elsewhere>>
            moved += move_subtree_priorities(
                todos, sibling, old_range, new_range,
                keep=placed | elsewhere,
            )
    return moved
@

The copy has to be taken before anything moves.  In a tree the item's
own subtree, which [[move_into_place]] moves first, never bounds a
sibling's range; with several parents it can, since a child of the
item may also be a top-level item or the child of another parent.  So
the caller that moves the subtree takes the copy before it does, with
[[list_before_move]], and hands it in; called on its own, the squeeze
takes it from [[todos]] as they are.  The items are copied one by one
because the moves change them in place, while their parents, which
nothing here changes, are shared.

<<child priority helpers>>=
def list_before_move(todos, item, old_place):
    """Return copies of `todos` as they are, with `item` at
    `old_place`, its `(priority, parents)`, or left out
    when `old_place` is `None` (a new item)."""
    before = [copy.copy(t) for t in todos if t is not item]
    if old_place is not None:
        stand_in = copy.copy(item)
        stand_in.priority, stand_in.parents = old_place
        before.append(stand_in)
    return before
@

The placed item and the subtree ranked under it are not compressed
with the sibling's.  In a tree they cannot be among the sibling's
descendants, but an item with several parents can be below its own
sibling, through another parent (\cref{sec:ranking-context}): X at
the top level and also under a child of the sibling S it is placed
under.  Its new number then lies in S's old range, and the compression
would move it a second time, away from the place just chosen for it,
together with the subtree that has just been moved under it.  The
subtree kept is the one ranked under X, whose ranking line passes
through X, and not all of X's descendants: one of them may be ranked
under S, and that one must be compressed with S's.

<<rebuild the list as it was before [[item]] moved>>=
if before is None:
    before = list_before_move(todos, item, old_place)
placed = keep
if placed is None:
    placed = {item.id} | ranked_subtree_ids(todos, item)
@

For the same reason only the descendants ranked under the sibling are
compressed with it.  A descendant ranked elsewhere is not part of the
range that shrank, even when its number lies in it: Y under a done
child of S, which is also a top-level item, ranks at the top level, and
may sit exactly at the floor of S's range as the next top-level item
below S.  Compressing it with S's subtree would lift it into S's range,
above the children it ranks over, and no renumbering would mend that,
since the next placement would squeeze it there again.  The sibling
has not moved, so the list before the move tells which descendants are
ranked under it.  This also keeps the placed item of the previous
paragraph whenever it was ranked outside S before it moved, which is
the case in the test below; the kept set stays, as it names the item
and its subtree by what was just done to them rather than by how they
were ranked before.

<<keep the descendants ranked elsewhere>>=
elsewhere = {
    d.id for d in descendants_of(before, sibling.id)
} - ranked_subtree_ids(before, sibling)
@

