Every command that gives an item a place calls one helper, so that
none of them can remember the item's own subtree and forget the
sibling above, or the other way round.  The order matters little but
is fixed: the item's own subtree moves first, into the range under its
new number, and then the sibling above is compressed into the range
over it; the two ranges do not overlap.  An item with several parents
must also stay at least as high as each of its places allows, and the
moves may have taken it, or a shared item below it, under the range of
one of them; [[keep_highest_places]] lifts such items back
(\cref{sec:ranking-context}).  Last, the helper checks whether the
numbers it touched still have room around them
(\cref{sec:renumber-priorities}), and, when some item has several
parents, whether the ranges still nest, renumbering in either case.

<<child priority helpers>>=
def settle_placement(
    todos: list[TodoItem],
    item: TodoItem,
    old_range: tuple | None = None,
    old_place: tuple | None = None,
    was_nested: bool | None = None,
) -> None:
    """Move the subtrees whose ranges `item`'s new place
    changed: its own, and its new upper sibling's.  Lift
    shared items that fell below one of their places, and
    renumber everything if that left too little room or,
    with shared items, ranges that do not nest.

    `old_range` is `item`'s child range before the
    change and `old_place` its `(priority, parents)`;
    both are `None` for an item that is new.  `was_nested`
    says whether the ranges nested before; by default it
    is checked on the list as it was before the move.
    """
    if was_nested is None:
        was_nested = list_has_shared_items(todos, old_place) and (
            ranking_is_consistent(
                list_before_move(todos, item, old_place)
            )
        )
    touched = move_into_place(todos, item, old_range, old_place)
    touched += keep_highest_places(todos)
    restore_nesting(todos, item, touched, was_nested)


def list_has_shared_items(todos, old_place=None):
    """Tell whether an item in `todos` has several parents,
    or had before a move from `old_place`."""
    return any(len(parents_of(t)) > 1 for t in todos) or (
        old_place is not None and len(old_place[1]) > 1
    )


def restore_nesting(todos, item, touched, was_nested):
    """Renumber if the numbers in `touched` lack room, or if
    the ranges nested before (`was_nested`) and do not any
    more; warn about `item` if that does not help."""
    def broken():
        return was_nested and not ranking_is_consistent(todos)
    if not gaps_too_narrow(todos, touched) and not broken():
        return
    for _ in range(RANKING_REPAIRS):
        renumber_priorities(todos)
        lifted = keep_highest_places(todos)
        if not gaps_too_narrow(todos, lifted) and not broken():
            return
    if broken():
        report_unnested_ranking(item)


def move_into_place(
    todos: list[TodoItem],
    item: TodoItem,
    old_range: tuple | None = None,
    old_place: tuple | None = None,
) -> list[TodoItem]:
    """Lift `item` to its highest place if it fell below
    one, then move its subtree and squeeze the sibling
    above it; return the items whose numbers changed."""
    before = list_before_move(todos, item, old_place)
    in_transit = (
        ranked_subtree_ids(before, item)
        if old_place is not None else set()
    )
    landed = [t for t in todos if t.id not in in_transit]
    lift_to_highest_place(landed, item)
    touched = [item]
    touched += move_subtree_priorities(
        todos, item, old_range,
        child_priority_range_or_none(landed, item),
    )
    touched += squeeze_sibling_above(
        todos, item, old_place, before,
        keep={item.id} | in_transit,
    )
    return touched
@

While the item moves, the subtree ranked under it is in transit: it
still carries the numbers of the old range while the item already has
its new one.  In a tree that does not matter, since those numbers are
read only as the subtree's own.  With several parents it does: a shared
child whose parent has just dropped below it suddenly looks ranked
under another of its parents, and as a sibling there it would cut the
range the item's subtree is about to be moved into.  So the new range,
and the lift before it, are computed as if the subtree in transit were
not there; it lands inside the new range, where it bounds nothing
outside.  The subtree is the one whose ranking line passes through the
item, read from the list as it was before the move.

<<child priority helpers>>=
def ranked_subtree_ids(todos, item):
    """Return the ids of the descendants of `item` that are
    ranked under it, directly or further down."""
    return {
        d.id for d in descendants_of(todos, item.id)
        if item.id in {a.id for a in ranking_path(todos, d)}
    }
@

