\subsection{Renumbering When the Room Runs Out}%
\label{sec:renumber-priorities}

Every placement takes a midpoint, and every squeeze compresses a range,
so the room between neighbouring numbers shrinks with use.  Adding
children at the bottom of one parent halves the gap above the floor
each time; inserting items directly below one sibling halves its range
each time, and its whole subtree with it.  A float has 52 bits of
fraction, so after some fifty such steps in one place two neighbours
round to the same number, and the order the user chose is lost.  No
choice of midpoint avoids this; only fresh room does.

The numbers themselves carry no meaning beyond their order---they are
an implementation detail, and the user ranks items by comparing them,
never by typing a number they have seen---so we are free to renumber.
After a placement has settled, we look at the numbers it wrote: the
placed item and every descendant the two subtree moves changed.  If
any of them is closer to its neighbour in the flat order than a
millionth of the larger magnitude (or of~1, near zero), the whole
ranked tree is renumbered.  A relative threshold is the right kind
because float resolution is relative; a millionth leaves some thirty
further halvings before rounding would bite, so renumbering happens
well before anything is lost, yet rarely, since the gaps that ordinary
use leaves are many orders of magnitude wider.  Checking only the
touched numbers means that an untouched tie in old data does not
renumber the list on every unrelated command.

<<child priority helpers>>=
def gaps_too_narrow(
    todos: list[TodoItem],
    touched: list[TodoItem],
) -> bool:
    """Tell whether a touched item has too little room
    next to its neighbours in the flat order."""
    ranked = sort_siblings_by_stored_priority([
        t for t in todos if t.status != "done"
    ])
    where = {t.id: i for i, t in enumerate(ranked)}
    for item in touched:
        i = where.get(item.id)
        if i is None:
            continue
        for j in (i - 1, i + 1):
            if 0 <= j < len(ranked):
                a, b = item.priority, ranked[j].priority
                room = RENUMBER_MIN_GAP * max(1.0, abs(a), abs(b))
                if abs(a - b) < room:
                    return True
    return False
<<constants>>=
RENUMBER_MIN_GAP = 1e-6
@

Renumbering keeps the order and the nesting and rebuilds the room.
It walks the tree of ranked, not-done items from the top level down,
one level at a time, and lays each family out again with
[[distribute_priorities_in_range]] inside the parent's range---the
same evenly spaced numbers the editor gives a family it creates.  The
order within a family is the order the stored numbers had, with the ID
breaking a tie.

A family is the set of items ranked under one parent.  An item with
several parents belongs to one family only, the one of its ranking
context (\cref{sec:ranking-context}), so that it gets one new number
and its other places see it where that number puts it.  The contexts
are read before anything is renumbered, since they follow from the
numbers that are about to change.

The range each family is laid out in is read off the layout one level
up rather than asked of [[compute_child_priority_range]]: an item's
range runs from its new number down to the next member of its family,
or, for the last member, down to the floor its own family was laid out
above.  For a freshly laid-out family that is exactly what
[[compute_child_priority_range]] would answer, without asking, in the
middle of the renumbering, which family each sibling belongs to while
half the numbers are new and half old.

The top level is kept as it is when its neighbours are at least a whole
unit apart, since that is room enough for everything below and its
numbers are the ones a user may have chosen with [[--prio]].
Otherwise it is respaced to whole numbers counting down to~1, as many
as there are top-level items; the lowest of them has the default floor
of ten below it.  Done and unranked items, and anything below them,
are not part of the ranked tree and keep their numbers.

<<child priority helpers>>=
def renumber_priorities(todos: list[TodoItem]) -> None:
    """Renumber the ranked, not-done tree in place,
    keeping its order and nesting and making room."""
    def family_order(items):
        return sorted(
            items, key=lambda t: (-t.priority, t.id)
        )

    def with_ranges(family, low):
        lows = [t.priority for t in family[1:]] + [low]
        return [
            (t, t.priority, below)
            for t, below in zip(family, lows)
        ]
    live = [
        t for t in todos
        if t.status != "done" and t.priority is not None
    ]
    by_id = {t.id: t for t in todos}
    children = {}
    for t in live:
        children.setdefault(
            ranking_context(todos, t, by_id), []
        ).append(t)
    level = family_order(children.get(ROOT_ID, []))
    <<respace the top level when it is crowded>>
    ranges = with_ranges(
        level, level[-1].priority - 10.0 if level else None
    )
    seen = {t.id for t in level}
    while ranges:
        below = []
        for parent, high, low in ranges:
            family = family_order([
                c for c in children.get(parent.id, [])
                if c.id not in seen
            ])
            numbers = distribute_priorities_in_range(
                len(family), high, low
            )
            for child, number in zip(family, numbers):
                child.priority = number
                seen.add(child.id)
            below += with_ranges(family, low)
        ranges = below
