\subsection{One Number for Several Places}%
\label{sec:ranking-context}

An item with several parents has a place under each of them but only
one number.  We do not give it a number per parent.  The flat listings,
[[next]] and [[ready]] rank every item against every other, so they
would need a rule to pick one of the numbers anyway, and that rule is
all the item needs.  The rule is that an item keeps the highest
priority any of its places gives it.

That works because priorities are one scale with nested ranges.  A
child is numbered between its parent and the parent's next lower
sibling (\cref{sec:child-priority-helpers}), so a number placed under
\#12 can be compared directly with one placed at the top level or
under~\#40.  Take top-level items A at~5.0 and D at~3.0, and A's child
B at~4.5.  When B is also put under D, any number in D's range is
below~3.0 and so below~4.5, and B keeps~4.5.  Among D's children B
then lists first, above D's range, which agrees with the flat order: B
is more urgent than D's own place.

\paragraph{Where the number came from.}
The parent whose child range contains the stored number is the item's
\emph{ranking context}: B is ranked under~A.  It is what the priority
code needs wherever it used to ask for \enquote{the} parent: the range
of the item's own children ends at the next item below it in that
parent's range, and re-ranking the item moves it among that parent's
children.  It differs from [[primary_parent]], which says which line
the user is working in.  Working on B under D, the user's line runs
through D, while B's number still belongs to A's range.  With one
parent per item the two agree, and the ranking context is simply that
parent.

The ranking context can be found without computing a single range.
Under the rule an item never sits below the range of one of its
parents (we make sure of that below), so it lies inside the range of
exactly those parents ranked above it.  Ranges either nest or do not
meet, so the narrowest of these, the one we want, belongs to the
parent ranked lowest among them; the first of them wins a tie.  An
item ranked above all its parents was placed at the top level, which
has no bounds.  An unranked item, or one whose parents leave no answer
(a hand-edited file), falls back to its [[primary_parent]].

Reading the context from the number has a limit, which follows from
the nesting.  An item can be ranked under a parent P only where none of
its other parents inside P's range lies above it.  Take X under A and
under A's child A1, ranked under A at~4.5, just above A1 at~4.0.  If A1
is re-ranked above X, X's number lies in A1's range now, and X reads as
ranked under A1, at the same number.  Likewise an item at the top level
and under D reads as ranked at the top level only while it is above D;
below D its number lies in D's range.  The number cannot tell the two
readings apart, and under the rule it does not need to: the number is
still the highest the item gets, and only the family the item is
re-ranked in changes.  To rank X among A's children again, the user
names a neighbour there, [[reprioritize --above]] or [[--below]] with
one of A's children, which ranks X in A whatever its context
(\cref{sec:reprioritize-in-a-place}).

A done parent is passed over like an unranked one.  Its number still
stands, but it orders nothing any more: [[next]] never offers it, a done
sibling bounds no range (\cref{sec:child-priority-helpers}), and
renumbering leaves the children of a done item where they are
(\cref{sec:renumber-priorities}).  An item that also lives under an
open parent is ranked there, where its number keeps meaning something;
otherwise the one parent it had stays its context.

That makes completing an item a change of context for its shared
direct children ranked under it; an item further down is ranked under
one of those children, or another item, and moves with it.  Such a
direct child keeps its number, but the
number now reads as ranked under another of its parents, in a family
that was not laid out around it: it cuts the range of the sibling it
now sits under, whose subtree may reach below it.  So the commands that
mark an item done do it through [[close_item]], which then places each
such child in its new family at the number it has, as a new arrival
there (\cref{sec:sibling-above-squeezed}): the sibling above is
squeezed, and the rule applies as for any placement.

<<child priority helpers>>=
def close_item(todos, item, closed_at=None):
    """Mark `item` done and place the shared children that
    were ranked under it in the families they are ranked
    in now."""
    by_id = {t.id: t for t in todos}
    moved = [
        (child, child_priority_range_or_none(todos, child))
        for child in children_of(todos, item.id)
        if child.status != "done"
        and child.priority is not None
        and len(parents_of(child)) > 1
        and ranking_context(todos, child, by_id) == item.id
    ]
    was_nested = bool(moved) and ranking_is_consistent(todos)
    set_status(item, "done", closed_at)
    for child, old_range in moved:
        settle_placement(todos, child, old_range, was_nested=False)
    if moved:
        restore_nesting(todos, moved[0][0], [], was_nested)
@

<<child priority helpers>>=
def ranking_context(
    todos: list[TodoItem],
    item: TodoItem,
    by_id: dict[int, TodoItem] | None = None,
) -> int:
    """Return the id of the parent `item` is ranked under.

    That is the parent whose child range holds `item`'s
    number: the lowest-ranked parent above it, or
    `ROOT_ID` for a top-level item ranked above all its
    parents.  With one parent it is that parent; an
    unranked item answers its `primary_parent`.
    """
    parents = parents_of(item)
    if len(parents) == 1:
        return parents[0]
    if by_id is None:
        by_id = {t.id: t for t in todos}
    if item.priority is not None:
        above = [
            by_id[p] for p in parents
            if p in by_id
            and by_id[p].priority is not None
            and by_id[p].status != "done"
            and by_id[p].priority > item.priority
        ]
        if above:
            return min(above, key=lambda p: p.priority).id
        if ROOT_ID in parents:
            return ROOT_ID
    return primary_parent(todos, item, by_id=by_id)
@

The line of ranking contexts up to the top level is the line of ranges
the item's number is nested in, which is what
[[compute_child_priority_range]] walks to find a floor.  It is built
like [[context_path]], root first and stopping at a missing parent or
a cycle, with the ranking context as the step.

<<child priority helpers>>=
def ranking_path(
    todos: list[TodoItem],
    item: TodoItem,
    by_id: dict[int, TodoItem] | None = None,
) -> list[TodoItem]:
    """Return the parents `item` is ranked under, from the
    top level down, as `context_path` does for lines."""
    if by_id is None:
        by_id = {t.id: t for t in todos}
    path = []
    seen = {item.id}
    current = item
    while True:
        parent_id = ranking_context(todos, current, by_id)
        if (
            parent_id == ROOT_ID
            or parent_id not in by_id
            or parent_id in seen
        ):
            break
        seen.add(parent_id)
        current = by_id[parent_id]
        path.append(current)
    path.reverse()
    return path
@

\paragraph{Who ranks among whom.}
A parent's children are of two kinds now.  Most are ranked under it.
A shared child ranked elsewhere is only a guest: its number was not
placed in this parent's range, and placing it there would contradict
the place that gave it.  So a guest is left out wherever the code
ranks the children of one parent against each other: it does not
bound a sibling's range, it is not a neighbour to place a new item
next to, and it does not move when the parent's subtree is squeezed.
[[ranked_under]] lists the children ranked under a parent, and
[[ranking_siblings_of]] the other items ranked where an item is.
[[children_of]] stays as it is, because listings and rollup ask about
all children.

<<child priority helpers>>=
def ranked_under(
    todos: list[TodoItem], parent_id: int,
) -> list[TodoItem]:
    """Return the children of `parent_id` that are ranked
    under it, leaving out shared children ranked under
    another of their parents."""
    by_id = {t.id: t for t in todos}
    return [
        t for t in children_of(todos, parent_id)
        if ranking_context(todos, t, by_id) == parent_id
    ]


def ranking_siblings_of(
    todos: list[TodoItem], item: TodoItem,
) -> list[TodoItem]:
    """Return the other items ranked under `item`'s
    ranking context."""
    return [
        t for t in ranked_under(
            todos, ranking_context(todos, item)
        )
        if t.id != item.id
    ]
