\paragraph{Falling below a place.}
The rule has a second half.  An item must also stay \emph{at least} as
high as each place puts it: under every parent it is a child of, it
must lie above the bottom of that parent's range.  Moves can break
that.  When A is demoted below D, B moves with A's subtree, as the
child ranked under A that it is (\cref{sec:subtree-follows-parent});
and B may land below D's range, where D's own children would outrank
it although B's place under D was above them.  The answer is the
lowest number that keeps B's highest place: the bottom of D's range,
below D's own children.

[[place_fallen_below]] finds the parent whose range an item has fallen
below, the one with the highest floor if there are several, since
lifting the item above that floor lifts it above the others too.  Only
items with several parents are asked.  One parent's range is kept by
the tree code above, and an item with one parent that lies outside its
parent's range was placed there by hand or before ranges existed,
which the commands leave as it is.  An unranked parent has no range to
fall below, and a done one has a range that orders nothing, so neither
holds an item up.  Nor does a parent that is itself below a done or an
unranked item: renumbering does not lay out its family
(\cref{sec:renumber-priorities}), so lifting an item into its range
would take the item out of the part of the list that is kept nested,
and the repair could not follow.  The parents that hold an item up are
those [[RankingIndex.laid_out]] accepts (\cref{sec:ranking-index}).

<<child priority helpers>>=
def place_fallen_below(
    todos: list[TodoItem],
    item: TodoItem,
    index: "RankingIndex | None" = None,
) -> TodoItem | None:
    """Return the parent of `item` whose child range
    `item` lies below, or `None`.

    Of several such parents, the one with the highest
    floor is returned.  Only ranked, not-done items with
    several parents are considered, and only parents that
    renumbering lays out hold an item up.  `index` may pass
    a `RankingIndex` of `todos`.
    """
    if (
        item.priority is None
        or item.status == "done"
        or len(parents_of(item)) < 2
    ):
        return None
    if index is None:
        index = RankingIndex(todos)
    fallen, highest = None, None
    for parent_id in real_parents_of(item):
        parent = index.by_id.get(parent_id)
        if parent is None or not index.laid_out(parent):
            continue
        _, low = index.range_of(parent)
        if item.priority <= low and (
            highest is None or low > highest
        ):
            fallen, highest = parent, low
    return fallen
@

The bottom of a range is the midpoint between its floor and the lowest
child ranked inside it, or the parent's own number if there is none.
That is what [[--bottom]] would give, computed so that it lies strictly
inside the range even when a child's number lies outside it, as only
old data has; [[place_among_siblings]] would step past such a child.

<<child priority helpers>>=
def bottom_of_range(
    todos: list[TodoItem],
    parent: TodoItem,
    item: TodoItem,
) -> float:
    """Return the number at the bottom of `parent`'s child
    range for `item`, below the children ranked there."""
    high, low = compute_child_priority_range(todos, parent)
    lowest = min(
        (
            t.priority for t in ranked_under(todos, parent.id)
            if t.id != item.id
            and t.status != "done"
            and t.priority is not None
            and low < t.priority < high
        ),
        default=high,
    )
    return (lowest + low) / 2.0


def lift_to_highest_place(
    todos: list[TodoItem], item: TodoItem,
) -> bool:
    """Raise `item` to the bottom of the range it fell
    below, if any, and tell whether it moved."""
    parent = place_fallen_below(todos, item)
    if parent is None:
        return False
    item.priority = bottom_of_range(todos, parent, item)
    return True
@

A lifted item is placed like any other: its subtree follows it and the
sibling it now sits under is squeezed, which is what
[[move_into_place]] does, and the lift is its first step.
[[settle_placement]] then asks every shared item, since one placement
can move several of them.  The loop ends: a lift only ever raises
numbers, its subtree move and squeeze included, and an item lifted
above a floor stays above it, so no item needs a second lift.  That
argument rests on numbers the commands wrote; a hand-edited file can
hold ranges that contradict each other, where lifting one item pushes
another back down.  So each item is lifted at most once per call, and
whatever is still below a place after that is left as it is rather
than lifted forever.  Each round reads the ranges from a fresh
[[RankingIndex]] (\cref{sec:ranking-index}), since the last lift
changed them, and a list without shared items has nothing to lift.

<<child priority helpers>>=
def keep_highest_places(
    todos: list[TodoItem],
) -> list[TodoItem]:
    """Lift every shared item that lies below one of its
    places, with its subtree; return the items moved."""
    touched = []
    lifted = set()
    if not any(len(parents_of(t)) > 1 for t in todos):
        return touched
    while True:
        index = RankingIndex(todos)
        fallen = next(
            (
                t for t in todos
                if t.id not in lifted
                and place_fallen_below(todos, t, index) is not None
            ),
            None,
        )
        if fallen is None:
            return touched
        lifted.add(fallen.id)
        touched += move_into_place(
            todos, fallen,
            child_priority_range_or_none(todos, fallen),
            (fallen.priority, list(fallen.parents)),
        )
@

\paragraph{A net under the moves.}
Each move above is made to keep the ranges nested, and with one parent
per item they do: the tree code is the argument of
\cref{sec:subtree-follows-parent,sec:sibling-above-squeezed}, and
nothing in it reads a ranking context, which with one parent cannot
change.  With several parents a move can change contexts it does not
touch.  A parent that moves past a shared child it is not ranking
changes which of the child's ranges holds the child's number, so the
child is suddenly ranked under the parent, at a number the parent's
subtree was not laid out around.  The moves cannot see that coming,
since they decide what to move by the contexts as they find them.

Rather than chase every such case in the moves, [[settle_placement]]
checks the result, and when it is not consistent it lays the ranked
items out again with [[renumber_priorities]], which builds nested
ranges from whatever ranking contexts the numbers give, and lifts the
shared items once more.  The check is the invariant itself: every
ranked, not-done item lies strictly inside the range of its ranking
context and above the floor of each of its other ranked parents, and
the items ranked under an item, at any depth, follow it directly in the
flat order.  Renumbering keeps every family's order, so the user's
ranking survives; only the numbers change, which is what renumbering
is for (\cref{sec:renumber-priorities}).  The check is confined to
what renumbering can repair: the items it lays out, those ranked under
live, ranked items all the way up.  A child of a done or an unranked
item is never moved by renumbering, so a check that counted it could
fail again after every repair.

The net repairs only what the placement broke.  The check runs on the
list as it was before the move and again after it, and renumbering
happens only when the ranges nested before and do not any more.  A
command that places several items in one go, as [[rm]] does with the
items it detaches and [[close_item]] with the children it moves, checks
the list once before it starts and repairs once after the last
placement ([[restore_nesting]]): checked before each placement, the
list would still hold the items not yet placed at their old numbers,
which need not nest, and the gate would stay shut.

The gate does not leave old numbers in place for good, though.  A list
whose ranges do not nest, because it was written before children were
placed by range, is normalised on the first write, before the command's
placement runs (\cref{sec:normalise-priorities}), so a placement
normally starts from a nested list and the gate is open.  It stays
closed only for what the normalisation could not nest, or for a hand
edit that lands while a command runs; there the net does not renumber
for the sake of numbers the command did not touch.  A list without
shared items skips both checks; it counts as one with shared items if it
has any before or after the placement, since an item that just lost its
second parent may have moved by the rules for shared items.

The repair is tried a few times, since lifting after a renumbering can
disturb the layout again, or leave too little room around the lifted
items: each lift takes the bottom of a range, half of what is left.
If the ranges still do not nest after that, something in the rules is
wrong.  The listings still work, they only show some sub-items away
from their parents, so the command does not fail; it says so on
standard error, naming the item it was placing, so that the case can be
reported and reproduced.  The tests and the fuzzers set
[[STRICT_RANKING]], which turns the warning into an exception, so that
a silent give-up cannot pass for success there.

<<constants>>=
STRICT_RANKING = False
<<child priority helpers>>=
class RankingNotNested(RuntimeError):
    """The ranges did not nest after the repairs."""


def report_unnested_ranking(item: TodoItem) -> None:
    """Warn that placing `item` left ranges that do not
    nest, or raise `RankingNotNested` under
    `STRICT_RANKING`."""
    message = (
        f"placing #{item.id} left priorities that do not nest; "
        "some sub-items may list away from their parents"
    )
    if STRICT_RANKING:
        raise RankingNotNested(message)
    typer.echo(f"Warning: {message}", err=True)
