The tests run strict, through a fixture that applies to every test of
the module, and two tests pin the gate and the give-up.  A legacy child
of A below~B, beside an unrelated shared item, is moved into A's range
by the first write, before the new item is placed; and a repair that
never succeeds, forced by a check that answers \enquote{nested} only
for the state before the move, warns, or raises in strict mode.

Strict mode catches a repair that gives up, but not a command that
leaves the ranges unnested without trying, and normalisation would
hide such a command (\cref{sec:normalise-priorities}): the next write
repairs what it left, and every read shows the repaired list.  A
command that writes twice, as [[rm]] does, even repairs itself.  So a
second fixture applies to every test of the module that stores a list:
it notes whether the list the test stored last, with [[save_todos]],
nested, and if it did, the list in the file must still nest when the
test ends, read as stored, with [[load_todos_raw]].  The tests that
check the ranges after a command also check that it printed no notice
of normalisation, which catches the repair within a command that
writes twice.

