\paragraph{Old numbers are normalised on the first write.}%
\label{sec:normalise-priorities}
The net above repairs only what a placement broke, and only in a list
whose ranges nested to begin with.  A list written before children were
placed by range does not nest, and stays that way: in a real list of
460 items, 266 of 318 open sub-items sat outside their parent's range.
There the gate would keep the net off for good, and every placement
would have to make do with ranges that contradict each other.

So the old numbers are normalised, automatically, the first time a
command writes the list.  [[mutate_todo_state]] checks the stored list
before it hands it to the command's callback, and when the ranges do
not nest, lays the ranked items out again with
[[renumber_priorities]] and lifts the shared ones
([[normalise_priorities]]).  Doing it \emph{before} the command is
what lets the command's own placement start from a nested list, with
the net armed; done after it, the command would place its item among
old numbers and the result would depend on them.  Renumbering keeps
every family's order, so what the user ranked stays ranked the same
way; but the flat order changes where a child sat outside its parent's
range, since it now sorts below its parent instead of among unrelated
items.  That is the intended change, accepted for single-parent lists
too.  Done and unranked items, and the items below them, are not laid
out and keep their numbers.  Commands that only read---[[ls]],
[[view]], [[ready]], and [[next]] until it starts something---never
write the file.

A command decides on a snapshot it read without the lock and commits
under the lock (\cref{sec:todo-read-modify-write}), so the snapshot
must show the numbers the commit will find, or a placement chosen
among old numbers would be stored among new ones: a child kept at its
number while it moves would land above its new parent.  So every read
normalises the list in memory, in the same deterministic way, without
a word and without writing; the reading commands show the order the
next write will store, and the commands that write decide on it.  Both
normalise the list as [[load_todo_state]] returns it, after the
statuses are brought in line with the active stack: which items are
laid out depends on their status, so the step must see the statuses
that both the snapshot and the commit see, whatever the
synchronisation may change.
For the same reason, a test that checks what a command stored reads
the file with [[load_todos_raw]]: [[load_todos]] would show it a list
normalised in memory, nested even when the file is not.

The step is never silent.  When it changed numbers it says how many,
on standard error, once, after the list is saved: a command that
refuses, say [[stop]] with nothing active, writes nothing and so has
renumbered nothing.  The first write need not come from a command the
user watches, though.  It may be a background writer---the hook that
finalises a detached run when its pane exits, or a [[next]] started by
cron---and there the notice goes where that process's standard error
goes, often nowhere.  We accept that: the renumbering keeps every
family's order, and there is no log file to put it in.

One pass of renumbering and lifting nests any list the commands can
produce, a few rounds at most where lifts disturb the layout.  If a
list still does not nest after that, the step leaves the numbers as
they were, rather than rewrite them on every write without ever getting
anywhere, and warns, once per process, or raises under
[[STRICT_RANKING]]; such a list is a bug to report.

<<child priority helpers>>=
def normalise_priorities(todos: list[TodoItem]) -> int:
    """Renumber `todos` into nested ranges, keeping each
    family's order, and return how many numbers changed.

    Done and unranked items, and those below them, keep
    their numbers.  The list may still not nest afterwards,
    which `ranking_is_consistent` tells.
    """
    before = {t.id: t.priority for t in todos}
    for _ in range(RANKING_REPAIRS):
        renumber_priorities(todos)
        keep_highest_places(todos)
        if ranking_is_consistent(todos):
            break
    return sum(1 for t in todos if t.priority != before[t.id])


def nest_stored_priorities(
    todos: list[TodoItem], warn: bool = True,
) -> int:
    """Normalise the priorities of a stored list that does
    not nest and return how many changed; with `warn`,
    report a list that cannot be nested."""
    if ranking_is_consistent(todos):
        return 0
    before = {t.id: t.priority for t in todos}
    changed = normalise_priorities(todos)
    if not ranking_is_consistent(todos):
        for t in todos:
            t.priority = before[t.id]
        if warn:
            report_unnestable_list()
        return 0
    return changed


def announce_renumbering(changed: int) -> None:
    """Say on standard error that `changed` stored numbers
    were renumbered, if any were."""
    if changed:
        typer.echo(
            f"Renumbered {changed} priorities so sub-items rank "
            "below their parents",
            err=True,
        )


def report_unnestable_list() -> None:
    """Warn, once per process, that the stored priorities
    cannot be nested, or raise under `STRICT_RANKING`."""
    global UNNESTABLE_WARNED
    message = (
        "the stored priorities could not be made to nest; "
        "they were left as they are"
    )
    if STRICT_RANKING:
        raise RankingNotNested(message)
    if not UNNESTABLE_WARNED:
        UNNESTABLE_WARNED = True
        typer.echo(f"Warning: {message}", err=True)
<<constants>>=
UNNESTABLE_WARNED = False
