\paragraph{Every range at once.}%
\label{sec:ranking-index}
The check asks for the range of every ranked parent, and
[[compute_child_priority_range]] answers one range by scanning the
parent's siblings and walking its line, each scan going over the whole
list to find ranking contexts.  Asked of every item, that is quadratic:
seconds for a few thousand items, on every placement.  Most of the work
is shared, though.  The floor a level inherits is the floor of the level
above, which is that level's own range, so the ranges can be worked out
from the top down, each from its parent's, with the contexts and the
families computed once.  [[RankingIndex]] does that, lazily and with a
memory of what it has worked out, and applies the same rule for a
level, [[level_floor]].  A cycle in the ranking line, which only a
hand-edited file has, makes a floor depend on where the walk started;
such floors are worked out but not remembered.

The closest lower sibling of a level is found by bisecting the sorted
numbers of its family, the live, ranked items with the same ranking
context; the level's own number is left out once, so that a sibling
level with it still counts as a tie.

<<child priority helpers>>=
class RankingIndex:
    """The ranking contexts and child ranges of `todos`,
    worked out once for many questions.

    `range_of(item)` answers what
    `compute_child_priority_range` answers, for any ranked
    item.  The index describes the list as it was built and
    must not be used after numbers or parents change.
    """

    def __init__(self, todos):
        self.todos = todos
        self.by_id = {t.id: t for t in todos}
        self.contexts = {
            t.id: ranking_context(todos, t, self.by_id)
            for t in todos
        }
        self.family = {}
        for t in todos:
            if t.status != "done" and t.priority is not None:
                self.family.setdefault(
                    self.contexts[t.id], []
                ).append(t.priority)
        for numbers in self.family.values():
            numbers.sort()
        self.lows = {}

    def closest_lower(self, item):
        """Return the closest lower sibling number of `item`."""
        numbers = self.family.get(self.contexts[item.id], [])
        at = bisect.bisect_right(numbers, item.priority)
        if item.status != "done":
            at -= 1
        return numbers[at - 1] if at > 0 else None

    def low(self, item, chain=frozenset()):
        """Return the floor of `item`'s child range."""
        if item.id in self.lows:
            return self.lows[item.id]
        sibling = self.closest_lower(item)
        inherited, clean = None, True
        if sibling is None or sibling >= item.priority:
            parent = self.by_id.get(self.contexts[item.id])
            if parent is not None and parent.priority is not None:
                if parent.id in chain or parent.id == item.id:
                    clean = False
                else:
                    inherited = self.low(parent, chain | {item.id})
                    clean = parent.id in self.lows
        low = level_floor(item.priority, sibling, inherited)
        if clean:
            self.lows[item.id] = low
        return low

    def range_of(self, item):
        """Return `item`'s child range as `(high, low)`."""
        return (item.priority, self.low(item))

    def laid_out(self, item):
        """Tell whether renumbering lays `item` out: it is
        ranked and not done, and so is every item on its
        ranking line up to the top level."""
        seen = set()
        while item.id not in seen:
            if item.status == "done" or item.priority is None:
                return False
            seen.add(item.id)
            context = self.contexts[item.id]
            if context == ROOT_ID:
                return True
            item = self.by_id.get(context)
            if item is None:
                return False
        return False
