\subsection{Minimal YAML Parser}
\label{sec:parse-yaml}

We need to parse YAML front matter blocks, but only a small subset:
flat key-value pairs with strings, numbers, and bracketed lists, plus
\emph{literal block scalars} for free text that spans several lines.
Rather than adding a PyYAML dependency for this narrow use case, we
implement a minimal parser. This keeps the dependency footprint small
and avoids YAML's many surprising edge cases (the \enquote{Norway
problem}, implicit type coercion, etc.).

The block scalars exist for long free text such as a description.
Every other value fits on its [[key: value]] line, but a description
is prose with paragraphs.  The parser is line oriented, so if such a
value were rendered inline and the user broke it into lines in the
editor, each continuation line would be silently dropped (no colon)
or, worse, become a bogus key (a colon somewhere in the sentence).  A
literal block, [[key: |]] followed by indented lines, gives the user a
clearly delimited region in which added lines are safe.

<<editor infrastructure>>=
def parse_yaml_block(text):
    """Parse a minimal YAML block into a dictionary.

    Handles flat key-value pairs with string, number,
    bracket-list and brace-dict values, and literal
    block scalars (``key: |``) whose indented lines are
    kept verbatim. This is sufficient for our front
    matter format.
    """
    result = {}
    lines = text.splitlines()
    idx = 0
    while idx < len(lines):
        stripped = lines[idx].strip()
        idx += 1
        if not stripped or stripped.startswith("#"):
            continue
        if ":" not in stripped:
            continue
        key, _, value = stripped.partition(":")
        key = key.strip()
        value = value.strip()
        if not key:
            continue
        indicator = parse_block_indicator(value)
        if indicator is None:
            result[key] = parse_yaml_value(value)
        else:
            <<collect the literal block>>
    return result
@

Where does a block end?  The tempting rule, \enquote{while the line is
indented at least as far as the first line}, fails on a value whose
first line is deeper than the rest---a pasted code line, an indented
list continuation: the block would end early and the rest would be
dropped without a word.  Instead the block runs through blank lines
and \emph{any} line that starts with whitespace, and ends only at the
first non-blank line in column~0.  This works because the renderer
(\cref{sec:render-yaml}) indents every content line by two spaces and
emits exactly one column-0 line after a block, the closing [[---]], so
whatever text the block holds, it comes back unchanged.  The price is
that a key following a block must sit in column~0, which the renderer
guarantees and a hand-written buffer must respect.

<<collect the literal block>>=
block = []
while idx < len(lines):
    line = lines[idx]
    if line.strip() and not line[0].isspace():
        break
    block.append(line)
    idx += 1
result[key] = join_literal_block(block, *indicator)
@

The indicator after the colon is [[|]] alone or with a single digit
and a chomping sign, in either order, as in YAML: [[|-]], [[|2]],
[[|2+]].  The digit is the block's indentation.  Without it we take
the \emph{smallest} indentation of any content line; YAML itself
requires the digit precisely when the first content line starts with
whitespace, because no parser can otherwise tell block indentation
from content, and the renderer emits it in that case.  We do not
recognise the folded indicator [[>]], since folding rewrites line
breaks and silently guessing would be worse than leaving the value
alone.

<<editor infrastructure>>=
def parse_block_indicator(value):
    """Return ``(indent, chomp)`` for a literal block indicator.

    Recognizes ``|`` with an optional indentation digit
    (1--9) and an optional chomping sign, in either
    order. Returns ``None`` for anything else.
    """
    if not value.startswith("|") or len(value) > 3:
        return None
    rest = value[1:]
    digits = [c for c in rest if c in "123456789"]
    signs = [c for c in rest if c in "+-"]
    if len(digits) > 1 or len(signs) > 1:
        return None
    if len(digits) + len(signs) != len(rest):
        return None
    indent = int(digits[0]) if digits else None
    chomp = signs[0] if signs else ""
    return indent, chomp
@

Joining strips \emph{up to} the block indentation from each line, not
exactly that much, so that a hand-written block with a first line
deeper than the rest does not come back truncated.  Blank lines come
back empty so that paragraph breaks survive.  A tab counts as one
character; the renderer never emits tabs, so a hand-typed tab can at
most leave a line with extra leading spaces, never truncate the block.
Trailing newlines are dropped, as [[notes]] are stripped, because a
trailing newline in a description is never meaningful; the [[+]]
chomping sign keeps them for a user who insists.  The value bypasses
[[parse_yaml_value]]: literal means literal, so a description that
happens to look like a number stays a string.

<<editor infrastructure>>=
def join_literal_block(block, indent=None, chomp=""):
    """Dedent and join the lines of a literal block.

    Strips up to ``indent`` leading characters from each
    line (the smallest indentation found when ``None``),
    turns whitespace-only lines into empty ones, and
    drops trailing newlines unless ``chomp`` is ``+``.
    """
    content = [line for line in block if line.strip()]
    if indent is None:
        indent = min(
            (len(line) - len(line.lstrip()) for line in content),
            default=0,
        )
    dedented = []
    for line in block:
        if not line.strip():
            dedented.append("")
        else:
            lead = len(line) - len(line.lstrip())
            dedented.append(line[min(indent, lead):])
    text = "\n".join(dedented)
    if chomp == "+":
        return text + "\n" if content else ""
    return text.rstrip("\n")
@

Inline values are coerced by type, as far as the subset goes:

<<editor infrastructure>>=
def parse_yaml_value(value):
    """Parse a single YAML value.

    Recognizes: bracketed lists, brace-delimited dicts,
    floats, integers, and plain strings. Quoted strings
    have quotes stripped.
    """
    if not value:
        return ""
    if value.startswith("[") and value.endswith("]"):
        inner = value[1:-1]
        if not inner.strip():
            return []
        return [
            v.strip().strip("'\"")
            for v in inner.split(",")
        ]
    if value.startswith("{") and value.endswith("}"):
        inner = value[1:-1]
        if not inner.strip():
            return {}
        result = {}
        for pair in inner.split(","):
            if ":" not in pair:
                continue
            k, _, v = pair.partition(":")
            result[k.strip()] = v.strip()
        return result
    if value.startswith(("'", '"')):
        return value.strip("'\"")
    try:
        return int(value)
    except ValueError:
        pass
    try:
        return float(value)
    except ValueError:
        pass
    return value
@

\subsection{Rendering YAML}
\label{sec:render-yaml}

We need the inverse of parsing to support round-trip editing: render
a todo back to the same document format so the user can re-edit it.
We only emit keys with non-default values so the editor document
stays uncluttered.

<<editor infrastructure>>=
def render_yaml_block(metadata):
    """Render a dictionary as minimal YAML front matter.

    Produces the ``---``-delimited block. Returns empty
    string if metadata is empty.
    """
    if not metadata:
        return ""
    lines = ["---"]
    for key, value in metadata.items():
        if value is None or value == "" or value == []:
            continue
        if isinstance(value, list):
            items = ", ".join(str(v) for v in value)
            lines.append(f"{key}: [{items}]")
        elif isinstance(value, dict):
            items = ", ".join(
                f"{k}: {v}" for k, v in value.items()
            )
            lines.append(f"{key}: {{{items}}}")
        elif needs_literal_block(key, value):
            lines.extend(render_literal_block(key, value))
        else:
            lines.append(f"{key}: {value}")
    lines.append("---")
    if len(lines) == 2:
        return ""
    return "\n".join(lines)
@

A string goes out as a literal block in three cases.  The description
always does, even when it is a single line: the block is what makes it
safe for the user to add lines (\cref{sec:parse-yaml}), and if the
form depended on the current value, so would the failure mode---a
description that is safe to expand today would silently lose text
tomorrow.  Any string spanning several lines does too, which also
closes a latent hole: [[command]] and [[wd]] may legally contain
newlines, and the inline form used to truncate them at the first
newline on the next round trip.  Finally, a string that would itself
read as a block indicator, such as a bare [[|]], would otherwise come
back as the start of a block that swallows the keys after it.

We split with [[splitlines()]] on both sides rather than on [[\n]]
alone: the parser sees the buffer through [[splitlines()]], which also
breaks at form feeds and Unicode line separators, so a value containing
one would be emitted as one physical line but read back as two, the
second unindented and the block cut short.  Blank lines go out empty
rather than as two spaces, since many editors strip trailing whitespace
on save and either form parses the same.  When the first content line
starts with whitespace we emit the indentation digit, as YAML requires
(\cref{sec:parse-yaml}).

<<editor infrastructure>>=
def needs_literal_block(key, value):
    """Return whether ``value`` must be rendered as a literal block."""
    if not isinstance(value, str):
        return False
    if key in LITERAL_BLOCK_KEYS:
        return True
    if len(value.splitlines()) > 1:
        return True
    return parse_block_indicator(value.strip()) is not None


def render_literal_block(key, value):
    """Render ``value`` as an indented literal block under ``key``."""
    lines = value.splitlines()
    first = next((line for line in lines if line.strip()), "")
    indicator = "|"
    if first[:1].isspace():
        indicator += str(len(LITERAL_BLOCK_INDENT))
    rendered = [f"{key}: {indicator}"]
    for line in lines:
        rendered.append(
            LITERAL_BLOCK_INDENT + line if line.strip() else ""
        )
    return rendered
<<constants>>=
LITERAL_BLOCK_KEYS = ("description",)
LITERAL_BLOCK_INDENT = "  "
@

\subsection{Testing the YAML Subset}

The block rules are exactly the kind of detail that drifts, so we pin
them down one by one, then check that whatever the renderer emits the
parser reads back unchanged.

<<test functions>>=
