\subsection{Parsing Editor Content}

The parser walks the document line by line, building a tree from
headings. The algorithm uses a stack to track the current nesting
level: a [[# Heading]] starts a level-1 child, [[## Heading]]
starts a level-2 child (nested under the preceding level-1), and
so on.

The key insight is that each heading level maps directly to tree
depth, so we maintain a stack of the current path from root to the
active item. When we encounter a heading at level~\(n\), we pop the
stack back to depth~$n-1$ and push a new child there.

<<editor infrastructure>>=
def parse_editor_content(text):
    """Parse editor content into a ParsedTodo tree.

    Format:
    - First non-empty line = title
    - Optional ``---``...``---`` in column 0 = YAML front matter
    - Text until first heading = parent notes
    - ``# Heading`` = level-1 child
    - ``## Heading`` = level-2 child (of preceding #)
    - Each heading may have its own YAML block + notes

    A front matter block that is never closed is kept as
    notes and reported in ``warnings`` rather than
    discarded.
    """
    lines = text.splitlines()
    warnings = []
    <<parse title from first line>>
    <<parse optional yaml block>>
    <<parse notes and headings>>
    return ParsedTodo(
        title=title,
        notes=parent_notes.strip(),
        metadata=parent_metadata,
        children=top_children,
        warnings=warnings,
    )
@

The title is simply the first non-empty line, analogous to how
[[git commit]] treats the first line of the commit message:

<<parse title from first line>>=
title = ""
idx = 0
while idx < len(lines):
    stripped = lines[idx].strip()
    if stripped:
        title = stripped
        idx += 1
        break
    idx += 1
@

After the title, an optional YAML front matter block provides
metadata. We detect it by looking for a line that is exactly
[[---]] in column~0.  The column matters: a literal block inside the
front matter (\cref{sec:parse-yaml}) may contain a [[---]] line of
its own, say a Markdown rule in a description, and that line is
indented, so requiring the delimiter to start the line is what keeps
block content from closing the front matter early.  Trailing
whitespace is still tolerated, since editors add and strip it freely.

The search for the closing delimiter stops at the end of the buffer or
at the first column-0 line beginning with [[#]].  That second stop is
what keeps a \emph{missing} closing delimiter from being catastrophic:
without it, the next [[---]] in the document would close the block, and
the next [[---]] after an unclosed parent block is the opening
delimiter of the first sub-item's own block.  The parent's notes and
the [[# Child]] heading between them would be handed to
[[parse_yaml_block]] as metadata, and the sub-items would vanish.  A
column-0 [[#]] is a safe place to stop because the renderer indents
every literal block line by two spaces (\cref{sec:render-yaml}), so no
line it emits inside front matter can start there; such a line can only
be the heading that opens a sub-item.  The price is that a YAML comment
written in column~0 inside the front matter would end the block early,
but the renderer has never emitted one and the format has never
promised them.

<<parse optional yaml block>>=
parent_metadata = {}
while idx < len(lines) and not lines[idx].strip():
    idx += 1
if idx < len(lines) and lines[idx].rstrip() == "---":
    opening = idx
    idx += 1
    closing = None
    while idx < len(lines):
        if lines[idx].rstrip() == "---":
            closing = idx
            break
        if lines[idx].startswith("#"):
            break
        idx += 1
    if closing is None:
        <<keep the parent block as notes>>
    else:
        yaml_text = "\n".join(
            lines[opening + 1:closing]
        )
        parent_metadata = parse_yaml_block(yaml_text)
        idx = closing + 1
@

When the delimiter never turns up we could report the buffer as
malformed and exit non-zero, but that would discard the whole editing
session---the user's only copy of the text they just typed---over one
missing pair of dashes.  Rewinding costs nothing instead: [[idx]] goes
back to the opening [[---]], and the loop below reads that line and
everything after it as ordinary notes and headings.  No byte is lost,
the sub-items are parsed as usual, and because the metadata stays empty
[[<<update parent from parsed>>]] leaves every field of the item alone
rather than overwriting it from a half-read block.  The stray [[---]]
shows up in [[view]], where the user can see it and fix it on the next
edit.

<<keep the parent block as notes>>=
idx = opening
warnings.append(
    "front matter opened with --- but never "
    "closed; kept it as notes"
)
@

The remaining content splits into parent notes (text before any
heading) and children (created by headings). We use the heading
level to maintain the tree structure via a stack.

<<parse notes and headings>>=
parent_notes_lines = []
top_children = []
heading_re = re.compile(r"^(#+)\s+(.*)")

<<initialize heading parser state>>

while idx < len(lines):
    line = lines[idx]
    heading_match = heading_re.match(line)
    if heading_match:
        <<handle heading line>>
    else:
        <<handle content line>>
    idx += 1

<<finalize last item notes>>
@

We use a stack of (level, children-list) pairs to handle arbitrarily
nested headings without recursion.  When a heading at level \(n\)
appears, we pop entries deeper than \(n\)---automatically closing any
open sub-items---then push the new item onto the stack at level \(n\).
This mirrors how markdown heading depth implies document structure.

<<initialize heading parser state>>=
current_item = None
current_notes_lines = []
current_yaml_lines = None
in_yaml = False
# Stack: list of (level, children_list) pairs
# Level 0 = top-level children
stack = [(0, top_children)]
@

When we encounter a heading, we finalize the previous item's
notes, determine where in the tree the new item belongs, and
push it onto the appropriate children list:

<<handle heading line>>=
<<finalize current item>>
level = len(heading_match.group(1))
item_title = heading_match.group(2).strip()
current_item = ParsedItem(title=item_title)
current_notes_lines = []
current_yaml_lines = None
in_yaml = False
# Pop stack to find parent at level-1
while len(stack) > 1 and stack[-1][0] >= level:
    stack.pop()
# Add to parent's children
stack[-1][1].append(current_item)
# Push this item's children list
stack.append((level, current_item.children))
@

Content lines go to either the parent notes (if no heading seen
yet) or the current item's notes. We also detect YAML blocks
within heading sections, again only in column~0.  Note that the
heading test above runs on every line, including those inside a
child's YAML block, and its regex is anchored at the start of the
line; this is why a literal block indents its content.  A [[#]] in
column~0 inside a child's block would not be content but a new
heading, finalizing the half-read child and creating a bogus one.

<<handle content line>>=
if current_item is None:
    parent_notes_lines.append(line)
else:
    if line.rstrip() == "---":
        if in_yaml:
            in_yaml = False
        elif current_yaml_lines is None:
            current_yaml_lines = []
            in_yaml = True
        else:
            current_notes_lines.append(line)
    elif in_yaml:
        current_yaml_lines.append(line)
    else:
        current_notes_lines.append(line)
@

A sub-item's block can be left open just as the parent's can, and the
remedy mirrors the parent's for the same reason: the lines the parser
had been setting aside as metadata are text the user typed, so they
belong in the notes rather than in the bit bucket.  Only the detection
differs.  The parent's block has to be scanned ahead for a delimiter
that may not be there, whereas a sub-item's block is closed implicitly
by the next heading or by the end of the buffer, so by the time we
finalize the item [[in_yaml]] already answers the question.  We splice
the [[---]] back in where it stood, after the notes collected before it
and before the lines collected after it, so that the notes read exactly
as they were typed.

<<finalize current item>>=
if current_item is not None:
    if in_yaml:
        <<keep the child block as notes>>
    elif current_yaml_lines is not None:
        current_item.metadata = parse_yaml_block(
            "\n".join(current_yaml_lines)
        )
    current_item.notes = "\n".join(
        current_notes_lines
    ).strip()
@

The warning names the sub-item, since a document may hold many of them
and the user needs to know which one to go and look at.

<<keep the child block as notes>>=
current_notes_lines = (
    current_notes_lines
    + ["---"]
    + current_yaml_lines
)
warnings.append(
    f"sub-item {current_item.title!r}: front matter "
    "never closed; kept it as notes"
)
@

<<finalize last item notes>>=
if current_item is not None:
    <<finalize current item>>
parent_notes = "\n".join(parent_notes_lines)
@

