\subsection{Rendering Todo as Markdown}

The renderer is the inverse of the parser: given a [[TodoItem]]
and its children (looked up from the full todo list), it produces
the same markdown format that the editor expects. This enables
round-tripping: render an existing todo, edit it, parse it back.

Children use the shared display ordering so the editor shows the same
mixture the rest of the CLI does: ranked items first, then unranked
items in deadline order.  That consistency matters because the editor is
not just a text blob; it is another view of the same priority model.

We only include metadata fields that differ from defaults to keep
the output clean. The [[wd]], [[command]], [[deadline]],
[[estimated]], [[who]], [[labels]], and [[description]] fields are
emitted when set.

\label{sec:editor-description}
The description is the one field that is neither the title nor the
body, and the body already has an owner: the text between the front
matter and the first heading is the notes, the running log that
[[note]] appends to, and [[#]] headings are children, so a
[[# Description]] section would simply be a child called
\enquote{Description}.  That leaves the front matter, where a literal
block (\cref{sec:render-yaml}) keeps the description editable as
prose.  The cost is that a long description must keep its two-space
indentation when edited or pasted; the benefit is that the standing
statement of the task and its running log are both in the buffer,
each in its own place.  The description goes last so that the short
keys stay in view above a block that may run for a screen.  As with
the other keys, an item without a description does not advertise the
key.  The discovery argument that makes [[add --edit]] always show
[[who]] (\cref{sec:add-via-editor}) does not carry here: two more
lines in every buffer would cost more than a rarely used field gains.

<<editor infrastructure>>=
def render_todo_markdown(
    item, todos,
):
    """Render a todo item as editor markdown.

    Produces the same format that parse_editor_content
    expects, enabling round-trip editing.
    """
    lines = [item.title]
    meta = build_item_metadata(item)
    yaml_block = render_yaml_block(meta)
    if yaml_block:
        lines.append(yaml_block)
    lines.append("")
    if item.notes:
        lines.append(item.notes)
        lines.append("")
    children = sort_todos_for_display(
        children_of(todos, item.id)
    )
    for child in children:
        lines.extend(
            render_child_markdown(child, todos, 1)
        )
    return "\n".join(lines)
@

We extract only non-default metadata fields so the YAML block stays
minimal---an empty dict produces no block at all, keeping the editor
document uncluttered for simple todos.

Two of the keys are read out of [[__dict__]] rather than off the item.
The buffer is not a report on the item, it is the item's editable
state: whatever it shows is what [[apply_metadata]] reads back and
stores when the user saves, so a line the user never typed is
indistinguishable from one they did.  For [[wd]] and [[command]]---the
two members of [[WORKER_DEFAULT_ATTRIBUTES]] this function can
emit---an ordinary attribute read answers with the worker's configured
default whenever the item stores nothing, and rendering \emph{that}
would let an unrelated edit freeze runtime policy into
[[todos.json]], which is exactly what [[to_dict]] refuses to do
(\cref{todo:worker-defaults}).  Asking [[__dict__]] asks the question
the buffer is actually posing: what does this item carry of its own?

The remaining keys stay plain reads.  No policy stands behind them, so
the stored value and the resolved value are the same value, and
writing them any other way would suggest a distinction that is not
there.

<<editor infrastructure>>=
def build_item_metadata(item):
    """Extract non-default metadata from a TodoItem.

    Worker defaults are deliberately not rendered: the
    buffer shows what the item stores, since whatever it
    shows is stored back on save.

    Returns a dict suitable for YAML front matter.
    """
    stored = item.__dict__
    meta = {}
    if stored.get("wd"):
        meta["wd"] = stored["wd"]
    if item.worktree:
        meta["worktree"] = item.worktree
    if stored.get("command"):
        meta["command"] = stored["command"]
    if item.labels:
        meta["labels"] = item.labels
    if item.deadline:
        meta["deadline"] = item.deadline
    if item.estimated:
        meta["estimate"] = item.estimated
    if item.who:
        meta["who"] = item.who
    if item.github:
        meta["github"] = item.github
    if item.description:
        meta["description"] = item.description
    return meta
@

