\subsection{Applying Metadata to an Item}
\label{sec:apply-metadata}

[[build_item_metadata]] turns an item into front matter; this is the
inverse direction, and until now it had no home.  Front-matter keys were
applied by an if-chain written out five times over---for the parent and
for the matched children of [[edit]], and for the parent and the new
children of [[add]], the latter twice because [[add]] creates children
in its own recursion.  Five copies of one rule drift, and these had
drifted: [[github]] was applied for the parent but for none of the
children, so a user who linked a sub-item to an issue in the editor
watched the link vanish on save.  Collecting the per-key normalizers
into a single table is what stops the next key from drifting the same
way: adding a key becomes one edit instead of five, and forgetting a
site stops being possible.

The contract is that a key \emph{present} in [[meta]] overwrites the
field while an \emph{absent} key leaves it alone.  Deleting
[[deadline:]] from the buffer therefore does not clear the deadline,
and deleting the description block does not clear the description;
[[edit N --deadline ""]] and [[edit N --description ""]] are how a
field is cleared.  The asymmetry is deliberate: the buffer is rendered
from the item and shows only the keys that are set, so a user who has
never seen a key cannot be said to have deleted it.  Keys the table
does not know are ignored rather than rejected, which leaves the front
matter open for a user's own annotations.

Each key carries the normalizer its own site already applied: [[wd]] is
canonicalized to an absolute path, [[worktree]] is validated as a name
without path separators, [[deadline]] is normalized to a comparable
string, and [[estimate]] is parsed into hours.  Gathering them here
changes none of them; it only stops them from being written out five
times, and makes the set of keys something a reader can take in at a
glance.

Every string-valued key coerces its value with [[str]] before storing
it.  That is not defensive habit but a consequence of the parser: our
minimal YAML subset (\cref{sec:parse-yaml}) types a bare number for
\emph{every} key, so a hand-typed [[wd: 2026]] arrives here as an
[[int]].  Coercing at this one point is what keeps the field types the
rest of the module assumes; without it the integer surfaces far from
the editor session that stored it, in [[canonicalize_wd]], in the
[[subprocess]] call that [[command]] feeds, or in the Markdown renderer
and the calendar export that read [[description]].  A list-valued key
gets the same treatment in its own shape: [[labels: grading]] is a
string, not a list, and wrapping it makes it the one label the user
meant rather than the six letters [[sorted(set(...))]] would otherwise
store.

[[github]] and [[env]] are the two dictionary-valued keys, and they get
the opposite treatment---a value that is not a dictionary is left
alone.  There is no honest dictionary to make out of [[github: 5]], and
storing something else in a field the [[gh]] wrappers index by [[repo]]
and [[number]] would only move the failure to the next [[sync]].  The
sentinel [[METADATA_UNCHANGED]] lets the table say that in the same
shape as every other entry: a normalizer that declines to produce a
value.

<<editor infrastructure>>=
METADATA_UNCHANGED = object()


def dict_or_unchanged(value):
    """Return `value` when it is a dict, else the sentinel.

    The sentinel `METADATA_UNCHANGED` tells
    `apply_metadata` to leave the field untouched.
    """
    if isinstance(value, dict):
        return value
    return METADATA_UNCHANGED
@

The table maps a front-matter key to the attribute it writes and the
normalizer that guards it.  [[command]] goes through
[[normalize_command]] (defined next to [[normalize_worktree_name]],
its worktree counterpart) rather than bare [[str]], so that a
[[command:]] line left empty clears the field instead of storing an
empty string that would shadow the worker default.  Like its
neighbours it is wrapped in a lambda: the table is built at import
time, before the helpers are defined, and the lambda defers the lookup
to the call.  Only [[estimate]] has a name of its own:
the key reads as a noun in the buffer while the field it feeds,
[[estimated]], reads as a stored fact, and renaming either now would
break every todo file and every buffer already written.

<<editor infrastructure>>=
METADATA_FIELDS = {
    "wd": ("wd", lambda v: canonicalize_wd(str(v))),
    "worktree": (
        "worktree",
        lambda v: normalize_worktree_name(str(v)),
    ),
    "command": (
        "command",
        lambda v: normalize_command(v),
    ),
    "labels": (
        "labels",
        lambda v: list(v) if isinstance(v, list) else [str(v)],
    ),
    "deadline": (
        "deadline",
        lambda v: normalize_deadline(str(v)),
    ),
    "estimate": (
        "estimated",
        lambda v: parse_hours(str(v)),
    ),
    "who": ("who", str),
    "github": ("github", dict_or_unchanged),
    "env": ("env", dict_or_unchanged),
    "description": ("description", str),
}


def apply_metadata(item, meta):
    """Apply editor front-matter keys to `item` in place.

    Each key present in `meta` overwrites the matching
    field; an absent key leaves the field alone, and an
    unknown key is ignored.
    """
    for key, (attribute, normalize) in METADATA_FIELDS.items():
        if key not in meta:
            continue
        value = normalize(meta[key])
        if value is not METADATA_UNCHANGED:
            setattr(item, attribute, value)
@

Creation sites cannot simply call the helper, because a new item also
inherits: a child takes its parent's labels, deadline and assignee, and
[[add]] takes whatever its command line said.  They construct the item
with the inherited values, then call [[apply_metadata]], then---on the
[[add]] parent, the only site with flags to consider---re-apply the
flags.  That order is the precedence rule made executable: inherited
default below front matter below command-line flag.

