\section{Placing an Item Without Asking}%
\label{todo:placement}

The binary search is the right tool when a human is at the keyboard
and the wrong one for a script.  An agent that creates a module's
worth of tasks already knows where each one belongs; what it lacks is
a way to say so, and the workaround---piping a stream of [[y]] or
[[n]] answers into the prompt---reaches only the two extremes and
exits on a broken pipe.

Four requests turn out to be the same request.  [[add --top]] and
[[add --bottom]] place a new item at one end of its sibling list;
[[reprioritize --above]] and [[--below]] place an existing item next
to a named neighbour; [[edit --top]] and [[--bottom]] re-rank where
the item already is; and the re-ranking after [[edit --parent]] does
it among siblings the item has just joined.  All of them ask
\enquote{put this item at this position among these siblings}, so they
share one helper.  Sharing is not merely tidy here: the interesting
placements are the ones \emph{between} two neighbours, and a caller
that computes its own midpoint is a caller that will eventually
disagree with the others about the empty list, the outer edges, or
which of the two priority numbers to interpolate.

\subsection{Naming a Position}

[[assign_priority_at_position]] already takes an insertion index, so
each flag only has to name one.  [[--top]] and [[--bottom]] name the
ends directly.  [[--above]] and [[--below]] name a neighbour instead,
which is the same thing once the siblings are sorted: the slot just
above a target is the target's own index, and the slot just below it
is the next one.

<<constants>>=
PLACE_TOP = "top"
PLACE_BOTTOM = "bottom"
PLACE_ABOVE = "above"
PLACE_BELOW = "below"
<<placement helpers>>=
def sibling_insert_position(
    ordered: list[TodoItem],
    placement: str,
    target: TodoItem | None = None,
) -> int:
    """Return the insertion index named by `placement`.

    `ordered` holds ranked siblings, highest stored
    priority first.  `PLACE_ABOVE` and `PLACE_BELOW`
    need `target`, a sibling present in `ordered`.
    """
    if placement == PLACE_TOP:
        return 0
    if placement == PLACE_BOTTOM:
        return len(ordered)
    index = next(
        i
        for i, sibling in enumerate(ordered)
        if sibling.id == target.id
    )
    if placement == PLACE_ABOVE:
        return index
    return index + 1
@

\subsection{Ordering by Stored Priority}

Placement reads the \emph{stored} priorities, not the effective ones.
A deadline boost is temporary by design
(\cref{todo:effective-priority}): interpolating between boosted scores
and storing the result would let a sibling one day from its deadline
donate its urgency to the item we are placing, and that borrowed
urgency would outlive the deadline that justified it.  This is the
argument the [[--append]] path has always made; it now covers every
placement, the interactive search included, which still \emph{asks}
in the boosted order the user sees (\cref{todo:binary-search}).

<<placement helpers>>=
def require_stored_priority(item: TodoItem) -> float:
    """Return the stored base priority of a ranked item."""
    if item.priority is None:
        raise ValueError(
            f"todo #{item.id} has no numeric priority"
        )
    return item.priority


def sort_siblings_by_stored_priority(
    siblings: list[TodoItem],
) -> list[TodoItem]:
    """Return the ranked siblings, highest stored
    priority first."""
    ranked = [
        sibling
        for sibling in siblings
        if sibling.priority is not None
    ]
    return sorted(
        ranked,
        key=require_stored_priority,
        reverse=True,
    )
@

\subsection{Turning a Position into a Number}%
\label{sec:placement-number}

The comparison set is one worker's siblings, because asking a user to
rank another worker's tasks against their own would be meaningless
(\cref{todo:sub-item-helpers}).  The child ranges, however, are
computed from every worker's siblings, since they share one flat list.
A number taken only from one worker's neighbours can therefore land on
another worker's item, or between that item and its children.  Two
workers who each add a first child under the same parent both get the
middle of the parent's range, the same number.

So the position the user chose is translated into a position among
\emph{all} the active ranked siblings before it becomes a number.  The
new item goes directly above the sibling of the same worker that it was
placed above, which is what the user said; at the bottom of that
worker's list it goes directly below the lowest one.  A worker with no
ranked sibling yet, where nothing was asked, starts at the bottom of
the level, which is the least presumptuous place among other people's
work.  Only the numbers of the neighbours and the parent's range then
matter, and [[assign_priority_at_position]] reads them as stored
numbers.  With a single worker the two lists coincide and nothing
changes.

<<placement helpers>>=
def placement_level(
    siblings: list[TodoItem],
    candidate: TodoItem | None,
    todos: list[TodoItem] | None,
) -> list[TodoItem]:
    """Return every active ranked sibling of `candidate`,
    of any worker, together with `siblings`.

    Without `todos` or `candidate` only `siblings` are
    known, and they are returned as they are.
    """
    ranked = [s for s in siblings if s.priority is not None]
    if todos is None or candidate is None:
        return ranked
    level = get_active_siblings(
        todos, candidate, ranked_only=True
    )
    known = {s.id for s in level}
    return level + [s for s in ranked if s.id not in known]


def number_at_position(
    own: list[TodoItem],
    position: int,
    level: list[TodoItem],
    bounds: tuple | None = None,
) -> float:
    """Return the stored priority for an insertion at
    `position` among `own`, interpolated in `level`.

    `own` is the comparison set in the order `position`
    refers to and must be part of `level`, all the
    ranked siblings.  `bounds` is the parent's child
    range, if any.
    """
    ordered = sort_siblings_by_stored_priority(level)
    index = [s.id for s in ordered]
    if position < len(own):
        at = index.index(own[position].id)
    elif own:
        at = index.index(own[-1].id) + 1
    else:
        at = len(ordered)
    return assign_priority_at_position(
        ordered, at,
        priority_of=require_stored_priority,
        bounds=bounds,
    )
@

