\subsection{The Shared Placement Helper}

With a position and an ordering, placement is one call.  The empty
comparison set is the only case needing separate treatment, and it
gets the rule the interactive path already uses
(\cref{sec:child-priority-helpers}) rather than a second opinion of
its own.  The ends of a child level are bounded by the parent's range,
exactly as in the interactive search (\cref{todo:binary-search}).

<<placement helpers>>=
def place_among_siblings(
    siblings: list[TodoItem],
    placement: str,
    target: TodoItem | None = None,
    parent: TodoItem | None = None,
    todos: list[TodoItem] | None = None,
    candidate: TodoItem | None = None,
) -> float:
    """Return a stored priority placing an item at
    `placement` among `siblings`.

    `placement` is one of the `PLACE_*` values;
    `PLACE_ABOVE` and `PLACE_BELOW` need `target`.
    `parent` and `todos` supply the parent's range and
    the fallback for a level with no ranked sibling
    yet; with `candidate`, the item being placed, the
    number also respects other workers' siblings.
    """
    ordered = sort_siblings_by_stored_priority(siblings)
    level = placement_level(ordered, candidate, todos)
    if not level:
        return default_priority_without_siblings(
            parent, todos
        )
    return number_at_position(
        ordered,
        sibling_insert_position(
            ordered, placement, target
        ),
        level,
        bounds=(
            child_priority_range_or_none(todos, parent)
            if todos is not None else None
        ),
    )
@

