\subsection{Re-ranking an Item That Already Exists}

Two commands re-rank an item already on disk.  [[reprioritize]] does
it on request; [[edit]] does it after [[--parent]] has moved the item,
because a number chosen among the old siblings says nothing about the
new ones.  Both need the same three answers---which parent bounds the
range, which siblings form the comparison set, and how a placement
becomes a number---so both ask one function rather than assembling
their own.

The comparison set is the item's active, ranked siblings for its own
worker.  Restricting it to one worker is what makes the resulting
number mean anything at all (\cref{todo:sub-item-helpers}); excluding
done siblings keeps the ordering about work that is still ahead.

<<placement helpers>>=
def comparison_siblings(
    todos: list[TodoItem], item: TodoItem
) -> list[TodoItem]:
    """Return the siblings `item` is ranked against.

    These are the active, ranked siblings assigned to the
    same worker, highest stored priority first.
    """
    return sort_siblings_by_stored_priority(
        get_active_siblings(
            todos,
            item,
            who=item.who or default_username,
            ranked_only=True,
        )
    )
@

An unranked parent is a boundary rather than an error at this level.
Children of an unranked parent stay unranked---that is the invariant
[[add]] enforces when it creates them---so a re-ranking helper that
raised here would only force every caller to pre-check the same
condition.  Returning [[None]] instead lets the caller store exactly
what it was handed: an item that has just moved under an unranked
parent becomes unranked itself, and the invariant survives the move.

<<placement helpers>>=
def place_existing_item(
    todos: list[TodoItem],
    item: TodoItem,
    placement: str | None = None,
    target: TodoItem | None = None,
) -> float | None:
    """Return a new stored priority for `item`.

    `placement` is one of the `PLACE_*` values, or `None`
    to ask the user through the interactive binary
    search.  Returns `None` when the item's parent is
    itself unranked, since a ranked child of an unranked
    parent has no interval to live in.
    """
    parent_ctx = get_parent(todos, item)
    if (
        parent_ctx is not None
        and parent_ctx.priority is None
    ):
        return None
    siblings = comparison_siblings(todos, item)
    if placement is None:
        return binary_search_priority(
            siblings,
            item,
            parent=parent_ctx,
            todos=todos,
        )
    return place_among_siblings(
        siblings,
        placement,
        target,
        parent=parent_ctx,
        todos=todos,
        candidate=item,
    )
@

