\subsection{Refusing Contradictory Instructions}

Every command that can place an item offers several ways to say where,
and no two of them can be honoured at once: [[--prio]] says
\enquote{this exact number}, [[--top]] and [[--bottom]] say
\enquote{derive one from the siblings}, and [[--skip-priority]] and
[[--keep-prio]] say \enquote{do not derive one at all}.  Silently
letting one win would make a script's mistake invisible for as long as
the two flags happen to agree, so we refuse the combination instead.

The check is table-driven because the three commands offer different
subsets of the flags and would otherwise each grow their own ladder of
pairwise comparisons; a caller lists the flags it accepts, in the order
it wants them named, and gets the same message shape everywhere.

<<placement helpers>>=
def reject_conflicting_flags(flags: dict) -> None:
    """Exit when more than one placement flag is set.

    `flags` maps option spellings to whether the caller
    used them; the first two that are set are named in
    the error.
    """
    used = [
        name for name, active in flags.items() if active
    ]
    if len(used) > 1:
        typer.echo(
            f"Error: {used[0]} and {used[1]} cannot be "
            "used together",
            err=True,
        )
        raise typer.Exit(1)
@

Let's verify that one flag passes, that two are rejected in the order
the caller listed them, and that the rejection is an exit rather than
an exception the CLI would show as a traceback:

<<test functions>>=
def test_reject_conflicting_flags_allows_one():
    """A single placement flag is not a conflict."""
    reject_conflicting_flags(
        {"--top": True, "--bottom": False}
    )


def test_reject_conflicting_flags_names_both():
    """Two flags exit and are named in listing order."""
    with pytest.raises(typer.Exit):
        reject_conflicting_flags(
            {"--top": True, "--bottom": True}
        )
