\paragraph{When GitHub closed an item}%
\label{sec:todo-github-closed}
An item that is done because its issue or pull request was closed on
GitHub was finished when GitHub closed it, not when [[sync]] or
[[import]] happened to notice.  Those can be days apart, so we take
the closing time (\cref{sec:todo-closed}) from GitHub's [[closedAt]].
Pull requests use [[closedAt]] too, not [[mergedAt]]: a merge closes a
pull request, and GitHub then sets [[closedAt]] to the moment of the
merge, while a pull request closed without merging has only
[[closedAt]].  The one field therefore covers both ways a pull request
ends.

The same reasoning applies to the other end.  An imported item began
when its issue was opened, so [[import]] stores GitHub's [[createdAt]]
as [[created]] rather than the time of the import.  This also keeps
the two stamps in order: an issue closed last month and imported today
would otherwise be closed before it was created.

GitHub gives the time in UTC with a [[Z]] suffix, whereas [[created]]
and every other stamp we write is local time without an offset.  We
convert with [[naive_local_datetime]], so that the stored values
compare and display alike.  The [[Z]] is replaced first because
[[datetime.fromisoformat]] accepts it only from Python~3.11, and nytid
also runs on~3.10; the age heuristic of [[import]] does the same with
[[createdAt]].  A missing or unreadable value gives [[None]], and
the caller then uses the current time, which is what the item would
have had without GitHub's help.

<<helpers>>=
def github_time(data, field):
    """Return the GitHub timestamp `data[field]` as local ISO time.

    `field` is a key such as `closedAt` or `createdAt`.  Returns
    `None` when the field is missing or unreadable.
    """
    value = data.get(field)
    if not value:
        return None
    parsed = naive_local_datetime(
        str(value).replace("Z", "+00:00")
    )
    if parsed is None:
        return None
    return parsed.isoformat()
@

Let's verify the conversion in a time zone we choose, so that the
expected local times can be written out rather than computed by the
same library call the helper makes.  [[TZ]] only takes effect after
[[time.tzset]], and has to be reset the same way afterwards, or every
later test would run in Stockholm time.

<<test functions>>=
@pytest.fixture
def stockholm_time(monkeypatch):
  """Run the test with local time set to Europe/Stockholm."""
  monkeypatch.setenv("TZ", "Europe/Stockholm")
  time.tzset()
  yield
  monkeypatch.undo()
  time.tzset()
@

Summer time is two hours ahead of UTC and winter time one.  The last
two cases straddle the end of summer time, at 01:00 UTC on 25~October
2026: two instants an hour apart that both read 02:30 locally.  That
is the price of storing naive local time, as [[created]] always has;
it costs an ambiguous hour once a year and keeps every stamp in one
form.  The remaining cases are the ways of having no usable value.

<<test functions>>=
def test_github_time_is_local_iso(stockholm_time):
  """A UTC GitHub timestamp becomes a naive local ISO string."""
  cases = {
    "2026-10-08T12:03:59Z": "2026-10-08T14:03:59",
    "2026-01-15T12:00:00Z": "2026-01-15T13:00:00",
    "2026-10-25T00:30:00Z": "2026-10-25T02:30:00",
    "2026-10-25T01:30:00Z": "2026-10-25T02:30:00",
  }
  for utc, local in cases.items():
    assert github_time({"closedAt": utc}, "closedAt") == local
  assert github_time({"closedAt": None}, "closedAt") is None
  assert github_time({"closedAt": "soon"}, "closedAt") is None
  assert github_time({}, "createdAt") is None
@

