Several commands share the same convention as [[view]] and [[done]]:
an explicit ID wins, otherwise we act on the current active todo.
When tmux labels identify one of several parallel hierarchies, that
means the current context's todo.  Outside tmux we still fall back to
the worker's active-stack top, but inside tmux an untracked pane or
window must not silently borrow another branch's active item.
Extracting that policy into a helper keeps the user-facing error
message consistent and avoids three copies of the same lookup.

Commands that modify both todos and the active stack should do so under
one lock.  These helpers keep that pattern concise and make the active
hierarchy easy to render and query.

The small wrappers that follow separate three concerns.  First, generic
locked mutation plumbing via [[apply_todo_mutation]].  Second,
read-only policy helpers such as choosing the next top-level task or
rendering the current hierarchy.  Third, state-changing wrappers for the
common transitions activate, stop, and complete, so command bodies can
state their intent directly.

When no explicit ID is given, stack-based commands resolve to the
current active todo.  Because the stack is global (shared across
workers), [[resolve_todo_id]] accepts an optional [[who]] together with
the full [[todos]] list and uses [[filter_ids_by_worker]] to narrow the
stack to items belonging to that worker.  This prevents one worker's
[[todo stop]] from accidentally targeting another worker's active item.

When multiple independent hierarchies are active in parallel---for
example, one todo tree in tmux window~A and a different one in
window~B---the global stack top is ambiguous.  The tracking system
already stores [[todo:<id>]] labels per tmux pane and window (see
\cref{track:window-auto-suspend}), so we can consult those labels to
determine which hierarchy the user is working in \emph{right now}.

[[resolve_todo_from_tmux_context]] reads the current pane's labels
first (most specific), then falls back to the tracked window's labels.
It extracts [[todo:<id>]] entries, intersects them with the active
stack, and returns the one that appears last in stack order.  Because
the stack grows by pushing children after their parents---the only way
it is mutated---the latest stack position is also the deepest child in
the current context.  If that invariant ever weakens, this helper
still has a well-defined answer (latest push wins), but callers that
really need the hierarchy leaf should instead rebuild from the
parents the way [[render_active_todo_forest]] does.

The earlier empty-list sentinel did encode the three states we needed,
but it did so by overloading the return type: callers had to remember
that this helper returned either an integer, [[None]], or a list that
was only ever meaningful when empty.  That made the low-level tmux
helper harder to read than the policy code built on top of it.

We now move the distinction to a cleaner boundary.  This helper is
specifically about tmux-local context, so being outside tmux is
exceptional for [[resolve_todo_from_tmux_context]] even though it is
perfectly normal for the CLI as a whole.  The helper therefore raises a
dedicated error outside tmux, returns [[None]] when tmux is active but
this pane/window has no matching tracked todo, and returns an integer
when it resolves a local active todo.  That keeps the return type
coherent while still preserving all three states.  Higher-level helpers
then catch the exception and apply their own policy: branch-context
commands fall back to the ordinary stack outside tmux, while tmux
sessions with no local context remain intentionally local.

<<helpers>>=
class NotInTmuxError(RuntimeError):
    """Raised when tmux-local context is requested outside tmux."""


def resolve_todo_from_tmux_context(
    who, stack,
):
    """Return the tmux-local active todo ID.

    Reads ``todo:<id>`` labels from the current pane
    or tracked window and returns the one that appears
    latest in the active stack (by stack position).

    Raises ``NotInTmuxError`` outside tmux.  Inside
    tmux, returns ``None`` when the current pane or
    tracked window has no matching ``todo:<id>``
    label, and otherwise returns the integer todo ID.
    """
    try:
        from nytid.cli.track import (
            get_current_tmux_server_key,
            read_pane_labels,
            read_window_labels,
            get_current_tracked_window_id,
        )
    except ImportError:
        raise NotInTmuxError()

    server_key = get_current_tmux_server_key()
    if server_key is None:
        raise NotInTmuxError()

    stack_set = set(stack)

    def extract_todo_ids(labels):
        """Parse todo IDs from labels, keep those on stack."""
        if labels is None:
            return []
        ids = []
        for label in labels:
            if label.startswith("todo:"):
                try:
                    tid = int(label[5:])
                except ValueError:
                    continue
                if tid in stack_set:
                    ids.append(tid)
        return ids

    pane_id = os.environ.get("TMUX_PANE")
    if pane_id:
        pane_labels = read_pane_labels(
            pane_id, who, server_key,
        )
        candidates = extract_todo_ids(pane_labels)
        if candidates:
            return max(
                candidates,
                key=lambda tid: stack.index(tid),
            )

    window_id = get_current_tracked_window_id(
        who, server_key,
    )
    if window_id:
        win_labels = read_window_labels(
            window_id, who, server_key,
        )
        candidates = extract_todo_ids(win_labels)
        if candidates:
            return max(
                candidates,
                key=lambda tid: stack.index(tid),
            )

    return None
@

With that helper in place, [[resolve_todo_id]] can resolve parallel
ambiguity transparently: callers that already pass an explicit ID are
unaffected.  This helper is the right place to centralize the implicit
single-item policy because all of [[view]], [[edit]], [[start]],
[[done]], [[note]], and friends mean the same thing when no ID is given:
\enquote{use the active todo if there is one; otherwise tell me to be
explicit}.  Outside tmux we catch the tmux-specific exception and fall
back to the stack top.  Inside tmux, however, [[None]] now means
something more precise: the current pane/window has no local tracked
todo, so there is no implicit item to act on here.

<<helpers>>=
def resolve_todo_id(
    todo_id, action, who=None, todos=None, stack=None
):
    """Return an explicit todo ID or current active todo.

    If ``todo_id`` is ``None``, use the current active
    todo.  When ``who`` and ``todos`` are given, only
    consider stack entries belonging to that worker.
    When ``stack`` is given, reuse it instead of
    reloading the authoritative state.

    In a tmux session the current pane or window labels
    are consulted first so that parallel hierarchies
    resolve correctly.

    Exits with an actionable error if no active todo
    exists.
    """
    if todo_id is not None:
        return todo_id

    if stack is None:
        stack = load_active_stack()
    if who is not None and todos is not None:
        stack = filter_ids_by_worker(
            todos, stack, who
        )
    if not stack:
        typer.echo(
            "No active todo. "
            f"Specify an ID to {action}.",
            err=True,
        )
        raise typer.Exit(1)

    try:
        context_id = resolve_todo_from_tmux_context(
            who or default_username, stack,
        )
    except NotInTmuxError:
        context_id = None
        in_tmux = False
    else:
        in_tmux = True
    if in_tmux and context_id is None:
        typer.echo(
            "No active todo. "
            f"Specify an ID to {action}.",
            err=True,
        )
        raise typer.Exit(1)
    if context_id is not None:
        return context_id

    return stack[-1]
@

The first helper below hides the tuple-shuffling needed by
[[mutate_todo_state]]. Each command-specific callback can return its
updated state plus any extra values it still needs after the lock is
released.

<<helpers>>=
def apply_todo_mutation(callback):
    """Run a todo mutation under the todo-state lock."""
    result_holder = {}

    def update(next_id, todos, stack):
        callback_result = callback(next_id, todos, stack)
        if len(callback_result) == 3:
            result_holder["extra"] = ()
            return callback_result
        result_holder["extra"] = callback_result[3:]
        return callback_result[:3]

    next_id, todos, stack = mutate_todo_state(update)
    return (
        next_id,
        todos,
        stack,
        *result_holder.get("extra", ()),
    )
@

The next two helpers are read-only policy operations. One picks the
best top-level candidate when [[todo next]] has no active parent; the
other renders the active stack as a forest based on real parent
relationships rather than raw stack position.  We apply the same
legacy-worker rule here too, so older top-level items without an
explicit [[who]] remain visible to the default user.

Each active item is drawn once, under the parent its line runs
through, with the stack itself as the context
(\cref{sec:todo-parents}): a parent that is active is the one the user
is working under.  An item whose parent is not active is a root of the
forest.

<<helpers>>=
def get_top_level_pending_by_priority(
    todos: list[TodoItem],
    who: str | None = None,
) -> TodoItem | None:
    """Return the highest-priority pending top-level todo.

    Treats ``who=None`` as ``default_username``.
    """
    candidates = [
        item for item in todos
        if ROOT_ID in parents_of(item)
        and item.status == "pending"
    ]
    if who:
        candidates = [
            item for item in candidates
            if re.search(
                who, item.who or default_username
            )
        ]
    now = datetime.datetime.now()
    candidates.sort(
        key=lambda item: todo_display_sort_key(item, now)
    )
    return candidates[0] if candidates else None


def render_active_todo_forest(
    items: list[TodoItem],
) -> list[str]:
    """Render active todos as an indented forest."""
    order = {
        item.id: index for index, item in enumerate(items)
    }
    by_id = {item.id: item for item in items}
    children_by_parent = {}
    roots = []
    for item in items:
        parent_id = primary_parent(
            items, item, order, by_id
        )
        if parent_id in by_id:
            children_by_parent.setdefault(
                parent_id, []
            ).append(item)
        else:
            roots.append(item)

    lines = []

    def render_tree(item: TodoItem, depth: int):
        lines.append(
            f"{'  ' * (depth + 1)}"
            f"#{item.id} {item.title} [{item.status}]"
        )
        for child in children_by_parent.get(item.id, []):
            render_tree(child, depth + 1)

    for root in roots:
        render_tree(root, 0)

    return lines
@

The final trio are state transitions. Each one performs just one kind
of stack mutation and returns both the resolved item and the normalized
state that follows from it. This keeps the command bodies declarative:
[[start]] activates, [[stop]] pauses, and [[done]] completes.

Activation resolves the ID (falling back to the current worker/tmux
context when [[who]] is given), verifies the item exists, claims it
for the acting worker (\cref{sec:todo-claim}), and pushes it onto the
shared stack.  Besides the usual state it returns the worker it took
the item from, or [[None]] when nothing was taken over; the caller
needs that name to stop the displaced claimant's tracking.

<<helpers>>=
def activate_todo_state(todo_id, who=None, force=False):
    """Push ``todo_id`` onto the authoritative active stack.

    Records ``who`` as the item's claimant.  Refuses an
    item that another worker has claimed, unless
    ``force``, which takes the claim over.  Returns the
    resolved ID, the item, the new state, and the worker
    whose claim was taken over (``None`` if none was).
    """

    def update(next_id, todos, stack):
        resolved_id = resolve_todo_id(
            todo_id, "start",
            who=who, todos=todos,
        )
        item = next(
            (t for t in todos if t.id == resolved_id),
            None,
        )
        if item is None:
            typer.echo(
                f"Error: todo #{resolved_id} not found",
                err=True,
            )
            raise typer.Exit(1)
        <<claim [[item]] for [[who]]>>
        if resolved_id not in stack:
            push_active(stack, resolved_id)
        return (
            next_id, todos, stack,
            resolved_id, item, displaced,
        )

    (
        next_id, todos, stack,
        resolved_id, item, displaced,
    ) = apply_todo_mutation(update)
    return resolved_id, item, next_id, todos, stack, displaced
@

\paragraph{Claiming an item}%
\label{sec:todo-claim}
An item on the active stack is being worked on in some worker's
session.  The stack is shared, though, and by itself it does not keep
a second worker from starting an item that another worker already
has active.  Both would then track time on it, and each one's
[[stop]] or [[done]] would cut the other's work short.  With agents
taking work from the same list as the user, that is not an exotic
accident.

Starting an item is therefore also a claim on it, recorded in
[[claimed_by]], and the first claim wins.  Activation records the
acting worker (\cref{sec:todo-acting-worker}) as the claimant, and
refuses an item that another worker has claimed.  The refusal says
whose it is, with the two ways forward: act as that worker with
[[--who]], or take the item over with [[start --force]].

The claimant is deliberately not the assignee.  An earlier version of
this rule used [[who]] for both, and each meaning got in the other's
way.  When the user started a task from an agent's queue, the item
stayed the agent's, so the user's own restart was refused and the
user's [[todo stop]] could not find it.  And since the assignee can be
edited at any time, [[edit --who]] on a running task looked exactly
like a takeover to the run that was still working on it.  With a
separate claimant, the assignee says whose queue an item is in and
changes only when someone reassigns it; the claimant says whose
session is working on it and changes only when an item is started.
Everything that asks whose session an active item belongs to goes
through [[session_worker]].

The check runs inside the locked callback, on the stack as it is when
the item is pushed (\cref{sec:todo-read-modify-write}).  A check on a
snapshot alone would leave the race open, since two [[start]]s could
both see the item idle and then both push it.

Three cases look similar but are deliberately left alone.
\begin{description}
\item[Ancestors] Only the item that was asked for is checked, never
  the ancestors that [[ensure_ancestors_active]] activates along with
  it.  A parent is typically shared: the user keeps a course's parent
  item active while an agent works through its children.  Starting
  a child must not be refused because its parent is somebody else's,
  and the parent is not taken from them either, since an ancestor
  that is already active is left exactly as it is.  An ancestor that
  is \emph{not} active yet is claimed by the acting worker, though,
  since it is activated as context for that worker's session and its
  labels are tracked there.
\item[Another worker's pending item] An item that is assigned to
  another worker but not active can be started by explicit ID, as
  before.  Assignment says whose queue an item is in, not who is
  working on it, and picking a task from a colleague's queue is the
  ordinary way to help out.  The starter becomes the claimant; the
  assignee stays as it is.
\item[A restart] An item that the acting worker has claimed already
  may be started again, which is what resuming after a stopped
  tracking session looks like.
\end{description}

[[claim_holder]] answers the one question both the locked check and
the early one in [[start]] ask: who, if anyone, stands in the acting
worker's way.  [[refuse_claim]] gives the refusal one wording for
both [[start]] and [[adopt]], so it names both remedies.

<<helpers>>=
def claim_holder(item, stack, who):
    """Return the worker whose claim on `item` bars `who`.

    `item` is claimed while it is on the active `stack`,
    by `session_worker(item)`.  Returns `None` when
    nothing bars `who`: `who` is `None`, the item is not
    active, or `who` holds the claim.
    """
    if who is None or item.id not in stack:
        return None
    holder = session_worker(item)
    return holder if holder != who else None


def refuse_claim(todo_id, holder):
    """Exit because `holder` has `todo_id` active."""
    typer.echo(
        f"Error: todo #{todo_id} is active for {holder}. "
        f"Pass --who {holder} to act as {holder}, or "
        f"take it over with: todo start {todo_id} --force",
        err=True,
    )
    raise typer.Exit(1)
@

A forced takeover moves the claim inside the same callback, so it
changes hands atomically, and remembers whom it was taken from.  The
displaced claimant's tracking session still runs the item's labels
and must be stopped, but once the claim has moved nothing else would
ever stop it.  The displaced claimant's ID-less [[stop]] no longer
finds the item, because it is no longer theirs, and the new
claimant's [[stop]] or [[done]] closes the new claimant's session.
The time would go on accruing to a worker who had stopped working on
the task.  The assignee is left alone: taking over the work does not
move the task to another queue.

The stop itself is tracking I/O and runs after the lock is released,
in the caller, which passes the displaced worker to
[[stop_todo_tracking]] explicitly.  Stopping first, on a decision
taken from a snapshot, would be the riskier order: the commit can
still fail, for instance when the item was removed meanwhile, and the
displaced claimant would then have lost the session for a takeover
that never happened.

<<claim [[item]] for [[who]]>>=
displaced = None
holder = claim_holder(item, stack, who)
if holder is not None:
    if not force:
        refuse_claim(resolved_id, holder)
    displaced = holder
if who is not None:
    item.claimed_by = who
    item.context_claim = False
@

The displaced claimant may also have active descendants of the item,
sub-tasks it started under it.  A takeover leaves them alone, for the
same reason that starting a child leaves an active parent alone: each
item carries its own claim, and taking over one item is not a claim
on the items around it.  The displaced worker may well still be busy
with a sub-task while someone else picks up the umbrella item, and
the labels those sub-tasks share with it stay protected when the
displaced tracking of the item is stopped.  Whoever wants the
sub-tasks too takes them over one by one.

A takeover also has to survive the displaced claimant's run, if one is
still going: when that process exits, its ending must not stop or
reset an item that is no longer its own.  The run's ending checks
for that itself (\cref{sec:todo-run-taken-over}).  Nor may the
displaced claimant's tmux pane or window bring the item's labels back
when it regains focus; \cref{sec:todo-resume-claim} covers that.

One gap remains.  Two sessions acting as the \emph{same} worker, two
agent runs as [[dan-claude]] for instance, can still both start one
item: the claim tells workers apart, not sessions, and for the
acting worker an active item of its own is simply a restart.
Closing that needs an identity per session, which the store does not
have, and is left for follow-up work.

\paragraph{Resuming a claim that moved}%
\label{sec:todo-resume-claim}
Auto-suspend (\cref{track:auto-suspend}) stops a worker's labels when
the worker's tmux pane or window loses focus and starts them again
when it regains it.  If the item was taken over in between, starting
them again would put the displaced claimant back on the task, with
nothing to stop it until the next takeover.  We could clear the
suspended labels at takeover time, but they live in several kinds of
file---a pane's suspend file, a window's label file---on any tmux
server that shares the state directory, and only [[track]] knows the
formats.  Instead [[track]] asks before it resumes
(\cref{track:resume-filters}), and we answer: a [[todo:<id>]] label
whose item is active and claimed by somebody else does not come back,
nor do that item's other labels, unless one of the worker's own
active items still carries them.  The answer uses the claims as they
are when the worker returns, so it is right however the claim moved
and however long ago.

The filter only knows the labels todos carry, not why they were
started.  A worker who also tracks one of those labels by hand, with
[[track start DD1310]] in the same pane, loses that clock with the
item's when the pane resumes after a takeover, unless one of the
worker's own active todos carries the label too.  We accept that: the
alternative is to keep every shared label running for a worker who no
longer works on the task, and a hand-started clock is restarted by
hand as easily as it was started.  [[track]] also keeps the resume
going if the filter fails, reading the store included, rather than
lose the suspended time (\cref{track:resume-filters}).

<<helpers>>=
def drop_taken_over_todo_labels(who, labels):
    """Return `labels` without those of todos taken from `who`.

    A todo is taken when it is active and claimed by a
    worker other than `who`.  Its labels are dropped too,
    unless one of `who`'s own active todos carries them.
    """
    todo_ids = set()
    for label in labels:
        if label.startswith("todo:"):
            try:
                todo_ids.add(int(label[len("todo:"):]))
            except ValueError:
                continue
    if not todo_ids:
        return labels
    _, todos, stack = load_todo_state()
    by_id = {t.id: t for t in todos}
    taken = [
        by_id[tid] for tid in todo_ids
        if tid in by_id
        and claim_holder(by_id[tid], stack, who) is not None
    ]
    if not taken:
        return labels
    still_needed = set()
    for sid in stack:
        own = by_id.get(sid)
        if own is not None and session_worker(own) == who:
            still_needed.update(todo_track_labels(own, sid))
    dropped = set()
    for item in taken:
        dropped.update(
            label
            for label in todo_track_labels(item, item.id)
            if label not in still_needed
        )
    return [label for label in labels if label not in dropped]
@

The filter is registered when this module is imported.  [[track]] is
optional here as everywhere else in this module, so a missing
[[track]] simply means there is nothing to resume.

<<registrations with other modules>>=
try:
    from nytid.cli.track import register_resume_label_filter
    register_resume_label_filter(drop_taken_over_todo_labels)
except ImportError:
    pass
