Completion combines resolution, marking the item done, and
removing it from the stack in a single locked mutation.

An explicit completion target needs one extra check beyond
[[resolve_todo_id]].  Stack filtering is enough when the ID is omitted,
but an explicit ID must still belong to the chosen worker; otherwise
[[todo done 17]] could silently mutate another worker's task merely
because todo IDs are global.  We therefore factor the read-only lookup
into [[resolve_done_target]] so the preflight descendant guard and the
locked state mutation share the same ownership rule.

The refusal is worded like the claim's refusal (\cref{sec:todo-claim}),
since for an active item it is the same situation: somebody else's
session is working on it.  For an item that is not active, the worker
in the way is the assignee, and the message says so instead.

<<constants>>=
DONE_REFUSAL = (
    "Error: todo #{todo_id} is {relation} {owner}. "
    "Pass --who {owner} to mark it done as {owner}."
)

<<helpers>>=
def resolve_done_target(todo_id, todos, who=None):
    """Resolve a completion target in one worker context."""
    resolved_id = resolve_todo_id(
        todo_id, "mark done",
        who=who, todos=todos,
    )
    item = next(
        (t for t in todos if t.id == resolved_id),
        None,
    )
    if item is None:
        typer.echo(
            f"Error: todo #{resolved_id} not found",
            err=True,
        )
        raise typer.Exit(1)
    owner = session_worker(item)
    if who is not None and owner != who:
        relation = (
            "active for" if item.status == "in-progress"
            else "assigned to"
        )
        typer.echo(
            DONE_REFUSAL.format(
                todo_id=resolved_id, relation=relation,
                owner=owner,
            ),
            err=True,
        )
        raise typer.Exit(1)
    return resolved_id, item


def complete_todo_state(todo_id, who=None):
    """Mark a todo done and remove it from the active stack."""

    def update(next_id, todos, stack):
        resolved_id, item = resolve_done_target(
            todo_id, todos, who=who
        )
        close_item(todos, item)
        stack = [sid for sid in stack if sid != resolved_id]
        return next_id, todos, stack, resolved_id, item

    next_id, todos, stack, resolved_id, item = apply_todo_mutation(update)
    return resolved_id, item, next_id, todos, stack
@

When all children of a parent become done, the parent is auto-completed.
This runs as a separate locked mutation so that tracking labels can be
stopped between the child completion and the parent status change.

The new worker-scoped completion rules add one guard here.  A worker may
complete only that worker's branch, so auto-completion must not let the
last child completed for one worker silently mark another worker's
parent done.  We therefore thread the resolved worker context into the
helper and stop at the boundary when the parent belongs to someone else.

One parent is nobody's work although it is claimed: a context claim
(\cref{sec:todo-release-context}) whose claimant has no active item
below it any more.  Releasing normally clears those, but one left
behind by an interrupted command, or written by an older version,
would otherwise keep the parent from ever being completed by the
worker who finished its last child.  Such a parent is completed like
one's own, and its claimant's tracking of it stops.  A parent that
another worker started explicitly stays theirs.

An item with several parents (\cref{sec:todo-parents}) is a child of
each of them, and completing it may complete any of them: each parent
is asked on its own whether all its children are done, and the worker
guard applies to each on its own too, so the last child of the user's
parent and of an agent's parent completes the one that is the
worker's and leaves the other.  The top level is not a parent that can
be completed.

<<helpers>>=
def auto_complete_parent(item, todos, who=None):
    """Auto-complete each parent of `item` whose children
    are now all done.

    Each parent is decided on its own, in a locked mutation
    of its own run after its tracking labels are stopped.
    When ``who`` is given, only parents owned by that
    worker are auto-completed.
    """
    for parent_id in real_parents_of(item):
        auto_complete_one_parent(item, parent_id, todos, who)


def auto_complete_one_parent(item, parent_id, todos, who=None):
    """Auto-complete `parent_id` if all its children are done,
    under the worker guard of `auto_complete_parent`."""
    if not check_parent_completion(todos, parent_id):
        return
    parent_item = next(
        (t for t in todos if t.id == parent_id),
        None,
    )
    if parent_item is None or parent_item.status == "done":
        return
    resolved_who = who
    if resolved_who is None:
        resolved_who = session_worker(item)
    holder = session_worker(parent_item)
    if holder != resolved_who and not is_abandoned_context(
        parent_item, todos, load_active_stack(),
    ):
        return

    stop_todo_tracking(parent_item.id, parent_item, todos)

    def mark_parent(next_id, todos, stack):
        parent = next(
            (t for t in todos
             if t.id == parent_id),
            None,
        )
        if parent and parent.status != "done":
            close_item(todos, parent)
            typer.echo(
                f"  Parent #{parent.id} "
                f"auto-completed: {parent.title}"
            )
        return next_id, todos, stack

    mutate_todo_state(mark_parent)


def is_abandoned_context(item, todos, stack):
    """Tell whether `item` is a context claim nobody uses.

    True when `item` is active, claimed as context, and
    its claimant has no active item below it.
    """
    if item.id not in stack or not item.context_claim:
        return False
    return not any(
        child.id in stack
        and session_worker(child) == item.claimed_by
        for child in descendants_of(todos, item.id)
    )
