Adding a comment works identically for issues and PRs because
GitHub's API treats them as the same comment endpoint. We use
[[gh issue comment]] which handles both:

<<helpers>>=
def gh_add_comment(repo, number, body):
    """Add a comment to a GitHub issue or PR.

    Works for both issues and pull requests since
    GitHub uses a shared comment endpoint.
    """
    result = subprocess.run(
        [
            "gh", "issue", "comment",
            str(number), "-R", repo,
            "--body", body,
        ],
        capture_output=True,
        text=True,
    )
    if result.returncode != 0:
        raise RuntimeError(
            f"gh comment failed: "
            f"{result.stderr.strip()}"
        )
@

\paragraph{Appending notes to items}
Several places need to add a note to a todo item---the [[note]]
command, headless output capture, timeout handling and detached-run
cleanup.  Rather than duplicating the GitHub-vs-local branching logic,
we extract shared helpers.  For GitHub-linked items we post a comment
\emph{and} store locally (belt-and-suspenders), because in
headless mode there is no human to notice a failed API call.

Those two halves are different kinds of work, so they are two helpers
rather than one.  Posting the comment is network I/O, which can take
seconds; storing the note is a change to the todo state, which must
happen on the freshly loaded item inside the caller's
[[mutate_todo_state]] callback.
Doing both in one call would force a choice between holding the todo
lock across a network round trip---stalling every other [[nytid todo]]
command meanwhile---and appending to a stale snapshot, which is how
concurrent notes used to get lost.  The caller therefore posts first,
outside the lock, and then appends in its callback.

<<helpers>>=
def post_note_comment(item, note_text):
    """Post a note as a GitHub comment if the item is linked.

    A failed post is logged rather than raised: the note
    is still stored locally by `append_note_to_item`.
    Call this outside the todo-state lock.
    """
    if not item.github:
        return
    try:
        gh_add_comment(
            item.github["repo"],
            item.github["number"],
            note_text,
        )
    except Exception:
        logging.warning(
            "Failed to post GitHub comment "
            f"for #{item.id}"
        )
@

\paragraph{Who wrote a note, and when}%
\label{sec:todo-note-headers}
Notes pile up from several writers over several days: the user, agents
working in their own sessions, and headless runs recording how they
ended.  A note such as \enquote{tests pass now} is only useful if one
can tell who wrote it and when, so every appended note begins with a
header line naming both:
\begin{verbatim}
[2026-10-08 14:03 dan-claude]
Tests pass now.

[2026-10-08 16:40 dbosk]
Reviewed; merge after the deadline.
\end{verbatim}
The time is local and given to the minute, which is as precise as a
reader needs.  The name is the note's author, whoever wrote it, which
is not always the worker the item is assigned to: when the user adds
an item for an agent, the notes on it are the user's instructions.  A
caller that knows the writer passes it: a run names the worker that
ran it (\cref{sec:todo-run-outcome}), and [[note]] names the worker it
acts as (\cref{sec:todo-acting-worker}), so an agent's
[[note --who dan-claude]] is signed by the agent and not by the user
whose login it runs under.  Every other note is signed by
[[default_username]], the user running the command.
A blank line separates two notes for the reason it separates two
[[--note]] paragraphs (\cref{sec:todo-add-notes}): [[view]] renders
notes as Markdown, which joins consecutive lines into one paragraph.
That same rule makes each header and the first paragraph of its text
one paragraph, so the stamp reads as the opening of the note it
belongs to.

We could store notes as a list of records instead, each with its time,
author and text.  We do not, because [[notes]] is also the body of the
editor buffer: [[edit -E]] and [[note -E]] open it as text and store
what comes back (\cref{sec:edit-via-editor}).  Records would need a
rendering into that text and a parser back out of it, and a user
fixing a typo in one note would have to keep the rendering intact for
the parser to accept it.  A header line is already text.  It survives
the editor round trip untouched, reads well in [[view]], and needs no
migration: notes written before headers existed are left as they are.
The header borrows its shape from the one [[view]] prints above each
GitHub comment, so both kinds of note look alike.

Only the local copy gets a header.  GitHub records the author and the
time of every comment itself, so [[post_note_comment]] is given the
plain text; a header there would repeat what GitHub shows, and the
author it named could differ from the [[gh]] account that posted it.
The text is stripped before it is stored, since a note read from
standard input or an editor ends in a newline that would otherwise
widen the gap before the next header.  A text that is empty once
stripped is not stored at all: a header with nothing under it records
only that somebody said nothing.  The [[note]] command refuses such a
text before it gets here, as it always has for an empty editor buffer
or empty standard input; for [[add --note]] an empty paragraph is
simply skipped.

The local half only touches the item it is given.  It neither loads
nor saves, so it can run on whatever item a callback has just looked
up, and the callback's return value is what persists it.

<<constants>>=
NOTE_TIME_FORMAT = "%Y-%m-%d %H:%M"
<<helpers>>=
def append_note_to_item(item, note_text, who=None, now=None):
    """Append `note_text` to the item's local notes.

    The note gets a header line with the time `now` (default:
    the current local time) and the worker `who` (default:
    `default_username`), and a blank line separates it from
    the notes before it.  A text that is blank after
    stripping is not stored.

    Pure: it changes `item` and nothing else.  Run it
    inside a `mutate_todo_state` callback so the note is
    added to the current stored notes.
    """
    if not note_text.strip():
        return
    if now is None:
        now = datetime.datetime.now()
    header = f"[{now.strftime(NOTE_TIME_FORMAT)} {who or default_username}]"
    entry = header + "\n" + note_text.strip()
    earlier = item.notes.rstrip()
    if earlier:
        item.notes = earlier + "\n\n" + entry
    else:
        item.notes = entry
@

Let's verify the format on two notes that follow one written before
headers existed.  The first note's trailing newline, and the seconds of
its time, must not reach the store:

<<test functions>>=
def test_append_note_writes_header_and_blank_line():
  """Each note gets a header; notes are a blank line apart."""
  item = TodoItem(id=1, title="Task", notes="Legacy note\n")
  append_note_to_item(
    item, "Tests pass now.\n", who="dan-claude",
    now=datetime.datetime(2026, 10, 8, 14, 3, 59),
  )
  append_note_to_item(
    item, "Reviewed.", who="dbosk",
    now=datetime.datetime(2026, 10, 8, 16, 40),
  )
  assert item.notes == (
    "Legacy note\n\n"
    "[2026-10-08 14:03 dan-claude]\nTests pass now.\n\n"
    "[2026-10-08 16:40 dbosk]\nReviewed."
  )


def test_append_note_signs_with_default_username():
  """Without `who`, the note is signed by the default user."""
  from nytid.cli.todo import default_username
  item = TodoItem(id=1, title="Task")
  append_note_to_item(item, "Hello")
  header, text = item.notes.split("\n")
  assert NOTE_HEADER_PATTERN.fullmatch(header)
  assert header.endswith(f" {default_username}]")
  assert text == "Hello"


def test_append_note_skips_blank_text():
  """A blank note leaves the notes as they were."""
  item = TodoItem(id=1, title="Task", notes="Kept")
  append_note_to_item(item, " \n\t")
  assert item.notes == "Kept"
@

Many tests elsewhere in this file are about \emph{where} a note ends
up---that a concurrent note survives, that a buffer replaces the
notes---and not about its header, whose time they cannot know in
advance.  They compare the notes with the header lines taken out:

<<test functions>>=
def without_note_headers(notes):
  """Return `notes` with its note header lines removed."""
  return re.sub(
    "^" + NOTE_HEADER_PATTERN.pattern + "\n", "", notes,
    flags=re.MULTILINE,
  )
@

