\enquote{Description} and \enquote{notes} name two fields whose
contents are easy to confuse, and the difference decides which of them
a piece of text belongs in.  The description is the item's standing
statement: one line saying what the task is and why it exists, short
enough to sit beside the title wherever the item is shown.  The notes
are everything that accumulates afterwards---what was found while
planning it, what [[note]] appended while working on it, what has to
happen before it can be called done.  A description is \emph{rewritten}
when the task changes; notes only ever \emph{grow}.

The help text has to carry that distinction, because the field names
alone do not: a user who reaches for [[--description]] to record three
paragraphs of detail is putting a growing log into the field the
listings expect to be one line.  It also records the one editing idiom
that cannot be guessed, that [[edit --description ""]] clears the
field.  Everywhere else in [[edit]] an omitted option means
\enquote{leave this alone}, so without the sentence the empty string
looks like the same no-op rather than the one way to erase a
description.

<<argument and option definitions>>=
description_opt = typer.Option(
    "--description",
    help="One-line statement of the item (what and "
         "why); put details in --note or the note "
         "command. On edit, an empty string clears it.")
@

<<add command options>>=
description: Annotated[str | None,
                       description_opt] = "",
@

