@

If the details belong in the notes, then creating an item with details
takes two commands---[[add]], then [[note]]---and what is a minor
annoyance once becomes the dominant cost when a script creates a
hundred items and pays two invocations for each.  [[--note]] closes
that gap by taking the note text at creation time.%
\label{sec:todo-add-notes}

The option repeats instead of taking one long string because a note is
a paragraph.  Each [[--note]] is one thought---a link, a constraint,
something to check---and several short quoted arguments are far easier
to type at a shell than one argument with embedded newlines.

<<argument and option definitions>>=
note_opt = typer.Option(
    "--note",
    help="Note paragraph, repeatable "
         "(each stored as a note of its own)")
@

<<add command options>>=
note: Annotated[list[str] | None,
                note_opt] = None,
@

Each paragraph is stored as a note of its own, with the header that
every appended note gets (\cref{sec:todo-note-headers}), so an item
created with three [[--note]]s reads exactly like one that received
three [[note]] commands.  The header names the user running [[add]],
not the assignee: [[add --who dan-claude --note ...]] hands work to an
agent, and the note is what the person handing it over wrote.  The
same call made by the agent itself, filing an item for its own queue,
is therefore signed with the user's name too, since [[add]] has no way
to tell the two apart.  An agent that wants its own name on a note
adds the note with [[note --who]] after creating the item.  The
notes are separated by a blank line, not
by a single newline, because notes are read back as Markdown by
[[view]].  Markdown folds consecutive lines into one paragraph, so
joining [[--note a --note b]] with a newline would render as
\enquote{a b}; the blank line is what keeps two notes two notes.

The editor path shows the paragraphs in its buffer instead
(\cref{sec:add-via-editor}), joined the same way but without headers.
That buffer is the body the user is writing, and what comes back from
it is stored as the notes, just as [[note -E]] stores its buffer.

<<join note paragraphs>>=
notes_text = "\n\n".join(note) if note else ""
@

Most of the time we infer priority interactively by comparing the new
item with its siblings. Sometimes, however, the caller already knows the
numeric value, or knows only that the item belongs at one end of the
list.  The [[--prio]] option handles the first case; [[--top]] and
[[--bottom]] keep the second non-interactive.

The [[--skip-priority]] flag covers a third case: capturing a task
without ranking it yet.  That is useful when triaging a backlog or when
the right comparison set is not available at creation time.  Unranked
items sort after ranked ones until [[reprioritize]] assigns them a numeric
position.

\paragraph{What the number means.}
A priority is a bare float with no scale attached, which leaves three
questions a caller cannot answer by experiment without writing to the
real task list: which direction is urgent, whether values below zero
are allowed, and why the number that comes back out of [[ls]] is
larger than the one that went in (\cref{todo:effective-priority}).  One
paragraph answers all three, and it lives in a constant so that the
four places a priority surfaces cannot answer them differently.

The paragraph deliberately says nothing about spelling, because
[[ls -p]] uses it too and has no value to spell.  Options that take a
value add that sentence themselves: a leading minus makes the argument
look like another option to the parser, so [[--prio=-10]] is the form
that works---the one thing a caller cannot discover from the type
annotation.

<<constants>>=
PRIORITY_HELP = (
    "Priority is a float and higher means more urgent; "
    "any value is allowed, negative ones included. "
    "Listings show the effective priority: the stored "
    "number plus a boost that grows as a deadline "
    "approaches."
)
PRIORITY_VALUE_HELP = (
    PRIORITY_HELP
    + " Write a negative value as --prio=-10; "
    "'ls -p' shows the effective one."
)
@

Let's verify that the paragraph actually reaches all four places.  The
help is rendered into a box and wrapped to the terminal width, so the
assertion compares whitespace-normalised text with the box rules
stripped; comparing the raw string would only test how wide the
terminal happened to be.

