We default [[--who]] to the logged-in user's username, so that todos
are scoped to the current user unless overridden.  This matches the
convention in [[schedule show --user]], where an empty string means
\enquote{all workers.}%
\label{todo:default-username}

Without [[\$USER]]---in a [[cron]] job, say---the shared helper
answers [["me"]] (\cref{cli:default-username}), the same name
[[track]] has always persisted for that case.  That is what makes
the normalisation
[[item.who or default_username]] used throughout this module safe:
an item created before the [[who]] field existed, or by an
environment with no [[\$USER]], resolves to a real name that
[[re.search]] and [[==]] can work with rather than to [[None]].

Under the old [[None]] fallback that normalisation hid a crash
waiting for the right environment.  [[find_pending_by_priority]] and
[[get_top_level_pending_by_priority]] pass its result straight to
[[re.search]], which raises [[TypeError]] on [[None]]: an explicit
[[--who]] on a machine without [[\$USER]] would abort on the first
ownerless item.  A string fallback removes the failure mode rather
than guarding against it at each call.

