\paragraph{Acting as a worker}%
\label{sec:todo-acting-worker}
A third role is the worker a command acts \emph{as}.  [[start]],
[[stop]], [[view]], [[note]], [[rm]] and [[adopt]] neither assign
work nor filter a list; they operate on one item, and the worker
decides two things about it.  Without an ID, it decides which item
that is: the worker's current active todo, since [[resolve_todo_id]]
narrows the shared stack to the entries the worker has claimed.  And when
[[start]] or [[adopt]] activates an item, the worker is the one
claiming it, which another worker's claim can refuse
(\cref{sec:todo-claim}).

The default is the logged-in user, as everywhere else, which is
exactly wrong for an agent that runs as [[dan-claude]] under the
user's login.  Before these commands took [[--who]], such an agent
could not follow the rule \enquote{always say who you are}: its
[[todo stop]] stopped the user's active item rather than its own, and
its [[todo note]] annotated whatever the user was working on.  An
explicit ID still means that item, whoever owns it, apart from the
claim; [[--who]] here chooses the actor, not a filter that hides
other workers' items.

[[done]] keeps its own option (\cref{sec:todo-done}): there the
worker is also an ownership check on explicit IDs, and its help text
says so.

<<argument and option definitions>>=
acting_who_opt = typer.Option(
    "--who", "-w",
    help="Worker to act as (default: $USER)",
    autocompletion=complete_todo_workers)
@

