[[add]] is the one command whose [[--who]] must default to [[None]]
rather than to [[default_username]].  Its editor buffer carries a
[[who:]] key (\cref{sec:add-via-editor}) and the flags are applied
\emph{after} the front matter, so a default value would be
indistinguishable from a typed [[-w]] and would overwrite whatever the
user changed that key to: the one field the buffer advertises would be
the one field the buffer could not set.  [[None]] is what makes
\enquote{the user did not choose} expressible at all, and the price is
that resolving it to a name moves out of the signature and into the
three places that need one---the sibling set to compare against, the
[[who:]] line the buffer opens with, and the item finally stored.

