\paragraph{Where an item was discovered}%
\label{sec:todo-discovered-from}

Auto-parenting reads more into an [[add]] than the user said.  Adding a
todo while another is active usually means \enquote{I found this while
doing that}, and filing the new item under the active one turns that
into \enquote{this is part of that}.  Often it is: the sub-task that
working on a task reveals is exactly what auto-parenting was made for.
But the bug an agent notices while fixing another one, or the paper
that came up while preparing a lecture, is not part of the task at
hand.  As a child it would keep that task from completing until the
stray item was done, and it would be ranked inside the task's priority
range, among work it has nothing to do with.

The issue tracker beads, written for coding agents, keeps the two
relations apart: beside parent and child it has a
\enquote{discovered-from} link, which says where an item came from and
nothing about where it belongs.  [[discovered_from]] is that link.  It
names the todo whose work turned the item up, and it is context and
nothing more: it does not move the item in the tree, complete
anything, or touch a priority.

The default stays auto-parenting.  That is the case in which the new
item \emph{is} a part, and it is the habit the command has taught
everyone who uses it.  What changes is the override.  [[--top-level]],
or a [[--parent]] other than the active todo, says \enquote{this is
not part of what I am doing}, but it does not say that the item came
from nowhere, so [[add]] then records the active todo as
[[discovered_from]] instead.  [[--no-discovered-from]]
is for the user who wants neither, and [[--discovered-from]] names the
source explicitly: an item found earlier, or found while working on
something that is no longer active.

<<argument and option definitions>>=
discovered_from_opt = typer.Option(
    "--discovered-from",
    help="ID of the todo this one was found while working on "
         "(default: the active todo, if the new one is not "
         "placed under it)",
    autocompletion=complete_todo_ids)
no_discovered_from_opt = typer.Option(
    "--no-discovered-from",
    help="Do not record the active todo as the one this "
         "was found while working on")
<<add command options>>=
discovered_from: Annotated[int | None,
                           discovered_from_opt] = None,
no_discovered_from: Annotated[bool,
                              no_discovered_from_opt] = False,
@

The two flags contradict each other, and an explicit source has to
exist.  Both are checked with the other flag checks, before the editor
opens, so that a mistyped ID does not cost the user a buffer.  The
existence check runs on the snapshot only.  The parent is looked up
again under the lock because a child of a removed parent would be
promoted to the top level; a source that is removed meanwhile does no
such harm.  IDs are never reused, so a [[discovered_from]] that
outlives its todo cannot be mistaken for a later one, and [[view]]
shows it as removed (\cref{sec:todo-view-provenance}).  For the same
reason [[rm]] leaves the field alone in the items that point at what it
removes: scrubbing it would throw away the one fact the field exists to
keep.

<<helpers>>=
def check_discovered_from(todos, discovered_from, no_discovered_from):
    """Exit unless the discovered-from options can be used.

    The two options contradict each other, and an explicit
    `discovered_from` must name an existing todo.
    """
    reject_conflicting_flags({
        "--discovered-from": discovered_from is not None,
        "--no-discovered-from": no_discovered_from,
    })
    if discovered_from is not None and not any(
        t.id == discovered_from for t in todos
    ):
        typer.echo(
            f"Error: --discovered-from #{discovered_from} "
            "not found",
            err=True,
        )
        raise typer.Exit(1)
<<validate add options>>=
check_discovered_from(todos, discovered_from, no_discovered_from)
@

The source recorded by default is the todo auto-parenting would have
used, found by the same [[get_current_context_todo]]: the active todo
of this tmux context, or outside tmux the one the logged-in user has
claimed (\cref{sec:todo-claim}).  A user with no active work of their
own has no such todo, so nothing is recorded, just as nothing is
auto-parented: another worker's task on top of the stack is not what
the user was doing.  It is not the active todo of the worker named
by [[--who]], because [[add]]'s [[--who]] is the assignee---whose queue
the item goes into---and handing a found bug to someone else does not
change in whose work it was found.  Sharing the lookup is also what
keeps the two rules complementary: the item that [[--top-level]] keeps
out of the active todo is exactly the item auto-parenting would have
put under it.

<<helpers>>=
def discovered_while_working_on(todos, parents):
    """Return the active todo a new item is not placed under.

    That is the active todo of the current context, as
    auto-parenting finds it, unless it is among `parents`,
    the new item's parents; `None` when there is no such
    todo.
    """
    active = get_current_context_todo(todos)
    if active is None or active.id in parents:
        return None
    return active
@

We only ask when auto-parenting was overridden.  Without an override
the active todo either became the parent, which already says where the
item came from, or there is none; a second link to the parent would
say the same thing twice, and the lookup reads the tmux state, which
costs a few [[tmux]] calls.  An explicit source is taken as given, and
the default is announced the way auto-parenting announces its parent,
since it is a decision the user did not spell out.  An item added under
several parents was kept out of the active todo only if that todo is
none of them: [[--parent 1 --parent 2]] with \#1 active files the item
under \#1 among other places, which says where it came from as well as
auto-parenting would.

<<resolve [[discovered_from]] for new todo>>=
resolved_discovered_from = discovered_from
if (
    discovered_from is None
    and not no_discovered_from
    and (parent or top_level)
):
    source = discovered_while_working_on(
        todos, resolved_parents
    )
    if source is not None:
        resolved_discovered_from = source.id
        typer.echo(
            f"Discovered from: #{source.id} {source.title}"
        )
