Priority selection has three modes. [[--prio]] bypasses all inference.
[[--top]] and [[--bottom]] keep the operation non-interactive by placing
the item at one end of the current sibling list. Otherwise we fall back
to the interactive binary search, which remains the default because it
preserves the existing
\enquote{compare against nearby tasks} workflow.  In all three modes
the sibling set is scoped to the target worker ([[--who]]) so that
priorities stay meaningful within one person's task list.

That scoping is why the first of the three resolutions of [[who]] sits
here.  [[get_active_siblings]] reads a [[None]] worker as
\enquote{every worker} rather than as \enquote{the current one}, so
handing it the unresolved flag would silently compare the new item
against other people's tasks whenever [[--who]] was omitted---which is
almost always.

[[--skip-priority]] is the one exception: it deliberately bypasses all
numeric placement and stores [[None]] instead.

An unranked parent is an even stronger constraint.  Until the parent
itself has a numeric priority there is no child interval to place new
work into, so every newly added child stays unranked regardless of
whether the caller asked for [[--prio]], [[--top]], [[--bottom]], or
the default interactive comparison.

Both ends go through [[place_among_siblings]]
(\cref{todo:placement}), which is where the reasoning about stored
versus effective priorities and about the still-empty level now lives.
Routing [[--bottom]] through it rather than through a bespoke
\enquote{one below the minimum} calculation is what guarantees that
[[--top]] and [[--bottom]] remain exact mirror images of each other as
that reasoning evolves.

The sibling lookup and the comparisons work on a candidate item, built
once so that they show the same context everywhere.  It is never
stored, so it gets an id that no stored item can have,
[[CANDIDATE_ID]].  That id must not be zero, although zero was free
once: zero is [[ROOT_ID]], the top level among parent ids
(\cref{sec:todo-parents}), and a candidate with that id would count
every top-level item as its child wherever its children are asked
for.

An item added under several parents is placed in each of its places
and keeps the highest number, the rule of
\cref{sec:ranking-context}.  The places are visited from the highest
down, in the order of [[places_highest_first]]
(\cref{sec:place-in-places}).  A place whose parent ranks
at or below the number already found cannot raise it, since everything
in its range lies below the parent, so it is skipped without a
question.  Each place gets its own candidate, whose only parent is
that place, so that the sibling lookup and the search ask about
exactly the siblings ranked there.  An unranked parent offers no place
to rank in; only when every place is unranked does the item stay
unranked, as a child of an unranked parent always has.

A number given with [[--prio]] has to fit one of the places: it must
lie in the range of one of the parents, unless the top level is among
them, which takes any number.  With one parent this is the check
[[add]] has always made.

<<constants>>=
CANDIDATE_ID = -1
<<resolve priority for new todo>>=
ranked_places = [
    place for place in resolved_parents
    if place == ROOT_ID or parent_items[place].priority is not None
]
if not ranked_places or skip_priority:
    priority = None
elif explicit_priority is not None:
    refuse_number_outside_places(
        todos,
        [parent_items.get(place) for place in ranked_places],
        explicit_priority,
        f"--prio {explicit_priority:g}", "--top or --bottom",
    )
    priority = explicit_priority
else:
    priority = None
    ranked_places = places_highest_first(ranked_places, parent_items)
    for place in ranked_places:
        parent_ctx = parent_items.get(place)
        if (
            priority is not None and parent_ctx is not None
            and parent_ctx.priority <= priority
        ):
            continue
        <<place the candidate under [[place]]>>
        priority = (
            number if priority is None else max(priority, number)
        )
@

<<place the candidate under [[place]]>>=
candidate = TodoItem(
    id=CANDIDATE_ID,
    title=priority_title,
    parents=[place],
    labels=inherited_labels,
    deadline=inherited_deadline,
    estimated=(
        parse_hours(estimate) if estimate else None
    ),
)
siblings = get_active_siblings(
    todos, candidate,
    who=who or default_username,
    ranked_only=True,
)
if top or bottom:
    number = place_among_siblings(
        siblings,
        PLACE_TOP if top else PLACE_BOTTOM,
        parent=parent_ctx,
        todos=todos,
        candidate=candidate,
    )
else:
    number = binary_search_priority(
        siblings,
        candidate,
        parent=parent_ctx, todos=todos,
    )
