Here is the second of the three resolutions of [[who]].  The flag path
has no front matter to wait for, so it can settle the assignee at
once.  We bind the result under a new name instead of reassigning
[[who]] because the two mean different things---one is what the user
asked for, the other is who the item ends up belonging to---and only
the second may be stored.  An item is always assigned to somebody: an
ownerless one would be invisible to [[ls]] and [[next]], which filter
by worker.

An item added in several places says which, in the form [[view]]
uses (\cref{sec:todo-view-parents}), since only the first of them
would show where it lands in [[ls]].

<<create and save new todo>>=
