\subsubsection{Adding via Editor}
\label{sec:add-via-editor}

When [[--edit]] is used, the editor opens with the title pre-filled
(if given on the command line). Any CLI flags such as [[--deadline]],
[[--estimate]], [[--wd]], and [[--description]], along with positional
labels, are rendered as a YAML
front\-matter block so the user can review and adjust them before
saving. Without this pre-population the flags would still take effect
(they are merged in [[<<create parent from parsed>>]]), but the user
would never see them in the editor---a confusing silent override.

We intentionally do 
not render [[--env]] into the editor preamble: a captured process
environment is often large, noisy, and not something the user wants to
edit as front matter.  The flag still applies when saving, just without
dumping the whole environment into the buffer.

The editor session and the priority search both wait for the user, so
they run on the snapshot, and the items are created afterwards inside
one locked callback (\cref{sec:todo-read-modify-write}).  That callback
re-checks the parent and draws every ID---the parent's and all its
children's---from the current [[next_id]], exactly as the flag path
does.  The creation chunks below assign to [[next_id]] and [[todos]] as
they go; inside the callback those names are its parameters, the fresh
state, so the recursive [[create_children_from_parsed]] picks them up
through [[nonlocal]] without knowing that a lock is involved.  The
parent is settled like any new item (\cref{sec:sibling-above-squeezed})
before its children are created in the range under it.

<<add via editor>>=
<<build initial editor metadata>>
initial = title if title else ""
yaml_header = render_yaml_block(initial_meta)
if yaml_header:
    initial = initial + "\n" + yaml_header
<<prefill notes into the buffer>>
content = open_editor(initial + "\n")
parsed = parse_editor_content(content)
if not parsed.title:
    typer.echo("No title provided.", err=True)
    raise typer.Exit(1)

<<echo parse warnings>>
priority_title = parsed.title
<<resolve parent id>>
<<resolve [[discovered_from]] for new todo>>
<<inherit from parent>>
<<resolve priority for new todo>>


def append_parsed_todos(next_id, todos, stack):
    for parent_id in parent_items:
        require_todo(todos, parent_id)
    <<create parent from parsed>>
    settle_placement(todos, parent_item)
    <<create children from parsed>>
    return next_id, todos, stack


mutate_todo_state(append_parsed_todos)
@

We build a metadata dictionary from CLI flags so the editor opens
with a pre-populated YAML block. The key names match those used by
[[parse_yaml_block]] and [[build_item_metadata]], ensuring
round-trip consistency. We omit keys whose values are empty to keep
the editor uncluttered.

We always emit [[who]] even when it matches [[default_username]].
This serves two purposes: it makes the assignment explicit so the
user can see who will own the task, and --- crucially --- it
reminds the user of the exact attribute name when they want to
assign a sub-item to someone else.  Without a visible [[who:]]
key in the parent block, a user adding children has no obvious
cue that children can carry their own [[who]] field.

\enquote{Always} is why this is the third resolution of [[who]] and
the only one written as an unconditional assignment.  Every other key
here is omitted when its flag was not given, and [[who]] used to
follow that shape only because the flag could not be absent; now that
it can, the guard would hide exactly the line the paragraph above
argues must be shown.

<<build initial editor metadata>>=
initial_meta = {}
if label:
    initial_meta["labels"] = list(label)
if deadline:
    initial_meta["deadline"] = normalize_deadline(
        deadline
    )
if estimate:
    initial_meta["estimate"] = parse_hours(estimate)
initial_meta["who"] = who or default_username
if wd_path:
    initial_meta["wd"] = canonicalize_wd(wd_path)
elif here:
    initial_meta["wd"] = os.getcwd()
if worktree:
    initial_meta["worktree"] = normalize_worktree_name(
        worktree
    )
if run_command:
    initial_meta["command"] = run_command
if description:
    initial_meta["description"] = description
@

The notes go below the front matter rather than into it, because that
is where the body already lives (\cref{sec:editor-description}): the
text between the front matter and the first heading is what
[[parse_editor_content]] reads back as [[notes]].  Pre-filling it
serves the same purpose as pre-filling the metadata keys---a flag
whose effect were invisible in the buffer would look like the editor
had thrown it away---and it has the same consequence: what the user
leaves in the buffer is what gets stored, so an edited or deleted
paragraph wins over what [[--note]] put there.

<<prefill notes into the buffer>>=
if notes_text:
    initial = initial + "\n" + notes_text
@

Let's verify both halves of that: the flag's text is in the buffer
handed to the editor, and the buffer's version is what is stored.

<<test functions>>=
def test_add_edit_note_prefills_buffer(temp_todo_dir):
  """--note fills the editor body, and the buffer wins."""
  seen = {}

  def fake_editor(text):
    seen["text"] = text
    return text.replace("From the flag.", "From the buffer.")

  with patch(
    "nytid.cli.todo.open_editor",
    side_effect=fake_editor,
  ):
    result = runner.invoke(
