@

The parent is built in three layers, in the order that makes the
precedence rule executable: the fields nobody else supplies, then the
front matter through [[apply_metadata]]
(\cref{sec:apply-metadata}), then the command-line flags on top.
Reading the layers downwards therefore reads the precedence upwards,
and the flags win because they are last, not because each field
remembers to check them.

Only the flags the user actually gave may overwrite, so each one is
guarded by its own truth test.  This is also why the flags cannot
simply be folded into the constructor: an unset flag must leave the
front-matter value standing, and a constructor argument has no way to
say \enquote{nothing}.

The bottom layer is not empty.  When [[--parent]] or auto-parenting
resolved a parent, [[<<inherit from parent>>]] has already worked out
the labels and deadline this item takes from it, and the constructor
is where they belong: inheritance is precisely what the front matter
and the flags are allowed to overrule, so a value assigned after them
would win where it should lose.  Passing them here is also what makes
the two halves of [[add]] agree---[[<<create and save new todo>>]] has
always constructed with the same two values, and a user should not
have to know whether [[--edit]] was given to predict whether a child
of [[#42]] arrives carrying [[#42]]'s deadline.

<<create parent from parsed>>=
now = datetime.datetime.now().isoformat()
parent_item = TodoItem(
    id=next_id,
    title=parsed.title,
    labels=list(inherited_labels),
    priority=priority,
    status="pending",
    created=now,
    deadline=inherited_deadline,
    parents=resolved_parents,
    notes=parsed.notes,
    discovered_from=resolved_discovered_from,
)
apply_metadata(parent_item, parsed.metadata)
<<let add flags win over the front matter>>
<<give the parent an assignee>>
todos.append(parent_item)
parent_id_for_children = next_id
next_id += 1
typer.echo(
    f"Added todo #{parent_item.id}: "
    f"{parent_item.title}"
)
@

Two of the flags are not simple overrides.  [[--here]] is a shorthand
for \enquote{capture both the directory and the environment}, so its
directory yields to a [[wd:]] the user typed in the buffer: a shorthand
should not beat something explicit.  Its environment capture is not
conditioned the same way---a hand-written [[env:]] block loses to
[[--here]]---which is the older rule kept as it was, from a time when
the front matter's [[env]] was not read here at all.  Since we
deliberately never render [[env]] into the buffer, this affects only a
buffer someone typed the key into by hand.

The [[--here]] guard reads the \emph{stored} directory out of
[[__dict__]] rather than [[parent_item.wd]].  Attribute reads for
[[wd]], [[env]] and [[command]]---the members of
[[WORKER_DEFAULT_ATTRIBUTES]]---fall back to the worker's configured
default, so a worker with a configured [[wd]]
would make the item look as though the front matter had set one, and
[[--here]] would then silently do nothing.  Worker defaults are runtime
policy; what we are asking here is what this item explicitly carries.

<<let add flags win over the front matter>>=
if here and parent_item.__dict__.get("wd") is None:
    parent_item.wd = os.getcwd()
if wd_path:
    parent_item.wd = canonicalize_wd(wd_path)
if here or capture_env:
    parent_item.env = dict(os.environ)
if label:
    parent_item.labels = list(label)
if deadline:
    parent_item.deadline = normalize_deadline(deadline)
if estimate:
    parent_item.estimated = parse_hours(estimate)
if who:
    parent_item.who = who
if worktree:
    parent_item.worktree = normalize_worktree_name(
        worktree
    )
if run_command:
    parent_item.command = run_command
if description:
    parent_item.description = description
@

All three layers can still leave [[who]] unset: no [[-w]], no [[who:]]
key---the user deleted the line the buffer opened with---and nothing
to inherit, since a parent contributes labels and a deadline but never
an assignee.  Only now, with every source that could name someone
exhausted, is [[default_username]] the right answer; asking for it any
earlier is what made a typed [[who:]] unable to survive the layer
above it.

An owner is not a nicety here.  [[ls]] and [[next]] filter by worker,
so an item left unassigned would be invisible to the person who just
created it.  The fallback also has to run before the children are
built, because they take their assignee from [[parent_item.who]]
(\cref{sec:add-via-editor}) and would otherwise inherit the hole.

<<give the parent an assignee>>=
if parent_item.who is None:
    parent_item.who = default_username
@

