@

The child is built the same way the [[edit]] side builds a new one
(\cref{sec:apply-metadata}): construct it with what it inherits, then
let its own section override.  Labels, deadline, assignee and
environment are what a child inherits; a description is not, because it
states what \emph{this} item is, so a child without one simply has
none.  The [[who]] inheritance is the one that matters most in
practice---without it, children typed in the editor without an explicit
[[who:]] key would be created unassigned, even though the user intended
the whole task tree to belong to the same person.  Environment is
inherited independently of the directory, so that pointing a child at
another directory does not silently discard the captured environment.

We append the new item to [[todos]] immediately so that recursive
calls can see it as a parent when processing grandchildren.
<<create and append child item>>=
child = TodoItem(
    id=next_id,
    title=parsed_child.title,
    labels=list(parent_labels),
    priority=prio,
    status="pending",
    created=now,
    deadline=parent_deadline,
    parents=[parent_id],
    who=parent_who,
    notes=parsed_child.notes,
    env=parent_env,
)
apply_metadata(child, parsed_child.metadata)
todos.append(child)
child_id = next_id
next_id += 1
typer.echo(
    f"  Added sub-item #{child.id}: "
    f"{child.title}"
)
@

The inherited deadline needs no [[normalize_deadline]] pass of its own.
It comes either from the parent item, which normalized it when it was
written, or from an ancestor child that went through
[[apply_metadata]]---so whatever reaches a grandchild is already
canonical, and normalizing again would only re-derive the same string.

A grandchild inherits from the child, not from the child's parent, so
the recursive call passes what the child ended up with after its own
section was applied.  Three of those four values are plain reads, but
[[env]] is one of [[WORKER_DEFAULT_ATTRIBUTES]]: reading
[[child.env]] answers with the worker's configured environment when the
child stored none, and passing \emph{that} down would write a runtime
default into a grandchild as though the user had typed it---exactly the
persistence [[TodoItem]] keeps [[to_dict]] free of.  Reading
[[__dict__]] asks the question we mean: what does this child explicitly
carry?

<<recurse into the grandchildren>>=
create_children_from_parsed(
    parsed_child.children,
    child_id,
    child.labels,
    child.deadline,
    nested_high, nested_low,
    child.who,
    child.__dict__.get("env"),
)
@

For nested children (grandchildren), the sub-range falls between the
current child's priority and the next child's priority. If this is the
last child, we use the range's lower bound instead.  Unranked children
propagate the unranked state downward by passing [[None]] for both ends
of the nested range:

<<nested child priority range>>=
if prio is None:
    nested_high = None
    nested_low = None
elif i + 1 < len(priorities):
    nested_high = prio
    nested_low = priorities[i + 1]
else:
    nested_high = prio
    nested_low = priority_low
@

The call site computes the parent's priority range from its sibling
context and passes the parent's [[who]] so that any child lacking
an explicit [[who:]] in its YAML block inherits the same assignee.

It reads the environment the same way the recursive call does, and for
the same reason: [[env]] is the only one of these four fields that
[[WORKER_DEFAULT_ATTRIBUTES]] covers, so [[parent_item.env]] answers
with the worker's configured environment whenever the parent stored
none, and every child would then be written to disk carrying a runtime
default nobody typed.  The other three are plain reads because no
policy stands behind them---[[labels]], [[deadline]] and [[who]] mean
exactly what the item holds.

<<create children from parsed>>=
if parent_item.priority is None:
    prio_high = None
    prio_low = None
else:
    prio_high, prio_low = compute_child_priority_range(
        todos, parent_item
    )
create_children_from_parsed(
    parsed.children,
    parent_id_for_children,
    parent_item.labels,
    parent_item.deadline,
    prio_high, prio_low,
    parent_item.who,
    parent_item.__dict__.get("env"),
)
