\paragraph{Every depth, or some}%
\label{sec:todo-depth-flag}

[[--all]] stops at one level because that is what keeps a listing of
a dozen projects scannable: the reader sees what each one is made of
without the tree under every part.  Sometimes the tree is the
question---which branch of a sprint is furthest behind---and for that
[[--recursive]] lists every depth.  Sometimes it is only the next
level or two, so the flag takes an optional number: [[-r 1]] is one
level below the top, the same listing as [[--all]], [[-r 2]] adds
the grandchildren, and a bare [[-r]] means all of them.  A number
never hides what [[--all]] shows, so the two flags compose as the
larger of the two depths.

Whether one wants the whole tree is a habit rather than a fact about
the data, so the default is read from configuration like the other
listing defaults.  The key takes the spellings of the flag, a number
of levels or [[true]] for every level, and [[-r 0]] on the command
line is how a configured default is switched off for one invocation.

Both the flag and the key are parsed by one function, which returns
the number of levels, or [[None]] when there is no limit---[[None]]
rather than a large number because the walkers below already read
[[None]] as \enquote{no cap}, and because a limit that is a number
should mean that number.  The same function serves as the [[cast]]
of [[default]] (\cref{cli.utils.defaults}), whose contract, that a
value it cannot parse falls back, is what keeps a mistyped key from
breaking every listing.

<<configuration>>=
def parse_depth(value):
    """Return `value` as a number of levels below, or None for all.

    Accepts an int, a bool (True is every level), or a string:
    digits, `all`, or the spellings of true and false the boolean
    keys accept.  Anything else raises `ValueError`.
    """
    if value is None or isinstance(value, bool):
        return None if value else 0
    if isinstance(value, int):
        if value < 0:
            raise ValueError(f"negative depth: {value}")
        return value
    text = str(value).strip().lower()
    if text in ("all", "true", "yes", "on"):
        return None
    if text in ("", "false", "no", "off"):
        return 0
    if text.isdigit():
        return int(text)
    raise ValueError(f"not a number of levels: {value!r}")


TODO_LS_RECURSIVE_CONFIG = "todo.ls.recursive"
DEFAULT_LS_RECURSIVE = default(
    TODO_LS_RECURSIVE_CONFIG, 0, parse_depth)
@

Let's verify the spellings, and that a bad value raises so that
[[default]] can fall back:

<<test functions>>=
def test_parse_depth():
    """Numbers are levels, true and all are unlimited, else 0."""
    assert parse_depth(2) == 2
    assert parse_depth("2") == 2
    assert parse_depth(True) is None
    assert parse_depth("all") is None
    assert parse_depth(False) == 0
    assert parse_depth("no") == 0
    with pytest.raises(ValueError):
        parse_depth("deep")
    with pytest.raises(ValueError):
        parse_depth(-1)
@

An option that may be given with or without a value is something
Typer does not offer: its parser takes a value or a flag, never one
that is sometimes the other.  What it does offer is the class of the
command, so the two commands that carry [[-r]] use one that looks at
the arguments before parsing and gives a bare [[-r]] the value
[[all]].  \enquote{Bare} means not followed by a number: a following
option, a following word, or the end of the line.  A following number
is taken as the depth, which also means that [[todo view -r 5]] reads
[[5]] as levels rather than as the id; the id goes first,
[[todo view 5 -r]].  Anything after [[--]] is left alone, as the
shell convention requires.

The rewrite is a function of the argument list, so it can be tested
without building a command, and the class is the thin adapter that
hands it Typer's arguments.

<<helpers>>=
def rewrite_depth_flags(args, flags=("-r", "--recursive")):
    """Return `args` with every bare depth flag given the value `all`.

    A flag is bare when it is last or not followed by a number.
    Arguments after `--` are left untouched.
    """
    rewritten = []
    for i, arg in enumerate(args):
        rewritten.append(arg)
        if arg == "--":
            rewritten.extend(args[i + 1:])
            break
        if arg not in flags:
            continue
        following = args[i + 1] if i + 1 < len(args) else ""
        if not following.isdigit():
            rewritten.append("all")
    return rewritten


class RecursiveDepthCommand(TyperCommand):
    """A command whose `-r` may be given with or without a depth."""

    def parse_args(self, ctx, args):
        return super().parse_args(ctx, rewrite_depth_flags(args))
@

Let's verify the three shapes a bare flag can take, that a number
is left to be the value, and that [[--]] ends the rewriting:

<<test functions>>=
def test_rewrite_depth_flags():
    """A bare -r gets `all`; a number after it is its value."""
    assert rewrite_depth_flags(["-r"]) == ["-r", "all"]
    assert rewrite_depth_flags(["-r", "--flat"]) == [
        "-r", "all", "--flat",
    ]
    assert rewrite_depth_flags(["1", "-r", "rich"]) == [
        "1", "-r", "all", "rich",
    ]
    assert rewrite_depth_flags(["--recursive", "2", "x"]) == [
        "--recursive", "2", "x",
    ]
    assert rewrite_depth_flags(["--", "-r"]) == ["--", "-r"]
