                          ls_who_opt] = DEFAULT_LS_WHO,
    flat: Annotated[bool, ls_flat_opt] = False,
    output_format: Annotated[LsFormat,
                             ls_format_opt] = DEFAULT_LS_FORMAT,
    status_filter: Annotated[TodoStatus | None,
                             ls_status_opt] = None,
    show_prio: Annotated[bool, ls_prio_opt] = False,
    output: Annotated[str | None,
                      ls_output_opt] = None,
):
    """List todo items in display order.

    Ranked items are shown first by effective priority; unranked
    items follow in deadline order. By default shows only top-level
    items assigned to the current user. Pass `--all` to see each
    item's direct sub-items, `-r` to see every depth or `-r N` the
    top N levels, and `-n N` to cap the output at N items; `-n 0`
    lifts the cap.

    `--who` defaults to $USER; pass an empty string
    (`--who ''`) to show all assignees.

    Output format defaults to `table`, which auto-detects the
    terminal: a rich table when stdout is a TTY, and headerless
    tab-separated rows in a pipe. `--format csv` adds a header row
    and commas; `--format json` gives structure.
    """
    _, todos = load_todos()

    <<treat [[-n 0]] as unlimited>>
    <<filter todos>>
    <<restrict [[filtered]] to the requested depth>>
    <<query tracked time>>

    <<determine effective format>>

    if effective_format == "json":
        <<output json>>
    elif effective_format == "rich":
        <<output rich table>>
    else:
        <<output csv>>
@

\paragraph{[[-n 0]] means all of them}

Every renderer applies the cap by slicing, and [[list[:None]]] is the
whole list, so \enquote{no cap} already had a spelling---but only from
inside the code, where [[count]] can be [[None]].  From the command
line every [[-n]] value is an integer, and the integer a user reaches
for to mean \enquote{do not limit me} is [[0]], which slicing reads as
the exact opposite: [[nytid todo ls -n 0]] listed nothing at all.

Mapping a non-positive count onto [[None]] here, once, gives all
three renderers the unlimited behaviour without any of them testing
for it.
It also gives the hint below (\cref{sec:todo-ls-hint}) a command it can
recommend, which is the reason the two changes belong together: a line
telling the reader that items are missing is only useful if the escape
it names works.

<<treat [[-n 0]] as unlimited>>=
if count is not None and count <= 0:
    count = None
@

