Filters such as [[--who]] narrow [[filtered]] to a subset without
regard to hierarchy.  A subitem assigned to one person may have a
parent assigned to someone else; after filtering by [[--who]], the
parent is gone but the child remains.  Treating that child as
invisible because it has a parent would silently hide an item the
filtered user is responsible for.

The right behaviour is to treat any item as a root when its parent
is absent from the filtered set---either because it is a true
top-level item, or because its parent was removed by a filter.  With
several parents (\cref{sec:todo-parents}) that reads: an item is a
root when the top level is among its parents, or when none of its
parents that are items is in the set.  We
factor this into a helper so that both the non-tree restriction step
and the tree renderer share exactly the same logic, with no
duplication:

<<sub-item helpers>>=
def get_visible_roots(
    items: list[TodoItem],
) -> list[TodoItem]:
    """Return items whose parent is absent from items.

    An item is a visible root when it is a true top-level item
    (`ROOT_ID` is among its parents) or when none of its parents is
    present in items---for example, because a --who filter removed
    it.  Pass a pre-filtered list, not the full todos.
    """
    ids = {t.id for t in items}
    return [
        t for t in items
        if ROOT_ID in parents_of(t)
        or not any(p in ids for p in real_parents_of(t))
    ]
@

The depth of the listing is decided here, once, before the format
branch.  Without [[--all]] we restrict [[filtered]] to its visible
roots: sub-items of a visible parent are subordinate detail the user
can reveal on demand.  With [[--all]] we keep those roots and their
direct children---one level, in every format.  [[-r]] names the
number of levels, or lifts the limit, and every format lists the
same ones.

That last clause is a change.  [[--all]] used to leave [[filtered]]
untouched, so a JSON or CSV listing carried every depth while the
rich table, which walked one level by hand, showed grandchildren to
nobody.  A flag whose meaning depends on the output format is a trap
for the script that switches formats, so the restriction now applies
before the renderer is chosen, and JSON, CSV, the flat table and the
tree all agree on what one level is.

The tree walker below (\cref{sec:todo-ls-walk}) already knows every
item's depth, so the restriction is a slice of its rows rather than
a second notion of depth.  The walker sorts, but so does every
renderer afterwards, so the order it leaves behind is harmless.

<<restrict [[filtered]] to the requested depth>>=
depth_limit = recursive
if show_tree and depth_limit is not None:
    depth_limit = max(depth_limit, 1)
if depth_limit is not None:
    rows, _ = get_tree_rows(filtered)
    filtered = [t for depth, t in rows if depth <= depth_limit]
@

To show tracked time, we load track data and sum durations for
entries matching [[todo:<id>]] labels. This avoids duplicating the
track system's storage logic.

All three output formats consume [[tracked_times]]---JSON and CSV
both carry a [[tracked_hours]] field and the rich table has a
Tracked column---so we cannot skip the query for any particular
format.  What we can skip is loading a multi-megabyte
tracking history to annotate a listing with nothing in it, which is
why this step runs \emph{after} the filters and the top-level
restriction: when [[filtered]] is empty, the load never happens.
The dictionary itself is initialized unconditionally, and every
consumer reads it through [[tracked_times.get(..., 0)]], so an
empty dictionary is indistinguishable from \enquote{no time tracked
yet} in all three formats.

<<query tracked time>>=
tracked_times: dict[int, float] = {}
if filtered:
    try:
        from nytid.cli.track import load_tracking_data

        entries = load_tracking_data()
        for entry in entries:
            for lbl in entry.labels:
                if lbl.startswith("todo:"):
                    try:
                        tid = int(lbl.split(":")[1])
                        hours = (
                            entry.duration().total_seconds()
                            / 3600
                        )
                        tracked_times[tid] = (
                            tracked_times.get(tid, 0) + hours
                        )
                    except (ValueError, IndexError):
                        pass
    except ImportError:
        pass
@

The empty-listing fast path is worth pinning: a future refactor
that hoists the load back before the filters would silently
reintroduce the cost.  We monkeypatch [[load_tracking_data]] to
raise, so the test fails loudly if an empty listing touches the
tracking history at all:

<<test functions>>=
def test_ls_empty_skips_tracking_load(temp_todo_dir, monkeypatch):
  """An empty listing never loads the tracking history."""
  import nytid.cli.track

  def explode():
    raise AssertionError(
      "load_tracking_data called for empty listing"
    )

  monkeypatch.setattr(
    nytid.cli.track, "load_tracking_data", explode
  )
  result = runner.invoke(cli, ["ls"])
  assert result.exit_code == 0
