\paragraph{One record, several encodings}%
\label{sec:todo-ls-record}

JSON, comma-separated CSV and the tab-separated rows a pipe receives
are all read by scripts, and a script that switches between them
should not have to learn a second set of fields.  So an item is
described \emph{once}, as a dictionary, and every format encodes that
same dictionary: JSON serializes it directly, and the two delimited
forms write its values in its key order.  Spelling the delimited
columns out separately is what let them drift apart until they offered
eight fields where JSON offered seventeen, [[labels]] among the
missing ones (\cref{sec:todo-ls-csv}).

The record is [[to_dict()]] plus the two values that are not stored on
the item: the numeric score, which only [[--prio]] asks for, and the
hours the track system has recorded against it.  Adding them after
[[to_dict()]] rather than in among the stored fields keeps the derived
values at the end of every record, and hence at the end of the CSV
header.

That rule held only while no stored field was added after the computed
columns existed.  The pipe form has no header, so scripts address its
columns by position with [[cut -f]] (\cref{sec:todo-ls-dialect}), and
a stored field that simply joined [[to_dict()]] would push
[[tracked_hours]], and the score under [[--prio]], one column to the
right.  A script reading the tracked hours would silently get the new
field instead.  The only change that cannot move an existing column is
one at the very end, so every stored field added since then is listed
in [[FIELDS_AFTER_COMPUTED]] and moved behind the computed values, in
the order of the list.  The list only grows: a later field is appended
to it, after the ones already there, which keeps each of their
positions too.  [[closed]] came first; [[claimed_by]] and
[[context_claim]] (\cref{sec:todo-claim}) followed it,
[[discovered_from]] (\cref{sec:todo-discovered-from}) followed them,
and the last two are described below.

A listing that extends [[ls]]'s record with computed columns of its
own, as [[ready]] does (\cref{sec:todo-ready}), meets the same problem
one level up.  Appending its columns to the finished record would put
them behind the late stored fields, and the next stored field would
then move them.  [[ls_record]] therefore takes such columns as
[[computed]] and places them after its own computed values but before
the late stored fields, so that in every record the late stored fields
are the very end, and a new one moves nothing.

[[parents]] (\cref{sec:todo-parents-field}) took over from
[[parent_id]], so it might seem to belong in [[parent_id]]'s column.
But a script that reads that column expects one id or nothing, and a
list there would break it as surely as a shifted column would.  The
derived [[parent_id]] that [[to_dict()]] writes keeps that column, with
the meaning it always had, and [[parents]] is the fifth field to move
behind the computed values.  The headerless rows thus grow by one
column at the end and lose none.

[[blocked_by]] (\cref{sec:todo-blocked-by-field}) is the sixth, after
[[parents]].  Whether the item is blocked \emph{now} is deliberately not
a computed column of its own.  The answer depends on other items, and
on more than the stored list, since a block can come from an ancestor
(\cref{sec:todo-blockers}); a script that wants it asks [[ready]], whose
records carry the verdict, or [[ls --blocked]], which selects the
blocked items (\cref{sec:todo-ls-blocked}).  A column added here would
also have to go somewhere: in front of the late stored fields, as
[[ready]]'s go, it would move every one of them in the pipe form.

<<constants>>=
FIELDS_AFTER_COMPUTED = (
    "closed", "claimed_by", "context_claim", "discovered_from",
    "parents", "blocked_by",
)
<<helpers>>=
def ls_record(item, now, tracked_times, show_prio, computed=None):
    """Return the dictionary `todo ls` serializes for `item`.

    Both the JSON and the CSV format encode this record, so the two
    always carry the same fields in the same order.  `now` dates the
    effective-priority computation, `tracked_times` maps item ids to
    tracked hours, and `show_prio` adds the numeric score.  The
    `computed` dict holds a caller's further computed columns; they
    follow the tracked hours, ahead of the late stored fields.
    """
    record = item.to_dict()
    late_fields = {
        name: record.pop(name) for name in FIELDS_AFTER_COMPUTED
    }
    if show_prio:
        record["effective_priority"] = effective_priority(
            item, now
        )
    record["tracked_hours"] = tracked_times.get(item.id, 0)
    record.update(computed or {})
    record.update(late_fields)
    return record
@

