\paragraph{Saying so when the cap cut the listing}%
\label{sec:todo-ls-hint}

A cap that hides items silently is a trap for anyone who reads the
listing as an answer.  The failure that prompted this is the one an
agent walks into: it adds an item, lists to confirm the add landed,
does not find the item below the cap, and concludes it has to add the
item again.  A rich table that was cut therefore ends by saying how
many items it left out and which flag reveals them.

Only the rich table.  The line is prose addressed to a person looking
at a terminal, and everything else this command emits is data: the
plain rows go to whatever read the pipe, the CSV to a spreadsheet or a
[[DictReader]], the JSON to a parser.  A sentence appended to any of
those is corruption rather than commentary---it makes the JSON
unparseable and gives the delimited forms a last line with one field.

That is also why the decision cannot be read off [[--format]].  The
default, [[table]], is one request with two renderings: the rich table
on a terminal and plain rows in a pipe.  It is the rendering that
tells us whether a human is reading, so the hint belongs to the
renderer that drew the table and to nothing else---which is exactly
where the code below puts it, with no flag to consult.

<<helpers>>=
def truncation_hint(hidden):
    """Return the line reporting `hidden` items that `-n` cut."""
    return f"\u2026 {hidden} more (use -n 0 to show all)"
@

Whichever chunk above did the slicing left [[hidden]] behind for us,
and the two chunks compute it differently on purpose: the flat listing
caps items, while the tree listing caps \emph{top-level} items and
lets each one bring its whole subtree along.  Reporting the difference
the renderer actually made is the only way the count matches what the
reader sees---which also means a tree of busy items can print many
rows and still, truthfully, report a small number.

The line is dimmed, the same treatment the progress summaries get: it
is commentary on the listing rather than a row of it, and dimming says
so without a legend.  We wrap it in [[Text]] for the reason every
other cell is wrapped---rich reads a bare string as console
markup---even though this particular string has no brackets in it; a
reader who later edits the wording should not have to discover that
the wording was what made the bare string safe.

<<show the truncation hint below the table>>=
if hidden:
    console.print(
        Text(truncation_hint(hidden), style="dim")
    )
@

