\paragraph{Walking the tree}%
\label{sec:todo-ls-walk}

The tree is a depth-first walk that starts at the visible roots and
descends only into children that are themselves in [[filtered]].
Membership in the filtered list, not the parents alone, decides
both where the walk starts and where it goes next, so the promotion
that [[get_visible_roots]] performs for a child whose parent a filter
removed holds at every depth: an orphaned grandchild is a root, not a
missing row.  It is also what lets one walker serve every depth.
[[<<restrict [[filtered]] to the requested depth>>]] has already cut
the list to one level under [[--all]], so the walk stops there on its
own; hand it the whole tree and it lists the whole tree.

The cap applies to roots, and each root brings its subtree along
uncut---the flat listing caps rows, the tree caps top-level items, as
[[truncation_hint]] explains.  Rows come back as (depth, item) pairs
so the renderer is one loop with an indentation rule, the same shape
the [[view]] command uses for its sub-items.

The walk needs a guard against cycles, as every walk over the parents
does (\cref{sec:todo-parents}), though not for the reason one would
first think.  A root is an item that is top-level or whose parents are
all absent, and with one parent per item a cycle contains no such
item, so a walk from the roots never enters one.  With several
parents it can: a hand-edited file may make an item top-level and,
at the same time, a sub-item of its own descendant.  The walk
therefore carries the ids on the path from its root and does not
descend into one of them again.  It is the path, not every id seen so
far, because an item below two shown parents is not a cycle and is
listed under each of them.

Listed, but expanded only once.  The subtree of a shared item is the
same under each of its parents, and showing it twice would make the
listing longer without saying more, and make a subtree look like two
pieces of work.  The walk keeps the set of items it has expanded, and
the second time it meets one it lists the item alone; the renderer
then says that its subtree was shown above (\cref{sec:todo-tree-notes}).

<<sub-item helpers>>=
def get_tree_rows(
    items: list[TodoItem], now=None, count=None,
) -> tuple[list[tuple[int, TodoItem]], int]:
    """Return the tree of `items` as (depth, item) rows.

    Visible roots come in display order, capped at `count` (None
    for no cap), each followed depth-first by its children that are
    present in `items`; an item met again is listed without its
    children.  The second value is the number of roots the cap
    left out.
    """
    roots = sort_todos_for_display(get_visible_roots(items), now)
    top_level = roots[:count]
    rows = []
    expanded = set()
    for root in top_level:
        rows.extend(get_subtree_rows(
            items, root, now, expanded=expanded,
        ))
    return rows, len(roots) - len(top_level)


def get_subtree_rows(
    items, item, now=None, depth=0, path=frozenset(),
    expanded=None,
):
    """Return `item` and its descendants in `items` as (depth, item).

    `path` holds the ids above `item` in this walk; a child among
    them, or `item` itself, closes a cycle and is not descended into.
    `expanded` collects the items whose children were listed; one
    already in it is listed without them.
    """
    rows = [(depth, item)]
    if expanded is None:
        expanded = set()
    if item.id in expanded:
        return rows
    expanded.add(item.id)
    path = path | {item.id}
    children = sort_todos_for_display(
        [c for c in children_of(items, item.id) if c.id not in path],
        now,
    )
    for child in children:
        rows.extend(get_subtree_rows(
            items, child, now, depth + 1, path, expanded,
        ))
    return rows
@

