\paragraph{Saying where else an item is}%
\label{sec:todo-tree-notes}
A shared item appears in a tree once under each of its shown parents,
and each appearance should say that it is not the only one: the user
reading D's children should learn that B also belongs to A, and that
finishing it completes something there too.  So the row carries a note
after the title, \enquote{(also under \#12, top level)}, naming the
item's other places.

The number needs a word as well.  An item ranks where its ranking
context puts it (\cref{sec:ranking-context}), and under its other
parents it sorts by that number, usually first, above the parent's own
children; read without the reason, that looks like a mistake.  A row
under any other parent than the ranking context therefore adds
\enquote{(ranked via \#12)}, or \enquote{(ranked at top level)}.
Last, a repeated item whose children the walk did not list again says
\enquote{(subtree shown above)}, so that a bare row is not mistaken for
a leaf.  Whether a subtree was shown is read off the rows themselves:
an earlier row of the item that is followed by a deeper one.  Asking
whether the item has children would claim too much, since the earlier
row may have shown none of them, all done and hidden, say, or cut by
the depth limit.

The notes are worked out from the rows rather than by the walk, which
keeps the walks about structure and lets [[view]], whose rows start
below the viewed item, use the same function.  A row's parent is the
row above it one level up; a row at the top has the top level as its
parent, or, in [[view]], the viewed item, which the caller passes as
[[top]].  A root that is there only because its parents are filtered
away has no parent in the listing, and nothing is said about it.

<<sub-item helpers>>=
def tree_row_notes(todos, rows, top=ROOT_ID):
    """Return a note for each (depth, item) row of a tree.

    A note names the item's places other than the row's parent,
    the parent it is ranked under if that is another, and whether
    its subtree was listed further up.  `todos` are all the items;
    rows at depth 0 hang under `top`.
    """
    by_id = {t.id: t for t in todos}
    above = []
    shown_below = set()
    notes = []
    for i, (depth, item) in enumerate(rows):
        expanded_here = i + 1 < len(rows) and rows[i + 1][0] > depth
        del above[depth:]
        if above:
            row_parent = above[-1].id
        elif top in parents_of(item):
            row_parent = top
        else:
            row_parent = None
        <<describe the row's other places>>
        if item.id in shown_below and not expanded_here:
            parts.append("subtree shown above")
        if expanded_here:
            shown_below.add(item.id)
        above.append(item)
        notes.append(" ".join(f"({part})" for part in parts))
    return notes
@

<<describe the row's other places>>=
parts = []
if row_parent is not None and len(parents_of(item)) > 1:
    others = [p for p in parents_of(item) if p != row_parent]
    parts.append("also " + ", ".join(
        "top level" if p == ROOT_ID else f"under #{p}"
        for p in others
    ))
    context = ranking_context(todos, item, by_id)
    if item.priority is not None and context != row_parent:
        parts.append(
            "ranked at top level" if context == ROOT_ID
            else f"ranked via #{context}"
        )
@

