Every parent gets a progress summary, not only the top-level ones.
Under [[--all]] a child with children of its own is the only sign
that a level exists below the listing, and in a deeper tree the
summary says how far along each branch is.  The count comes from
[[todos]], not [[filtered]], so it reports the whole family whatever
the filters removed.  The summary sits in the Title column, indented
like the title above it, and the leading empty cells depend on
whether the priority column is shown, as explained above.

<<add a progress row under a parent>>=
all_children = children_of(todos, item.id)
if all_children:
    done_count = sum(
        1 for c in all_children
        if c.status == "done"
    )
    total = len(all_children)
    progress = Padding(
        Text(f"[{done_count}/{total} done]", style="dim"),
        (0, 0, 0, 2 * depth),
    )
    progress_row = [""]
    if show_prio:
        progress_row.append("")
    progress_row.append(progress)
    table.add_row(*progress_row)
@

The cells of a row are built by a function rather than inline, so
that the flat listing, the tree and any other table that shows todos
the way [[ls]] does format the deadline, the estimate and the tracked
time identically.  A table that needs a column of its own appends its
cell to the list this function returns, which keeps [[ls]]'s columns
in the same places wherever they appear.

The title cell is wrapped in [[Text]] because rich parses bare
strings as console markup: a bracketed word in a title, as in
\enquote{Fix [parser] crash}, would be read as a style tag and
silently dropped.
Titles are the only user-authored text in these rows---the other
cells are ids, numbers, dates and status words we generate---so they
are the only cells that need the literal treatment.
The [[view]] command's renderer and [[print_rows]]
(\cref{cli.utils.output}) make the same move.

Depth becomes indentation of the title, as a [[Padding]] around the
cell rather than spaces in front of it.  Titles routinely outrun
their column, and when [[rich]] wraps a cell it keeps leading spaces
only on the first line, so a nested title that wraps shows its
continuation lines flush with its parent---the nesting vanishes
exactly when the tree is deep enough to need it.  A padding indents
every wrapped line; [[view]] indents its sub-items the same way for
the same reason.

<<helpers>>=
def ls_row_cells(
    item, now, tracked_times, show_prio, depth=0, place="",
):
    """Return the rich-table cells `todo ls` shows for `item`.

    The cells match the columns of the `todo ls` table: the
    priority cell is present only when `show_prio` is true, and the
    title is indented `depth` levels and followed by `place`, the
    tree's note on where else the item is, dimmed.
    """
    from rich.padding import Padding
    from rich.text import Text

    ep = effective_priority(item, now)
    dl_str = ""
    if item.deadline:
        try:
            dl = datetime.datetime.fromisoformat(
                item.deadline
            )
            dl_str = dl.strftime("%b %d")
        except ValueError:
            dl_str = item.deadline[:10]
    est_str = (
        f"{item.estimated:.1f}h"
        if item.estimated
        else ""
    )
    tracked = tracked_times.get(item.id, 0)
    tracked_str = (
        f"{tracked:.1f}h" if tracked > 0 else ""
    )
    return [
        str(item.id),
        *([
            "" if ep is None else f"{ep:.1f}"
        ] if show_prio else []),
        Padding(
            Text(("\u2514\u2500 " if depth else "") + item.title)
            + Text(f" {place}" if place else "", style="dim"),
            (0, 0, 0, 2 * depth),
        ),
        item.who or "",
        dl_str,
        est_str,
        tracked_str,
        item.status,
    ]
@

Both of [[ls]]'s renderers add one such row per item, at the depth
their walk reached.

<<add item row to rich table>>=
table.add_row(
    *ls_row_cells(
        item, now, tracked_times, show_prio, depth, place,
    )
)
@

\paragraph{Flattening the record into cells}%
\label{sec:todo-ls-csv}

Two of the record's values are not strings, and a cell has to be.
Labels are a list, joined here with semicolons rather than commas so
that the value stays one word to a reader that splits on the delimiter
even after the quoting has been stripped away.  The parents are a list
of ids and are joined the same way, the top level included as~[[0]]
(\cref{sec:todo-parents-field}), and so are the blockers
(\cref{sec:todo-blocked-by-field}).  The GitHub link is a
dictionary of repository, type and number, rendered as [[repo#number]]:
the spelling [[gh]] itself accepts and the one a reader would write in
a commit message.  Flattening it drops the issue-versus-pull-request
distinction, which is the price of a single cell; a consumer that needs
that distinction reads JSON.  The ancestor path that [[ready]] adds to
its records (\cref{sec:todo-ready}) is a list of id and title pairs;
its cell is the line of titles the [[ready]] table shows, and a
consumer that needs the ids reads JSON for the same reason.

That leaves [[env]], a dictionary of arbitrary environment variables
with no one-line spelling at all.  Encoding it would mean inventing a
nesting convention inside a cell, and the cell would routinely be
longer than the rest of the row together, so the delimited formats omit
the field and JSON stays the format that carries it.

<<constants>>=
CSV_OMITTED_FIELDS = ("env",)
@

<<helpers>>=
def csv_cell(name, value):
    """Return `value` rendered as the CSV cell of column `name`.

    Labels, parents and blockers are joined with semicolons, a GitHub
    link becomes `repo#number`, a path becomes its titles, and
    `None` becomes the empty string.  Every other value is rendered with `str`,
    matching what JSON would show.
    """
    if name == "labels":
        return ";".join(value or [])
    if name in ("parents", "blocked_by"):
        return ";".join(str(p) for p in value or [])
    if name == "path":
        return format_todo_path(value)
    if name == "github":
        if not value:
            return ""
        return (f"{value.get('repo', '')}"
                f"#{value.get('number', '')}")
    return "" if value is None else str(value)
@

A pipe consumer counts lines, so a record has to \emph{be} a
line---and two of these fields, [[notes]] and [[description]], are
free text that routinely is not one.  The pipe form therefore
renders each cell through a wrapper that writes a line break as the
two characters [[\n]] and a tab as [[\t]], which is what shell
readers of escaped text already expect and what keeps the row
countable.  We spell the wrapper out rather than passing a flag to
[[csv_cell]] so that the two dialects stay two named things, one per
audience.

The encoding is lossy in one direction: a description that already
contained the two characters [[\n]] reads back as a line break.
That is the right trade here, because this form exists to be cut
apart rather than restored---fidelity is what the named form and
[[--format json]] are for.

<<helpers>>=
def tsv_cell(name, value):
    """Return `csv_cell(name, value)` as a single line.

    Line breaks become the two characters backslash and n, tabs
    backslash and t, so one record occupies one line and the text
    stays legible.  The substitution is not reversible; use the
    comma-separated form or JSON when it has to be.
    """
    return (
        csv_cell(name, value)
        .replace("\r\n", "\\n")
        .replace("\n", "\\n")
        .replace("\r", "\\n")
        .replace("\t", "\\t")
    )
@

\paragraph{Two encodings of the same rows}%
\label{sec:todo-ls-dialect}

One schema, then, but two ways of writing it down, and which one is
right depends on who is going to read it.

A pipe is a case nytid has already decided (\cref{cli.utils.output}):
headerless, tab-separated rows.  The tab is the delimiter [[cut]] and
[[awk]] already default to, and a header line is a row that every
shell idiom counts, sorts or word-splits as if it were data.  That is
what the auto-detected [[table]] format falls through to when stdout
is not a terminal, so [[nytid todo ls | cut -f2]] keeps working and
[[todo ls]] reads in a pipe the way [[track]]'s listings do.

[[--format csv]] is a different request.  It names a format, by
someone who will hand the result to a spreadsheet or to a
[[csv.DictReader]], and such a reader looks a column up by name
instead of counting fields.  Counting is what a consumer of the old
headerless output had to do, and it is how the bug in this format was
found: the empty [[estimated_h]] cell of an unestimated item was
counted as a [[labels]] column, which the format did not have at all.
So the explicit request gets commas and a header row, always---and
because the header is taken from the record's keys, the absent column
is absent no longer.

An [[--output]] file is that same case whichever format asked for it.
It outlives the command that wrote it and will be opened by something
that was not watching it run, so it has to say what its columns are.

<<choose the csv dialect>>=
named = bool(output) or output_format == LsFormat.csv
cell = csv_cell if named else tsv_cell
@

We build the whole table in memory before writing a byte of it, which
is what lets the header be derived rather than declared: the column
names are the keys of the records we just built, minus the omitted
ones, so a field added to [[TodoItem]] reaches both encodings without
anyone remembering to add it here.  Writing through a [[StringIO]]
also keeps the rows on the same stream as every other message the
command prints---[[typer.echo]]'s---instead of on whichever
[[sys.stdout]] happened to be bound when [[csv.writer]] was
constructed, which is what a test runner or any caller that redirects
output needs.

[[csv.writer]] does the quoting, so a title with a comma in it
survives being read back by a parser that knows the dialect.  Only
the named form relies on that: a quoted line break is still a line
break, and the pipe form has no parser waiting for it, which is why
its cells were escaped to one line above.

An empty listing still prints nothing at all, header included.  A
header with no rows under it looks like a one-line result to [[wc -l]],
and tells a reader nothing that silence does not; this is the rule
[[print_rows]] states for every other listing
(\cref{cli.utils.output}).

<<output csv>>=
now = datetime.datetime.now()
sorted_items = sort_todos_for_display(
    filtered, now
)[:count]
if not sorted_items:
    return
records = [
    ls_record(item, now, tracked_times, show_prio)
    for item in sorted_items
]
<<choose the csv dialect>>
<<write the records as csv>>
<<deliver the csv text>>
@

<<write the records as csv>>=
columns = [
    name for name in records[0]
    if name not in CSV_OMITTED_FIELDS
]
buffer = io.StringIO()
writer = csv.writer(
    buffer, delimiter="," if named else "\t"
)
if named:
    writer.writerow(columns)
for record in records:
    writer.writerow([
        cell(name, record[name])
        for name in columns
    ])
@

A file named with [[--output]] is meant to be opened later, by a
spreadsheet or by another program, so it receives the text and nothing
else; the confirmation that it was written goes to the terminal
instead.

<<deliver the csv text>>=
if output:
    with open(output, "w", newline="") as dest:
        dest.write(buffer.getvalue())
    typer.echo(f"Wrote todos to {output}")
else:
    typer.echo(buffer.getvalue(), nl=False)
