\subsection{The [[ready]] Command}%
\label{sec:todo-ready}

[[ls]] answers how the work is organised: projects at the top, each
one's parts below it.  That is the wrong shape for the question one
asks most often, \enquote{what can I do now?}  The answer to that is
usually a sub-item a level or two down, under a parent that ranks
high, and the tree hides it behind [[--all]] or [[-r]], in among
parents that cannot be worked on as such and items that someone is
already working on.  [[ready]] answers the question directly: one
flat list, ranked the way [[ls]] ranks, of the items that could be
started now.

The two listings differ in what they show and agree on how they
filter, rank and render:
\begin{description}
\item[What differs] [[ls]] lists a tree, top-level items by default,
  in every status but done.  [[ready]] lists every depth, flat, and
  only the items that are ready (defined below); each row carries the
  path of its ancestors, since a flat row has no tree around it to
  say where it belongs.
\item[What is shared] The positional labels, [[--who]] and its
  default, the display order, [[-n]] with [[-n 0]] for all, the four
  formats with their defaults and dialects, [[--prio]] and
  [[--output]].  These are [[ls]]'s own code, called rather than
  copied, so a filter that changes in [[ls]] changes here too.
\end{description}

[[ready]] does not walk the tree the way [[next]] does.  [[next]]
starts from the current context and descends one level at a time, so
it finishes the project it is in before moving on; [[ready]] ranks the
ready items of every project against each other, which is the view
one wants when choosing what to switch to.

\paragraph{What ready means}

An item is ready when it is pending and none of its children is
still open.  The status condition is plain: a done item needs no work
and an in-progress item already has a worker.  The condition on
children is what makes the list a list of things one can actually
do.  A parent is marked done by rollup when its last child is done,
so working on a parent \emph{means} working on one of its children,
and listing the parent as well would offer the same work twice.  The
children counted are all of them, whoever they are assigned to: a
parent whose remaining child belongs to an agent is not ready for its
owner either, because it completes when the agent finishes.  So the
parent drops out of its owner's [[ready]] list while the child shows
up in the agent's, and that is intended: the parent is waiting on the
agent, and an owner who wants to see what is waiting asks for
[[--all]].

Readiness is decided in one function, [[is_ready]], rather than in
the command.  [[ready]] is the first caller, but not the only
candidate: [[next]] and the schedule both pick pending items to work
on, and a condition added to readiness---for instance an item that
waits for another---has to reach every caller at once, which it does
only if there is one definition to add it to.

[[--all]] widens the listing without changing the definition: it also
lists pending items whose children are open, for the user who wants
to see the projects that are waiting.  The flag is a parameter of
[[is_ready]] rather than a second test in the command, so that
whatever else readiness comes to require still applies to those rows.

A caller that asks about every item, as [[ready]] does, would pay for
[[children_of]]'s scan of the whole list once per item, which is
quadratic in the number of items.  Such a caller builds a map from
parent to children once and passes it along.  The map is optional,
and only a faster way to answer the same question, so the definition
stays one function that a caller with a single item can use as is.

<<sub-item helpers>>=
def children_by_parent(todos):
    """Return a map from each parent id to its children in `todos`."""
    index = {}
    for todo in todos:
        for parent_id in real_parents_of(todo):
            index.setdefault(parent_id, []).append(todo)
    return index


def is_ready(todos, item, allow_open_children=False, children=None):
    """Return whether `item` is ready to be worked on.

    An item is ready when it is pending and none of its children in
    `todos` is open (not done).  With `allow_open_children`, a
    pending item with open children counts as well.  `children` may
    pass `children_by_parent(todos)` to avoid scanning `todos`.
    This is the single definition of readiness; callers should not
    test the conditions themselves.
    """
    if item.status != "pending":
        return False
    if allow_open_children:
        return True
    if children is None:
        item_children = children_of(todos, item.id)
    else:
        item_children = children.get(item.id, [])
    return all(child.status == "done" for child in item_children)
@

Let's verify the cases: a pending leaf is ready, a parent with an open
child is not unless asked, neither an in-progress nor a done item is
ever ready, the map gives the same answers as the scan, and a child
counts whoever it is assigned to.

<<test functions>>=
def test_is_ready_leaves_only_by_default():
  """Pending leaves are ready; open parents only when allowed."""
  todos = [
    TodoItem(id=1, title="Parent"),
    TodoItem(id=2, title="Open child", parents=[1]),
    TodoItem(id=3, title="Done child", parents=[1], status="done"),
    TodoItem(id=4, title="Active", status="in-progress"),
    TodoItem(id=5, title="Done", status="done"),
  ]
  by_id = {t.id: t for t in todos}

  assert is_ready(todos, by_id[2])
  assert not is_ready(todos, by_id[1])
  assert is_ready(todos, by_id[1], allow_open_children=True)
  assert not is_ready(todos, by_id[4], allow_open_children=True)
  assert not is_ready(todos, by_id[5], allow_open_children=True)

  children = children_by_parent(todos)
  for item in todos:
    for allow in (False, True):
      assert is_ready(
        todos, item, allow_open_children=allow, children=children
      ) == is_ready(todos, item, allow_open_children=allow)


def test_is_ready_counts_children_of_every_worker():
  """A child assigned to someone else still keeps its parent open."""
  todos = [
    TodoItem(id=1, title="Parent", who="me"),
    TodoItem(id=2, title="Child", parents=[1], who="dan-claude"),
  ]
  assert not is_ready(todos, todos[0])
@

\paragraph{Where a row belongs}

The path is the line of ancestors that [[ranking_path]] returns, root
first, which is the order one reads a path in.  An item with several
parents has several lines; the path follows the one its number was
placed in (\cref{sec:ranking-context}), which is the line that explains
where the row sorts, and the table adds \enquote{(+N)} for the item's
other places.  [[ready]] has no active line to follow, as [[start]]
does, and the parents themselves are in the record for a script that
wants them all.  Two encodings of it
are needed.  A person reads titles, so the table shows them joined by
[[>]], the separator the shell prompt already uses for the same chain
of ids (\cref{prompt}); it is ASCII, so it reads the same in every
terminal and every CSV consumer.  A script wants to act on an
ancestor, and a title can itself contain the separator, so the record
carries the path as a list of id and title pairs, from which the line
of titles is derived.  The line is therefore the lossy form, and JSON
the lossless one: a script that needs to know which ancestor a title
belongs to reads JSON.

<<constants>>=
TODO_PATH_SEPARATOR = " > "
<<helpers>>=
def todo_path(todos, item, by_id=None):
    """Return the ancestors of `item` as `{id, title}` dicts.

    The root comes first; a top-level item has an empty path.
    `by_id` may pass a prebuilt map from id to item.
    """
    return [
        {"id": ancestor.id, "title": ancestor.title}
        for ancestor in ranking_path(todos, item, by_id=by_id)
    ]


def format_todo_path(path, others=0):
    """Return the titles of `path` as one line, root first,
    with ` (+N)` for `others` other places."""
    line = TODO_PATH_SEPARATOR.join(
        step["title"] for step in path or []
    )
    if others:
        line += f" (+{others})"
    return line
@

Let's verify both encodings on a three-level chain:

<<test functions>>=
def test_todo_path_lists_ancestors_root_first():
  """The path names the ancestors, root first, and none at the top."""
  todos = [
    TodoItem(id=1, title="Course X"),
    TodoItem(id=2, title="Lab 2", parents=[1]),
    TodoItem(id=3, title="Grade", parents=[2]),
  ]
  path = todo_path(todos, todos[2])
  assert path == [
    {"id": 1, "title": "Course X"},
    {"id": 2, "title": "Lab 2"},
  ]
  assert format_todo_path(path) == "Course X > Lab 2"
  assert todo_path(todos, todos[0]) == []
