The record [[ready]] serializes is [[ls]]'s record
(\cref{sec:todo-ls-record}) with two derived values added.  They go
where [[ls_record]] puts its own derived values, after the stored
fields that existed when the computed columns did and before the ones
added since.  Appending them at the very end instead would let every
new stored field push them one column to the right, which in the
headerless pipe form, read by position, is a silent change of meaning;
passing them to [[ls_record]] as [[computed]] keeps [[path]] and
[[ready]] in their columns as the late stored fields grow.  The
[[ready]] field is
[[is_ready]]'s verdict on the row.  Without [[--all]] it is always
true, which looks redundant until one notices what it buys: under
[[--all]] it is how a script tells the parents that the flag let in
from the items it can start.

<<helpers>>=
def ready_record(todos, item, now, tracked_times, show_prio,
                 by_id=None, children=None):
    """Return the dictionary `todo ready` serializes for `item`.

    This is the `todo ls` record with `path`, the item's ancestors
    as `{id, title}` dicts, and `ready`, whether the item is ready
    by `is_ready`, among its computed columns, ahead of the stored
    fields `ls` lists last.  `by_id` and `children` may pass
    prebuilt maps from id to item and from parent id to children.
    """
    return ls_record(
        item, now, tracked_times, show_prio,
        computed={
            "path": todo_path(todos, item, by_id),
            "ready": is_ready(todos, item, children=children),
        },
    )
@

\paragraph{The options}

All but two options are [[ls]]'s option objects, so the flags, their
help and their configuration keys are the same.  The row cap is one
exception and gets a key of its own: a list of things to start is
read differently from an overview, and one may well want the overview
long and the choice short.  Its fallback is [[ls]]'s cap rather than a
number of our own, so someone who configured [[todo.ls.count]] and
never heard of [[ready]] gets the cap they chose.  [[--all]] is the
other, because its meaning differs from [[ls]]'s.  What [[ls]] has and
[[ready]] lacks is the tree and status options---[[--recursive]],
[[--flat]], [[--status]] and [[--done]]---since a ready list is flat,
and readiness decides the status.

<<configuration>>=
TODO_READY_COUNT_CONFIG = "todo.ready.count"
DEFAULT_READY_COUNT = default(
    TODO_READY_COUNT_CONFIG, DEFAULT_LS_COUNT, int)
<<argument and option definitions>>=
ready_all_opt = typer.Option(
    "--all", "-a",
    help="Also list pending items whose sub-items are still open")
ready_count_opt = typer.Option(
    "-n",
    help="Limit output to top N items; 0 shows all; "
         "defaults to todo.ls.count. "
         + config_hint("todo.ready.count"))
@

\paragraph{The command}

The body has [[ls]]'s shape and reuses its chunks where the variables
mean the same thing: [[-n 0]] is lifted to no cap, the tracked time is
queried for the rows in [[filtered]], and the format is resolved from
the terminal.  What differs is the selection, and that every format
sorts and caps the same list, since there is no tree to cap by roots.

<<ready command>>=
@cli.command(name="ready")
def ready(
    label_filter: Annotated[list[str] | None,
                            label_filter_arg] = None,
    include_parents: Annotated[bool, ready_all_opt] = False,
    count: Annotated[int | None,
                     ready_count_opt] = DEFAULT_READY_COUNT,
    who_filter: Annotated[str | None,
                          ls_who_opt] = DEFAULT_LS_WHO,
    output_format: Annotated[LsFormat,
                             ls_format_opt] = DEFAULT_LS_FORMAT,
    show_prio: Annotated[bool, ls_prio_opt] = False,
    output: Annotated[str | None,
                      ls_output_opt] = None,
):
    """List the items that are ready to work on, by priority.

    An item is ready when it is pending and has no open sub-items.
    Every depth is listed flat, in the order `todo ls` uses, with
    the path of each item's ancestors.  Pass `--all` to also list
    pending items whose sub-items are open.

    Labels, `--who` (default $USER, `''` for all), `-n` (`0` for
    all), `--format`, `--prio` and `--output` work as in `todo ls`.
    The table and CSV show the path as titles; `--format json` gives
    it losslessly, as the ids and titles of the ancestors.
    """
    _, todos = load_todos()
    by_id = {t.id: t for t in todos}
    children = children_by_parent(todos)

    <<treat [[-n 0]] as unlimited>>
    <<select the ready items>>
    <<query tracked time>>

    <<determine effective format>>

    now = datetime.datetime.now()
    ranked = sort_todos_for_display(filtered, now)
    shown = ranked[:count]
    if effective_format == "json":
        <<output the ready json>>
    elif effective_format == "rich":
        <<output the ready rich table>>
    else:
        <<output the ready csv>>
@

The two maps built first, from id to item and from parent to
children, are what keeps the command linear: readiness is asked of
every item and the path of every row, and both would otherwise scan
the whole list each time.

The selection is readiness first, then [[ls]]'s filters.  The order
matters for correctness and not only for speed: readiness looks at
\emph{all} of an item's children, so it has to be asked of the whole
list, while the filters only decide which of the ready items to show.

<<select the ready items>>=
filtered = filter_by_labels_and_who(
    [
        t for t in todos
        if is_ready(todos, t, allow_open_children=include_parents,
                    children=children)
    ],
    label_filter,
    who_filter,
)
@

The three renderers follow [[ls]]'s.  JSON and the delimited formats
encode the record, the latter through [[ls]]'s CSV chunks, so the path
reaches a CSV cell as its line of titles (\cref{sec:todo-ls-csv}).
An empty listing prints an empty JSON array and nothing as CSV, as [[ls]]
does.

<<output the ready json>>=
import json as json_mod

print(json_mod.dumps(
    [
        ready_record(todos, t, now, tracked_times, show_prio,
                     by_id, children)
        for t in shown
    ],
    indent=2,
))
<<output the ready csv>>=
if not shown:
    return
records = [
    ready_record(todos, item, now, tracked_times, show_prio,
                 by_id, children)
    for item in shown
]
<<choose the csv dialect>>
<<write the records as csv>>
<<deliver the csv text>>
@

The table is [[ls]]'s table with a Path column appended.  Appending,
rather than placing it beside the title, keeps every column where a
reader of [[ls]] expects it.  The table can append where the record
cannot, because it shows none of the late stored fields that the
record keeps behind the path.  The path is user-authored text like
the title, so it is wrapped in [[Text]] for the same reason.  An empty
table says so, and a capped one says how much it hid, both as [[ls]]
does.

<<output the ready rich table>>=
from rich.console import Console
from rich import box
from rich.table import Table
from rich.text import Text

if not shown:
    typer.echo("No ready todos found.")
    return

console = Console()
<<create the rich table with [[ls]]'s columns>>
table.add_column("Path")
for item in shown:
    table.add_row(
        *ls_row_cells(item, now, tracked_times, show_prio),
        Text(format_todo_path(
            todo_path(todos, item, by_id),
            len(parents_of(item)) - 1,
        )),
    )
console.print(table)
hidden = len(ranked) - len(shown)
<<show the truncation hint below the table>>
@

