\subsection{The [[view]] Command}

The [[view]] command displays a single todo with all its details and
children in a readable format. This is the read-only counterpart to
the editor---you can see exactly what [[--edit]] would show, without
opening an editor.

When no ID is given, [[view]] shows the current active task---the one
you're currently working on.  In tmux that means the current pane or
window context.  If tmux is active but this pane/window is untracked,
there is no implicit active todo and the user must supply an ID.
Outside tmux it falls back to the worker's active-stack top.  This is
the natural question a returning user asks:
\enquote{What was I doing?}  The worker is the logged-in user unless
[[--who]] names another (\cref{sec:todo-acting-worker}), which is how
an agent asks the same question about its own work.

Because [[note]] and [[done]] follow the same convention, [[view]] now
uses the shared [[resolve_todo_id()]] helper rather than reimplementing
 the stack lookup inline.

\paragraph{Two readers, two renderings}

[[view]] has the same two audiences as the listings in
\cref{cli.utils.output}, and here the gap between them is widest.
Notes and descriptions are Markdown, so a person at a terminal is
best served by rendering them---and by a title that stands out, a
metadata block that lines up and a sub-item table---while an agent
running [[nytid todo view]] to plan its work wants the notes exactly
as written, ready to quote or extend with [[note]].
We therefore keep two renderers: a rich one for terminals and the
original plain one for everything else, chosen by [[isatty()]] as
[[ls]] does.
The plain renderer is deliberately the old output, byte for byte, so
that the switch changes nothing for pipes.

The rich renderer is also where [[rich]] gets imported, and only there:
\cref{sec:cli-import-cost} explains why the import must stay out of
module scope, and keeping it out of the plain branch too means an agent
calling [[view]] in a loop never pays for a library whose output it
does not want.

[[--format]] overrides the detection in either direction.
Forcing [[rich]] serves the user who pipes into [[less -R]] to read a
long note; forcing [[plain]] serves the one who wants to copy the
Markdown source out of a terminal.
Unlike [[ls]] there is no configuration key for it: the automatic
choice is right for both audiences, so the override is an occasional
tool rather than a preference.

<<constants>>=
class ViewFormat(str, Enum):
    """Output formats for ``todo view``."""
    auto = "auto"
    rich = "rich"
    plain = "plain"
@

<<argument and option definitions>>=
todo_id_arg = typer.Argument(
    help="Todo ID (default: current active todo)",
    autocompletion=complete_todo_ids)
view_format_opt = typer.Option(
    "--format", "-f",
    help="Output format: auto (rich on a terminal, "
         "plain otherwise), rich, plain")
@

\paragraph{Reading a whole group}

A sub-item's line carries its title and, for a direct child, the first
line of its notes.  That preview answers \enquote{what is under
here?}, which is what a parent is usually asked.  It does not answer
\enquote{what is this group about?}---the question put to a parent
whose dozen children each keep their details in their own notes---and
answering that one today costs a separate [[view]] per child.

[[--notes]] prints every visible sub-item's description and notes in
full, underneath its line.  It is a flag rather than the default
because the two questions want opposite amounts of text: a dozen
children with a screen of notes each would push the parent's own
metadata off the top of the terminal every time someone only wanted to
see what was left to do.  With the flag off the preview line is
exactly what it was.

The other half of reading a group is the finished work.  [[view]]
hides done sub-items, and [[done]] is the \emph{only} status it
hides---no other value is filtered---so the whole history of a group is
one flag away.  That flag already exists as [[--done]]; [[--all]] is a
second spelling of it rather than a second flag, because \enquote{all}
is the word that comes to mind beside [[--notes]] when what is wanted
is everything, and one switch under two names can never disagree with
itself.  Widening the set is all it does: it does not imply
[[--notes]], and the sub-item lines still carry the bracketed
[[done]] mark (green in the rich renderer), so finished children stay
distinguishable from the rest.

<<argument and option definitions>>=
view_notes_opt = typer.Option(
    "--notes",
    help="Show each sub-item's description and notes")
view_all_opt = typer.Option(
    "--all", "--done", "-d",
    help="Include done sub-items")
@

\paragraph{Listing what is underneath}

The item is the subject of [[view]], and its direct children are
what it is made of, so they are listed with it.  Below them the tree
is context rather than composition, and context that grows with the
item's importance: the parent of a term's work, opened for its own
notes, would otherwise show every level first and the notes
somewhere below.  [[--recursive]] lists every depth, and [[-r 1]]
one more level---the grandchildren---on the terms [[ls]] set
(\cref{sec:todo-depth-flag}): an optional number of levels, all of
them when it is left out, parsed by [[parse_depth]] and put in place
by [[RecursiveDepthCommand]].  The reader who wants the whole tree
every time sets [[todo.view.recursive]], and [[-r 0]] switches a
configured default off for one invocation.

<<configuration>>=
TODO_VIEW_RECURSIVE_CONFIG = "todo.view.recursive"
DEFAULT_VIEW_RECURSIVE = default(
    TODO_VIEW_RECURSIVE_CONFIG, 0, parse_depth)
<<argument and option definitions>>=
view_recursive_opt = typer.Option(
    "--recursive", "-r",
    metavar="[LEVELS]", parser=parse_depth,
    help="List sub-items this many levels below the children; "
         "bare -r lists every level. "
         + config_hint("todo.view.recursive"))
@

Let's verify that the key is advertised:

<<test functions>>=
def test_view_help_advertises_recursive_key():
    """--help shows the todo.view.recursive key."""
    result = runner.invoke(cli, ["view", "--help"])
    assert result.exit_code == 0
    rendered = re.sub(r"[\s\u2502]", "", result.output)
    assert "[config:todo.view.recursive]" in rendered
@

\paragraph{Bounding a busy group}

The flags above widen what [[view]] prints; nothing yet narrows it.
A parent that collects a sprint's worth of work has dozens of
children, and display order already puts the ones that matter first,
so printing the whole set spends the top of the terminal---where the
parent's own metadata was---on a tail nobody asked about.

[[-n]] caps the sub-items, and it caps them \emph{per parent} rather
than capping the flat list of rows the renderers walk.  A row cap
would spend its budget on whichever branch comes first: one child
with fifteen grandchildren would fill the listing on its own and its
siblings would never appear, which is the opposite of what someone
scanning a group wants.  Capping each level separately bounds the
listing without changing its shape---the first [[N]] children, and
under each of them the first [[N]] of its own children, at every
level---so what the reader gets is a sample of the whole group rather
than all of one corner of it.

<<argument and option definitions>>=
view_count_opt = typer.Option(
    "-n",
    help="Limit sub-items to the top N per parent; "
         "0 shows all. "
         + config_hint("todo.view.subitems"))
@

Five is a smaller default than [[ls]]'s ten because these lines are
already nested under an item the reader chose to open: [[view]] is
the detail of one todo, and five children still leave that detail on
the screen beside them.

The number is configurable for the reason every listing default is:
it is a property of the reader's terminal and habits, not of the
data.  Resolving it through [[default]]
(\cref{cli.utils.defaults.cache}) at import time---once per process,
from a cache that cannot write back---is also what puts the number in
[[--help]], since Typer reads the parameter's default when it builds
the help text and a value computed inside the command body would
leave the reader nothing to read.

<<configuration>>=
TODO_VIEW_SUBITEMS_CONFIG = "todo.view.subitems"
DEFAULT_VIEW_SUBITEMS = default(
    TODO_VIEW_SUBITEMS_CONFIG, 5, int)
@

\paragraph{[[-n 0]] means all of them here too}

The command line has no spelling for [[None]], so the integer a
reader reaches for to lift a cap is [[0]]---the one value slicing
reads as the exact opposite.  [[ls]] met that trap first and maps a
non-positive count onto [[None]] before any slicing happens; what
[[view]] uses below is that same chunk rather than a copy of it,
which works because both commands call the parameter [[count]].
Sharing it is what keeps [[todo ls -n 0]] and [[todo view -n 0]] from
ever drifting into meaning different things.

Both renderers show the same things, so the command first gathers
what there is to show---the metadata fields, the notes or GitHub
content, the visible sub-items---and only then renders it.
Sharing the gathered data rather than sharing chunks between the two
renderers is what keeps them from drifting apart: a field added to
[[<<collect metadata fields>>]] appears in both outputs, and neither
renderer can show something the other lacks.

<<view command>>=
@cli.command(cls=RecursiveDepthCommand)
def view(
    todo_id: Annotated[int | None,
                       todo_id_arg] = None,
    include_done: Annotated[bool,
                            view_all_opt] = False,
    show_notes: Annotated[bool,
                          view_notes_opt] = False,
    count: Annotated[int | None,
                     view_count_opt] = DEFAULT_VIEW_SUBITEMS,
    recursive: Annotated[int | None,
                         view_recursive_opt] = DEFAULT_VIEW_RECURSIVE,
    output_format: Annotated[ViewFormat,
                             view_format_opt] = ViewFormat.auto,
    who: Annotated[str, acting_who_opt] = default_username,
):
    """View a todo item with full details.

    If no ID given, shows the current active task of the
    worker given by --who.
    The item's children are listed; `-r` adds every level below
    them and `-r N` the next N.  Done sub-items are hidden; pass
    --done (or --all) to
    include them.  At most N sub-items are shown under each parent;
    `-n N` changes that bound and `-n 0` lifts it.
    Sub-items show a one-line note preview; pass --notes to
    print their descriptions and notes in full instead.
    Notes are rendered as Markdown on a terminal and printed as
    written when piped; --format rich or --format plain forces
    either.
    """
    _, todos = load_todos()

    todo_id = resolve_todo_id(
        todo_id, "view",
        who=who, todos=todos,
    )

    item = next(
        (t for t in todos if t.id == todo_id), None
    )
    if item is None:
        typer.echo(
            f"Error: todo #{todo_id} not found",
            err=True,
        )
        raise typer.Exit(1)

    <<decide whether to render richly>>
    <<collect metadata fields>>
    <<fetch GitHub content>>
    <<treat [[-n 0]] as unlimited>>
    <<collect visible sub-items>>
    if use_rich:
        <<render view with rich>>
    else:
        <<render view as plain text>>
@

<<decide whether to render richly>>=
if output_format == ViewFormat.auto:
    use_rich = sys.stdout.isatty()
else:
    use_rich = output_format == ViewFormat.rich
@

The metadata is an ordered list of label--value pairs.
Priority is the one field that is always present---an unranked item
says so explicitly, because a missing priority is the usual reason an
item sorts unexpectedly and the user should not have to infer it from
an absent line.

A done item also says when it was closed (\cref{sec:todo-closed}).
The field comes first, so that the rich grid puts it right under the
title line, which has just said that the item is done; the plain
renderer gives it a line of its own after the two shared ones.  The time
is shown to the minute, like the note headers
(\cref{sec:todo-note-headers}): the stored seconds and microseconds are
there for sorting, not for reading.  A value that does not parse, which
only a hand-edited file can hold, is shown as written rather than
hidden.

<<collect metadata fields>>=
fields = []
if item.closed:
    closed_at = naive_local_datetime(item.closed)
    fields.append((
        "Closed",
        closed_at.strftime(NOTE_TIME_FORMAT)
        if closed_at else str(item.closed),
    ))
if item.priority is not None:
    fields.append(("Priority", f"{item.priority:.1f}"))
else:
    fields.append(("Priority", "unset"))
if item.deadline:
    fields.append(("Deadline", str(item.deadline)))
if item.estimated:
    fields.append(("Est", f"{item.estimated:.1f}h"))
if item.labels:
    fields.append(("Labels", ", ".join(item.labels)))
if item.who:
    fields.append(("Worker", item.who))
if item.wd:
    fields.append(("WD", item.wd))
if item.worktree:
    fields.append(("Worktree", item.worktree))
if item.command:
    fields.append(("Command", item.command))
if item.github:
    gh = item.github
    fields.append((
        "GitHub",
        f"{gh['repo']}#{gh['number']} ({gh.get('type', 'issue')})",
    ))
@

