\paragraph{Where the item came from}%
\label{sec:todo-view-provenance}

An item records the todo whose work turned it up in
[[discovered_from]] (\cref{sec:todo-discovered-from}), and [[view]]
shows it with that todo's title: the
number alone would send the reader to a second [[view]] to find out
what it stands for.  The link outlives its target, since [[rm]] does
not scrub it, and then there is no title to show.  The number is still
worth showing, though.  IDs are never reused, so it identifies the
removed todo as well as it ever did, and \enquote{removed} explains
why viewing it fails.

The link is also read the other way.  The question a finished task
raises is what it left behind, and the items it turned up are
precisely the ones that are \emph{not} its children, so the sub-item
listing does not show them.  \enquote{Led to} lists them, in the order they
were created.  Finding them takes one pass over the todos [[view]] has
already loaded, which is cheap enough to do on every [[view]] rather
than behind a flag.

<<collect metadata fields>>=
if item.discovered_from is not None:
    source = next(
        (t for t in todos if t.id == item.discovered_from),
        None,
    )
    fields.append((
        "Discovered from",
        f"#{item.discovered_from} "
        + (source.title if source else "(removed)"),
    ))
discoveries = [t for t in todos if t.discovered_from == item.id]
if discoveries:
    fields.append((
        "Led to",
        ", ".join(f"#{t.id} {t.title}" for t in discoveries),
    ))
@

