For GitHub-linked items, we fetch the issue body and comments
directly from GitHub so the user always sees the latest content.
For local-only items, we display the stored [[notes]] field. This
design means GitHub is the source of truth for notes on linked
items---we never cache them locally, avoiding stale data.

The fetch normalises each comment into an (author, date, body) triple
so that neither renderer has to know the shape of the [[gh]] JSON.
A failed fetch is reported on stderr right here rather than by the
renderers: stderr is a separate stream, so the position only matters
on a terminal, and there a warning that the notes are missing reads
better above the todo than buried below its metadata.

<<fetch GitHub content>>=
gh_body = None
gh_comments = []
if item.github:
    try:
        gh = item.github
        if gh.get("type", "issue") == "pr":
            data = gh_fetch_pr(gh["repo"], gh["number"])
        else:
            data = gh_fetch_issue(gh["repo"], gh["number"])
        gh_body = data.get("body")
        gh_comments = [
            (
                comment.get("author", {}).get("login", "unknown"),
                comment.get("createdAt", "")[:10],
                comment.get("body", ""),
            )
            for comment in data.get("comments", [])
        ]
    except Exception as e:
        typer.echo(
            f"  (Could not fetch GitHub data: {e})",
            err=True,
        )
@

Children and grandchildren use the same display ordering as [[ls]]:
ranked items first, then unranked items in deadline order.  That keeps
[[view]] consistent with the rest of the interface while still showing
tasks that have not been numerically ranked yet.

Done sub-items are hidden by default for the same reason as in
[[ls]]---a long list of completed children clutters the view of
what remains to be done.  Passing [[--done]] includes them.  When
items are hidden we count them so the renderers can print a short
hint; without it a parent could appear childless even though it has
completed sub-items.

Ordering one parent's children and dropping the done ones is asked
for at every level of the walk below, and again for the branch under
a child the cap cut, so it becomes a helper instead of being written
out at each place.  It hands back the count of what it dropped alongside the
list, because the caller is the only one that knows whether those
drops belong in a hint the reader will see.

<<sub-item helpers>>=
def get_visible_sub_items(
    todos: list[TodoItem], parent_id: int,
    include_done: bool,
) -> tuple[list[TodoItem], int]:
    """Return one parent's shown children and its done count.

    The list holds the children of `parent_id` in display order,
    with done ones dropped unless `include_done`; the integer
    counts the ones that were dropped.
    """
    shown = []
    hidden_done = 0
    for child in sort_todos_for_display(
        children_of(todos, parent_id)
    ):
        if child.status == "done" and not include_done:
            hidden_done += 1
            continue
        shown.append(child)
    return shown, hidden_done
@

The result is a flat list of (depth, item) rows, children at depth
zero, grandchildren at depth one, and so on down, in the order they
are to appear.  Flattening here means each renderer is a single loop
with an indentation rule, instead of nested loops that would each
have to repeat the done-filtering---and the cap.

The two rules compose in one direction only: the done filter runs
first and the cap applies to what survives it.  Capping first would
let finished work spend the budget and push pending siblings out of a
listing whose whole purpose is to show what is left.
A done child still takes its subtree with it uncounted: it belongs to
a branch the user chose not to see, and [[--done]] is what asks for
it back.

Applying the cap again under each child that got through is what
makes [[-n]] a bound on the listing's shape rather than on its
length, and applying it at every level is what a recursion gives for
free: the walk under a child is the same walk, one level down.

A child the cap cut is worth more than one line to the reader:
[[-n 0]] would bring it back together with everything under it, so
the count charges the child and its whole visible subtree.  Charging
only the child would understate what the flag reveals; charging its
done descendants too would overstate it, since [[-n 0]] alone still
does not show those.  The size of that subtree is the same walk with
the cap lifted---one traversal, defined once, rather than a second
counting routine that could drift from it.

The complement is taken as the slice past [[len(shown)]] rather than
past [[count]], because [[count]] is [[None]] under [[-n 0]]---and
[[None]] means \emph{the whole list} on both sides of the colon, so
slicing from [[count]] would report every child as hidden at exactly
the moment nothing is.

The walk starts at the item the user asked to [[view]], which may lie
on a cycle of parents that a hand-edited file contains.  Like the tree
walk of [[ls]] (\cref{sec:todo-ls-walk}), it carries the ids on its
path and does not descend into one again, so the cycle ends where it
closes and an item below two parents still shows under each.  It also
shares [[ls]]'s rule for such an item: its subtree is listed at the
first appearance only.  A cut child's subtree is counted by a walk of
its own, as if nothing had been expanded yet, which can count a shared
item that the listing shows elsewhere; the hint is an estimate of what
[[-n 0]] adds, and this keeps it from depending on the order the
children were cut in.

The depth limit is the same rule as the cap, applied to levels
rather than siblings, and the two compose the same way: a level the
listing does not descend into is not charged to the cap either,
since [[-n 0]] would not show it.  What a cut child is charged is
therefore its subtree \emph{down to the limit}---the same walk
again, with the remaining depth.

<<sub-item helpers>>=
def get_visible_sub_item_rows(
    todos: list[TodoItem], parent_id: int,
    include_done: bool, count: int | None,
    max_depth: int | None = None, depth: int = 0,
    path: frozenset = frozenset(),
    expanded: set | None = None,
) -> tuple[list[tuple[int, TodoItem]], int, int]:
    """Return the shown subtree of `parent_id` as (depth, item) rows.

    Under each parent the first `count` visible children are kept
    (all of them when `count` is None), each followed by its own
    kept subtree one level deeper, down to `max_depth` (every
    level when None).  The two integers count the done items
    dropped and the items the cap left out, the latter including
    everything under a cut child that `-n 0` would show.  `path`
    holds the ids above `parent_id` in this walk, and a child among
    them closes a cycle and is left out; `expanded` collects the
    items whose children were listed, and one met again is listed
    without them.
    """
    rows = []
    hidden_by_cap = 0
    if expanded is None:
        expanded = set()
    expanded.add(parent_id)
    path = path | {parent_id}
    children, hidden_done = get_visible_sub_items(
        todos, parent_id, include_done
    )
    children = [c for c in children if c.id not in path]
    shown = children[:count]
    descend = max_depth is None or depth < max_depth
    for child in shown:
        rows.append((depth, child))
        if descend and child.id not in expanded:
            branch, dropped, cut = get_visible_sub_item_rows(
                todos, child.id, include_done, count,
                max_depth, depth + 1, path, expanded,
            )
            rows.extend(branch)
            hidden_done += dropped
            hidden_by_cap += cut
    for child in children[len(shown):]:
        hidden_by_cap += 1
        if descend:
            branch, _, _ = get_visible_sub_item_rows(
                todos, child.id, include_done, None,
                max_depth, depth + 1, path,
            )
            hidden_by_cap += len(branch)
    return rows, hidden_done, hidden_by_cap
@

Let's verify the walk on three generations: the cap applies under
every parent, a cut child is charged with its whole subtree, and
with the cap lifted a done child still takes its branch with it.

<<test functions>>=
def three_generation_items():
    """A root with three children, each with three children."""
    items = [
        TodoItem(id=1, title="Root", created="2026-02-20T10:00:00"),
    ]
    for c in (2, 3, 4):
        items.append(TodoItem(
            id=c, title=f"Child {c}", parents=[1],
            priority=float(5 - c), created="2026-02-20T10:00:00",
        ))
        for g in range(3):
            items.append(TodoItem(
                id=10 * c + g, title=f"Grandchild {c}.{g}",
                parents=[c], priority=float(3 - g),
                created="2026-02-20T10:00:00",
            ))
    return items


def test_get_visible_sub_item_rows_caps_every_level():
    """The per-parent cap applies at each depth; the cut is charged."""
    rows, hidden_done, hidden_by_cap = get_visible_sub_item_rows(
        three_generation_items(), 1, False, 2
    )
    assert [(d, t.id) for d, t in rows] == [
        (0, 2), (1, 20), (1, 21),
        (0, 3), (1, 30), (1, 31),
    ]
    assert hidden_done == 0
    assert hidden_by_cap == 1 + 1 + (1 + 3)


def test_get_visible_sub_item_rows_uncapped_skips_done_branch():
    """Without a cap every visible row comes back; a done child
    takes its children with it and is counted once."""
    items = three_generation_items()
    next(t for t in items if t.id == 3).status = "done"
    rows, hidden_done, hidden_by_cap = get_visible_sub_item_rows(
        items, 1, False, None
    )
    assert [(d, t.id) for d, t in rows] == [
        (0, 2), (1, 20), (1, 21), (1, 22),
        (0, 4), (1, 40), (1, 41), (1, 42),
    ]
    assert hidden_done == 1
    assert hidden_by_cap == 0


def test_get_visible_sub_item_rows_depth_limit():
    """With `max_depth` 0 only children are listed, and a cut child
    is charged alone, since the level below is not shown anyway."""
    rows, hidden_done, hidden_by_cap = get_visible_sub_item_rows(
        three_generation_items(), 1, False, 2, max_depth=0
    )
    assert [(d, t.id) for d, t in rows] == [(0, 2), (0, 3)]
    assert hidden_done == 0
    assert hidden_by_cap == 1
