    typer.echo("Notes:")
    print_markdown(item.notes, indent=2, terminal=False)
@

Only children, not grandchildren, get the first line of their notes as
a preview: at two levels down the extra line would read as a fourth
level of nesting.  Under [[--notes]] that restraint is beside the
point---the reader has asked for the text---so every row gets its
blocks, and the preview steps aside rather than repeating the first
line above them.

The first line of the notes is usually a note header
(\cref{sec:todo-note-headers}), which says who wrote and when but
nothing about the work.  The preview therefore skips header lines and
blank lines and shows the first line of text.  A header is recognized
by its whole shape, not just by an opening bracket, so a note of the
user's own that begins with a bracketed word or a Markdown link still
previews.

<<constants>>=
NOTE_HEADER_PATTERN = re.compile(
    r"\[\d{4}-\d{2}-\d{2} \d{2}:\d{2} [^\]\n]*\]"
)
<<helpers>>=
def note_preview(notes):
    """Return the first line of `notes` that is note text.

    Blank lines and note header lines are skipped; returns
    the empty string when no such line exists.
    """
    for line in notes.splitlines():
        if line.strip() and not NOTE_HEADER_PATTERN.fullmatch(
            line.strip()
        ):
            return line
    return ""
@

Let's verify that the preview finds the text under a header, that a
bracket of the user's own is text, and that [[NOTE_HEADER_PATTERN]]
recognizes the header [[append_note_to_item]] actually writes---the
two are written separately and must not drift apart:

<<test functions>>=
def test_note_preview_skips_headers():
  """The preview is the first line of text, not a header."""
  assert note_preview(
    "[2026-10-08 14:03 dan-claude]\nTests pass now.\nMore"
  ) == "Tests pass now."
  assert note_preview("[draft] my words") == "[draft] my words"
  assert note_preview("") == ""

  item = TodoItem(id=1, title="Task")
  append_note_to_item(item, "Hello", who="dan-claude")
  assert note_preview(item.notes) == "Hello"
