@

Each line also names the flag that lifts its own rule and no other,
which is the property that makes two hints better than one combined
total: a reader who wants the finished work back and a reader who
wants the rest of a busy group back need different flags, and a
single \enquote{$n$ sub-items hidden} could not tell them which.

The blocks are indented one level past the line they belong to, so the
nesting on the page matches the nesting in the tree, and they go
through [[print_markdown]] in the same verbatim form as the parent's
own notes: an agent reading this output means to quote or extend the
text, and a rendering would have cost it the [[#]] markers and list
punctuation it needs.  An empty description or an empty notes field
prints nothing at all rather than an empty heading.

<<print a sub-item's blocks as plain text>>=
if child.description:
    print_markdown(
        child.description, indent=len(indent) + 2,
        terminal=False,
    )
if child.notes:
    print_markdown(
        child.notes, indent=len(indent) + 2,
        terminal=False,
    )
