\paragraph{The rich renderer}

Every string that reaches rich here is wrapped in [[Text]] first.
Rich parses bare strings as console markup, in which anything in
square brackets---the bracketed status we print, or a title such as
\enquote{Fix [parser] crash}---is a style tag that silently disappears
from the output.
[[Text]] takes the string literally, which is the only safe treatment
for user-written titles and for the bracketed status we print.

<<render view with rich>>=
from rich import box
from rich.console import Console
from rich.padding import Padding
from rich.table import Table
from rich.text import Text

console = Console()
console.print(Text.assemble(
    (f"#{item.id} ", "cyan"),
    (item.title, "bold"),
    " ",
    (f"[{item.status}]",
     VIEW_STATUS_STYLES.get(item.status, "")),
))
<<print metadata as a grid>>
<<print description and notes richly>>
<<print sub-items richly>>
@

A two-column grid lines the values up under each other, which the
plain layout's shared lines cannot; the labels are dimmed so the values
carry the line.

<<print metadata as a grid>>=
grid = Table.grid(padding=(0, 2))
grid.add_column(style="dim")
grid.add_column()
for label, value in fields:
    grid.add_row(label, Text(value))
console.print(Padding(grid, (0, 0, 0, 2)))
