@

The rich renderer has two shapes for the sub-items, because a table is
the right container for one line each and the wrong one for a screen
each.  A table's columns are sized against every row, so a single
child with a long note squeezes every other row's title into a narrow
ribbon; and a cell holds a rendered Markdown block only by giving it
that column's width, which is not the width of the terminal the reader
is looking at.  So [[--notes]] drops the table and prints each
sub-item as a line followed by its blocks, and without it the table
stays exactly as it was.

<<print sub-items richly>>=
if rows:
    console.print()
    console.print(Text("Sub-items", style="bold"))
    if show_notes:
        <<print each sub-item with its blocks>>
    else:
        <<print sub-items as a table>>
if hidden_done:
    console.print(Text(
        f"  ({hidden_done} done sub-items hidden,"
        f" use --done to show)",
        style="dim",
    ))
if hidden_by_cap:
    console.print(Text(
        f"  ({hidden_by_cap} more sub-items hidden,"
        f" use -n 0 to show all)",
        style="dim",
    ))
if hidden_below:
    console.print(Text(
        f"  ({hidden_below} deeper sub-items hidden,"
        f" use --recursive to list)",
        style="dim",
    ))
@

The pair is repeated here rather than shared with the plain renderer
because the two hints differ in more than their text: both are dimmed
and wrapped in [[Text]], the treatment this renderer gives every
piece of commentary and every string that a user could have written a
bracket into.  What must not differ is the wording and the order,
which is why the two hints were added to both renderers in the same
change and are written in the same shape in both.

The table uses the same [[Table]] arguments as [[ls]]
(\cref{sec:todo-code-outline}), so the two commands look like parts of
one interface.
Depth becomes indentation of the title, the way [[ls]] indents its
tree, and the note preview gets a folding column so a long first line
wraps instead of being cut off.
The indentation is a [[Padding]] around the cell rather than spaces
in front of the title.  Titles routinely outrun the column, and when
[[rich]] wraps a cell it keeps leading spaces only on the first line;
a grandchild whose title wraps to four lines then shows three of them
flush with its parent, and once every title wraps the nesting
disappears from the table altogether---which is exactly how a capped
view of a busy item read at first.  A padding indents every wrapped
line, so the structure survives any terminal width; the [[--notes]]
renderer below indents the same way for the same reason.

<<print sub-items as a table>>=
table = Table(show_header=True, header_style="bold",
              box=box.SIMPLE, show_edge=False,
              pad_edge=False)
table.add_column("ID", justify="right", style="cyan")
table.add_column("Status")
table.add_column("Title")
table.add_column("Note", overflow="fold")
for (depth, child), place in zip(rows, row_places):
    mark = VIEW_STATUS_MARKS.get(child.status, child.status)
    note = ""
    if depth == 0:
        note = note_preview(child.notes)
    table.add_row(
        str(child.id),
        Text(mark, style=VIEW_STATUS_STYLES.get(child.status, "")),
        Padding(
            Text(child.title)
            + Text(f" {place}" if place else "", style="dim"),
            (0, 0, 0, 2 * depth),
        ),
        Text(note),
    )
console.print(Padding(table, (0, 0, 0, 2)))
