\paragraph{Testing the sub-item cap}

The cap has two visible effects---rows that are missing and a line
that says how many---and the second is the one worth pinning down,
because a wrong count is worse than no hint at all: it tells the
reader there is nothing more to see when there is.  So every test
below asserts the number, not just the presence of the line.

A parent with seven children is the smallest case that shows the
default doing something.  The same invocation is also where we check
that [[--help]] advertises the number, since a configurable default
the user cannot read is one they have to guess at.

<<integration tests>>=
def test_view_caps_sub_items_at_the_default(temp_todo_dir):
    """Five children are shown by default, and the rest counted."""
    save_todos(9, [
        TodoItem(
            id=1, title="Parent task",
            created="2026-02-20T10:00:00",
        ),
    ] + [
        TodoItem(
            id=n, title=f"Child {n}", parents=[1],
            priority=float(10 - n),
            created="2026-02-20T10:00:00",
        )
        for n in range(2, 9)
    ])

    result = runner.invoke(cli, ["view", "1"])

    assert result.exit_code == 0
    assert "Child 2" in result.stdout
    assert "Child 6" in result.stdout
    assert "Child 7" not in result.stdout
    assert "Child 8" not in result.stdout
    assert "2 more sub-items hidden" in result.stdout

    usage = runner.invoke(cli, ["view", "--help"])
    assert usage.exit_code == 0
    assert "[default: 5]" in " ".join(usage.stdout.split())
