\subsection{Merging Incremental Label Edits}

The [[--add-label]]/[[--remove-label]] flags need a single, well-defined
merge rule, and the same rule serves [[track edit]].  Rather than duplicate
it in both commands, we import [[apply_label_edits]] from the shared
\texttt{cli.utils.labels} module (\cref{cli.utils.labels}), which documents
the merge rule and its edge cases.  We translate its [[ValueError]] (same
label added and removed) into a non-zero exit and surface its warnings on
stderr.

\subsection{The [[edit]] Command}

Editing updates specific fields of an existing todo. The [[--edit]]
/ [[-E]] flag opens the interactive editor with the todo rendered as
markdown (using [[render_todo_markdown]]). After editing, the parser
reconciles changes: existing children are matched by title to preserve
their IDs and status, new headings become new children, and removed
headings are deleted.

Like [[view]], [[note]], and [[done]], [[edit]] treats an explicit ID
as authoritative but otherwise falls back to the current active todo.
The contrast between the two forms is the key rule:
[[todo edit 7 --title "Updated title"]] edits item~7 even if another
todo is active, while [[todo edit --title "Updated title"]] edits the
current active task.  This keeps the common
\enquote{resume what I was working on} workflow short without removing
the explicit escape hatch.

Without [[-E]], the command updates individual fields directly.  We
reuse the same context-capture options as [[add]]: [[--who]] changes
ownership, [[--wd]] stores an explicit working directory,
[[--worktree]] stores the git worktree name (an empty string clears
it), [[--env]] refreshes the stored environment,
[[--here]] captures the current directory and environment, and
[[--command]] updates the default command to launch when the task is
started.  If any of those three remain unset on the item, runtime lookup
still checks [[todo.worker.<worker>.wd]],
[[todo.worker.<worker>.env]], and
[[todo.worker.<worker>.command]].

An empty [[--command ""]] clears the stored command, the same way an
empty [[--worktree]] clears the worktree.  There is no other way to
say \enquote{stop launching this}: the pass-through form needs at
least one word, and [[resolve_command_argv]] in the [[track]] module
rejects an empty string for exactly that reason.  So [[edit]] answers
the question before asking the resolver: a blank value means clear,
anything else is parsed as before.  The cleared item falls back to
the worker default at runtime
(\cref{todo:worker-defaults}); to run nothing even then, use
[[start --no-command]].  [[add]] keeps
rejecting an empty command, since a new item has nothing to clear.

Labels deserve a finer instrument than [[--label]].  [[--label]]/[[-l]]
\emph{replaces} the whole set, so adding one label to an item that already
has five means re-typing all six.  For incremental edits we add
[[--add-label]]/[[-a]] and [[--remove-label]]/[[-r]], each repeatable.
These are \emph{relative} to the item's current labels, so scripts can add
or drop a single label without knowing the rest.  Because [[-l]] is
absolute and [[-a]]/[[-r]] are relative, combining them is contradictory
and we reject it; [[-a]] and [[-r]] may be combined as long as no label
appears in both.  Adding a label already present or removing one that is
absent is treated as an idempotent no-op that warns on stderr but still
succeeds, which keeps the flags safe to run repeatedly from automation.

