\paragraph{Moving to another parent}%
\label{sec:todo-edit-parent}

The [[--parent]] flag moves an item in the hierarchy.  Items are
accidentally created as children when the user runs [[todo add]]
while another item is on the active stack --- the add command
automatically parents new items to the active one.  Without a way to
reparent, the only fix would be to delete and re-add the item,
losing its creation timestamp and any notes.

We use [[0]] as a sentinel for \enquote{promote to top-level}
because valid IDs always start from [[1]], so [[0]] is unambiguous;
it is the id the top level has among parent ids, [[ROOT_ID]]
(\cref{sec:todo-parents}).  Passing a positive integer instead
reassigns the item under a different parent.

Either way [[--parent]] replaces the item's whole list of parents
(\cref{sec:todo-parents-field}): the item ends up in exactly the one
place named.  With one parent per item that is what a move always
meant.  An item with several places (\cref{sec:todo-edit-places}),
though, loses all the others, which the user may not have had in mind, so the
command names the places the item left, as in \enquote{\#5 is no
longer under \#1 or at the top level}.  It says so after the change
is stored, so that a move refused under the lock reports nothing it
did not do.

<<edit via cli flags>>=
left_places = []
if parent is not None:
    if parent == ROOT_ID:
        new_parents = [ROOT_ID]
    else:
        new_parent = next(
            (t for t in todos if t.id == parent),
            None,
        )
        if new_parent is None:
            typer.echo(
                f"Error: parent #{parent} not found",
                err=True,
            )
            raise typer.Exit(1)
        require_acyclic_move(todos, item, parent)
        new_parents = [parent]
    left_places = [
        p for p in item.parents if p not in new_parents
    ]
    item.parents = new_parents
@

A move must not make an item its own ancestor.  Both
[[edit 5 --parent 5]] and a move of~5 under one of its own
descendants would.  The
moved subtree would then be a cycle that no root leads into:
[[get_visible_roots]] never lists any of it, so the item and
everything below it would vanish from [[ls]], and a walk down the
tree without a guard, such as the sub-item listing of [[view]], would
never end.  We refuse such a move.  The question is whether the item
lies at or above the new parent, which is what [[ancestors_of]]
answers, along every parent (\cref{sec:todo-parents}).  The check
runs on the snapshot, so that it fails before any prompt, and again
under the lock, because another command may have moved the new parent
under the item in between.

The places are worded for a reader: the parents that are items by
their ids, the root as \enquote{the top level}.

<<helpers>>=
def describe_places(parent_ids):
    """Return `parent_ids` as words, such as
    `under #1 or #3 or at the top level`."""
    under = [f"#{p}" for p in parent_ids if p != ROOT_ID]
    places = []
    if under:
        places.append("under " + " or ".join(under))
    if ROOT_ID in parent_ids:
        places.append("at the top level")
    return " or ".join(places)
<<helpers>>=
def require_acyclic_move(todos, item, parent_id):
    """Exit with an error if `parent_id` is `item` or below it.

    Making such an item `item`'s parent would make `item` its
    own ancestor.  Inside a `mutate_todo_state` callback the
    exit also abandons the write.
    """
    parent = next(
        (t for t in todos if t.id == parent_id), None
    )
    if parent is None:
        return
    if parent.id == item.id or item.id in {
        a.id for a in ancestors_of(todos, parent)
    }:
        typer.echo(
            f"Error: cannot move #{item.id} under "
            f"#{parent_id}, which is #{item.id} itself "
            "or below it",
            err=True,
        )
        raise typer.Exit(1)
@

