@

\paragraph{Adding and removing places}%
\label{sec:todo-edit-places}
[[--parent]] replaces every place an item has.  Giving an item a
second place, or taking one away, needs a finer instrument, for the
reason labels have [[--add-label]] and [[--remove-label]] next to
[[--label]]: [[--add-parent]] and [[--remove-parent]]
change the list relative to what it is, and can be repeated.
[[--add-parent 0]] makes the item top-level as well, and
[[--remove-parent 0]] takes it off the top level.  Removing the last
parent that is an item leaves the item at the top level, which is
where an item with no parent is (\cref{sec:todo-parents-field}).

The relative flags contradict [[--parent]], which says what the whole
list should be, so combining them is refused; and an id both added and
removed is refused as well.  Adding a place the item already has, or
removing one it does not have, is a no-op that warns, as for labels.
Each added parent must exist and must not lie below the item, the
check [[--parent]] makes (\cref{sec:todo-edit-parent}).  The ranking
context the item had before the change is kept, since the priority
below depends on whether the change took it away.

<<edit via cli flags>>=
added_places, removed_places = [], []
if add_parent or remove_parent:
    <<apply relative parent edits>>
@

<<apply relative parent edits>>=
for flag, used in [
    ("--add-parent", add_parent), ("--remove-parent", remove_parent),
]:
    reject_conflicting_flags({
        "--parent": parent is not None, flag: bool(used),
    })
for parent_id in add_parent or []:
    if parent_id == ROOT_ID:
        continue
    if not any(t.id == parent_id for t in todos):
        typer.echo(
            f"Error: parent #{parent_id} not found", err=True,
        )
        raise typer.Exit(1)
    require_acyclic_move(todos, item, parent_id)
try:
    new_parents, warnings = apply_parent_edits(
        item.parents, add_parent or [], remove_parent or [],
    )
except ValueError as exc:
    typer.echo(f"Error: {exc}", err=True)
    raise typer.Exit(1)
for warning in warnings:
    typer.echo(f"Warning: {warning}", err=True)
old_context = ranking_context(todos, item)
added_places = [p for p in new_parents if p not in item.parents]
removed_places = [p for p in item.parents if p not in new_parents]
item.parents = new_parents
@

The edit itself is a function of the list alone, so that the commit can
apply it again to the list as it is stored then.  Removed places go
first and added ones are appended, keeping the order of the places the
item keeps.  The warnings name the places in words, with
[[describe_places]].

<<helpers>>=
def apply_parent_edits(parents, add, remove):
    """Return `parents` with `add` added and `remove` removed,
    and the warnings for no-op edits.

    Raises `ValueError` when an id is both added and removed.
    An empty result is the top level, `[ROOT_ID]`.
    """
    both = sorted(set(add) & set(remove))
    if both:
        raise ValueError(
            f"#{both[0]} cannot be both added and removed"
        )
    warnings = [
        f"already {describe_places([p])}"
        for p in add if p in parents
    ] + [
        f"not {describe_places([p])}"
        for p in remove if p not in parents
    ]
    kept = [p for p in parents if p not in remove]
    new = normalize_parents(kept + [p for p in add if p not in kept])
    if ROOT_ID in remove and new == [ROOT_ID]:
        warnings.append("the top level is the only place left")
    return new, warnings
@

\paragraph{Ranking in a changed set of places.}
The item keeps one number, the highest any of its places gives it
(\cref{sec:ranking-context}), and a change of places changes what that
is.

An added place can only raise the number.  The item is ranked in it as
[[add]] ranks a new item in each of its places: through a stand-in
whose only parent is the added place, by the interactive search or by
[[--top]] or [[--bottom]], and the higher of the two numbers is kept.
[[--prio]] is different: it names the number, here as everywhere in
[[edit]], and the number is stored as given; if another place keeps
the item higher, the rule lifts it when it is stored, and the command
says which number it stored, so that no input is dropped without a
word.  A parent ranked at or below the item's number cannot
raise it, so the question is skipped: B at~4.5 under A gains a place
under D at~3.0 without being asked anything.  [[--keep-prio]] keeps the
number as it is, but, as with [[--parent]], only when it is a position
in the new place: it may lie inside the parent's range or above it,
not below it, where the item would rank under the parent's own
children.

A removed place matters only if it is the one that gave the number,
the ranking context.  The number then belongs to a range the item has
left and is wrongly high, so the item is placed again, the way
[[--parent]] places a moved item: interactively, or by [[--top]],
[[--bottom]] or [[--prio]], unless [[--keep-prio]] keeps the number.
It is placed in all the places it has left, from the highest down, as
[[add]] places a new item (\cref{sec:place-in-places}), and keeps the
highest number.  Asking only the first remaining parent would be
asking the wrong question whenever another place ranks higher: the
answer would be overruled by the rule at once, and the item lifted to
the bottom of the higher place, wherever the user had put it.  A place
added in the same command is one of those places, so it needs no
question of its own.  Removing any other place than the ranking context
leaves the number alone.

<<rank the item in its changed places>>=
if top:
    placement = PLACE_TOP
elif bottom:
    placement = PLACE_BOTTOM
else:
    placement = None
lost_rank = (
    old_context in removed_places and item.priority is not None
)
if lost_rank and not keep_prio:
    <<place the item again where it is still ranked>>
else:
    <<rank the item in each added place>>
@

<<place the item again where it is still ranked>>=
if explicit_priority is not None:
    item.priority = explicit_priority
else:
    new_priority = place_in_places(
        todos, item, item.parents, placement,
    )
    if new_priority is None:
        typer.echo(
            f"#{todo_id} is now unranked: none of its places "
            "is ranked"
        )
    item.priority = new_priority
