A priority is a position in one list, not a property of the item.  The
number~[[5.0]] means \enquote{above the [[4.5]] next to me}; carried
into a different set of siblings it means whatever those siblings
happen to be numbered, which is to say nothing.  Moving an item with
[[--parent]] therefore invalidates its priority, and leaving the stale
number behind made every move cost a follow-up [[reprioritize]] the
user had to remember.  We re-rank as part of the move instead, running
the same binary search [[add]] runs, so that the default outcome of a
move is a placed item rather than an arbitrary one.

The placement flags say what to do instead of asking.  [[--keep-prio]]
keeps the stored number---the old behaviour, and still the right answer
when the caller has already decided where the item lands, as a script
moving items it just numbered has.  [[--prio]] sets the number
outright; [[--top]] and [[--bottom]] derive it from the new siblings.
All three also work \emph{without} [[--parent]], where they re-rank the
item among the siblings it already has, which is what makes [[edit]]
enough for the whole flow of moving and ranking a batch.
[[--keep-prio]] without [[--parent]] asks for exactly what would happen
anyway, so it is a no-op rather than an error.

