<<refuse a kept number outside the new parent's range>>
rerank = top or bottom or (
    parent is not None and not keep_prio
)
if added_places or removed_places:
    <<rank the item in its changed places>>
elif explicit_priority is not None:
    item.priority = explicit_priority
elif rerank:
    <<place the edited item among its siblings>>

<<commit the flag edits under the lock>>
typer.echo(f"Updated todo #{todo_id}: {item.title}")
if (
    explicit_priority is not None
    and kept_priority is not None
    and kept_priority > explicit_priority
):
    typer.echo(
        f"#{todo_id} has priority "
        f"{format_stored_priority(kept_priority)}: "
        f"--prio {explicit_priority:g} lies below the range of "
        "another of its places"
    )
if left_places or removed_places:
    typer.echo(
        f"#{todo_id} is no longer "
        f"{describe_places(left_places or removed_places)}"
    )
if added_places:
    typer.echo(
        f"#{todo_id} is now also {describe_places(added_places)}"
    )
@

A kept number is only a position if it falls inside the new parent's
child range (\cref{sec:child-priority-helpers}).  Outside it, the item
would sort above its new parent or below the parent's next sibling,
away from the rest of its family, and the subtree helpers could not
repair that: the range the item should live in is exactly the one it
is not in.  So [[--keep-prio]] under a ranked parent is honoured only
when the number lies strictly inside that range, and otherwise refused
with the flags that would place the item instead.  Moving to the top
level keeps any number, since nothing bounds that level; the sibling
the item lands below is compressed as for any placement
(\cref{sec:sibling-above-squeezed}).  An explicit [[--prio]] is held
to the same rule, here as in [[add]], since a number the user typed is
no more a position in the wrong range than one the item carried along.
For an item with several places the rule is [[add]]'s too: the number
must lie in the range of one of the places the item has after the
edit, or anywhere if the top level is one of them
([[refuse_number_outside_places]]).  It is checked once, here, for a
plain [[--prio]] and for one given with [[--add-parent]] or
[[--remove-parent]] alike, so that the three cannot disagree about what
fits.

<<refuse a kept number outside the new parent's range>>=
if keep_prio and parent and item.priority is not None:
    refuse_number_outside_range(
        todos, get_parent(todos, item), item.priority,
        f"priority {item.priority:g} of #{todo_id}",
        "--top, --bottom or --prio",
    )
if explicit_priority is not None:
    refuse_number_outside_places(
        todos,
        [
            next((t for t in todos if t.id == p), None)
            for p in item.parents
        ],
        explicit_priority,
        f"--prio {explicit_priority:g}", "--top or --bottom",
    )
@

Everything above changed the snapshot copy [[item]], and the
placement search may have prompted the user on the way.  The commit
replays those changes onto the item as it is stored now
(\cref{sec:todo-read-modify-write}), so fields the flags did not
mention keep any change another process made in the meantime.  A new
parent is checked again for the reason [[add]] checks its parent: it
may have been removed while the user was answering questions.

[[--add-label]] and [[--remove-label]] are the exception to the
replay.  They say how to change the labels, not what the labels should
be, so the commit applies them again to the fresh labels and keeps the
label list out of the replay; otherwise a label another process added
meanwhile would vanish.  [[--label]] replaces the set outright and is
replayed like any other field.  [[--add-parent]] and
[[--remove-parent]] are relative in the same way and get the same
treatment: the parents are edited again from the fresh list, so that a
place another process gave the item meanwhile survives.  Every parent
the item ends up with is checked again, for existence and for a cycle,
whenever the list changes.

A new number or a new parent gives the item a new child range, and its
descendants move into it (\cref{sec:subtree-follows-parent}); the
sibling it lands below is compressed into the range left above it
(\cref{sec:sibling-above-squeezed}).  Here the replay, not a single
assignment, changes the number, so instead of
[[set_priority_with_subtree]] we read the item's range and place before
the replay ourselves and hand them to [[settle_placement]] after it,
from the stored state as the other commands do.  A [[--parent]] move
changes the range even when [[--keep-prio]] keeps the number, since
the range is bounded by the new siblings; the subtree follows then
too, so that it stays between the item and the sibling below it at its
new place.  An edit that touches neither the number nor the parent
leaves every range as it was, and nothing moves.

<<commit the flag edits under the lock>>=
def commit_flag_edits(next_id, todos, stack):
    fresh = require_todo(todos, todo_id)
    relative_parents = bool(add_parent or remove_parent)
    if relative_parents:
        new_parents, _ = apply_parent_edits(
            fresh.parents, add_parent or [], remove_parent or [],
        )
    elif item.parents != before["parents"]:
        new_parents = list(item.parents)
    else:
        new_parents = list(fresh.parents)
    if new_parents != fresh.parents:
        for parent_id in new_parents:
            if parent_id != ROOT_ID:
                require_todo(todos, parent_id)
                require_acyclic_move(todos, fresh, parent_id)
    old_range = child_priority_range_or_none(todos, fresh)
    old_place = (fresh.priority, list(fresh.parents))
    relative_labels = bool(add_label or remove_label)
    replay_item_edits(
        fresh, before, item,
        skip=(
            ({"labels"} if relative_labels else set())
            | ({"parents"} if relative_parents else set())
        ),
    )
    if relative_labels:
        fresh.labels, _ = apply_label_edits(
            fresh.labels, add_label or [], remove_label or [],
        )
    fresh.parents = new_parents
    settle_placement(todos, fresh, old_range, old_place)
    return next_id, todos, stack, fresh.priority


*_, kept_priority = mutate_todo_state(commit_flag_edits)
