A move is where the flags earn their place.  Without one, the item is
re-ranked among its new siblings by the interactive search; with
[[--keep-prio]] it arrives carrying the number it had, which is the
behaviour this command had before; and with [[--top]] or [[--bottom]]
it arrives at a known end of the new list.  In the fixture B's child
sits in the middle of B's range $(-4.0, 6.0)$, at~[[1.0]], and the
mover in the middle of A's, at~[[8.0]].  [[--top]], like a
\enquote{yes} to the search's only question, puts the mover halfway
between B's child and B, [[3.5]], and [[--bottom]] halfway between the
child and the floor of B's range, [[-1.5]]
(\cref{todo:binary-search}).  With [[--keep-prio]] the mover keeps
[[8.0]], which only the top level accepts: as a number in A's range it
lies above B.

