@

\subsubsection{Editing via the Editor}
\label{sec:edit-via-editor}

We render the todo as markdown, open the editor, parse the result,
and reconcile. Reconciliation matches existing children by title
(case-insensitive, whitespace-stripped) to preserve their IDs,
status, and tracked time. New headings create new children; removed
headings delete children.

The editor session lasts as long as the user likes, so the buffer is
rendered from the snapshot and applied to the stored state inside a
locked callback afterwards (\cref{sec:todo-read-modify-write}).  An
agent may well have added a note or a sub-item in the meantime, and
the buffer knows nothing of either.  We therefore remember every item
the buffer showed, the todo and its whole subtree, as [[shown]].  It
serves twice.  It is the \enquote{before} against which the user's
changes to each item are measured and replayed, and it tells a child
the user deleted from one that did not exist when the buffer was
rendered.

<<edit via editor>>=
<<refuse a buffer with shared sub-items>>
md = render_todo_markdown(item, todos)
shown = buffer_baseline(
    item, todos, parse_editor_content(md)
)
as_read = {
    t.id: item_fields(t)
    for t in [item, *descendants_of(todos, item.id)]
}
content = open_editor(md)
parsed = parse_editor_content(content)
if not parsed.title:
    typer.echo("No title provided.", err=True)
    raise typer.Exit(1)

<<echo parse warnings>>
<<update parent from parsed>>
<<commit the buffer under the lock>>
typer.echo(f"Updated todo #{todo_id}: {item.title}")
@

The buffer knows nothing of items with several parents.  For the edited
item itself that is harmless: the buffer has no key for parents, so its
places are left as they are, and its number is not in the buffer
either.  A shared item \emph{below} it is another matter.  The buffer
shows it as one heading among the children, and reconciliation treats
it as one: deleting the heading would remove the item from every one of
its places, not only this one, and the children's numbers are
redistributed over this parent's range, although the item may be ranked
under another (\cref{sec:ranking-context}).  Until reconciliation
learns to detach and to leave such items' numbers alone, the editor is
refused for an item with a shared item below it, with the flags that do
the same work named instead; nothing is lost, since nothing has been
typed yet.

<<refuse a buffer with shared sub-items>>=
shared = [
    d for d in descendants_of(todos, item.id)
    if len(parents_of(d)) > 1
]
if shared:
    typer.echo(
        f"Error: #{shared[0].id} below #{item.id} has several "
        "parents, which the editor cannot keep yet; edit the "
        "items with flags such as --title, --add-parent and "
        "--remove-parent instead",
        err=True,
    )
    raise typer.Exit(1)
@

The \enquote{before} must be the item as the \emph{unedited} buffer
would store it, not the item as it is stored.  Saving does not copy
the buffer verbatim: the parser strips the title and the notes, and
[[apply_metadata]] normalizes the front matter.  An item whose notes
end in a newline, as notes read from standard input usually do, would
otherwise look edited on every save merely because the buffer lost
that newline, and the replay would then overwrite a note that arrived
during the session.  [[buffer_baseline]] therefore saves the untouched
buffer onto copies of the shown items, through the same steps the real
save takes, and records the fields that results in.  Whatever the user
changed differs from that baseline; whatever they left alone does not,
however it was formatted in the store.  The fields as read are kept
too, in [[as_read]], so that the replay can still tidy a field nobody
else touched (\cref{sec:todo-read-modify-write}).

Sections are paired with items the way reconciliation pairs them, by
normalized title, so every item the buffer showed gets an entry.

<<helpers>>=
def buffer_baseline(item, todos, parsed):
    """Return what saving an untouched buffer would store.

    `parsed` is the parse of the buffer rendered from
    `item`.  Returns `item_fields` of copies of `item`
    and its shown descendants after the buffer's sections
    are applied to them, keyed by ID.
    """
    baseline = {}

    def settle(original, section):
        saved = TodoItem.from_dict(item_fields(original))
        saved.title = section.title
        saved.notes = section.notes
        apply_metadata(saved, section.metadata)
        baseline[original.id] = item_fields(saved)
        by_title = {
            c.title.strip().lower(): c
            for c in children_of(todos, original.id)
        }
        for child_section in section.children:
            child = by_title.get(
                child_section.title.strip().lower()
            )
            if child is not None:
                settle(child, child_section)

    settle(item, parsed)
    return baseline
@

The front matter is handed to [[apply_metadata]]
(\cref{sec:apply-metadata}), which owns the present-versus-absent rule
and the normalization of each key.  What stays here is what is not
metadata.  The title and the notes come from the document body rather
than from the front-matter block, and they are assigned
unconditionally, which is the opposite of the metadata rule and
deliberately so: the parser refuses a buffer with no title, and an
emptied body really does mean an emptied [[notes]], since there is no
key to delete or leave out.  These assignments go to the snapshot copy;
only the fields they actually change are carried over at commit time.

<<update parent from parsed>>=
item.title = parsed.title
item.notes = parsed.notes
apply_metadata(item, parsed.metadata)
@

The commit replays the parent's changes and then reconciles the
children against the fresh state.  Reconciliation creates children and
removes them, so it has to run inside the callback: the new IDs come
from the [[next_id]] the callback receives, and [[reconcile_children]]
reaches that [[next_id]] and [[todos]] through [[nonlocal]].

<<commit the buffer under the lock>>=
def commit_the_buffer(next_id, todos, stack):
    edited = require_todo(todos, todo_id)
    replay_item_edits(
        edited, shown[todo_id], item, as_read[todo_id]
    )
    <<reconcile children>>
    reconcile_children(edited, parsed.children)
    return next_id, todos, stack


mutate_todo_state(commit_the_buffer)
@

Reconciliation matches children by normalized title. Existing
children keep their ID, status, and tracked time. New headings get
fresh IDs. Priorities are distributed within the parent's context
range (using [[compute_child_priority_range]]) so that children
sort correctly in flat view, rather than using absolute sequential
values.  Unranked children are the exception: if a child already has no
priority, reconciliation keeps it that way instead of silently assigning
one.  New children only receive numeric priorities when the parent has a
numeric priority and the new child is entering the ranked subset.

Reconciliation has to recurse, because the buffer the user edited does.
[[render_child_markdown]] emits each child's own children at one deeper
heading level and [[parse_editor_content]] parses them back into
[[parsed_child.children]], so a reconciliation that walked only the
first level would show the user their grandchildren, accept their
edits, report success, and discard them.  Silently losing text the user
typed is the worst failure mode an editor round-trip can have.

We express the recursion as a nested \emph{function} rather than a
reusable chunk.  The level being reconciled is described entirely by
[[parent]] and [[parsed_children]]; making those parameters means a
recursive call cannot accidentally inherit the outer level's variables,
which is exactly the mistake a chunk reference would invite.  This also
mirrors [[create_children_from_parsed]] on the [[add]] side, which has
recursed from the start.

<<reconcile children>>=
def reconcile_children(parent, parsed_children):
    """Match parsed sections against a parent's children.

    Sections are matched to existing children by
    normalized title so that they keep their ID,
    status, and tracked time.  Unmatched sections
    become new children, unmatched children are
    removed with their subtrees, and each matched
    child is reconciled against its own sections.
    """
    nonlocal next_id, todos
    <<build existing children index>>
    <<distribute child priorities>>
    <<record the children's ranges before they move>>
    <<update or create each parsed child>>
    <<remove deleted children>>
    <<carry each child's subtree to its new range>>
    <<reconcile each child against its own sections>>
@

We normalize titles to lowercase with stripped whitespace so that
minor formatting changes in the editor do not accidentally create
duplicate children.

The titles a section is matched against are the ones the buffer
showed, not the ones stored now.  The buffer carries no IDs, so the
title is the only thing that connects a section to an item, and the
connection was made when the buffer was rendered.  If another process
renames a shown child during the session, matching on the stored title
would find no section for it: the child would be deleted as if the
user had removed its heading, and its unchanged section would come
back as a new child, losing the ID, the notes and the tracked time.
Keying a shown child by its title in [[shown]] keeps the connection;
the replay then leaves the new name alone, since the user did not
change the title in the buffer.  Children the buffer never showed are
keyed by their stored title, and a shown child wins a clash, because
the section was written about it.
<<build existing children index>>=
children = children_of(todos, parent.id)
existing_by_title = {
    c.title.strip().lower(): c
    for c in children if c.id not in shown
}
existing_by_title.update({
    shown[c.id]["title"].strip().lower(): c
    for c in children if c.id in shown
})
@

Priority distribution uses the parent's sibling-context range so
that ranked children sort correctly relative to other top-level items
when the list is shown flat.  Without this, all children of every
parent would receive priorities in [0, 1] and would interleave
arbitrarily.

Existing unranked children do not consume a numeric slot.  They stay in
the unranked tail until the user runs [[reprioritize]], while ranked or
new children still share the parent's numeric range.
<<distribute child priorities>>=
if parent.priority is None:
    ranked_titles = []
    priorities = []
else:
    ranked_titles = [
        parsed_child.title.strip().lower()
        for parsed_child in parsed_children
        if (
            parsed_child.title.strip().lower()
            not in existing_by_title
            or existing_by_title[
                parsed_child.title.strip().lower()
            ].priority is not None
        )
    ]
    if ranked_titles:
        prio_high, prio_low = compute_child_priority_range(
            todos, parent
        )
        priorities = distribute_priorities_in_range(
            len(ranked_titles), prio_high, prio_low,
        )
    else:
        priorities = []
priority_iter = iter(priorities)
@

For each section in the parsed document we either update the matching
existing child (preserving its ID and status) or create a new one.
Both branches end in [[apply_metadata]] (\cref{sec:apply-metadata}),
and the difference between them is only what the item holds before it
is called.  A matched child holds what it already had, so an absent key
keeps the value the user saw in the buffer.  A new child holds what it
inherits: the parent's labels, deadline and assignee, so that adding a
sub-task in the editor never silently leaves it unassigned or
undated---and then its own section overrides any of them.  Inheriting
in the constructor rather than through [[child_meta.get(key, default)]]
is what lets one helper serve both branches, and it puts the fallback
where a reader looks for it, next to the field it fills.

Routing both branches through the helper also settles a difference
that had no reason to exist: a matched child now takes [[github]] from
its section, which the parent has always done and no child ever did, so
linking a sub-item to an issue in the editor is no longer silently
discarded on save.  Both branches gain [[env]] for the same reason,
which no site read before.

Popping each match out of [[existing_by_title]] doubles as the
seen-marker: whatever is still in the index afterwards had no section
and was therefore deleted in the editor.

Each settled child is collected with its section, and its own
sections are reconciled only once the whole level is settled (below).

<<update or create each parsed child>>=
settled = []
for parsed_child in parsed_children:
    norm_title = parsed_child.title.strip().lower()
    existing_child = existing_by_title.get(norm_title)
    if (
        parent.priority is None
        or (
            existing_child is not None
            and existing_child.priority is None
        )
    ):
        prio = None
    else:
        prio = next(priority_iter, None)
    if existing_child is not None:
        child = existing_by_title.pop(norm_title)
        <<apply the section to the matched child>>
    else:
        now = datetime.datetime.now().isoformat()
        child = TodoItem(
            id=next_id,
            title=parsed_child.title,
            labels=list(parent.labels),
            priority=prio,
            status="pending",
            created=now,
            deadline=parent.deadline,
            parents=[parent.id],
            who=parent.who,
            notes=parsed_child.notes,
        )
        apply_metadata(child, parsed_child.metadata)
        todos.append(child)
        next_id += 1
        typer.echo(
            f"  Added sub-item #{child.id}: "
            f"{child.title}"
        )
    settled.append((child, parsed_child))
@

A matched child is updated the way the parent is: the section is
applied to a copy of the child as the untouched buffer would have
stored it, and only what the section changed is replayed onto the
stored child, so that a note
added to a sub-item during the session survives.  A child that matched
a section but was not shown was added by someone else under the same
title meanwhile; there is no earlier version to compare with, so the
section applies to it directly.
<<apply the section to the matched child>>=
shown_child = shown.get(child.id)
if shown_child is None:
    edited_child = child
else:
    edited_child = TodoItem.from_dict(
        copy.deepcopy(shown_child)
    )
edited_child.title = parsed_child.title
edited_child.notes = parsed_child.notes
edited_child.priority = prio
apply_metadata(edited_child, parsed_child.metadata)
if edited_child is not child:
    replay_item_edits(
        child, shown_child, edited_child,
        as_read[child.id],
    )
@

Children remaining in [[existing_by_title]] were not present in the
parsed document, meaning the user deleted their headings.  Their own
subtrees go with them: an item whose parent is gone is not hidden but
promoted to a visible root by [[get_visible_roots]], so leaving
grandchildren behind would move them into the main list instead of
removing them.  Only a child the buffer showed can have been deleted
in it, though.  A child that another process added while the editor
was open is missing from the buffer for a different reason, and
removing it would silently undo that process's work.
<<remove deleted children>>=
for removed in existing_by_title.values():
    if removed.id not in shown:
        continue
    doomed = {removed.id} | {
        d.id
        for d in descendants_of(todos, removed.id)
    }
    todos = [t for t in todos if t.id not in doomed]
    typer.echo(
        f"  Removed sub-item #{removed.id}: "
        f"{removed.title}"
    )
@


A child's own range is bounded by its siblings' numbers
(\cref{sec:child-priority-helpers}), so the level below can only be
placed once every child at this level has its final number.
Reconciling each child as soon as it was settled computed its range
against siblings still carrying their old numbers: a first child
moved from~4.9 to~4.5 next to a sibling about to go from~3.2 to~4.0
saw the range $(3.2, 4.5)$ and spread its children below where that
sibling would land.  We therefore settle the whole level and remove
what was deleted first, and only then descend.

The new numbers are also new child ranges.  The recursive call
redistributes the grandchildren the buffer shows, but not one another
process added under a child during the session, so before descending
we carry each existing child's subtree from its old range to its new
one (\cref{sec:subtree-follows-parent}); the old ranges are recorded
before any number changes.  A newly created child has no subtree yet
and no old range.

<<record the children's ranges before they move>>=
old_ranges = {
    c.id: child_priority_range_or_none(todos, c)
    for c in children
}
<<carry each child's subtree to its new range>>=
for child, _ in settled:
    move_subtree_priorities(
        todos, child, old_ranges.get(child.id),
        child_priority_range_or_none(todos, child),
    )
@

A newly appended child is on [[todos]] by the time we descend, which
is what lets [[children_of]] find its own level on the recursive call,
so a freshly created child can already receive grandchildren in the
same save.

<<reconcile each child against its own sections>>=
for child, parsed_child in settled:
    reconcile_children(child, parsed_child.children)
@

