@

\subsection{The [[reprioritize]] Command}

When priorities shift, a todo may need repositioning among its
siblings. This reruns the same binary search algorithm used during
[[add]], but for an existing item.  The comparison set is limited to
siblings belonging to the same worker, because cross-worker priority
comparisons are not meaningful.

An explicit ID still reprioritizes one specific item.  But omitting the
ID now serves backlog triage: we walk the current worker's unranked,
non-done items and ask the same binary-search questions for each.  We do
parents before children so that a child can use its newly ranked parent
as the upper bound of its own insertion range.

Four flags answer the questions in advance.  [[--top]] and [[--bottom]]
name the ends of the sibling list; [[--above]] and [[--below]] name a
neighbour to sit next to, which is how a script expresses an ordering
it already knows without having to know the numbers behind it.  All
four go through the shared placement helper
(\cref{todo:placement}), so they agree with [[add]] about what
\enquote{the top} means.

The command's own help text carries the shared priority paragraph,
which the docstring cannot: a docstring is a literal, and the constant
exists precisely so the wording has one home.  Typer prefers the
[[help]] argument when both are present, so the docstring stays as the
short summary a reader of [[help(reprioritize)]] wants.

<<constants>>=
REPRIORITIZE_HELP = (
    "Set a todo item's priority.\n\n"
    "With no placement flag this reruns the interactive "
    "binary search. With no ID it reprioritizes every "
    "unranked, non-done item for the current worker.\n\n"
    + PRIORITY_HELP
    + "\n\nAlias: reprio"
)
@

\subsection{Naming the Priority That Was There Before}

Both paths through the command end in the same sentence: which item was
reprioritized, what its priority was, and what it is now.  The
\enquote{what it was} half is the awkward one, because an unranked item
has no number to print.  The batch path spelled that state
\enquote{unset} while the single-item path interpolated the raw value
and printed \enquote{None}, so one command reported the same transition
two ways depending on how it was invoked.

The state is a property of the stored priority, not of either code
path, so the spelling belongs to a function of that value rather than
to a format string at each echo site.  Giving it one home is also what
keeps the two paths from drifting again the next time the number
formatting changes.

We format the number the way the batch path already did, with one
decimal: priorities are midpoints of midpoints and can carry more
digits than a user wants to read, and the echo is a confirmation of
\emph{where} the item landed, not a value anyone retypes.

<<helpers>>=
def format_stored_priority(value: float | None) -> str:
    """Return `value` as the reprioritize echo spells it.

    An unset (unranked) priority reads `unset`; a
    ranked one is rounded to one decimal.
    """
    if value is None:
        return "unset"
    return f"{value:.1f}"
@

Let's verify both states, since it is exactly their disagreement that
this helper exists to remove:

<<test functions>>=
def test_format_stored_priority_spells_out_unset():
  """The unranked state reads 'unset', not 'None'."""
  assert format_stored_priority(None) == "unset"
  assert format_stored_priority(6.0) == "6.0"
@

<<reprioritize command>>=
@cli.command(help=REPRIORITIZE_HELP)
def reprioritize(
    todo_id: Annotated[int | None,
                       todo_id_arg] = None,
    top: Annotated[bool, top_opt] = False,
    bottom: Annotated[bool, bottom_opt] = False,
    above: Annotated[int | None, above_opt] = None,
    below: Annotated[int | None, below_opt] = None,
):
    """Rerun binary search priority for a todo item.

    If no ID given, reprioritizes all current-worker
    items without numeric priority, excluding done
    items.

    Alias: reprio
    """
    _, todos = load_todos()
    <<validate reprioritize placement>>
    if todo_id is None:
        targets = [
            t for t in todos
            if t.priority is None
            and t.status != "done"
            and (t.who or default_username)
            == default_username
        ]
        if not targets:
            typer.echo(
                "No unprioritized todos found for "
                f"{default_username}."
            )
            return
        targets = sort_todos_for_display(targets)
        targets.sort(
            key=lambda item: todo_depth(todos, item)
        )
        chosen = {}
        for item in targets:
            parent_ctx = get_parent(todos, item)
            if (
                parent_ctx is not None
                and parent_ctx.priority is None
            ):
                typer.echo(
                    f"Skipping #{item.id}: parent "
                    f"#{parent_ctx.id} is still unranked."
                )
                continue
            siblings = get_active_siblings(
                todos,
                item,
                who=item.who or default_username,
                ranked_only=True,
            )
            new_priority = binary_search_priority(
                siblings,
                item,
                parent=parent_ctx,
                todos=todos,
            )
            old_priority = item.priority
            item.priority = new_priority
            chosen[item.id] = new_priority
            typer.echo(
                f"Reprioritized #{item.id}: "
                f"{format_stored_priority(old_priority)}"
                f" -> {format_stored_priority(new_priority)}"
            )
        <<store the chosen priorities under the lock>>
        return
    item = next(
        (t for t in todos if t.id == todo_id), None
    )
    if item is None:
        typer.echo(
            f"Error: todo #{todo_id} not found",
            err=True,
        )
        raise typer.Exit(1)

    parent_ctx = get_parent(todos, item)
    if (
        parent_ctx is not None
        and parent_ctx.priority is None
    ):
        typer.echo(
            f"Error: cannot reprioritize #{todo_id} while "
            f"parent #{parent_ctx.id} is unranked; "
            "reprioritize the parent first",
            err=True,
        )
        raise typer.Exit(1)

    <<resolve the reprioritize placement>>
    new_priority = place_existing_item(
        todos, ranked_as, placement, target
    )
    old_priority = item.priority

    <<store the new priority under the lock>>
    typer.echo(
        f"Reprioritized #{todo_id}: "
        f"{format_stored_priority(old_priority)}"
        f" -> {format_stored_priority(new_priority)}"
    )
@

Both paths decide on the snapshot, because the binary search asks the
user one question after another, and the batch path may ask about many
items in a row.  The decisions are stored afterwards in one locked
callback (\cref{sec:todo-read-modify-write}), and they are stored as
the numbers the user arrived at, without searching again.  The batch
path keeps a map from ID to chosen priority rather than writing its
whole snapshot back, which would undo whatever else changed during the
session.  An item removed meanwhile is the one thing it cannot store,
and after a long series of questions it skips that item with a warning
rather than discarding every other answer.

Each number is stored through [[set_priority_with_subtree]]
(\cref{sec:subtree-follows-parent}).  The batch only visits unranked
items, which have no child range to move from, so usually nothing else
changes; but an item that another process ranked during the session
is a re-ranked parent by the time we store it, and its subtree must
follow the number we store just like on the single-item path.

<<store the chosen priorities under the lock>>=
def store_priorities(next_id, todos, stack):
    by_id = {t.id: t for t in todos}
    for chosen_id, priority in chosen.items():
        if chosen_id in by_id:
            set_priority_with_subtree(
                todos, by_id[chosen_id], priority
            )
        else:
            typer.echo(
                f"Warning: #{chosen_id} no longer exists; "
                "its priority was not stored",
                err=True,
            )
    return next_id, todos, stack


mutate_todo_state(store_priorities)
@

The single-item path has only one answer to lose, so a vanished item
is an error here, reported through [[require_todo]] like in the other
commands, and nothing is written.  Only [[priority]] is set on the
item; any other change made to it during the search stays as it was
stored.

The item's descendants move with it into its new child range
(\cref{sec:subtree-follows-parent}).  Both ranges are read here, from
the state about to be saved, not from the snapshot the search ran on:
a child added under the item, or a sibling moved, while the user was
answering questions is part of the subtree and the ranges we map
between.

The number reported is the one stored, which is not always the one
chosen: for an item with several places the rule may keep it higher,
at the bottom of another place's range (\cref{sec:ranking-context}),
and the report should say where the item is, not where it was asked to
go.

<<store the new priority under the lock>>=
def store_priority(next_id, todos, stack):
    stored = require_todo(todos, todo_id)
    set_priority_with_subtree(todos, stored, new_priority)
    return next_id, todos, stack, stored.priority


*_, new_priority = mutate_todo_state(store_priority)
@

