\subsection{The [[rm]] Command}

Removing a todo requires confirmation.  The subtree goes with
it---children, grandchildren, and deeper---because the store has no
notion of a parentless sub-item: [[get_visible_roots]] promotes any
item whose parent is missing to the top level, so a surviving
grandchild would not be tidied away but instead reappear in the main
[[ls]] list, detached from the tree the user just deleted.

An item with several parents (\cref{sec:todo-parents}) is the
exception.  It is part of the removed todo, but also of something
else, and it is that other part's no less than the removed one's.  So
a descendant is removed only if \emph{all} its parents are removed;
one that keeps a parent is detached from the removed ones and stays
where it still is.  Whether a parent is removed depends on its own
parents in turn, so the answer is worked out top-down, until nothing
changes: in a diamond, the item below both sides goes once both sides
have gone.  A parent that no longer exists keeps nothing alive, and
neither does the top level, which is never removed and so keeps an item
that is also top-level.

<<sub-item helpers>>=
def removal_plan(todos, todo_id):
    """Return what removing `todo_id` removes and detaches.

    The first list holds the descendants all of whose parents
    are removed (or missing); the second the descendants that
    keep another parent and are only detached from the removed
    ones.  `todo_id` itself is in neither.
    """
    by_id = {t.id: t for t in todos}
    below = descendants_of(todos, todo_id)
    doomed = {todo_id}
    changed = True
    while changed:
        changed = False
        for item in below:
            if item.id not in doomed and all(
                p in doomed or (p != ROOT_ID and p not in by_id)
                for p in parents_of(item)
            ):
                doomed.add(item.id)
                changed = True
    removed = [t for t in below if t.id in doomed]
    detached = [
        t for t in below
        if t.id not in doomed
        and any(p in doomed for p in parents_of(t))
    ]
    return removed, detached
@

A detached item may have lost the place its number came from, its
ranking context (\cref{sec:ranking-context}).  The number then belongs
to a range that no longer exists, and is wrongly high, so the item is
placed again where it is still ranked, as [[edit --remove-parent]]
does.  [[rm]] does not stop to ask where, since the user's question was
about removing, not ranking: the item goes to the bottom of that place,
the least presumptuous position, and the confirmation says so, so that
the user who disagrees can answer no or [[reprioritize]] afterwards.

That is also why the counts shown in the confirmation prompt are the
transitive ones.  The prompt is the user's last chance to notice that
[[rm]] is about to take more than they had in mind, so it must state
the real size of what will disappear, and what will stay behind.

Items discovered from a removed todo are not part of its subtree and
stay.  Their [[discovered_from]] keeps pointing at the removed ID
rather than being cleared: IDs are never reused, so the link cannot
come to mean another item, and [[view]] shows it as removed
(\cref{sec:todo-view-provenance}).

We stop any active tracking for the removed item and clean up stale
labels left by previously removed todos---otherwise orphaned
[[todo:N]] labels accumulate in the session and generate phantom
tracking entries.

An omitted ID means \enquote{remove the current active todo}, which mirrors
the way [[done]], [[note]], and [[edit]] already target the same
context-aware default; [[--who]] says whose active todo
(\cref{sec:todo-acting-worker}).

The prompt can wait as long as the user likes, so it is asked on the
snapshot, and the plan is worked out again inside the locked
callback that carries it out (\cref{sec:todo-read-modify-write}).  A
sub-item added while the question was open has to go with its parent
for the same reason the subtree goes at all, and the report afterwards
counts what was actually removed.  If that count differs from the one
the prompt showed, the report says so, since the user agreed to the
smaller or larger number.  Tracking is stopped after the
removal is stored, against the state without the removed items, which
is what [[stop_todo_tracking]] needs to see in order not to protect
their labels.

Here [[--force]] only waives the confirmation prompt; the removal it
skips asking about is the same one either way.  That is a different
promise from [[done --force]], which changes what the command
\emph{does}, so the two carry separate option objects under
command-qualified names.

<<argument and option definitions>>=
rm_force_opt = typer.Option(
    "--force", "-f",
    help="Skip confirmation")
@

<<remove command>>=
@cli.command()
def rm(
    todo_id: Annotated[int | None,
                       todo_id_arg] = None,
    force: Annotated[bool, rm_force_opt] = False,
    who: Annotated[str, acting_who_opt] = default_username,
):
    """Remove a todo item.

    If no ID given, removes the current active task of the
    worker given by --who.  Sub-items go too, except those
    that also have another parent: they stay there.
    """
    _, todos = load_todos()
    todo_id = resolve_todo_id(
        todo_id, "remove",
        who=who, todos=todos,
    )
    item = next(
        (t for t in todos if t.id == todo_id), None
    )
    if item is None:
        typer.echo(
            f"Error: todo #{todo_id} not found",
            err=True,
        )
        raise typer.Exit(1)

    descendants, detached = removal_plan(todos, todo_id)
    if not force:
        <<confirm the removal plan>>

    confirmed = None if force else len(descendants)

    def remove_subtree(next_id, todos, stack):
        require_todo(todos, todo_id)
        descendants, detached = removal_plan(todos, todo_id)
        <<detach the items that keep another parent>>
        return next_id, todos, stack, descendants, detached, moved

    _, todos, _, descendants, detached, moved = mutate_todo_state(
        remove_subtree
    )

    <<stop tracking if active>>
    release_context_ancestors(item, session_worker(item))

    typer.echo(f"Removed #{todo_id}: {item.title}")
    <<report the removed sub-items>>
@

The prompt names the two counts, and each detached item that loses
the place it was ranked in, with the place it will be last in.  Those
are the detached items whose ranking context is among the removed ones;
unranked items have no number to correct.  The place is not simply the
highest one the item keeps: the bottom of the top level is below every
parent's range, so an item that keeps the top level and a ranked parent
ends at the bottom of the parent's range by the rule
(\cref{sec:ranking-context}).  Rather than repeat that reasoning, the
prompt asks the placement itself, on copies of the items: placing at
the bottom asks no questions, so the copies end where the removal will
put the items, and the place named is the ranking context there.

<<predict where the detached items will be last>>=
copies = [copy.copy(t) for t in todos if t.id not in doomed]
copy_of = {t.id: t for t in copies}
for t in copies:
    t.parents = [p for p in t.parents if p not in doomed]
last_in = {}
for d in detached:
    if d.priority is None or ranking_context(todos, d) not in doomed:
        continue
    twin = copy_of[d.id]
    twin.priority = place_in_places(
        copies, twin, twin.parents, PLACE_BOTTOM,
    )
    last_in[d.id] = (
        None if twin.priority is None
        else ranking_context(copies, twin)
    )

<<confirm the removal plan>>=
doomed = {todo_id} | {d.id for d in descendants}
msg = f"Remove #{todo_id}: {item.title}?"
counts = []
if descendants:
    counts.append(f"{len(descendants)} sub-items")
if detached:
    counts.append(f"{len(detached)} kept under their other parents")
<<predict where the detached items will be last>>
for d_id, place in last_in.items():
    counts.append(
        f"#{d_id} to the bottom {describe_places([place])}"
        if place is not None else f"#{d_id} unranked"
    )
if counts:
    msg += f" ({'; '.join(counts)})"
if not typer.confirm(msg):
    typer.echo("Cancelled.")
    raise typer.Exit(0)
@

Inside the callback the detached items' ranges and places are read
before anything is removed, since both depend on the removed parents.
Then the removed items go, each detached item drops its removed
parents, and one that lost its ranking context is given the bottom of
the highest place it keeps, through the same helper as [[edit]]
(\cref{sec:place-in-places}), and settled like any placed item
(\cref{sec:sibling-above-squeezed}).  A place whose parent is unranked
leaves it unranked, as [[edit]] does.  All detached items drop their
removed parents before the first is placed, so that none is placed
next to another that still refers to a parent that is gone; and the
ranges are checked once, before the removal, and repaired once, after
the last placement (\cref{sec:ranking-context}).  To its new family
such an item is new: its old place was in a family that no longer
exists, so it is settled without one, and the sibling it lands under is
compared with the family as it was without it.

<<detach the items that keep another parent>>=
doomed = {todo_id} | {d.id for d in descendants}
before = {
    d.id: (
        child_priority_range_or_none(todos, d),
        (d.priority, list(d.parents)),
        ranking_context(todos, d) in doomed,
    )
    for d in detached
}
was_nested = bool(detached) and list_has_shared_items(todos) and (
    ranking_is_consistent(todos)
)
todos = [t for t in todos if t.id not in doomed]
for d in detached:
    d.parents = [p for p in d.parents if p not in doomed]
moved = []
for d in detached:
    old_range, old_place, lost_rank = before[d.id]
    if lost_rank and d.priority is not None:
        d.priority = place_in_places(
            todos, d, d.parents, PLACE_BOTTOM,
        )
        if d.priority is not None:
            moved.append(d)
        old_place = None
    settle_placement(
        todos, d, old_range, old_place, was_nested=False,
    )
if detached:
    restore_nesting(todos, detached[0], [], was_nested)
@

The usual report is a bare count.  When the user confirmed a
different number, we add that number so the difference is visible,
including the case where everything confirmed was already gone and
nothing more was removed.  With [[--force]] nothing was confirmed, so
there is nothing to compare with.  The items that stayed are counted
too, and each that was moved says where it is now last.

<<report the removed sub-items>>=
changed = (
    confirmed is not None
    and confirmed != len(descendants)
)
if descendants or changed:
    report = f"  Also removed {len(descendants)} sub-items"
    if changed:
        report += f" ({confirmed} when you confirmed)"
    typer.echo(report)
if detached:
    typer.echo(
        f"  Kept {len(detached)} sub-items under their other "
        "parents"
    )
for d in moved:
    typer.echo(
        f"  #{d.id} is now last "
        f"{describe_places([ranking_context(todos, d)])}"
    )
@
