\subsection{The [[next]] Command}

The [[next]] command answers the question \enquote{What should I work
on now?}  It encapsulates the agent's decision loop into a single
CLI call, replacing fragile shell-script logic with a first-class
command.

Because [[next]] delegates its launch step to [[start]], it also passes
through execution-mode options such as [[--headless]], [[--pane]],
[[--window]], [[--auto-suspend]], the [[--command]] and
[[--no-command]] pair, and [[--shell]]/[[--no-shell]].  This keeps
the policy question \enquote{what should start?} separate from the
execution question \enquote{how should it run?}

The behaviour depends on whether the current context resolves to an
active task:
\begin{description}
\item[Active context] Start the highest-priority pending
  \emph{child} of the current tmux-context task.  This is the
  decomposition workflow: you started a parent, created sub-tasks, and
  now [[next]] picks the most urgent child in that branch.
\item[No active context] Start the highest-priority pending task among
  top-level items only (filtered by [[--who]]).
\end{description}

Inside tmux, an untracked pane/window counts as
\enquote{no active context}: [[next]] may still choose top-level work,
but it must not descend into a branch that is only active somewhere
else.

If there is nothing to do (no pending task matches), the command logs a
warning and exits non-zero.  That makes failed scheduling decisions
visible to automation rather than silently returning success.

The [[block]] option defaults to [[None]] rather than a boolean so
that [[next_cmd]] can resolve it before delegating to [[start]]: tmux
launches ([[--pane]]/[[--window]]) default to non-blocking (detached),
while plain launches default to blocking.  Resolving here avoids
passing a [[None]] into [[start]], which would otherwise have to
repeat the same defaulting logic.

Because [[start]] now takes the click context as a required first
parameter, [[next_cmd]] passes [[ctx=None]] explicitly: it has no
context of its own to forward, and [[start]] tolerates a missing
context (its [[resolve_command_argv]] guards on [[if ctx]]).

[[next_cmd]] also hands its [[--who]] on to [[start]], as the worker
to act as (\cref{sec:todo-acting-worker}).  The item came from that
worker's queue, so whatever [[start]] decides on a worker's behalf
should be decided for that worker and not for whoever happens to be
logged in.

Here, though, [[--who]] is a regular expression: [[next]] passes it to
[[re.search]] to choose among the queues.  Usually it is the plain
name of the worker asking, and then it names the actor too.  A
pattern that matches several workers, [[dan-.*]] or [[alice|bob]],
names nobody in particular, and handing it on would make [[start]]
act as a worker called [[dan-.*]], whose claim would never match an
owner.  So is the empty pattern, which means \enquote{any worker}.
In both cases [[start]] acts as the default user, as it always did.

A pattern is a plain name when escaping it for a regular expression
changes nothing: then it contains no syntax, and reads as the same
literal text whether taken as a pattern or as a name.  It may still
match more than one worker, since [[re.search]] is not anchored and
[[alice]] also finds [[malice]], but the worker it names is not in
doubt, and that is the one asking.

Two characters that [[re.escape]] escapes are let through all the
same, because worker names use them.  The hyphen is special only
inside a character class, which needs an opening bracket that would
itself be escaped; without the exception the canonical agent name
[[dan-claude]] would count as a pattern.  The dot is special
everywhere, since it matches any character, but in a name like
[[daniel.bosk]] that can only widen the filter, to [[danielXbosk]]
as well, never narrow it.  And what [[start]] does with the acting
worker is to record it as the claimant and to resolve the worker's
own active item, where the literal name is what anyone typing a
dotted login name means.  A pattern built to match several workers
needs more than dots and hyphens---a star, a bar, a bracket---and
those still mark it as a pattern.

<<helpers>>=
def acting_worker_from_filter(pattern):
    """Return the worker a `next --who` pattern names.

    A non-empty pattern without regular-expression syntax,
    dots and hyphens aside, names that worker; anything
    else, including the empty "any worker" pattern, means
    the default user.
    """
    escaped = (
        re.escape(pattern or "")
        .replace("\\-", "-")
        .replace("\\.", ".")
    )
    if pattern and escaped == pattern:
        return pattern
    return default_username
@

Let's verify the three kinds of pattern, including a hyphenated and a
dotted name:

<<test functions>>=
def test_acting_worker_from_filter():
  """Only a plain name passes through as the acting worker."""
  assert acting_worker_from_filter("dan-claude") == "dan-claude"
  assert acting_worker_from_filter("alice") == "alice"
  assert (
    acting_worker_from_filter("daniel.bosk") == "daniel.bosk"
  )
  assert acting_worker_from_filter("dan-.*") == default_username
  assert acting_worker_from_filter("a|b") == default_username
  assert acting_worker_from_filter("") == default_username
  assert acting_worker_from_filter(None) == default_username
