@

Starting a todo delegates to the track system. We start tracking with
the todo's labels plus a special [[todo:<id>]] label, and set the
todo's status to \enquote{in-progress}. The track worker is the
acting worker, who has just become the item's claimant
(\cref{sec:todo-claim}).

The ordering now matters more than before.  We first update the
authoritative active stack under the todo lock, then start the matching
track labels.  This means that auto-parenting, [[todo status]], and
subsequent [[todo next]] decisions immediately see the new active
hierarchy even if the launched subprocess has not yet done anything.

The key enhancement: if the todo has a [[wd]], [[start]] automatically
spawns a subprocess---the stored [[command]], a [[--command]] override, or
[[$SHELL]]. This combines the patterns from launch-enabled
[[track start]] (tracking bookends around a subprocess) and
[[courses data]] shell (PS1 modification and [[wd]] control).
When the subprocess exits, tracking stops and blocking launches ask
whether the todo should be marked done.

Without a [[wd]], the command behaves as before: it starts tracking
without spawning anything.

As with [[view]] and friends, omitting the ID means \enquote{start the
current active todo}.  In tmux that follows the pane or window labels;
an untracked tmux pane/window has no implicit active todo, while
outside tmux it falls back to the worker's active-stack top.  This is
useful when tracking stopped but the todo itself remains the current
focus.  The worker is the one given by [[--who]]
(\cref{sec:todo-acting-worker}), so an agent restarting its own task
does not pick up the user's.

<<start command options>>=
who: Annotated[str, acting_who_opt] = default_username,
@

Typer injects the click context into whichever parameter is annotated
with [[typer.Context]], but only if its detection
([[lenient_issubclass(annotation, click.Context)]]) sees a bare
[[Context]] subclass.  A [[None]] default defeats this: on Python up to
3.10, [[typing.get_type_hints]] rewrites [[ctx: typer.Context = None]]
into [[Optional[typer.Context]]], a union that fails the subclass test,
so Typer treats the context as an ordinary option and aborts with
\enquote{Type not yet supported}.  Python 3.11 dropped that implicit
[[Optional]], which is why the bug only surfaced on older interpreters.
We therefore make [[ctx]] the first parameter with no default---matching
[[add]] and [[edit]]---so the annotation stays a bare [[Context]]
everywhere.

<<start command>>=
@cli.command(cls=PassThroughCommand)
def start(
    ctx: typer.Context,
    todo_id: Annotated[int | None,
                       todo_id_arg] = None,
    <<start command options>>
):
    """Start tracking time for a todo item.

    If no ID given, starts the current active task.

    Refuses a todo that another worker has active; --force
    takes it over, stopping that worker's tracking of it.

    If the todo has a working directory, spawns a
    shell or command in that directory with tracking.

    The canonical override form is
    ``nytid todo start 7 -- prog args``.
    """
    try:
        run_argv, run = resolve_command_argv(run, ctx)
    except ValueError as exc:
        typer.echo(f"Error parsing command: {exc}", err=True)
        raise typer.Exit(1)
    except typer.BadParameter as exc:
        typer.echo(f"Error: {exc}", err=True)
        raise typer.Exit(1)
    <<reject a command together with [[--no-command]]>>
    <<peek at the todo to start>>
    <<refuse another worker's active todo early>>
    <<resolve worktree for todo start>>
    (
        todo_id, item, next_id, todos, stack, displaced,
    ) = activate_todo_state(todo_id, who=who, force=force)
    <<stop the displaced owner's tracking>>

    <<resolve tmux context sync policy>>
    <<auto-activate ancestors>>
    <<start tracking via track module>>

    typer.echo(
        f"Started: #{todo_id} {item.title}"
    )

    <<spawn subprocess when the todo has work to launch>>
@

The context-detection invariant above is easy to break by accident---a
later edit that gives [[ctx]] a [[None]] default would reintroduce the
crash, but only on Python up to 3.10, so a modern test run would not
catch it.  We therefore assert the shape of the signature directly,
which fails on every interpreter the moment [[ctx]] stops being a bare,
undefaulted first parameter.

<<test functions>>=
def test_start_ctx_has_no_default():
  """Guard Typer context detection for the start command.

  A None default on the context parameter becomes Optional on
  Python <= 3.10, which defeats Typer's context detection and
  crashes CLI construction.  Keep ctx a bare, undefaulted first
  parameter.
  """
  import inspect
  from nytid.cli.todo import start
  sig = inspect.signature(start)
  assert list(sig.parameters)[0] == "ctx"
  assert (
    sig.parameters["ctx"].default is inspect.Parameter.empty
  )
@

A todo with a worktree---stored on the item or given as the one-off
[[--worktree]] override---runs in that worktree instead of directly in
[[wd]].  We create or reuse it \emph{before} anything is recorded, for
the same reason [[track start]] validates [[--wd]] early: a base
directory outside any git repository must fail before the todo is
pushed onto the active stack and before any tracking entry---the
item's or an auto-activated ancestor's---has been opened.  That forces
a read-only peek at the item ahead of [[activate_todo_state]], which
is otherwise the first thing to look at it; the peek resolves the ID
the same way, so an ID-less start with no active todo fails here with
the same message it always did.  The base directory is the item's
[[wd]] (which may itself come from the worker config) or the current
directory, exactly as the launch itself would have used, and the
launch lands in the matching subdirectory of the worktree
(\cref{track:worktrees}).  Because [[ensure_worktree]] reuses an
existing worktree, restarting the todo lands in the same checkout with
whatever was left there.

<<peek at the todo to start>>=
