Detached tmux cleanup happens after the pane process exits, so it cannot
reuse the in-memory todo item from the original [[start]] call.  We
therefore provide a hidden helper command that reloads the todo,
stops it through the same hierarchy-unwinding helper as manual
[[todo stop]], and applies the post-run policy in a fresh process.

That helper must be robust against exactly the kinds of changes that may
happen during a long-running task.  The working directory might have been
renamed or deleted before the pane exits, so cleanup must not depend on
[[cd]] into [[wd]].  Likewise, [[nytid]] may be running inside a Poetry
environment or another non-system interpreter, so the detached helper
must reuse [[sys.executable]] rather than assuming [[python3]] points at
the correct environment.

Because nobody is watching a detached run, the exit code is the only
report it ever files.  We give it the same treatment the blocking
headless path gives its subprocess: record it as a note so the outcome
survives in the item's history, and hand a failed task to
[[--reassign-to]] so it lands in a human's queue instead of quietly
returning to [[pending]] indistinguishable from a run that simply did
not finish.  Without that, the entire exit-code apparatus---the status
file, [[read_detached_exit_code]] and its signal mapping---would deliver
a number with no consumer.

The note says \enquote{no output} because a detached pane writes to the
terminal, not to a pipe we can capture; the wording matches the blocking
path so both kinds of run leave notes in one format.

\paragraph{Storing the outcome of a run}%
\label{sec:todo-run-outcome}
Both kinds of run end the same way: stop the hierarchy, decide what
the run amounted to, and store that decision.  The deciding is where
the time goes.  The blocking path may ask the user whether the task is
done, and the answer can take as long as the user likes; meanwhile the
agent's own session, or anyone else, may have added a note or
completed the item.  The old way of storing the decision---writing
back the snapshot that [[stop_todo_hierarchy]] returned---erased all of
that.

[[record_run_outcome]] therefore takes the decision as data and applies
it inside one locked callback (\cref{sec:todo-read-modify-write}).  The
decision has three parts: notes to append, whether the item is done,
and whom to hand an unfinished item to.  The callback re-checks the
one assumption the decision rests on, that the item is not done yet.
If it was completed in the meantime, a \enquote{not done} answer must
not reopen it, because that answer was about the run, not about the
completion somebody else recorded.  An item that was removed
meanwhile is reported and left removed.  Descendants that the stop
deactivated go back to [[pending]] unless they are done, as before.

Posting a note to GitHub is network I/O and stays outside the lock:
callers post with [[post_note_comment]] first and pass the same text
here to be mirrored locally.

The notes are signed by the worker that launched the run,
[[launched_by]] (\cref{sec:todo-note-headers}), not by whoever owns
the item when they are stored.  The output and exit code are what
that worker's agent produced, and after a takeover
(\cref{sec:todo-run-taken-over}) the new owner did not write them.
They are appended before the item is handed to [[reassign_to]], so a
failed run is still signed by the agent that failed, not by the human
who receives the task.  A run started by an older version records no
launcher; its notes fall back to [[item.who]], and
[[append_note_to_item]] in turn to [[default_username]].

<<helpers>>=
def record_run_outcome(
    todo_id, descendant_ids, *,
    notes=(), done=False, reassign_to=None,
    launched_by=None,
):
    """Store how a run of `todo_id` ended.

    Appends `notes` locally.  Unless the item is already
    done, marks it done if `done`, otherwise resets it to
    pending and assigns it to `reassign_to` if given.
    Descendants in `descendant_ids` that are not done
    are reset to pending.  If the item is no longer
    `launched_by`'s, only the notes and a line saying
    who took it over are stored.  Returns the stored item
    (or `None` if it no longer exists) and the stored
    todos.
    """
    def update(next_id, todos, stack):
        item = next(
            (t for t in todos if t.id == todo_id), None
        )
        if item is None:
            typer.echo(
                f"Warning: todo #{todo_id} no longer exists; "
                "the outcome of its run was not stored",
                err=True,
            )
            return next_id, todos, stack, None
        for note_text in notes:
            append_note_to_item(
                item, note_text, who=launched_by or item.who,
            )
        <<only note a run whose item was taken over>>
        if item.status != "done":
            if done:
                close_item(todos, item)
            else:
                if reassign_to:
                    item.who = reassign_to
                set_status(item, "pending")
        for child in todos:
            if (
                child.id in descendant_ids
                and child.status != "done"
            ):
                set_status(child, "pending")
        return next_id, todos, stack, item

    _, todos, _, item = mutate_todo_state(update)
    return item, todos
@

\paragraph{Runs that outlive their claim}%
\label{sec:todo-run-taken-over}
A run can outlast its claim on the item.  An agent's session is
still running when the user takes the item over with
[[start --force]] (\cref{sec:todo-claim}); the takeover stops the
agent's tracking and moves the claim to the user, but the agent's
process carries on until it exits.  Left to itself, its ending would
undo the takeover: the stop would remove the item from the stack and
close the tracking of the item's \emph{current} claimant, the new
one, and the outcome would reset the item to pending, hand it to the
[[--reassign-to]] worker, or, after a \enquote{yes} at the prompt,
mark it done under the new claimant's hands.

What the run's ending may change is what the run still holds.  Every
run therefore remembers the acting worker that launched it,
[[launched_by]], and the ending compares that worker with the item's
claimant.  If the item is still active and someone else holds the
claim, it belongs to them now: it stays active, their tracking and
the descendants are left alone, and the run neither asks whether the
item is done nor stores done or pending.  All the run still does is
end its launcher's own tracking of the item and leave its record:
the run's note, if it has one, and a line saying who took the item
over.

The claimant is the right thing to compare because only a start
moves it, and only a forced start moves it away from someone who
holds it (\cref{sec:todo-claim}).  Reassigning the item with
[[edit --who]] while it runs changes the assignee and nothing else,
so the run ends as it always did.  A restart by the same worker
keeps the claim where it was.  An item that is no longer active at
all was stopped or completed by someone, which is not a takeover and
is handled as before.  Runs started before this check existed carry
no launcher, and [[None]] keeps their old behaviour.

Unlike the claim check, this comparison reads [[claimed_by]] itself
and not [[session_worker]], whose fallback to the assignee is meant
for items activated before claims were recorded.  A run's ending
needs evidence of a takeover before it holds back, and an empty
[[claimed_by]] on an active item is no such evidence: a takeover
always writes the new claimant.  The field is empty when an older
version of nytid saved the store meanwhile and dropped the unknown
key, or when a command was interrupted between its writes.  Falling
back to the assignee there would mistake the run's own item for
somebody else's whenever an agent ran a task from the user's queue,
and leave it on the stack with nobody's clock running.  For the same
reason a run's stop closes the session of the worker that launched it
by name, as [[stop_todo_hierarchy]] does when given [[launched_by]],
rather than the session it would derive from the item.

<<helpers>>=
def run_taken_over_by(item, stack, launched_by):
    """Return the worker who took `item` from a run.

    `launched_by` is the acting worker that launched the
    run.  Returns the claimant if `item` is active on
    `stack` and claimed by someone else, otherwise `None`:
    also when nobody is recorded as its claimant, and when
    `launched_by` is `None` (an older run that did not
    record its launcher).
    """
    if launched_by is None or item.id not in stack:
        return None
    holder = item.claimed_by
    if holder is None or holder == launched_by:
        return None
    return holder
@

