The comparison is made twice, both times under the lock, because the
run's ending takes two steps with a prompt between them.  The stop is
the first, and it must not happen at all for a run that was taken
over: [[stop_todo_state]] raises instead of writing.  An exception
fits because every caller that passes [[launched_by]] must handle the
case, and an exception cannot be ignored the way a flag in a return
tuple can; raising inside the callback is also what abandons the
write (\cref{sec:todo-read-modify-write}).

<<helpers>>=
class RunTakenOver(Exception):
    """A run ended after another worker took its item."""

    def __init__(self, todo_id, item, holder):
        super().__init__(
            f"todo #{todo_id} was taken over by {holder}"
        )
        self.todo_id = todo_id
        self.item = item
        self.holder = holder
<<refuse to stop a run that was taken over>>=
holder = run_taken_over_by(item, stack, launched_by)
if holder is not None:
    raise RunTakenOver(resolved_id, item, holder)
