The [[--command]] option lets the user override the todo's stored
command and any worker-config command from
[[todo.worker.<worker>.command]] at start time:

<<start command options>>=
run: Annotated[str | None,
               command_opt] = None,
@

[[--no-command]] is the negative counterpart: it says \enquote{no
command this time} without editing the item.  The typical case is
taking over an agent's task by hand.  The item carries [[claude]]---or
inherits it from the worker default---and the user wants the tracking
and the working directory but not the launcher.  Substituting a shell
with [[--command]] would work but names the wrong thing; what the user
means is \enquote{as if no command were stored}, and that is exactly
what the flag does: the launch decision and the command resolution
both treat the item as command-less, so a [[wd]] still opens a tracked
shell there while an item with only a command starts tracking and
spawns nothing.

<<argument and option definitions>>=
no_command_opt = typer.Option(
    "--no-command",
    help="Start without running the stored or "
         "worker-default command")
@

<<start command options>>=
no_command: Annotated[bool, no_command_opt] = False,
@

A command and a request for none contradict each other, and both
spellings of a command---the [[--command]] string and the [[--]]
pass-through---arrive as a non-[[None]] [[run_argv]], so one check
covers both.  We reuse the placement-flag validator; it names the two
flags in its message, which is what the user needs to see.

<<reject a command together with [[--no-command]]>>=
reject_conflicting_flags({
    "--no-command": no_command,
    "--command": run_argv is not None,
})
@

[[--no-command]] silences a launcher; [[--no-shell]] silences the
shell that a working directory alone would open.  The two answer
different questions, which is why they are separate flags rather
than one \enquote{launch nothing} switch: a task carrying both a
[[wd]] and [[claude]] should still run [[claude]] under
[[--no-shell]], and still open a shell under [[--no-command]].
The shell is the one launch reason that is a preference---it exists
only to carry the prompt marker and the [[cd]], both of which a
tracked tmux window and the shell prompt (\cref{prompt}) provide
without a nested shell---so it is the one with a configured default
(\cref{todo:start-settle}).  The flag is tri-state for that reason:
[[None]] means \enquote{ask the configuration}.  It governs only a
launch in the caller's own terminal; [[--pane]] and [[--window]]
must run \emph{something} in the pane or window they create, so a
command-less task still gets a shell there.

<<argument and option definitions>>=
shell_opt = typer.Option(
    "--shell/--no-shell",
    help="Open a shell in the task's wd when it has no "
         "command (default: todo.shell config, else yes); "
         "--pane/--window always get a shell")
@

<<start command options>>=
shell: Annotated[bool | None, shell_opt] = None,
@

Likewise [[--worktree]] overrides the stored worktree name for this
one start, without editing the item.  This is the one-off counterpart
of [[todo add --worktree]]: a task normally lives in the same worktree
across restarts, but sometimes the user wants to try it on a fresh
checkout, or run a task that has no stored worktree in one just this
once.

<<start command options>>=
worktree: Annotated[str | None,
                    worktree_opt] = None,
@

For cron and agent workflows, we need a non-interactive mode that
skips the [[typer.confirm]] prompt after subprocess exit.  In headless
mode, the logic is: if the subprocess (e.g.\ [[claude]]) already called
[[todo done]] during its run, the task is done; otherwise we reset to
pending so the next invocation can retry.

\paragraph{Full lifecycle in headless mode}
\label{sec:headless-lifecycle}
When [[--headless]] is combined with [[--timeout]], the [[start]]
command manages the full lifecycle internally: capturing output,
posting it as a note, handling timeout, and reassigning timed-out
tasks.  This eliminates the race condition where an external
shell script queries for the active task after the subprocess has
already reset the status (see \cref{sec:agent-automation}).

<<start command options>>=
headless: Annotated[bool, headless_opt] = False,
@

The [[--timeout]] and [[--reassign-to]] options are orthogonal to
[[--headless]]: timeout specifies when to kill a runaway subprocess
(using GNU [[timeout]] format like [[50m]] or [[1.5h]]), and
[[--reassign-to]] specifies who receives timed-out or failed tasks.
Keeping them separate makes each parameter's purpose clear and
allows independent configuration.

<<start command options>>=
timeout: Annotated[str | None,
                   timeout_opt] = None,
reassign_to: Annotated[str | None,
                       reassign_opt] = default_username,
@

Tmux gives us a middle ground between blocking the current terminal and
running fully headless.  [[--pane]] keeps the task visible beside the
current shell, while [[--window]] moves it to another tmux window in the
same session.  Because both modes rely on tmux owning the terminal, they
require [[TMUX]] to be set and cannot be combined with [[--headless]].
By default tmux launches detach immediately so the caller can keep using
other panes while the task remains tracked.  [[--block]] switches back to
the interactive completion workflow: when the pane command exits,
tracking stops and the user decides whether the task is done. Detached
tmux runs still stop tracking automatically and reset the todo to
pending unless it was already marked done from inside the pane.

<<start command options>>=
pane: Annotated[bool, pane_opt] = False,
window: Annotated[bool, window_opt] = False,
block: Annotated[bool | None,
                 block_opt] = None,
@

When running in a tmux pane, tracking can automatically suspend
when the pane loses focus and resume when it regains focus.  The
[[--auto-suspend]]/[[--no-auto-suspend]] flag overrides the
per-worker and global config defaults; see the [[track]] module
(\cref{track:auto-suspend}) for the resolution order.

<<start command options>>=
auto_suspend: Annotated[
    bool | None, auto_suspend_opt
] = None,
@

We reject impossible combinations before building the subprocess command.
This keeps the later chunks focused on one execution mode at a time and
produces clear CLI errors for mutually exclusive options.

<<validate tmux options>>=
try:
    from nytid.cli.track import validate_tmux_options
    validate_tmux_options(pane, window)
except ImportError:
    if pane and window:
        typer.echo(
            "Error: --pane and --window are mutually exclusive",
            err=True,
        )
        raise typer.Exit(1)
    if (pane or window) and not os.environ.get("TMUX"):
        typer.echo(
            "Error: --pane/--window require an active tmux session",
            err=True,
        )
        raise typer.Exit(1)
except ValueError as exc:
    typer.echo(str(exc), err=True)
    raise typer.Exit(1)
if (pane or window) and headless:
    typer.echo(
        "Error: --pane/--window cannot be used with --headless",
        err=True,
    )
    raise typer.Exit(1)
if block is None:
    block = not (pane or window)
if block and not (pane or window):
    pass
if not block and not (pane or window):
    typer.echo(
        "Error: --no-block requires --pane or --window",
        err=True,
    )
    raise typer.Exit(1)
@

We import and use the track module's start/stop helpers directly rather
than shelling out to the CLI. This keeps todo launches aligned with the
same on-disk locking and persistence semantics as the track commands.

Before we start tracking the explicitly requested todo, we ensure that
all its ancestors are already active and tracked.  This way, starting
a sub-item \enquote{just works} without requiring the user to manually
start each ancestor first.

The same tmux-context rule applies to both ancestor and main-item
tracking.  Detached tmux launches should not first publish labels into
the source shell's pane or window; they should wait for the tmux launch
helper to declare the new pane or window as tracked.

<<resolve tmux context sync policy>>=
update_tmux_context = not (pane or window)
@

<<auto-activate ancestors>>=
ensure_ancestors_active(
    item,
    todos,
    stack,
    update_tmux_context=update_tmux_context,
)
@

A task's tracking label set---its own labels plus the
[[todo:<id>]] marker---is needed at three sites: starting,
auto-suspend publication, and adoption.  One function keeps the
three from drifting apart; the id goes in, not the title, because
titles change and ids do not.

<<helpers>>=
def todo_track_labels(item, todo_id):
    """Return the track labels for working on a todo."""
    return list(item.labels) + [f"todo:{todo_id}"]
@

<<start tracking via track module>>=
try:
    from nytid.cli.track import (
        start_tracking_labels,
    )

    worker = session_worker(item)

    track_labels = todo_track_labels(item, todo_id)
    started, _, _ = start_tracking_labels(
        track_labels,
        start_time=datetime.datetime.now(),
        who=worker,
        update_tmux_context=update_tmux_context,
    )

    if started:
        typer.echo(
            f"  Tracking: {', '.join(sorted(started))}"
        )
        if worker != "me":
            typer.echo(f"  Worker: {worker}")
except ImportError:
    typer.echo(
        "Warning: track module not available, "
        "time tracking disabled",
        err=True,
    )
@

\subsubsection{Spawning a Subprocess}
\label{todo:start-spawn}

We spawn a subprocess whenever the todo carries enough information to
launch something on its own (a stored [[command]] or [[wd]]) and also
whenever the caller supplies a launch flag at invocation time
([[--command]], [[--pane]], or [[--window]]). The stored [[command]]
must trigger a spawn by itself: otherwise users who configured a
launcher such as [[claude]] on the todo would see tracking start but
never see their command run unless they remembered to pass a launch
flag such as [[-W]].  [[--no-command]] removes the command from that
list of reasons, and only the command: a [[wd]], a worktree or a tmux
flag still launches, now with a shell.  The [[wd]] and worktree
reasons are in turn subject to the shell preference
([[should_spawn_shell]]): they only ever launch a shell, so when the
user has asked for no shell they are no reasons at all, and the
start settles in the current terminal instead
(\cref{todo:start-settle}).
The environment restoration starts from the stored [[env]] (if
available) and overlays terminal-related variables ([[TERM]],
[[DISPLAY]], [[SSH_*]]) from the current environment to ensure the
terminal works correctly.

The PS1 prefix should only be injected when we are actually launching an
interactive shell.  Arbitrary commands such as [[claude]], [[opencode]],
[[echo]], or [[false]] must run exactly as resolved; otherwise the shared
shell helper would incorrectly replace the requested command with the
user's login shell.

We resolve auto-suspend before determining the command, so that
determining the command is the last step before dispatch.  For a
direct shell that step allocates a temporary prompt init-file, and the
[[else]] branch's [[try]]/[[finally]] removes it; running the
fallible auto-suspend lookup earlier keeps it outside that window so a
failure there cannot leak the file.

<<spawn subprocess when the todo has work to launch>>=
use_shell = should_spawn_shell(
    item.who or default_username, shell,
)
if (
    run or pane or window
    or (item.command and not no_command)
    or ((item.wd or worktree_dir) and use_shell)
):
    <<validate tmux options>>
    <<prepare subprocess environment>>
    <<resolve todo auto-suspend>>
    <<determine command to run>>
    <<initialize run outcome>>
    if pane or window:
        <<run in tmux>>
        if block:
            <<remove worktree after todo run>>
            <<handle subprocess exit>>
    else:
        <<adopt the current window for a direct launch>>
        <<run subprocess>>
        <<remove worktree after todo run>>
        <<handle subprocess exit>>
else:
    <<settle in the current terminal>>
@

A direct launch blocks the caller's own terminal, so the task runs in
a window the user opened---the very window that
[[start_tracking_labels]] cannot mark as tracked, since it only adds
labels to pane and window files that already exist.  The launched
command is exactly the caller that suffers from that: [[claude]] or a
shell running [[todo done]] or [[todo next]] without an ID has no way
to name the task it is working on, and the ID-less resolution falls
back to whichever \emph{other} window is tracked---or to
\enquote{No active todo}.  Adopting the window first gives the child
the same context a [[--window]] launch would have given it.  That is
the argument the settle path already makes
(\cref{todo:start-settle}), so the two paths call one helper,
introduced there.

We adopt without arming a finalize hook, the one difference from the
settle path.  A pane-exit hook fires when the \emph{pane} dies, not
when our child exits, and this pane outlives the launch: control
returns here, [[<<handle subprocess exit>>]] stops the hierarchy and
settles the status, and the user carries on in the same shell.  A hook
armed now would therefore fire much later---at the end of the terminal
session, on a task that was finished, reassigned or restarted in the
meantime---and reset it.  The settle path has no such moment to react
to, which is why it keeps the pane-history decision: there the shell
the user eventually leaves is the only exit signal there is.

<<adopt the current window for a direct launch>>=
if not headless:
    adopt_untracked_window_for_start(
        todo_id, item, todos, auto_suspend,
        arm_finalize=False,
    )
@

Let's verify the four launch shapes: an interactive direct launch in
an untracked window adopts it, hook-free and without losing the
spawn, and carries the ancestors along; a tracked window, a tmux
launch, and a headless run are left alone.  The pane is given
\emph{no} exit hooks of its own here, so the hook mock would fire if
the launch ever fell back to the pane-history decision.

<<test functions>>=
def invoke_launching_start(args, tracked, user_input=None):
    """Invoke a launching start with the spawn mocked.

    Returns the result together with the adopt, register,
    exit-hook and subprocess mocks.
    """
    with patch.dict(
        os.environ,
        {"TMUX": "session", "TMUX_PANE": "%3"},
        clear=True,
    ), patch(
        "nytid.cli.track.is_current_tmux_context_tracked",
        return_value=tracked,
    ), patch(
        "nytid.cli.todo.pane_has_exit_hooks",
        return_value=False,
    ), patch(
        "nytid.cli.track.adopt_current_window",
        return_value=dict(ADOPTED_WINDOW),
    ) as adopt_mock, patch(
        "nytid.cli.track.register_tmux_context_labels",
    ) as register_mock, patch(
        "nytid.cli.track.install_tmux_exit_hooks",
    ) as hooks_mock, patch(
        "nytid.cli.todo.run_todo_tmux_command",
        return_value=0,
    ), patch(
        "nytid.cli.todo.subprocess.run",
        return_value=subprocess.CompletedProcess(
            ["true"], 0, stdout="",
        ),
    ) as run_mock:
