@

A stored [[env]] \emph{replaces} the inherited environment rather than
merging into it: capturing an environment (via [[--here]] or
[[--capture-env]], or worker-scoped [[env]] defaults) is only useful
if the task later runs in that environment, not in
whatever the launching shell happens to have accumulated since.  But a
captured snapshot also freezes variables that identify the
\emph{session} the capture happened in, and those must not travel with
the task.  The spawned shell runs ID-less [[nytid]] subcommands whose
active-todo resolution reads [[USER]] (worker filtering in
[[resolve_todo_id]]) and [[TMUX]]/[[TMUX_PANE]] (the pane and window
[[todo:<id>]] labels in [[resolve_todo_from_tmux_context]]).  With
capture-time values, that resolution consults the pane the todo was
\emph{created} in---not the pane it was just started in, where the
parent process wrote the labels moments earlier---so
[[nytid todo view]] inside the spawned shell claimed there was no
active todo.
The same reasoning covers the terminal and forwarding variables
([[TERM]], [[DISPLAY]], [[SSH_*]], \ldots): they describe the
session in front of the user, never the task.

We therefore \emph{mirror} the live values of these session-identity
variables into the captured environment.  Mirroring works in both
directions: a variable present in the live environment overrides the
captured value, and a captured variable \emph{absent} from the live
environment is dropped.  The deletion half matters as much as the
override half---a stale [[TMUX]] alone convinces the child it is
inside tmux, and a stale [[SSH_AUTH_SOCK]] points at a dead socket.

<<mirror live session identity into [[proc_env]]>>=
for key in list(proc_env):
    if (
        key in SESSION_ENV_VARS
        or key.startswith("SSH_")
    ) and key not in os.environ:
        del proc_env[key]
for key in os.environ:
    if (
        key in SESSION_ENV_VARS
        or key.startswith("SSH_")
    ):
        proc_env[key] = os.environ[key]
@

The explicit list names the terminal identity an earlier version
already overlaid, plus the tmux and worker identity whose omission
caused the bug above.  [[SSH_*]] variables are matched by prefix
instead, since agent forwarding uses several of them.

<<constants>>=
SESSION_ENV_VARS = (
    "TERM", "DISPLAY", "COLORTERM",
    "TERM_PROGRAM", "TMUX", "TMUX_PANE",
    "USER",
)
