The command resolution follows a priority chain: [[--command]] overrides
the stored [[command]], which overrides [[$SHELL]].  We parse legacy
command strings with [[resolve_command_argv()]] so quoted arguments survive
intact, while the canonical [[-- prog args]] form keeps an exact argv list.
When the resolved launcher is [[claude]] or [[opencode]] and the user
specified only the bare command, we append the todo's notes or
description as a prompt argument.  If the user already supplied flags
such as [[-p]], we respect that command line exactly.

This gives two distinct workflows.  Storing plain [[claude]] means the
todo text itself is the prompt source, so later edits to notes or
description automatically change what the agent sees.  Storing
[[claude -p "..."]], by contrast, freezes the prompt in the command,
which is useful when the launcher instruction should stay stable even if
the todo text evolves.  Both fields are visible in [[edit --edit]]
(\cref{sec:editor-description}), the notes as the body and the
description in the front matter, and the notes win when both are set:
a prompt typed into the description of an item that already has notes
is not what the agent sees.

The notes reach the agent as they are stored, note headers included
(\cref{sec:todo-note-headers}).  We pass them through rather than strip
them, because they are useful context: the agent can tell the user's
instructions from what an earlier run reported, and which of them is
most recent.

When no command is named, we defer to [[build_launch_shell]]---the same
helper [[track start]] uses---so that the plain-versus-prompted shell
choice is made in exactly one place.  We can decide it here, before the
tmux/direct split, because [[pane]] and [[window]] are already known:
the helper returns a plain login shell for a tmux launch and a
prompt-prefixed shell for a direct one, seeding the prompt context as
part of building it, along with any temporary prompt files to clean up
afterwards.

An explicit command builds no shell and so misses that seeding, so we
apply the context to its environment here instead---mirroring
[[track start]].  It does nothing for a non-shell launcher such as
[[claude]] but lets an explicitly launched shell still carry the marker.

[[--no-command]] is checked here rather than on the item because
[[item.command]] is a resolved read: it answers with the worker
default when nothing is stored (\cref{todo:worker-defaults}), and the
flag has to silence that default as much as a stored command.  Nulling
the attribute would not do, since a later save must not persist the
override.  Guarding the fallback sends the launch down the shell
branch exactly as for a command-less item.

<<determine command to run>>=
from nytid.cli.track import build_launch_shell

cmd = run_argv
if cmd is None and item.command and not no_command:
    cmd, _ = resolve_command_argv(item.command, None)
if cmd:
    apply_context_prompt(todo_context, proc_env)
    prompt_text = item.notes or item.description
    if cmd[0] in ("claude", "opencode"):
        has_prompt = any(
            part in ("-p", "--prompt")
            for part in cmd[1:]
        )
        if prompt_text and not has_prompt:
            cmd = cmd + [prompt_text]
else:
    cmd, prompt_file, prompt_dir = build_launch_shell(
        todo_context, proc_env,
        tmux=bool(pane or window),
    )
@

Tmux gives the subprocess its own terminal while keeping the launch
under our control.  We reuse the [[track]] module's helper so both
commands share the same pane startup handshake, exit-status recording,
and blocking semantics.  A detached worktree run appends the shared
worktree cleanup segment (\cref{track:worktrees}) to the finalize
command, since by the time the pane dies this process is long gone.
For detached todo runs we swap the blocking
wait channel for a follow-up shell command that re-enters [[todo]] and
applies the pending/done cleanup policy after the pane process exits.
Blocked launches now rejoin the same post-exit path as direct
subprocesses: once tmux reports that the pane session is over, we still
reload the todo state, stop tracking, and ask whether the item should be
marked done.

Auto-suspend resolution follows the same pattern as [[track start]]:
the CLI flag overrides config, and we warn about [[focus-events]] at
launch time.  We recompute [[worker]] and [[track_labels]] here
rather than relying on the earlier tracking block, which may have
been skipped if the [[track]] module was unavailable.

<<resolve todo auto-suspend>>=
worker = session_worker(item)
track_labels = todo_track_labels(item, todo_id)
use_auto_suspend = should_auto_suspend(
    worker, auto_suspend,
)
if use_auto_suspend and (pane or window):
    warn_if_focus_events_off()
focus_labels_for_tmux = track_labels
focus_who_for_tmux = worker
focus_window_for_tmux = bool(window)
@

The labels are published \emph{unconditionally}---auto-suspend
travels separately as [[run_tmux_command]]'s [[auto_suspend]]
parameter.  An earlier version withheld the labels when
auto-suspend was off, which silently disabled every feature built
on label publication rather than suspension: the launched pane or
window was never registered as tracked, so ID-less todo commands
inside it could not resolve their task and [[todo next]] lost its
context.  [[track start]] was fixed to decouple the two long ago
(see the rationale in its [[<<run launched process>>]] chunk);
this brings [[todo]] in line.

The [[focus_window_for_tmux]] flag mirrors the computation in
[[track start]]'s [[<<run launched process>>]] chunk.  Without it,
[[run_tmux_command]] treats a [[--window]] launch as a plain pane
launch for auto-suspend purposes: pane-focus hooks then suspend
tracking the moment the user alt-tabs away, instead of the deferred
window-event buffering that keeps a tracked window \enquote{sticky}
across window switches.  Passing it makes [[todo ... -W]] register
the new window as a tracked window in exactly the same way as
[[track start -W]].

A more defensive API would have [[run_tmux_command]] derive
[[focus_window]] internally from [[window and focus_labels is not None]],
so that no caller could ever forget to opt in.  We keep it as an
explicit parameter for now---the [[track]] module already exposes
[[focus_window]] as a public knob and changing its semantics would
ripple through the tracked-window state machine---but the
duplicated computation here and in [[track]]'s
[[<<run launched process>>]] is a smell worth revisiting if a
third call site ever appears.

<<run in tmux>>=
try:
    exit_code_file = None
    if block:
        result = type(
            "TmuxResult", (),
            {
                "returncode": run_todo_tmux_command(
                    cmd,
                    cwd=proc_wd,
                    env=proc_env,
                    window=window,
                    window_name=(
                        todo_context
                        if window else None
                    ),
                    focus_labels=focus_labels_for_tmux,
                    focus_who=focus_who_for_tmux,
                    focus_window=focus_window_for_tmux,
                    auto_suspend=use_auto_suspend,
                ),
                "stdout": None,
            },
        )()
    else:
        exit_fd, exit_code_file = tempfile.mkstemp(
            prefix="nytid-todo-",
            suffix=".status",
        )
        os.close(exit_fd)
        finalize_cmd = build_detached_todo_finalize_command(
            todo_id, proc_wd, exit_code_file,
            reassign_to, launched_by,
        )
        if worktree_dir:
            finalize_cmd += \
                build_detached_worktree_cleanup_command(
                    worktree_dir
                )
        run_todo_tmux_command(
            cmd,
            cwd=proc_wd,
            env=proc_env,
            window=window,
            window_name=(
                todo_context
                if window else None
            ),
            block=False,
            exit_status_file=exit_code_file,
            on_exit_shell=finalize_cmd,
            focus_labels=focus_labels_for_tmux,
            focus_who=focus_who_for_tmux,
            focus_window=focus_window_for_tmux,
            auto_suspend=use_auto_suspend,
        )
        result = None
except subprocess.CalledProcessError as exc:
    typer.echo(
        f"tmux failed with exit code {exc.returncode}",
        err=True,
    )
    if exit_code_file is not None:
        try:
            os.unlink(exit_code_file)
        except OSError:
            pass
    raise typer.Exit(1)
except FileNotFoundError:
    typer.echo("tmux not found", err=True)
    if exit_code_file is not None:
        try:
            os.unlink(exit_code_file)
        except OSError:
            pass
    raise typer.Exit(1)
@

