@

The subprocess must behave differently depending on context.
Interactive users need a live terminal connection (pass-through
stdin/stdout/stderr), while headless agents have no terminal at
all---trying to read from a closed stdin would hang.  Redirecting
to [[subprocess.DEVNULL]] and [[subprocess.PIPE]] solves both
problems: no hang risk, and we capture output for the note.

Python's [[subprocess.run]] accepts a [[timeout]] parameter
natively, so we can retire the external [[timeout]] command
that wrapped the shell invocation
(see \cref{sec:agent-automation}).

As with all interactive child-process spawns, we ignore
\textsc{sigint} in the parent while the child is running.
[[subprocess.run]] resets [[SIG_IGN]] signals to [[SIG_DFL]] in
the child before [[exec]] (via [[restore_signals=True]]), so
Ctrl+C still reaches the child normally.  The parent resumes after
the child exits and can record the outcome.  We restore the
original handler at the start of the [[finally]] clause, before
the prompt-file cleanup, so the handler is reinstated even when
the cleanup itself fails.

<<run subprocess>>=
result = None
old_sigint = signal.signal(
    signal.SIGINT, signal.SIG_IGN
)
try:
    typer.echo(
        f"  Spawning in {proc_wd}: "
        f"{' '.join(cmd)}"
    )
    timeout_secs = (
        parse_timeout(timeout)
        if timeout else None
    )
    if headless:
        result = subprocess.run(
            cmd,
            cwd=proc_wd,
            env=proc_env,
            stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL,
            stdout=subprocess.PIPE,
            stderr=subprocess.STDOUT,
            text=True,
            timeout=timeout_secs,
        )
    else:
        result = subprocess.run(
            cmd,
            cwd=proc_wd,
            env=proc_env,
            stdin=sys.stdin,
            stdout=sys.stdout,
            stderr=sys.stderr,
            timeout=timeout_secs,
        )
except subprocess.TimeoutExpired as exc:
    <<handle subprocess timeout>>
except FileNotFoundError:
    typer.echo(
        f"Command not found: {cmd[0]}",
        err=True,
    )
finally:
    signal.signal(signal.SIGINT, old_sigint)
    <<cleanup temp prompt files>>
@

When a [[--timeout]] fires, [[subprocess.TimeoutExpired]] carries
any partial output captured before the kill signal.  We post that
partial output as a note so the teacher sees what the agent managed
to do, and reset the item to pending.

What we deliberately do \emph{not} do here is reassign the task, even
though this is where we learn that it timed out.  Reassigning is part
of the outcome, and the outcome is decided in one place, the exit
handler, after the stop and only if the run still holds the item
(\cref{sec:todo-run-taken-over}).  For an item activated by an older
version, which has no claimant, the stop also still takes the worker
from the assignee, and reassigning first would aim it at somebody who
never started tracking.  Recording [[timed_out]] lets the exit
handler apply the reassignment on the far side of the stop, which is
exactly what the non-timeout failure path in
[[<<handle headless exit status>>]] already does.

The note is posted to GitHub before the lock is taken and stored
inside a callback on the item as it is now
(\cref{sec:todo-read-modify-write}); the agent was running until a
moment ago and may well have added notes of its own.  For the same
reason the reset to pending is conditional: an item that is already
done was completed by someone---quite possibly by the agent itself,
just before the timeout killed it---and a timeout is no reason to
reopen it.  This is the check [[record_run_outcome]] makes too
(\cref{sec:todo-run-outcome}), and the note is signed the way its
notes are, by the worker that launched the run.  A takeover during
the run needs no check here: the item is still on the stack, so the
stored status stays \enquote{in-progress} whatever we write, and
whether it really goes back to pending is decided by the exit
handler below, which does check (\cref{sec:todo-run-taken-over}).

<<handle subprocess timeout>>=
timed_out = True
partial = (
    exc.stdout if exc.stdout else ""
)
_, todos = load_todos()
item = next(
    (t for t in todos if t.id == todo_id),
    None,
)
if item is not None:
    note = (
        f"**Timed out** after {timeout}\n\n"
    )
    if partial.strip():
        note += (
            f"Partial output:\n"
            f"```\n{partial.strip()}\n```"
        )
    post_note_comment(item, note)

    def record_timeout(next_id, todos, stack):
        timed = next(
            (t for t in todos if t.id == todo_id),
            None,
        )
        if timed is not None:
            append_note_to_item(timed, note, who=launched_by)
            if timed.status != "done":
                set_status(timed, "pending")
        return next_id, todos, stack

    mutate_todo_state(record_timeout)
typer.echo(
    f"  Timed out after {timeout}",
    err=True,
)
@

After the subprocess exits, we stop the todo through the same shared
hierarchy helper used by manual [[todo stop]] and detached tmux cleanup
(by way of [[stop_run]], which leaves an item that was taken over
during the run alone, \cref{sec:todo-run-taken-over}), then prompt
the user about completion status.  The prompt is the reason this
handler only \emph{decides} on the snapshot and leaves the storing to
[[record_run_outcome]] (\cref{sec:todo-run-outcome}): the question
can stay open for hours, and a note the agent's session adds in that
time must not be erased by the answer.  In headless mode we skip
the prompt: if the subprocess already marked the todo done (via
[[todo done]]), we respect that; otherwise we reset the todo and any
auto-stopped descendants to pending for retry.

When headless mode captured output (via [[subprocess.PIPE]]),
we post it as a note before updating the status.  This ensures
the teacher can review what the agent did, even if the task
failed or was reset.

<<handle subprocess exit>>=
todo_id, item, descendants, taken_by = stop_run(
    todo_id, launched_by
)
if item is None:
    return
run_notes = []
<<post headless output as note>>

mark_done = False
hand_to = None
if item.status == "done" or taken_by is not None:
    pass
elif timed_out:
    <<handle timeout exit status>>
elif headless:
    <<handle headless exit status>>
elif typer.confirm(
    f"Mark todo #{todo_id} as done?"
):
    mark_done = True

item, todos = record_run_outcome(
    todo_id,
    {child.id for child in descendants},
    notes=run_notes,
    done=mark_done,
    reassign_to=hand_to,
    launched_by=launched_by,
)
if item is not None and item.status == "done":
    auto_complete_parent(item, todos, who=launched_by)
@

The parent is auto-completed on behalf of [[launched_by]], the worker
whose run it was.  The item handed back here was reloaded after the
stop, which released its claim, so the worker that
[[auto_complete_parent]] would derive from it is the assignee: when an
agent ran a task from the user's queue, a completed run would then be
judged as the user's, and the agent's own parent would never be
completed.  Let's verify that case:

<<test functions>>=
