@

The exit code and captured output form a diagnostic record---the
teacher can review what the agent did without needing to check
system logs or reproduce the run.

<<post headless output as note>>=
if headless and result is not None:
    output = getattr(result, "stdout", None)
    code = result.returncode
    if output and output.strip():
        note = (
            f"Exit code: {code}\n\n"
            f"```\n{output.strip()}\n```"
        )
    else:
        note = f"Exit code: {code} (no output)"
    post_note_comment(item, note)
    run_notes.append(note)
@

This is the deferred half of the timeout handling: tracking has now
been stopped against the worker who started it, so we are finally free
to hand the task to whoever should look at it.

The branch sits ahead of the [[headless]] one and ahead of the
interactive prompt because a killed process never reported an outcome.
Asking \enquote{mark todo as done?} about work we ourselves cut short
invites the user to record a completion that did not happen, so a
timed-out task always goes back to pending regardless of how it was
launched---unless it was recorded as done before the kill, in which
case the branch is not reached at all.  Leaving [[mark_done]] unset
is all that takes, since
[[record_run_outcome]] resets an unfinished item to pending; the
branch only has to say who receives it.

<<handle timeout exit status>>=
hand_to = reassign_to
@

In headless mode, a non-zero exit code indicates failure.
If [[--reassign-to]] is set, we hand the task back to a human
so they can investigate.  A zero exit
code where the subprocess did not mark the task done means the
agent finished without completing the task---we reset to
pending for the next retry.  As in the timeout branch, the reset is
[[record_run_outcome]]'s default, so only the reassignment is spelled
out here.

<<handle headless exit status>>=
if (
    result is not None
    and result.returncode != 0
):
    hand_to = reassign_to
@

