\subsection{The [[adopt]] Command}
\label{todo:adopt}

[[todo start --window]] always creates the window it tracks.  The
[[adopt]] command covers the windows it cannot: a shell the user
already has open and now wants to count as work on a task, and a
task window that lost its tracked status (state-file loss, see the
recovery discussion in \cref{track:adopt}).  It is to
[[todo start --window]] what [[track adopt]] is to
[[track start --window]]---and it is built on exactly that
machinery: the [[track]] module's [[adopt_current_window]] helper
does all window-state work, while this command adds the todo-side
bookkeeping around it: activating the task and its ancestors,
deriving the label set, the [[todo:<id>]] window-name convention,
and the finalize-on-exit hook.

The ID is required, unlike other todo commands.  Their ID-less
forms resolve the task from the current window's tracked
labels---precisely what does not exist in a window that adopt is
about to fix.

<<argument and option definitions>>=
adopt_id_arg = typer.Argument(
    ..., help="Todo ID to work on in this window",
    autocompletion=complete_todo_ids)
adopt_rename_opt = typer.Option(
    "--rename",
    help="Rename the window to the todo:<id> "
         "naming used by start --window")
@

<<adopt command>>=
@cli.command()
def adopt(
    todo_id: Annotated[int, adopt_id_arg],
    auto_suspend: Annotated[
        bool | None, auto_suspend_opt
    ] = None,
    rename: Annotated[bool, adopt_rename_opt] = False,
    who: Annotated[str, acting_who_opt] = default_username,
):
    """Adopt the current tmux window as work on a todo.

    Marks the window as tracked with the task's labels and starts
    them, activating the task (and its ancestors) if needed --
    like `todo start --window`, but for a window that already
    exists.  Also the recovery path when a task's window lost its
    tracked status.
    """
    <<fail adopt early outside tmux>>
    <<activate adopted todo and ancestors>>
    adopt_window_for_todo(
        todo_id, item, worker, auto_suspend, rename=rename,
    )
    typer.echo(
        f"Adopted window for: #{todo_id} {item.title}"
    )
@

The window half of the command is a function of its own, for the
same reason [[track]] splits [[adopt_current_window]] from its
[[adopt]] command: [[start]] adopts the window it runs in when it
launches nothing there (\cref{todo:start-settle}), and it needs
precisely these steps---the finalize decision, the call into
[[track]], and the hook---minus the activation, which it has
already done in its own order.  The helper returns [[track]]'s
result so the caller can report what was started; on a window that
cannot be resolved it exits with the message below, which reads
the same from both commands.

[[arm_finalize]] exists because that hook is the one step a caller
can be sure is wrong for it.  The default, [[None]], means
\enquote{decide from the pane's history}, which is the right answer
whenever the adopted window is where the work will end.  A blocking
direct launch knows better: it will handle the child's exit itself
(\cref{todo:start-spawn}), so it passes [[False]] and leaves the
pane hook-free.

<<helpers>>=
def adopt_window_for_todo(
    todo_id, item, worker, auto_suspend, rename=False,
    arm_finalize=None,
):
    """Adopt the current tmux window as work on `item`.

    Publishes the task's labels on the window through the track
    module, arms the finalize hook, and optionally renames the
    window `todo:<id>`.  `arm_finalize` defaults to arming the
    hook only when the pane carries none; pass False to leave the
    pane alone when the caller handles the exit itself.  Returns
    the adoption result dict from `adopt_current_window`; exits
    with status 1 when the tmux window cannot be resolved.
    """
    <<decide whether to arm the finalize hook>>
    <<adopt window via track module>>
    <<arm todo finalize hook for adopted window>>
    return adopted
@

The tmux check comes before any state change: adoption of the
stack and labels is not atomic with window adoption, so a doomed
invocation must fail before it mutates anything.

<<fail adopt early outside tmux>>=
if not (
    os.environ.get("TMUX")
    and os.environ.get("TMUX_PANE")
):
    typer.echo(
        "Not inside a tmux pane; "
        "run adopt in the window to use.",
        err=True,
    )
    raise typer.Exit(1)
@

Whether adopt should arm a finalize hook depends on the window's
history, and must be decided \emph{now}, before adoption installs
its own pane-exit hooks and muddies the evidence.  A window that
[[todo start]] or [[track start]] launched already carries
exit machinery installed at launch: a wrapper around the command
plus pane-exit hooks, sharing one completion guard so finalize
runs exactly once.  That pair survived whatever state loss brought
the user here (it lives in the pane and its hooks, not in nytid's
state files), so re-arming would make finalize run twice---the
fresh hook cannot share the launch-time guard.  A manually created
window, by contrast, has no pane-exit hooks at all.  So the pane's
exit hooks are the discriminator---unless the caller has already
answered the question, in which case we take its answer and do not
even ask tmux.

<<helpers>>=
def pane_has_exit_hooks(pane_id):
    """Return whether a pane carries pane-exit hooks."""
    result = subprocess.run(
        ["tmux", "show-hooks", "-p", "-t", pane_id],
        capture_output=True,
        text=True,
        check=False,
    )
    if not isinstance(result.stdout, str):
        return False
    return (
        "pane-exited" in result.stdout
        or "pane-died" in result.stdout
    )
<<decide whether to arm the finalize hook>>=
if arm_finalize is None:
    arm_finalize = not pane_has_exit_hooks(
        os.environ["TMUX_PANE"]
    )
@

Activation precedes publication, mirroring [[todo start]]: the
stack must name the task before any window state points at it.
[[activate_todo_state]] tolerates an already active task (no
duplicate stack entry), so adopting mid-recovery is idempotent---as
long as the task is active for the worker adopting it.  A task that
another worker has active is refused by the claim
(\cref{sec:todo-claim}), and recovering that worker's window means
adopting as that worker, with [[--who]].  [[adopt]] has no
[[--force]]: it repairs a window, and taking a task from someone is
a decision for [[start --force]].
Ancestor activation keeps parents' time running, exactly as a
fresh [[start]] would; window publication below carries only the
task's own labels, again exactly as [[todo start --window]] does.

<<activate adopted todo and ancestors>>=
todo_id, item, next_id, todos, stack, _ = (
    activate_todo_state(todo_id, who=who)
)
ensure_ancestors_active(
    item, todos, stack,
    update_tmux_context=False,
)
worker = session_worker(item)
@

The window work is one call into [[track]].  If the tmux context
cannot be resolved after all (the server died between our early
check and now), the task stays active---the same state a plain
[[todo start]] outside tmux produces---and we say so rather than
pretending nothing happened.

<<adopt window via track module>>=
from nytid.cli.track import (
    adopt_current_window,
    build_context_name,
)

adopted = adopt_current_window(
    todo_track_labels(item, todo_id),
    worker,
    auto_suspend,
    rename_to=(
        build_context_name("todo", [str(todo_id)])
        if rename else None
    ),
)
if adopted is None:
    typer.echo(
        "Could not resolve the tmux window; "
        f"#{todo_id} is active but the window "
        "is not tracked.",
        err=True,
    )
    raise typer.Exit(1)
if adopted["started"]:
    typer.echo(
        f"  Tracking: "
        f"{', '.join(sorted(adopted['started']))}"
    )
if adopted["already_tracking"]:
    typer.echo(
        f"  Already tracking: "
        f"{', '.join(sorted(adopted['already_tracking']))}"
    )
@

For a hook-virgin window we arm the finalize half that a launch
would have installed.  Only the hook half is retrofittable: the
wrapper half wraps the launched command, and this window's shell
is already running.  The hook alone suffices---it records the exit
status from tmux's [[#{pane_dead_status}]] formats instead of the
wrapper's [[$?]]---so when the adopted shell exits, the task is
finalized just as if [[todo start --window]] had created it.

<<arm todo finalize hook for adopted window>>=
if arm_finalize:
    from nytid.cli.track import (
        build_tmux_hook_command,
        install_tmux_exit_hooks,
        make_tmux_completion_guard,
    )
    exit_fd, exit_code_file = tempfile.mkstemp(
        prefix="nytid-todo-",
        suffix=".status",
    )
    os.close(exit_fd)
    finalize_cmd = (
        build_detached_todo_finalize_command(
            todo_id, os.getcwd(), exit_code_file,
            launched_by=worker,
        )
    )
    hook_command = build_tmux_hook_command(
        status_file=exit_code_file,
        on_exit_shell=finalize_cmd,
        completion_guard=(
            make_tmux_completion_guard(exit_code_file)
        ),
    )
    install_tmux_exit_hooks(
        adopted["pane_id"], hook_command,
    )
@

Let's verify the failure mode, the delegation to [[track]], and
both finalize decisions:

<<test functions>>=
