\section{ICS Export}

iCalendar (\textsc{ics}) defines two relevant component types: [[VEVENT]]
for time-anchored occurrences and [[VTODO]] for tasks. We use [[VTODO]]
because todos have a deadline and a duration but no fixed start
time---they express \emph{when something must be done by} and
\emph{how long it will take}, not \emph{when you will do it}. Most
calendar apps (Thunderbird, Apple Calendar, Nextcloud) can import
[[VTODO]] components from a [[.ics]] file and display them as tasks
in a separate task list.

\subsection{The [[export]] Command}

As with [[todo ls]], the export format is a closed set of meaningful
choices.  Modeling it as an enum lets Typer validate and complete the
accepted values instead of treating [[--format]] as arbitrary text.

<<constants>>=
class TodoExportFormat(str, Enum):
    """Export formats for `todo export`."""
    vtodo = "vtodo"
@

<<argument and option definitions>>=
export_output_opt = typer.Option(
    "--output", "-o",
    help="Output file (stdout if not specified)")
export_all_opt = typer.Option(
    "--all", "-a",
    help="Include done items")
export_format_opt = typer.Option(
    "--format", "-f",
    help="Export format (vtodo)")
@

<<export command>>=
@cli.command()
def export(
    output: Annotated[str | None,
                      export_output_opt] = None,
    all_items: Annotated[bool,
                         export_all_opt] = False,
    format: Annotated[TodoExportFormat,
                      export_format_opt] = TodoExportFormat.vtodo,
):
    """Export todos as ICS (VTODO)."""
    _, todos = load_todos()

    if not all_items:
        todos = [t for t in todos if t.status != "done"]

    if not todos:
        typer.echo("No todos to export.", err=True)
        return

    if format == TodoExportFormat.vtodo:
        ics_data = export_vtodo(todos)
    else:
        typer.echo(
            f"Unknown format: {format}", err=True
        )
        raise typer.Exit(1)

    if output:
        with open(output, "w") as f:
            f.write(ics_data)
        typer.echo(
            f"Exported {len(todos)} todos to {output}"
        )
    else:
        print(ics_data)
@

\subsection{VTODO Export}

The VTODO format maps naturally to our data model. ICS priority uses
a 1--9 scale (1 = highest, 9 = lowest), so we need to invert and
clamp our float priority.  Unranked items are still exported, but we do
not invent an ICS priority for them.
The [[VERSION]] and [[PRODID]] properties are required by RFC~5545;
[[icalendar]] does not add them implicitly, so we must.

<<vtodo export function>>=
def export_vtodo(todos: list[TodoItem]) -> str:
    """Export todos as VTODO components in ICS format.

    Maps our float priority to the ICS 1-9 scale and
    includes deadline, duration, status, and labels.
    """
    import icalendar

    cal = icalendar.Calendar()
    cal.add("VERSION", "2.0")
    cal.add("PRODID", "-//dbosk//nytid//EN")

    now = datetime.datetime.now()
    max_prio = max(
        (
            require_effective_priority(t, now)
            for t in todos
            if t.priority is not None
        ),
        default=1.0,
    )

    for item in todos:
        todo = icalendar.Todo()
        todo.add("SUMMARY", item.title)
        if item.description:
            todo.add("DESCRIPTION", item.description)

        <<map priority to ics scale>>
        <<set vtodo deadline and duration>>
        <<set vtodo status>>
        <<relate the vtodo to its parents>>

        cal.add_component(todo)

    return cal.to_ical().decode()
@

ICS priority 1 is highest, 9 is lowest. We normalize our effective
priority into this range, but only for items that already have a numeric
priority.

<<map priority to ics scale>>=
ep = effective_priority(item, now)
if ep is not None:
    if max_prio > 0:
        normalized = ep / max_prio
        ics_prio = max(1, min(9, round(
            9 - normalized * 8
        )))
    else:
        ics_prio = 5
    todo.add("PRIORITY", ics_prio)
@

A VTODO cannot carry both [[DUE]] and [[DURATION]] (RFC~5545 makes
them mutually exclusive), so a deadline takes precedence: knowing
\emph{when} something is due is more actionable for a calendar app
than knowing how long it takes.
<<set vtodo deadline and duration>>=
deadline_set = False
if item.deadline:
    try:
        todo.add("DUE", datetime.datetime.fromisoformat(
            item.deadline
        ))
        deadline_set = True
    except ValueError:
        pass
if item.estimated and not deadline_set:
    todo.add("DURATION", datetime.timedelta(
        hours=item.estimated
    ))
@

An item's parents travel along as relations, so that a task manager
reading the file can show the hierarchy, several parents included.
RFC~5545 relates components by their [[UID]], which every [[VTODO]]
is required to have anyway, and RFC~9253 adds the [[RELTYPE]]
[[PARENT]] that says which way a relation points; a component may carry
several of them, one per parent.  The [[UID]] is made from the todo id,
which is never reused, so it stays the same from one export to the
next and an application that imports again updates its tasks instead
of duplicating them.  The top level is not an item and has no
component, so it gets no relation.

Every component carries the [[UID]], so the export of a plain tree
changes too: it gains one line per task, which is what a calendar
application needs to recognise a task it has seen before.  The id is
unique only within one data directory, though.  Two users who both
export their lists into one calendar get the same [[UID]] for their
items with the same number, and the calendar takes them for one task.
That is a known limit of deriving the [[UID]] from the id; a [[UID]]
unique across data directories would have to be stored with each item.

<<relate the vtodo to its parents>>=
todo.add("UID", vtodo_uid(item.id))
for parent_id in real_parents_of(item):
    todo.add(
        "RELATED-TO", vtodo_uid(parent_id),
        parameters={"RELTYPE": "PARENT"},
    )
<<vtodo export function>>=
def vtodo_uid(todo_id):
    """Return the iCalendar UID of todo `todo_id`."""
    return f"todo-{todo_id}@nytid"
@

<<set vtodo status>>=
status_map = {
    "pending": "NEEDS-ACTION",
    "in-progress": "IN-PROCESS",
    "done": "COMPLETED",
}
todo.add("STATUS", status_map.get(
    item.status, "NEEDS-ACTION"
))
@

\subsection{The [[import]] Command}

The [[import]] command fetches GitHub issues and/or pull requests
and creates local todo items for them. This is the primary
onboarding path for GitHub-tracked work into the local priority
system.

The [[--skip-priority]] flag deserves special attention. When
importing many items, running interactive binary search for each
would be tedious. Instead, we offer an age-based heuristic:
older issues get lower priority, since items that have lingered
are implicitly less urgent than recent ones. We scale
[[created_at]] timestamps linearly across the batch so the
newest item gets the highest priority.

An unranked parent overrides both ranking modes. Without a
numeric parent priority there is no child interval to place the
imported items into, so children of such a parent stay unranked
until the parent itself is reprioritized.

Imported items are assigned to the current user by default
(via [[--who]]), matching the behavior of [[todo add]] and
[[todo ls]]. This ensures that imported items appear when
filtering by worker, without requiring an extra flag.

As [[todo import]] grows beyond simple issue ingestion, it should feel
like the batch counterpart of [[todo add]]: imported items may belong
under the currently active parent task, carry deadlines or estimates,
and capture the working context in which we discovered them.

<<github commands>>=
<<import command>>
<<sync command>>
<<note command>>
@

The [[--type]] flag accepts exactly three values.  Modelling it as an
enumeration rather than a free string gives us two things for free:
Typer rejects a misspelled value with a helpful message instead of
silently importing nothing, and the shell completes the valid values.
Because the members subclass [[str]], the existing comparisons against
literal strings ([[item_type in ("all", "issue")]]) keep working
unchanged.

<<constants>>=
class ImportType(str, Enum):
    """Which GitHub item kinds ``todo import`` fetches."""
    issue = "issue"
    pr = "pr"
    all = "all"
@

The compatibility we rely on is that each member equals its string
value and participates in membership tests, so the import command's
existing string comparisons need no change:

<<test functions>>=
def test_import_type_enum_values():
  """Members equal their string values for back-compat."""
  assert ImportType.issue == "issue"
  assert ImportType.pr == "pr"
  assert ImportType.all == "all"
  assert ImportType.all in ("all", "issue")
@

