When [[--number]] is given we bypass the list API entirely and
call [[gh_fetch_issue]] or [[gh_fetch_pr]] directly---fetching
100 items just to extract one is wasteful, especially in large
repos where pagination would be needed. The fetch functions do
not include [[number]] in their JSON fields (the view commands
already know it), so we take it from the CLI argument.  We do not
inject a placeholder [[createdAt]], although earlier versions did:
the fetches now return the real one, and the age heuristic supplies
its own fallback for an item without it.  A placeholder would also
be stored as the item's creation time.

Issues and PRs share the same number sequence in a
repository---GitHub's internal model treats PRs as a
specialisation of issues---so [[#5]] is \emph{either} an issue
or a PR, never both. When [[--type]] is [[all]] and [[--number]]
is given, we cannot determine the type from the number alone. We
default to [[issue]] (by far the common case); the user must
pass [[--type pr]] explicitly to import a pull request.

<<fetch github items>>=
gh_items = []
if number is not None:
    <<fetch single github item>>
else:
    <<fetch all github items>>
@

<<fetch single github item>>=
effective_type = (
    item_type if item_type != "all" else "issue"
)
if effective_type == "pr":
    data = gh_fetch_pr(repo, number)
else:
    data = gh_fetch_issue(repo, number)
data["_type"] = effective_type
data["number"] = number
gh_items.append(data)
@

We fetch issues, PRs, or both depending on the [[--type]] flag:

<<fetch all github items>>=
if item_type in ("all", "issue"):
    for issue in gh_list_issues(repo):
        issue["_type"] = "issue"
        gh_items.append(issue)
if item_type in ("all", "pr"):
    for pr in gh_list_prs(repo):
        pr["_type"] = "pr"
        gh_items.append(pr)
@

We skip items that already exist locally by matching on repo,
type, and number---preventing duplicate imports.  The match is on the
GitHub identity only, never on the local item's placement or status: an
issue imported a month ago as a forgotten top-level item is still the
same issue, and importing it again under today's parent would leave two
local todos claiming the same GitHub number, with [[done]] closing the
issue from whichever one the user happens to finish first.

Skipping silently, however, turned out to be a trap.  After
[[import owner/repo -n 127]] printed nothing but
[[Imported 0 items.]], the user concluded the import was broken, while
in fact the issue already lived as an unrelated, long-forgotten local
item.  The information that would have resolved the confusion is the
local ID: with it, the user can [[todo show]] the existing item or
[[todo edit N --parent M]] it into place; without it, the only visible
fact is that nothing happened.  We therefore remember which local item
each skipped GitHub item collided with, so that the command can report
the collision below instead of swallowing it.

<<filter already imported>>=
existing = {
    github_key(t): t for t in todos if t.github
}

new_items = []
skipped_items = []
for gh_item in gh_items:
    key = (
        repo,
        gh_item["_type"],
        str(gh_item["number"]),
    )
    if key in existing:
        skipped_items.append((gh_item, existing[key]))
    else:
        new_items.append(gh_item)
@

The identity is needed twice, here and again when the import is
committed, so it is a function rather than an inline tuple.

<<helpers>>=
def github_key(item):
    """Return the (repo, type, number) a todo is linked to."""
    return (
        item.github["repo"],
        item.github.get("type", "issue"),
        item.github["number"],
    )
@

The age-based priority heuristic scales creation timestamps
linearly so that the newest item in the batch gets the highest
priority. If the user prefers interactive ranking, we fall back
to binary search.

When importing from multiple repositories, filtering by
repository is the most common need---but requiring users to
remember [[--label owner/repo]] on every import is error-prone.
We therefore always include the repository name as a label.
GitHub's own labels (such as [[bug]] or [[enhancement]]) tend
to be project-specific and would clutter a cross-repository
task list, so they are only included when [[--github-labels]]
is passed. Any additional labels supplied via [[--label]] on
the command line are appended last.

An issue that is already closed on GitHub is imported as done, and it
gets its status through [[set_status]] like any other completion
(\cref{sec:todo-closed}).  Its closing time is the one GitHub reports
(\cref{sec:todo-github-closed}), so an issue closed last month does
not look as if it had been finished today; only when GitHub gives no
time does the import stamp its own.  The creation time is GitHub's
too, for the same reason and so that the item is not closed before it
was created.

Taking [[created]] from GitHub has one effect beyond the record.  The
deadline boost measures how long an item has waited by the span from
[[created]] to the deadline (\cref{todo:effective-priority}).  An old
issue imported with a near deadline therefore starts with most of its
boost, as an item that has waited that long should, where the import
time would have made it look brand new.

<<create todos from github items>>=
if skip_priority and new_items:
    <<compute age-based priorities>>
else:
    priorities = {}

imported = []
for gh_item in new_items:
    gh_labels = list(inherited_labels)
    if repo not in gh_labels:
        gh_labels.append(repo)
    if github_labels:
        gh_labels.extend(
            lb["name"]
            for lb in gh_item.get("labels", [])
        )
    if label:
        gh_labels.extend(label)

    gh_state = gh_item.get("state", "OPEN")
    status = (
        "done"
        if gh_state in ("CLOSED", "MERGED")
        else "pending"
    )

    if parent_item is not None and parent_item.priority is None:
        priority = None
    elif skip_priority:
        priority = priorities.get(
            gh_item["number"], 0.0
        )
    else:
        candidate = TodoItem(
            id=CANDIDATE_ID,
            title=gh_item["title"],
            labels=gh_labels,
            parents=[resolved_parent],
            deadline=inherited_deadline,
            estimated=(
                parse_hours(estimate)
                if estimate else None
            ),
        )
        active = get_active_siblings(
            todos, candidate, who=who
        )
        priority = binary_search_priority(
            active, candidate,
            parent=parent_item,
            todos=todos,
        )

    item = TodoItem(
        id=next_id,
        title=gh_item["title"],
        labels=gh_labels,
        priority=priority,
        who=who,
        created=(
            github_time(gh_item, "createdAt")
            or datetime.datetime.now().isoformat()
        ),
        deadline=inherited_deadline,
        estimated=(
            parse_hours(estimate)
            if estimate else None
        ),
        description=description or "",
        parents=[resolved_parent],
        wd=resolved_wd,
        worktree=normalize_worktree_name(worktree),
        env=resolved_env,
        command=run_command,
        discovered_from=resolved_discovered_from,
        github={
            "repo": repo,
            "type": gh_item["_type"],
            "number": str(gh_item["number"]),
        },
    )
    set_status(
        item, status,
        closed_at=github_time(gh_item, "closedAt"),
    )
    todos.append(item)
    next_id += 1
    imported.append(item)
@

The items above are built on the snapshot, and each is appended to it
so that the next item's binary search compares against the ones
imported before it.  Their IDs are therefore provisional: they come
from the snapshot's [[next_id]], which another command may have used
meanwhile.  The commit gives each item its real ID from the fresh
state (\cref{sec:todo-read-modify-write}), re-checks the parent as
[[add]] does, and runs the duplicate check once more, since the
fetching and the questions leave ample time for an overlapping
[[import]] of the same repository.  An item that turns out to be
stored already is left out, which is what the check above would have
done had it seen it.  It is also added to [[skipped_items]], in the
shape that check gives its entries, so that the skipped table and its
count in [[<<report skipped duplicates>>]] include it; the extra line
printed here says why it was not caught earlier.  Each item that is
stored goes through [[settle_placement]], as [[add]]'s does
(\cref{sec:sibling-above-squeezed}): it may cut short the range of
the sibling above it, and the next item is placed against the result.

<<commit the imported todos under the lock>>=
def append_imported(next_id, todos, stack):
    if resolved_parent is not None:
        require_todo(todos, resolved_parent)
    stored = {
        github_key(t): t for t in todos if t.github
    }
    committed = []
    for item in imported:
        local = stored.get(github_key(item))
        if local is not None:
            typer.echo(
                f"  Skipped {item.title}: imported "
                f"meanwhile as #{local.id}"
            )
            skipped_items.append((
                {
                    "_type": item.github["type"],
                    "number": item.github["number"],
                },
                local,
            ))
            continue
        item.id = next_id
        next_id += 1
        todos.append(item)
        settle_placement(todos, item)
        committed.append(item)
    return next_id, todos, stack, committed


*_, committed = mutate_todo_state(append_imported)
imported_count = len(committed)
