\subsection{The [[sync]] Command}

The [[sync]] command implements bidirectional synchronization:
local done status propagates to GitHub (close the issue), and
GitHub closed status propagates locally (mark as done). Title
changes on GitHub are also pulled in.

We intentionally continue on individual item failures---a
network blip for one issue should not prevent syncing the rest.

<<argument and option definitions>>=
sync_repo_opt = typer.Option(
    "--repo", "-r",
    help="Only sync items from this repo",
    autocompletion=complete_known_repos)
@

<<sync command>>=
@cli.command()
def sync(
    repo: Annotated[str | None,
                    sync_repo_opt] = None,
):
    """Sync todo items with GitHub issues/PRs."""
    require_gh()
    _, todos = load_todos()
    changes = 0
    touched = []

    for item in todos:
        if not item.github:
            continue
        if repo and item.github["repo"] != repo:
            continue

        touched.append((item_fields(item), item))
        gh = item.github
        item_type = gh.get("type", "issue")
        try:
            if item_type == "pr":
                data = gh_fetch_pr(
                    gh["repo"], gh["number"]
                )
            else:
                data = gh_fetch_issue(
                    gh["repo"], gh["number"]
                )
        except Exception as e:
            typer.echo(
                f"  Warning: #{item.id} "
                f"fetch failed: {e}",
                err=True,
            )
            continue

        gh_state = data.get("state", "OPEN")
        <<sync state from github>>
        <<sync state to github>>
        <<sync title from github>>

    <<store the synced fields under the lock>>
    typer.echo(f"Sync complete. {changes} changes.")
@

A sync makes one network round trip per linked item, so it can run
for a while, and it works on the snapshot throughout.  Each item it
looks at is remembered as it was read, and the commit replays only
what the sync changed---a status or a title---onto the stored items
(\cref{sec:todo-read-modify-write}).  A note added to an item during
the sync therefore stays.  An item removed meanwhile is skipped with a
warning, as [[reprioritize]] does, rather than failing the whole
sync.

The closing time is the one change the replay must not always carry.
If the stored item was completed while the sync was fetching---the
user ran [[todo done]] meanwhile---it already has a closing time, and
an item that is already done keeps its stamp (\cref{sec:todo-closed}).
Had the sync seen the fresh item, [[set_status]] would have left that
stamp alone; replaying the snapshot's [[closed]] would instead replace
it with GitHub's time.  So for an item that is done in the store we
skip [[closed]], and the stored stamp stays.

<<store the synced fields under the lock>>=
def store_synced(next_id, todos, stack):
    by_id = {t.id: t for t in todos}
    for before, item in touched:
        if item.id in by_id:
            fresh = by_id[item.id]
            keep = ("closed",) if fresh.status == "done" else ()
            replay_item_edits(fresh, before, item, skip=keep)
        elif item.to_dict() != before:
            typer.echo(
                f"  Warning: #{item.id} no longer exists; "
                "its sync changes were not stored",
                err=True,
            )
    return next_id, todos, stack


mutate_todo_state(store_synced)
@

