\subsection{The [[note]] Command}

The [[note]] command adds a note to a todo item. For
GitHub-linked items, it posts a comment on the issue or PR. For
local items, it records the text in the [[notes]] field: appended
when the text arrives as a fragment, replacing the field when the
user edited the notes as a whole.

Like [[view]] and [[done]], [[note]] defaults to the currently active
todo when no ID is given.  This keeps the common workflow short:
start work, then record progress without retyping the task number.
An agent passes [[--who]] with its own name, so that its notes land
on its own task rather than on the user's
(\cref{sec:todo-acting-worker}).

Message source follows a priority chain: [[--message]] flag
first, [[--edit]] opens an editor, and if neither is given, we
read from standard input. This mirrors [[git commit]]'s
[[--message]] vs editor workflow.

<<argument and option definitions>>=
note_message_opt = typer.Option(
    "--message", "-m",
    help="Note text (inline)")
@

<<note command>>=
@cli.command()
def note(
    todo_id: Annotated[int | None,
                       todo_id_arg] = None,
    message: Annotated[str | None,
                       note_message_opt] = None,
    edit: Annotated[bool, editor_opt] = False,
    who: Annotated[str, acting_who_opt] = default_username,
):
    """Add a note to a todo item.

    If no ID given, notes the current active task of the
    worker given by --who.

    With `--edit`, a local item opens its current notes for
    editing and the saved buffer becomes the new notes; a
    GitHub-linked item opens an empty buffer for a new comment.
    """
    _, todos = load_todos()
    todo_id = resolve_todo_id(
        todo_id, "add a note",
        who=who, todos=todos,
    )
    item = next(
        (t for t in todos if t.id == todo_id), None
    )
    if item is None:
        typer.echo(
            f"Error: todo #{todo_id} not found",
            err=True,
        )
        raise typer.Exit(1)

    <<resolve note text>>
    <<record the note>>
@

The three sources differ in more than where the characters come
from.  [[--message]] and standard input hand us a fragment: the
user typed it without seeing what is already stored, so the only
thing it can mean is \enquote{add this to what is there.}  The
editor on a local item is the opposite.  It opens on the whole of
[[item.notes]], so what comes back \emph{is} the notes as the user
now wants them---including the lines the user rewrote or deleted.
Appending that buffer stored every pre-filled line a second time,
which made the pre-fill a trap rather than a convenience.

GitHub-linked items keep appending even under [[--edit]], because
there the unit of writing is a comment and not the issue body.
The buffer starts empty, the text becomes a new comment, and
[[item.notes]] is only a local mirror of the comment stream;
replacing that mirror with a single comment would drop the earlier
ones that GitHub still shows.

The editor branch therefore records not only the text but whether
it stands for the whole of the notes:

<<resolve note text>>=
replace_notes = False
if message:
    note_text = message
elif edit:
    initial = ""
    if not item.github and item.notes:
        initial = item.notes
    note_text = open_editor(initial)
    replace_notes = not item.github
else:
    note_text = sys.stdin.read()
if not note_text.strip():
    typer.echo("Empty note, cancelled.")
    raise typer.Exit(0)
@

An empty buffer keeps the meaning it always had: the user is
cancelling, not clearing.  That reading costs nothing, because
clearing is already what [[edit -E]] does when the body under the
front matter is deleted (\cref{sec:edit-via-editor}), and it
protects the far more common case of an editor closed by accident
on notes that took a week to accumulate.  What we do store is
stripped, for the same reason [[parse_editor_content]] strips the
notes it parses: the trailing newline an editor leaves behind is
not part of what the user wrote.

The check comes after all three sources, so a [[--message]] of only
blanks is cancelled the same way, rather than posted as an empty
GitHub comment or stored as a bare note header
(\cref{sec:todo-note-headers}).

Both paths store the note inside a locked callback on the item as it
is \emph{now} (\cref{sec:todo-read-modify-write}); [[item]] is the
snapshot the command started from, and an editor session can last long
enough for an agent to add a note of its own.  For the replacing path
that raises a question: whose notes win?  We let the buffer win only if
the user changed it.  A buffer saved as it was opened says nothing
about the notes, so storing it would erase whatever arrived meanwhile
for no reason; a changed buffer is the user's version and replaces the
stored one, as it always has.

\enquote{Changed} has to be judged on what saving would store, not on
the raw text.  Saving strips the buffer, so an untouched buffer opened
on notes that end in a newline---as notes read from standard input
usually do---would differ from [[item.notes]] by that newline alone
and pass for an edit.  We therefore compare with [[initial]] stripped
the same way: that is exactly what the untouched buffer stores.  When
the stored notes are still the ones the buffer was opened on, nobody
else wrote meanwhile, and we store the buffer either way, which keeps
the tidying an untouched save has always done.

<<record the note>>=
if replace_notes:
    edited_notes = note_text.strip()

    def replace_the_notes(next_id, todos, stack):
        fresh = require_todo(todos, todo_id)
        if (
            edited_notes != initial.strip()
            or fresh.notes == item.notes
        ):
            fresh.notes = edited_notes
        return next_id, todos, stack

    mutate_todo_state(replace_the_notes)
    typer.echo(f"Notes of #{todo_id} updated")
else:
    <<post or append the note>>
@

The appending path keeps its two halves.  A GitHub-linked item needs a
working [[gh]] before we promise that the comment was posted;
[[post_note_comment]] posts it, before the lock is taken, and
[[append_note_to_item]] mirrors it locally inside the callback.  The
two messages name the outcome rather than the command, so a user
who expected one path and got the other sees it immediately.

<<post or append the note>>=
if item.github:
    require_gh()
post_note_comment(item, note_text)


def append_the_note(next_id, todos, stack):
    append_note_to_item(
        require_todo(todos, todo_id), note_text, who=who,
    )
    return next_id, todos, stack


mutate_todo_state(append_the_note)
if item.github:
    gh = item.github
    typer.echo(
        f"Comment posted on "
        f"{gh['repo']}#{gh['number']}"
    )
else:
    typer.echo(f"Note added to #{todo_id}")
